9e6e3277-ed59-4903-98c6-bb4766209af4	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b816-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Eastern Cape</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (other)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Lutho Mvelase</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Male</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">55–64</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Urban (city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Teacher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Storytelling (iintsomi) and praise poetry (izibongo)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Spiritual or religious activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Hypertension (high blood pressure)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1–2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Vegetarian</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">9e6e3277-ed59-4903-98c6-bb4766209af4</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I don&#x27;t have a gym—what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Every morning, I wake up like a clock, and I go straight to the kitchen. I think, 'What’s for breakfast?' My wife says, 'Lutho, the bread is still warm, and there’s a pot of milk.' I grab a slice of white bread and pour milk on it. I don’t even think about blood sugar. I just eat. 'Why would bread make me feel tired?' I ask myself. 'I’m a teacher, I need energy to teach.' But after a while, I feel weak, my hands shake, and I have to sit down. I don’t know why. I thought I was strong. Then I remember, I used to eat at the school canteen — they had rice and beans. That felt good. But today, I didn’t feel good. I just wanted to go to work and teach. I didn’t think about what I was eating. That’s when I started to wonder — is it the food or just me? Maybe I need to change what I eat.\n\nOne day, I went to the clinic. The nurse, a young woman named Nomsa, looked at me and said, 'Lutho, you have diabetes. Your body needs to know what to eat.' I said, 'But I eat what’s easy. I don’t want to change.' She smiled and said, 'Not everything is hard. We can start small. Try eating more beans, lentils, and green vegetables. No sugar, no white bread. These foods don’t make your blood sugar go up fast.' I said, 'But I don’t like beans. They taste bitter.' She said, 'Try them with a little salt and a bit of onion. It will taste better. And you can cook them with your old pot — the one you use for stew.' I said, 'Okay, I’ll try.' I didn’t believe it would help, but I wanted to go to the next meeting — a community health day — and I needed to feel strong.\n\nI tried beans the next day. I cooked them with onion and a little salt. I ate them with a piece of brown bread. But my wife said, 'Lutho, you didn’t eat the green beans — you only ate the beans.' I said, 'I didn’t know they were green beans.' I thought beans were just the same. The next day, I felt a little better. But I still drank milk and ate white bread in the evening. My feet started to feel hot. I felt tired. I thought, 'I must be doing something wrong.' Then I remembered — I have to go to the community health day next week. I need to be strong to speak about health. So I said to myself, 'I can’t fail this time. I’ll eat only what helps me stay clear and strong.' I started to plan meals. I made a list: beans, spinach, carrots, and lentils. I even cooked them with my old pot — just like Nomsa said.\n\nAfter a week, I started to feel different. I didn’t feel weak in the afternoon. I could walk to the church without resting. My wife said, 'Lutho, you look better. You’re not shaking anymore.' I said, 'Yes, I am. I eat what helps my body. I don’t eat white bread or sugar. I eat beans, green vegetables, and brown bread. I even made a stew with carrots and lentils — it tasted good. I told the community group at the health day, 'I used to think food didn’t matter. Now I know it does.' They all smiled. I felt proud. I didn’t just change my food — I changed how I think. I now know that small changes, like eating beans, can make a big difference. I will keep going. I will teach others how to eat well, too.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9e6e3277-ed59-4903-98c6-bb4766209af4_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9e6e3277-ed59-4903-98c6-bb4766209af4_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9e6e3277-ed59-4903-98c6-bb4766209af4_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9e6e3277-ed59-4903-98c6-bb4766209af4_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9e6e3277-ed59-4903-98c6-bb4766209af4_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9e6e3277-ed59-4903-98c6-bb4766209af4_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9e6e3277-ed59-4903-98c6-bb4766209af4_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9e6e3277-ed59-4903-98c6-bb4766209af4_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:33.850171+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Sizwe Mbeki is a 25–34 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Teacher, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: he has none. Religion: Christianity (Presbyterian). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Rugby. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: Anxiety or depression. Diet restrictions: Vegetarian. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 3–4 days. Support network: I live alone or have limited support. Health goals: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . his food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Sizwe Mbeki and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task t