r>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1d83e9e5-f70f-43d2-9e3d-4b6c95a70680</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Can I just walk for exercise, or should I do other types too?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You see, I'm Fatima Abubakar, and my days are filled with work and taking care of my family. I love eating pap en vleis with my siblings, but I've been feeling really tired lately. I have to take insulin injections because I have Type 2 Diabetes, but I'm not really sure what foods are good for me. The other day, I had to finish a big project at work, but I just couldn't focus. I was so exhausted that I had to take a nap, and I ended up missing the deadline. My boss was not happy with me, and now I have an even bigger project coming up. What am I going to do?\n\nI decided to go to the clinic to ask for help. The health worker, Sister Nomsa, was very kind and explained to me that I need to eat foods that won't make my blood sugar spike. She told me to try eating more brown rice, chicken, and vegetables like spinach and carrots. She also said I should drink more water and limit my intake of sugary drinks. I was a bit resistant at first, I mean, who doesn't love a cold glass of Coke on a hot day? But Sister Nomsa explained that it's all about balance and making small changes to my daily routine. She gave me a simple meal plan to follow, and I felt hopeful that I could make a change.\n\nSo, I started trying to follow the meal plan, but it wasn't easy. I had to adjust my routine and make time to cook healthy meals. I struggled at first, and I had a minor setback when I accidentally ate a whole bag of chips while watching TV. But I didn't give up. I knew I had to make a change, especially with the bigger project coming up. I started taking short walks during my lunch break and tried to eat more mindfully. It wasn't easy, but I persisted. My family was very supportive, and they even started joining me on my walks.\n\nIt's been a few weeks now, and I can see a gradual improvement in my energy levels. I'm not as tired as I used to be, and I'm able to focus more at work. I even finished the bigger project ahead of schedule, and my boss was very impressed. I feel more confident now, and I'm open to continuing to improve my health. I've started exploring different types of exercise, like yoga and swimming, and I've found that I really enjoy them. I'm grateful for the support of my family and Sister Nomsa, and I know that I can make even more positive changes to my lifestyle.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/1d83e9e5-f70f-43d2-9e3d-4b6c95a70680_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/1d83e9e5-f70f-43d2-9e3d-4b6c95a70680_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/1d83e9e5-f70f-43d2-9e3d-4b6c95a70680_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/1d83e9e5-f70f-43d2-9e3d-4b6c95a70680_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/1d83e9e5-f70f-43d2-9e3d-4b6c95a70680_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/1d83e9e5-f70f-43d2-9e3d-4b6c95a70680_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/1d83e9e5-f70f-43d2-9e3d-4b6c95a70680_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/1d83e9e5-f70f-43d2-9e3d-4b6c95a70680_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:29:34.129925+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "Llama-3.3-70B", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Chinelo Okafor is a 35-44 year old, \\nCulture: Hausa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Trader, lives in a Suburban (near city or town) in Kano. Disability: she has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Storytelling. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Halal. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 5-7 days. Support network: Community or religious group. Health goals: Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?. her food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Chinelo Okafor and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "crisis_avoidance", "audio_prompts": ["Fatima Abubakar sighing and rubbing her temples, with the sound of a keyboard typing in the background, and a faint noise of traffic outside, with Fatima Abubakar's voice saying \\"What am I going to do?\\" in a soft and worried tone.", "Sister Nomsa's voice, warm and gentle, explaining the meal plan to Fatima Abubakar, with a faint sound of a pen scratching on paper, and a quiet beeping of a machine in the background, with Fatima Abubakar's voice responding with uncertain questions.", "The sound of Fatima Abubakar's footsteps, with birds chirping and leaves rustling in the background, and the distant sound of children laughing, with Fatima Abubakar's voice saying \\"I can do this\\" in a determined tone.", "Fatima Abubakar's voice, happy and confident, talking to her family members, with the sound of utensils clinking and food being served, and a faint noise of a TV playing in the background, with Fatima Abubakar's voice saying \\"I'm so proud of myself\\" in a cheerful tone."], "image_prompts": ["Fatima Abubakar sitting at her desk, looking tired and frustrated, with a half-eaten plate of pap en vleis in front of her, in a medium shot, with a window showing a busy street in the background.", "Sister Nomsa, a middle-aged woman with a kind smile, standing in a clinic, with a white coat and a stethoscope around her neck, talking to Fatima Abubakar, who is sitting on an examination table, in a wide shot.", "Fatima Abubakar walking in a park, with her family members in the background, all smiling and chatting, on a sunny day, with trees and flowers surrounding them, in a wide shot.", "Fatima Abubakar sitting at her desk, looking happy and confident, with a healthy meal in front of her, and a certificate of achievement on the wall behind her, in a medium shot, with a cityscape visible through the window."], "language_code": "en-NG", "avg_time_taken": 7.72532412482471, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are the best types of exercise for a 35-44 year old female Hausa farmer with type 2 diabetes living in suburban Kano, Nigeria?\\",\\n  \\"Are there diabetes-friendly traditional Hausa dishes that are also lactose-free and easy to prepare with foods available in Kano?\\",\\n  \\"How does the climate and farming schedule in suburban Kano affect recommended times for outdoor exercise for women with moderate health conditions?\\",\\n  \\"Is there community support or exercise groups for Muslim women near Kano city who want to manage type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What are effective ways to incorporate physical activity into a busy farming routine for a woman in northern Nigeria?\\",\\n  \\"What are the risks of high alcohol consumption for women with type 2 diabetes in the Hausa community?\\",\\n  \\"Which local clinics or hospitals in Kano offer diabetes education or support programs targeted at Hausa women?\\",\\n  \\"Are there culturally accepted alternatives to walking for exercise that align with Islamic practices in suburban Kano?\\",\\n  \\"How can I balance farming work, family duties, and regular physical activity as a Hausa mother with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Which traditional herbs used in Hausa culture may interact with insulin or 