     </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Pharmacy or local chemist</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Only when necessary</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5-7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">01edd29e-df4f-4646-b8bd-27f96b725b75</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Amina, and I work at the market down by the river. It’s a busy place, always full of people buying and selling. I sell spices – turmeric, ginger, all sorts of lovely smells. Most days, I grab a quick pap en rusvet brood from the spaza shop near my house. It's cheap, and it fills my tummy. It’s easy, you know? But sometimes, after a long day shouting about prices, my legs feel all wobbly, and my head feels a bit funny. Like I’m walking in slow motion. I just put it down to being tired, honestly. It’s a simple life, isn’t it? But I always feel a bit sluggish.\n\nYesterday, Mr. Nkosi asked me to help him sort the fresh vegetables for the big order he’s taking to the hotel. He said it was important, and he needed someone who could work quickly. I wanted to do a good job, so I agreed. But I was still feeling a bit off. I grabbed a banana and a biscuit – that’s what I always do when I’m busy. But halfway through, I just… slumped. I couldn’t lift the heavy boxes of tomatoes, and my hands were shaking. Mr. Nkosi looked really disappointed. He said, ‘Amina, you need to take care of yourself. You can’t work well if you’re not feeling okay.’ It made me feel a bit silly, but he was right, wasn’t he?\n\nDr. Khumalo at the clinic explained that the food I was eating – the pap and biscuits – they can make my blood sugar go up and down. She said it’s important to eat things that won’t do that, like fruits and vegetables, and maybe some chicken or fish. She gave me a little booklet with pictures of healthy food. It looked complicated, to be honest. I asked her, ‘But how can I afford all this fancy food when I’m just selling spices?’ She smiled and said, ‘Small changes, Amina. Start with one thing at a time.’ She also told me to check my sugar levels regularly. It all felt a bit overwhelming, but I promised to try.\n\nI started small. Instead of a pap and biscuit, I grabbed an apple and a handful of nuts after my shift. It wasn’t as quick, but it felt… better. My legs didn’t feel so wobbly, and my head was clearer. Mr. Nkosi even noticed! He said, ‘Amina, you seem much brighter today. You’re working like a machine!’ And then, the big order came through – a huge delivery for the hotel. I sorted the vegetables with confidence, lifting the boxes easily. It wasn’t just about doing a good job; it was about feeling good while I did it. Now, I think I’ll even try adding some spinach to my meals. It’s a small step, but it feels like a big one. I'm feeling good, and that's what matters, isn't it?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/01edd29e-df4f-4646-b8bd-27f96b725b75_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/01edd29e-df4f-4646-b8bd-27f96b725b75_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/01edd29e-df4f-4646-b8bd-27f96b725b75_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/01edd29e-df4f-4646-b8bd-27f96b725b75_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/01edd29e-df4f-4646-b8bd-27f96b725b75_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/01edd29e-df4f-4646-b8bd-27f96b725b75_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/01edd29e-df4f-4646-b8bd-27f96b725b75_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/01edd29e-df4f-4646-b8bd-27f96b725b75_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:29:37.98311+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "gemma-3-4b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Nari Kazehara is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Hausa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Entrepreneur, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Kano. Disability: she has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Storytelling. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic