                 <td class="p-2">Rural (small community or countryside)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Limited access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Farmer</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Islamic study</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Spiritual or religious activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">â€” None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Only when necessary</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5-7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">d8f81a59-cfff-4106-a2bc-f43ecbc7ec55</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouragedâ€”how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Yakubu, and I live out here, near the big dusty road. It’s a good life, farming with my dad, growing millet and maize. We sell it at the market in Sokoto, you know? Most days, I start before the sun even thinks about waking up. I eat a bit of porridge – mostly millet, sometimes a little sugar if we have it – and then it’s straight to the fields. It’s hard work, pushing the plough with the ox. Sometimes, I feel tired, like my legs are heavy, but I keep going. My mum always says, ‘A farmer must work, Yakubu, or we won’t eat.’  Do you think it’s okay to just keep going, even when you feel like you can’t?  I take my insulin shots when they remind me, but honestly, I don’t always remember to take them. It’s just… a lot to think about, you see?\n\nYesterday, I was helping my brother, Musa, with the irrigation. We needed to dig a new channel to get water to the new field. It was hot, hotter than usual. I was sweating like a pig, and I felt that tiredness creeping in, like a stone in my legs. I tried to lift the shovel, but it felt like it weighed a ton! I dropped it, and it clattered on the ground. Musa just shook his head and said, ‘Yakubu, you need to look after yourself. You can’t work like this, not with your blood sugar.’ He’s right, of course, but it’s just… so much work.  I really wanted to get this channel finished before the rains came.  It's important, you know, to get the crops in on time.\n\nThe health worker, Alhaji Ibrahim, at the clinic, he was a good man. He listened to me talking about how tired I always felt. He said, ‘Yakubu, your body needs movement. Just a little bit, every day. A short walk, even just around the village, can make a big difference. And eat some fruits, some vegetables, not just millet all the time. It’s important to give your body the good stuff.’ He showed me a chart with little steps, saying I should start with 20 minutes, three times a week. He said, ‘It's like planting seeds, Yakubu. You have to water them, and give them time to grow.’ I nodded, but it sounded a bit complicated.  I was worried about slowing down work, but he said, ‘A healthy farmer works better, longer.’\n\nI started small. I walked to the well to fetch water	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d8f81a59-cfff-4106-a2bc-f43ecbc7ec55_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d8f81a59-cfff-4106-a2bc-f43ecbc7ec55_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d8f81a59-cfff-4106-a2bc-f43ecbc7ec55_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d8f81a59-cfff-4106-a2bc-f43ecbc7ec55_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d8f81a59-cfff-4106-a2bc-f43ecbc7ec55_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d8f81a59-cfff-4106-a2bc-f43ecbc7ec55_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d8f81a59-cfff-4106-a2bc-f43ecbc7ec55_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d8f81a59-cfff-4106-a2bc-f43ecbc7ec55_3.mp3}	How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?	exercise	male	consistency	2026-03-23 13:29:38.750122+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gemma-3-4b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Nari Kazehara is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Hausa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Entrepreneur, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Kano. Disability: she has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Storytelling. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Halal. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 0 days. Support network: Healthcare provider or counsellor. Health goals: Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?. her food access Limited access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Nari Kazehara and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "iteration", "audio_prompts": ["Sound of crickets chirping softly, overlaid with the distant sound of a plough being pulled. Yakubu Danladi, 28 years old, Hausa male, speaking in a slightly raspy voice, with a South African English accent. He says, ‘My name is Yakubu, and I live out here, near the big dusty road…’  His voice is low and thoughtful, with a slight pause after each sentence. Background ambience: Gentle breeze.", "Sound of shovels hitting earth, mixed with the sounds of a hot, dry wind. Yakubu Danladi, 29 years old, voice slightly strained, struggling to lift a shovel. He says, ‘Musa, it’s… it’s too heavy. I don’t feel so good.’ Musa’s voice, a calm and reassuring male voice, is heard in the background, ‘Yakubu, you need to look after yourself…’  The wind picks up slightly.", "Sound of a clinic interior - quiet, with the gentle hum of a fan and the muted voices of patients and Alhaji Ibrahim, 50s, a calm and reassuring male voice, speaking in Hausa. Yakubu Danladi, 30 years old, voice hesitant, asking a question. Alhaji Ibrahim responds in a clear, measured tone, ‘…and eat some fruits, some vegetables…’  Background: Soft, instrumental Islamic music.", "Sound of footsteps on dry earth, combined with the rustling of millet stalks. Yakubu Danladi, 31 years old, voice slightly stronger and more confident, as he walks with Musa. He says, ‘It’s not so bad, Musa. I feel… better.’ Musa’s voice, encouraging and supportive, is heard in the background.  The sound of birds chirping increases slightly, signifying the setting sun.”"], "image_prompts": ["Close-up photo of Yakubu Danladi, 28 years old, Hausa man, sweating slightly, wearing a worn cotton shirt and trousers, holding a small hoe in a dusty field at sunrise. Focus on the texture of the soil and the early morning light. Expression: Determined but weary.", "Medium shot of Yakubu Danladi, 29 years old, kneeling in the mud, attempting to lift a heavy shovel while his brother Musa, 30 years old, a Hausa man with a concerned expression, watches him. The background shows a partially dug irrigation channel and a hot, dusty landscape. Lighting: Harsh sunlight.", "Wide shot of Yakubu Danladi, 30 years old, sitting on a stool outside the local clinic in Sokoto. Alhaji Ibrahim, a middle-aged Hausa health worker, is pointing to a simple chart showing walking steps. Yakubu looks slightly apprehensive but attentive. The clinic is a small, simple building with a corrugated iron roof. Background: A busy market street.", "Medium shot of Yakubu Danladi, 31 years old, walking with Musa, 30 years old, through a millet field, both smiling. They are carrying woven baskets. The sun is setting, casting long shadows. Focus on the texture of the millet stalks and the golden light. Expression: Contentment and a hint of pride."], "language_code": "en-NG", "avg_time_taken": 9.490534574557573, "task_parent_id": "be77b90b-1abd-42c9-97ae-48cccb3dbdfb", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some culturally appropriate, gluten-free meal options for a Hausa Muslim farmer living in rural Sokoto, Nigeria with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How does regular physical activity, such as farming work, affect blood sugar levels for someone like Yakubu Danladi who has type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Ar