         \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">4767a5af-b950-4e38-b569-dfcefcad0f13</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I don&#x27;t have a gymâ€”what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	I’m Chinonso, a farmer who works the land every morning before the sun is high. I used to eat late at night — after work, when the day is done and the farm is quiet. I’d eat a piece of bread with a bit of meat, just to feel full. I never thought it would matter. 'What’s the harm?' I’d say to myself. 'I’m not sick, I’m just tired.' But then I started feeling dizzy at night, like my head was spinning. My hands would shake when I tried to pick up a bucket. I didn’t know then that eating late at night could make my blood sugar go up and down. I just thought it was the heat or the work. I didn’t know what insulin was for — I only knew it was something I had to take. So I kept eating late, and my body started to complain. And now, I’m wondering — if I stop eating late, will I still be strong enough to work the farm tomorrow? Or will I fall down like I did last week?\n\nOne day, I went to the clinic near the market. The nurse, Mrs. Nandi, was kind and said, 'Chinonso, you’re not eating right. Your blood sugar goes high at night, and that makes your body weak. You need to eat small meals during the day and stop eating after 7 p.m.' I said, 'But I’m a farmer. I work hard. I need energy.' She smiled and said, 'Yes, but your body needs balance. If you eat too much at night, your body can’t use the sugar properly. You’ll feel tired and weak.' I didn’t believe her at first. I thought, 'I’ve been doing this for years.' But she gave me a simple plan — eat a banana with a spoon of milk before bed, not bread or meat. And I agreed. I said, 'Okay, I’ll try it. But I don’t want to lose strength.' She said, 'You won’t. You’ll just be stronger in the morning.'\n\nI tried the banana and milk at first. I thought, 'This is too soft. I need something heavy.' So I ate a piece of bread with it, just to feel full. The next day, I felt dizzy again. I fell off the ladder while watering the plants. I was shaking. I knew I’d made a mistake. I didn’t want to fail. But then I remembered — next week, I have to help my cousin build a new fence on the farm. It’s a big job. I can’t fall and break a leg before that. So I told myself: 'I’ll do it again. I’ll eat only the banana and milk. No bread. No meat. Just what the nurse said.' I tried it again. And this time, I felt better. Not perfect, but better.\n\nAfter a week, I noticed a change. I didn’t feel dizzy at night. My hands didn’t shake. I woke up feeling strong. I could walk the farm without stopping. I even helped my cousin with the fence. We built it in two days — faster than usual. When I saw the fence, I smiled. I said, 'This is what happens when you listen to your body.' I didn’t think I’d ever feel this good. Now, I tell my friends — 'Don’t eat late at night. Your body will thank you.' I still take my insulin every day, but now I feel like I’m in control. I know what I need. And I know that small changes can make a big difference. I’m not just a farmer. I’m a man who listens to his body. And that’s how I stay strong.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/4767a5af-b950-4e38-b569-dfcefcad0f13_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/4767a5af-b950-4e38-b569-dfcefcad0f13_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/4767a5af-b950-4e38-b569-dfcefcad0f13_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/4767a5af-b950-4e38-b569-dfcefcad0f13_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/4767a5af-b950-4e38-b569-dfcefcad0f13_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/4767a5af-b950-4e38-b569-dfcefcad0f13_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/4767a5af-b950-4e38-b569-dfcefcad0f13_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/4767a5af-b950-4e38-b569-dfcefcad0f13_3.mp3}	I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? 	diet	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:29:39.900297+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Aisha Bello is a 35-44 year old, \\nCulture: Hausa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Trader, lives in a Suburban (near city or town) in Kano. Disability: she has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Storytelling. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Halal. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 1-2 days. Support network: Close friends. Health goals: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . her food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Aisha Bello and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "crisis_avoidance", "audio_prompts": ["Chinonso breathing slowly, voice soft and tired, saying 'I used to eat late at night... I never thought it would matter' while standing on a farm path, wind rustling dry grass, distant farm animals, low hum of a generator in the background, slight pause after'matter', volume fades slightly", "Chinonso speaking clearly to Mrs. Nandi, a woman in her 50s, voice calm and polite, 'I’m a farmer. I need energy' — she replies with a warm tone, 'Yes, but your body needs balance' — soft background of clinic sounds: a clock ticking, light footsteps, a radio playing a quiet song, Chinonso’s voice slightly hesitant at the end", "Chinonso breathing heavily, voice shaky, saying 'I fell off the ladder' — his voice drops, breaths short, a faint sound of wood creaking, the wind blowing, distant chickens crowing, a short pause before he says 'I’ll try again' — voice steadies slightly, more controlled", "Chinonso and Sipho working on the fence, Chinonso hammering wood with a steady rhythm, voice clear and confident, saying 'We built it in two days' — background of tools clinking, soil shifting, distant laughter from other farmers, Chinonso’s voice warm and proud, no pause, natural breathing"], "image_prompts": ["Chinonso standing on a dusty farm path at dusk, holding a plate of bread and meat, his eyes tired and slightly closed, one hand resting on a wooden fence, the setting sun casting long shadows on the cracked earth, wind blowing dry grass, dust floating in the air, light from the sun fading behind him", "Chinonso sitting on a plastic chair at a small clinic waiting room, a nurse named Mrs. Nandi standing beside him, both wearing simple clothes, a white clipboard in her hand, soft morning light through the window, Chinonso looking unsure but polite, one hand on his knee, the room has a few posters about diabetes and healthy food", "Chinonso standing on a farm ladder, holding a bucket of water, his hand shaking slightly, face pale, eyes wide with worry, the farm is surrounded by tall maize, the sky is grey and overcast, wind rustling the leaves, a small banana and cup of milk on a wooden table nearby, not in his hand", "Chinonso and his cousin Sipho, a young man in his 20s, working together to build a wooden fence on a farm, both wearing simple clothes, Chinonso holding a hammer, Sipho placing a plank, the sun is high, shadows long, soil and dirt on their boots, grass and dust in the air, Chinonso smiling slightly, hands steady, looking confident"], "language_code": "en-NG", "avg_time_taken": 63.62262771068475, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are simple vegan meal recipes using locally available foods in Kano for a Hausa urban resident like me?\\",\\n  \\"How can I maintain regular physical activity at home in Kano city without access to a gym, considering I have type 2 diabetes and work as a farmer?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any community fitness groups or outdoor exercise options in Kano that are culturally appropriate for young Hausa Muslim men?\\",\\n  \\"What are safe and effective ways to manage type 2 diabetes with lifestyle changes in an urban Nigerian context, especially when I also smoke tobacco weekly and rarely drink alcohol?\\",\\n  \\"How can I incorporate traditional Hausa stories or storytelling sessions into light physical activity with friends and family in Kano?\\",\\n  \\"What local resources or support groups exist in Kano for young adults with type 2 diabetes who are interested in a vegan diet?\\",\\n  \\"Are there Hausa traditional exercises or activities suitable for urban settings that help with diabetes management when physical activity is currently zero days per week?\\",\\n  \\"What everyday household items can I use for effective at-home workouts suitable for young male farmers in Kano?\\",\\n  \\"How does easy access to healthy foods in Kano urban areas influence vegan diet planning for someone with diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What are the cultural or religious considerations for participating in physical activity and exercise in Kano for a practicing Muslim?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any Islamic or community-led wellness initiatives in Kano that support physical activity and healthy lifestyles for young men?\\",\\n  \\"What practical tips exist for reducing tobacco smoking frequency and increasing physical activity for Hausa Muslims living in Nigerian cities like Kano?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": ["city_town", "name", "gender", "describe_location", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "t2d_status", "diet_restrictions", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
