                <td class="p-2">Suburban (near city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Entrepreneur</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Islamic study</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Spiritual or religious activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">E â€” Hypertension (high blood pressure)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5-7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">101f68b1-609c-4976-b05f-5ac6d5d94b07</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I like to snackâ€”what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	I'm Kazuki Nakamura, a 58-year-old man from Kano, and I run my own small business selling traditional Hausa goods. I wake up early, go to work, and come home to pray. I know I have diabetes and high blood pressure, but I don’t really think about how my snacks affect my work or my energy. I just eat what’s around — like roasted groundnuts or sweet dates — when I feel hungry. I don’t check my blood sugar, and I don’t plan snacks. I think, 'If I eat now, I’ll feel strong for the day.' But I don’t know that sugar spikes can make me tired or dizzy later. I just follow what I’ve always done.\n\nOne morning, I had a meeting with a health worker at the clinic. She said, 'You need to eat small, healthy snacks every 3 to 4 hours, not just when you’re hungry.' I said, 'But I don’t have time. I’m busy with my shop and prayers.' She smiled and said, 'Try a handful of roasted groundnuts or a slice of unripe plantain. No sugar, no milk. Just good, simple food.' I thought, 'Maybe I can do this.' But I didn’t believe it would help. I tried it once — after my prayer, I ate a few groundnuts — and I felt okay. But the next day, I forgot. I ate a whole date, and I felt dizzy. I told the health worker, 'I don’t know how to do this right.' She said, 'Just start small. One snack a day. You’ll get used to it.'\n\nI tried again, but I messed up. I had a big meeting at the shop, and I got so busy I skipped my snack. After that, I felt weak and my hands shook. I didn’t want to go to the clinic again. But then I heard that the city is hosting a big Islamic business fair in two weeks — I have to present my goods and talk to other entrepreneurs. I said to myself, 'If I don’t feel strong, I won’t be able to speak clearly or answer questions.' So I started again. I took a small handful of roasted groundnuts before work, and I ate a piece of unripe plantain after my prayer. It wasn’t easy — I had to stop eating sweet things. But I kept doing it. I felt better, and I didn’t get dizzy.\n\nNow, I eat a small, healthy snack every 3 to 4 hours. I don’t skip meals. I use roasted groundnuts, unripe plantain, or a few slices of banana. I check my blood sugar once a week and talk to the health worker. I even brought a small tin of roasted groundnuts to the business fair. When I spoke, I felt strong. The people listened. I didn’t feel dizzy. I felt proud. I told my friends, 'This is how I manage my health now.' I know I can do more — maybe add more vegetables or try baobab tea. But today, I feel confident. I know my body is strong, and I know I can keep going.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/101f68b1-609c-4976-b05f-5ac6d5d94b07_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/101f68b1-609c-4976-b05f-5ac6d5d94b07_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/101f68b1-609c-4976-b05f-5ac6d5d94b07_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/101f68b1-609c-4976-b05f-5ac6d5d94b07_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/101f68b1-609c-4976-b05f-5ac6d5d94b07_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/101f68b1-609c-4976-b05f-5ac6d5d94b07_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/101f68b1-609c-4976-b05f-5ac6d5d94b07_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/101f68b1-609c-4976-b05f-5ac6d5d94b07_3.mp3}	I like to snack—what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?	diet	male	consistency	2026-03-23 13:29:40.641502+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Aisha Bello is a 35-44 year old, \\nCulture: Hausa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Trader, lives in a Suburban (near city or town) in Kano. Disability: she has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Storytelling. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Halal. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 1-2 days. Support network: Close friends. Health goals: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . her food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Aisha Bello and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "resistance", "audio_prompts": ["Kazuki Nakamura sits on a wooden stool in a small shop in Kano, softly chewing a handful of roasted groundnuts. His breathing is slow and steady, with a slight pause between bites. The sound of a distant prayer call echoes faintly in the background. A faint hum of traffic from the street outside. His voice is soft, low, and slightly tired, with a quiet tone. He says, 'I don’t know if this will help.' The ambient sound is warm and dusty, with a subtle echo of market noise.", "Kazuki Nakamura stands in front of a clinic in Kano, talking to a young female health worker. The woman speaks clearly and calmly. Kazuki’s voice is hesitant, slightly low, with a pause after each sentence. He says, 'I don’t have time. I’m busy with my shop.' The background has soft footsteps and the sound of a clock ticking. The audio is clear, with a natural room tone, and a faint hum of a refrigerator in the background.", "Kazuki Nakamura sits on a wooden bench in a courtyard, holding a small plate with roasted groundnuts and unripe plantain. He takes a deep breath, then slowly eats a piece. His breathing is shallow and slow, with a slight tremor in his voice. He says, 'I forgot again. I didn’t eat.' The sound of wind and distant voices from the market is faint. The tone is tired, but not angry — just worn out from effort.", "Kazuki Nakamura stands in front of a crowd at a business fair in Kano, speaking into a microphone. His voice is clear, steady, and strong. He says, 'I feel strong today. I know my body is working.' The crowd is quiet, and there’s a soft applause. The background has the sound of people moving, a few laughs, and a distant radio. His breathing is even, and his tone is calm and confident. The audio is warm, natural, and full of pride."], "image_prompts": ["Kazuki Nakamura sits on a wooden stool in a small shop in Kano, wearing a white cotton shirt and a traditional Hausa cap, eating a handful of roasted groundnuts with his hands, while a pile of woven baskets and cloth goods are behind him. The shop is in a narrow alley near a mosque, with faded blue walls and sunlight streaming through a cracked window. Dust floats in the air, and the ground is slightly uneven. Kazuki looks tired, his eyes slightly closed, one hand resting on the table, the other holding the groundnuts. The texture of the groundnuts is visible — brown and cracked. Natural lighting, midday, dusty, realistic.", "Kazuki Nakamura stands in front of a clinic building in Kano, wearing a white shirt and a small cap, talking to a young female health worker in a green uniform. She is pointing at a chart with a pencil, and Kazuki is leaning slightly forward, one hand on his waist, eyes wide with doubt. The clinic is a small white building with a red door, and a few cars are parked outside. The sky is cloudy, and the air has a faint smell of medicine. Kazuki’s posture shows hesitation — shoulders slumped, head tilted slightly down. The setting is a public clinic in a suburban area, with a sign that says 'Diabetes & Hypertension Care'.", "Kazuki Nakamura sits on a wooden bench in a courtyard outside his shop, holding a small plate with a few roasted groundnuts and a piece of unripe plantain, while a group of men in traditional clothes walk past. One man is looking at him with a smile. Kazuki is frowning slightly, one hand touching his forehead, the other holding the plate. The courtyard is dusty, with old stone walls and a few hanging clothes. Sunlight hits the ground, casting long shadows. The plantain is slightly brown and soft, and the groundnuts are scattered. The scene feels tense and real, like a moment of struggle.", "Kazuki Nakamura stands confidently in front of a crowd at a business fair in Kano, wearing a clean white shirt and a red tie, speaking into a microphone. Behind him, a small table has a tin of roasted groundnuts and a plate of unripe plantain. A few people are listening, smiling. Kazuki is standing straight, hands open, eyes focused. The fair is in a large open space with colorful tents and banners. The sun is high, and the air is warm. The background shows a mix of Hausa flags and business signs. Kazuki’s face is calm, relaxed, and proud. He looks like he has earned his place."], "language_code": "en-NG", "avg_time_taken": 63.62262771068475, "task_parent_id": "3f85c39e-2497-49c2-8042-052c4557ce78", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some Hausa-culture inspired, lactose-free snack options suitable for someone managing diabetes and hypertension near Kano, Nigeria?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find easy-to-access stores or markets in suburban Kano with affordable, healthy snacks that fit a lactose-free and diabetic-friendly diet?\\",\\n  \\"How can I balance my Qurâ€™an recitation and Islamic study routine with recommendations for healthy snacking and blood sugar management?\\",\\n  \\"Are there local support groups or healthcare seminars in Kano for male entrepreneurs around 55â€“64 years old managing type 2 diabetes and hypertension?\\",\\n  \\"What are safe, herbal or traditional snack options commonly used in Hausa culture for people with diabetes and high blood pressure?\\",\\n  \\"How often should I have small snacks throughout the workday to help manage my blood sugar as an active, working adult following both Islamic teachings and dietary restrictions?\\",\\n  \\"What healthy snack recipes reflect Hausa food traditions but are also lactose-free and suitable for someone with type 2 diabetes in Nigeria?\\",\\n  \\"What advice do healthcare providers at local clinics in suburban Kano give about snacking frequency for older adults on insulin and oral medication?\\",\\n  \\"Are there commonly available snack options at religious gatherings or mosques in Kano that are suitable for someone with lactose intolerance and diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How does physical activity 5-7 days per week affect snack timing and choices for middle-aged men with type 2 diabetes and hypertension?\\",\\n  \\"What are the cultural considerations for snacking during Ramadan for a Muslim with diabetes, living in suburban areas near Kano?\\",\\n  \\"How do I use lifestyle changes and diet, alongside medication, to manage cravings for unhealthy snacks after religious or spiritual activities in Hausa communities?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "culture", "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
3546d696-5a1a-441f-99d6-965ae4ce9153	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b819-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Hausa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kano</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Islam</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Chinaza Okafor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">25-34</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Suburban (near city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Trader</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Islamic study</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Spiritual or religious activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">â€” None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5-7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I live alone or have limited support</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3546d696-5a1a-441f-99d6-965ae4ce9153</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	I am Chinaza, a Hausa woman from Kano. I work every day as a trader in the market, and I go to the same place every morning. I buy things like beans, millet, and fish. I cook with what I find. I know I have diabetes, and I take insulin every day. But I don’t always think about how my food and how I move affect my body. I used to think eating a big meal would make me strong, but now I see it makes me tired. I don’t know how to mix food with my work or how to move without hurting my body. I just do what I know. That’s how I live.\n\nOne day, I went to the clinic near my home. A kind nurse told me, 'Chinaza, your blood sugar is high, and you need to eat better and move more. You don’t need to stop working, but you can change how you eat and walk.' She said, 'Try small meals with vegetables and beans. And walk after your work, even just for ten minutes.' I said, 'But I don’t have time!' She smiled and said, 'You do. Just walk after you finish your work. It’s not much, but it helps your body feel better.' I didn’t say yes right away. I was scared it would take me away from my trade. But I remembered I have a big task coming — I need to open my own small shop soon, and I want to be strong for that.\n\nI tried walking after work. At first, I didn’t like it. My legs felt heavy. I used to walk to the market with my cart, and now I had to stop. I thought, 'Why can’t I just go straight to my stall?' One day, I forgot to walk, and I felt dizzy. My hands shook. I knew I had made a mistake. I went back to the clinic and asked the nurse again. She said, 'You just need to start small. Walk with your cart, just five minutes. Then you’ll get used to it.' I did it again. I walked slowly with my cart, and I felt better. I told myself, 'If I can do this, I can open my shop and be strong for my family.' That kept me going.\n\nNow, every day after work, I walk with my cart for ten minutes. I eat millet with spinach and a little fish. I don’t eat too much sugar. I feel more energy, and my hands don’t shake as much. I even shared my story with a few women in the market. They said, 'Chinaza, you are helping us too!' I feel proud. I know I can keep going. I will keep walking, keep eating well, and one day I will open my own shop — not just with money, but with health and strength. I am not just a trader. I am a woman who is learning to care for herself.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3546d696-5a1a-441f-99d6-965ae4ce9153_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3546d696-5a1a-441f-99d6-965ae4ce9153_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3546d696-5a1a-441f-99d6-965ae4ce9153_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3546d696-5a1a-441f-99d6-965ae4ce9153_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3546d696-5a1a-441f-99d6-965ae4ce9153_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3546d696-5a1a-441f-99d6-965ae4ce9153_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3546d696-5a1a-441f-99d6-965ae4ce9153_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3546d696-5a1a-441f-99d6-965ae4ce9153_3.mp3}	What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?	exercise	female	consistency	2026-03-23 13:29:40.895122+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Aisha Bello is a 35-44 year old, \\nCulture: Hausa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Trader, lives in a Suburban (near city or town) in Kano. Disability: she has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Storytelling. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Halal. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 1-2 d