  <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1-2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Lactose-free</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">2987c5d9-6f29-4481-b173-9c557c720cee</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	I wake up every morning, thinking about what to eat for breakfast. You see, I love reading and exercising, but I never thought about how food affects my body. I just eat whatever is available, like pap and stew, or sometimes leftovers from last night's dinner. But one day, I had a small task to complete, like tidying up my backyard garden. I started feeling tired and dizzy after just a few minutes of work. I thought to myself, "What's wrong with me? Why am I so tired?" I didn't finish the task, and it bothered me. I wondered, "Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?"\n\nThe next day, I decided to visit our local healthcare clinic. The nurse there, Sister Thembi, is a woman with a kind face and a gentle voice. She asked me about my diet and exercise routine. I told her about my love for reading and exercising, but also about my limited access to healthy foods. She explained to me, in simple terms, how some foods can make my blood sugar levels go up. She advised me to start eating more fruits, vegetables, and whole grains, like brown rice and whole-wheat bread. She also gave me a sample daily menu to follow. I was a bit resistant at first, thinking it would be hard to change my eating habits. But Sister Thembi reassured me, saying, "It's all about making small changes, Wanjiku. You can do it."\n\nI tried to follow Sister Thembi's advice, but it wasn't easy. I had to adjust my routine and find time to buy and prepare healthy foods. I struggled at first, and there were times when I went back to my old eating habits. But I reminded myself of the larger task I had to complete, like participating in our community's annual fun run. I wanted to be able to finish it without feeling tired and dizzy. So, I persisted, and slowly, I started to notice a difference. I had more energy, and I could exercise for longer periods without getting tired.\n\nAfter a few weeks of eating healthy and exercising regularly, I noticed a significant improvement in my overall health. I had more energy, and I could complete my daily tasks without feeling tired. I was confident that I could finish the fun run without any problems. And when the day of the fun run arrived, I was ready. I ran with my community, and it was an amazing feeling. I realized that taking care of my health was worth it, and I was open to continuing to make healthy changes in my life. I thought to myself, "I'm glad I learned about healthy eating and exercising. It's made a big difference in my life."	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/2987c5d9-6f29-4481-b173-9c557c720cee_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/2987c5d9-6f29-4481-b173-9c557c720cee_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/2987c5d9-6f29-4481-b173-9c557c720cee_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/2987c5d9-6f29-4481-b173-9c557c720cee_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/2987c5d9-6f29-4481-b173-9c557c720cee_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/2987c5d9-6f29-4481-b173-9c557c720cee_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/2987c5d9-6f29-4481-b173-9c557c720cee_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/2987c5d9-6f29-4481-b173-9c557c720cee_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	female	control	2026-03-23 13:10:35.782481+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "Llama-3.3-70B", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Wanjiku Mwangi is a 25-34 year old, \\nCulture: Kikuyu female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Accountant, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Kiambu. Disability: she has none. Religion: Christianity (Methodist). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Storytelling. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking