       <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">629d8c7e-ab94-46eb-b171-2d2f78932836</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouragedâ€”how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Man, life on the coffee farm isn’t easy, especially when you’re also trying to keep your body right. I liked snacking throughout the day when I worked, grabbing anything quick from the kitchen—usually chapati or mandazi because they’re tasty and filling. But honestly, I never thought much about how those snacks were hitting my sugar levels or how that could mess up my stamina on the farm. One afternoon, I had to help with sorting the coffee cherries, but I felt wiped out halfway through. I kept asking myself, ‘Why am I feeling so tired already?’ Turns out, those quick snacks weren’t doing me any favours. How was I supposed to keep going if I kept picking the wrong foods?\n\nSo, I went to the local clinic in Kiambu, hoping to get some advice that made sense for a coffee farmer like me. The nurse was patient, explaining that snacking isn’t the enemy, but what you snack on and how often really makes a difference. She suggested I try small portions of roasted groundnuts or fresh pawpaw instead of the usual fried treats. She said snacking twice or three times a day, spaced out, can keep my energy steady without spiking my blood sugar. It wasn’t all about cutting out food I love, but choosing better options and not overdoing it. Still, I wasn’t sure it would be easy to change—why fix what I’ve always done, right?\n\nI decided to give it a go, swapping out the mandazi for roasted peanuts on a busy day. But wow, it wasn’t smooth sailing. Sometimes I forgot and grabbed my usual snacks because that’s just what I was used to. One morning, I felt sleepy during planting, almost wiped out again, and I realized changing habits on the farm was tougher than I thought. Still, I thought about the big coffee pruning project coming up next week—it needs me strong and focused. That thought kept me pushing to try better snacks and pace myself, even if it felt like a fight.\n\nSlowly things started to get better. With healthier snacks, I noticed less energy crashes and could keep working longer without feeling drained. During the big pruning task, I was more alert and even felt good after a long day outside. I’m still figuring out the rhythm, but now I’m open to learning more about how small things like snacking can make a big difference. Maybe managing my diabetes doesn’t have to be a battle—it can be part of the daily rhythm that helps me stay strong on the farm.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/629d8c7e-ab94-46eb-b171-2d2f78932836_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/629d8c7e-ab94-46eb-b171-2d2f78932836_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/629d8c7e-ab94-46eb-b171-2d2f78932836_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/629d8c7e-ab94-46eb-b171-2d2f78932836_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/629d8c7e-ab94-46eb-b171-2d2f78932836_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/629d8c7e-ab94-46eb-b171-2d2f78932836_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/629d8c7e-ab94-46eb-b171-2d2f78932836_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/629d8c7e-ab94-46eb-b171-2d2f78932836_3.mp3}	I like to snack—what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?	diet	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:10:40.362333+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Wanjiku Njoroge is a 35-44 year old, \\nCulture: Kikuyu female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Commercial farmer, lives in a Suburban (near city or town) in Murang'a. Disability: she has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Horticulture. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: Anxiety or depression. Diet restrictions: Halal. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 3-4 days. Support network: Family (parents, siblings, children). Health goals: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . her food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Wanjiku Njoroge and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story p