of Kiambu. I'm a retail trader, working long hours, and relying on my energy to manage my store. I've been feeling tired lately, but I just thought it was due to the stress of running a business. I don't think much about my diet; I just eat what's available and affordable. My typical day starts with a quick breakfast, maybe some tea and a piece of bread, and then I'm off to the market. I try to eat lunch at work, but it's often a snack or leftovers from the previous night. I don't really pay attention to what I'm eating; as long as it's filling, I'm good. I've been experiencing some minor health issues, like fatigue and occasional dizziness, but I just write it off as stress. I'm not sure what I can do to improve my health, but I know I need to make some changes. My main priority is my work, and I'm not sure how to balance that with taking care of myself.\n\nI decided to visit the local health clinic to get some advice on how to manage my fatigue. The health worker listened to me and asked some questions about my diet and lifestyle. She explained that my symptoms could be related to a lack of essential nutrients and recommended some changes to my diet. She suggested that I start eating more vegetables and fruits, and to limit my intake of processed foods. She also recommended that I take a multivitamin and to drink plenty of water. I was a bit skeptical at first, but she explained everything in a way that made sense to me. She also offered to provide me with some recipes and nutrition tips to help me get started. I left the clinic feeling hopeful and determined to make some changes.\n\nI tried to implement some of the changes the health worker recommended, but it's been tough. I've been trying to eat more vegetables and fruits, but it's hard to break the habit of eating what's convenient. I also found it challenging to limit my intake of processed foods, especially when I'm in a rush. I did manage to start taking a multivitamin, and I've been drinking more water. However, I've been struggling to stick to my new routine. I've had a few setbacks, like when I forgot to pack a healthy lunch and had to settle for a snack. But overall, I'm trying my best to make these changes a part of my daily life.\n\nIt's been a few weeks since I started making changes to my diet and lifestyle, and I can already feel the difference. I have more energy, and my symptoms have subsided. I'm not perfect, and I still have days when I slip up, but I'm getting better at managing my health. I've learned to prioritize my well-being and make adjustments to my routine as needed. I'm proud of myself for taking control of my health, and I'm excited to see how far I can go. I know it's a journey, and I'm committed to making it a lifestyle change.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/f14e4600-b266-4e61-8203-a161800d9d02_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/f14e4600-b266-4e61-8203-a161800d9d02_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/f14e4600-b266-4e61-8203-a161800d9d02_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/f14e4600-b266-4e61-8203-a161800d9d02_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/f14e4600-b266-4e61-8203-a161800d9d02_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/f14e4600-b266-4e61-8203-a161800d9d02_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/f14e4600-b266-4e61-8203-a161800d9d02_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/f14e4600-b266-4e61-8203-a161800d9d02_3.mp3}	What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:10:38.220098+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "Llama-3.1-8B", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Kendi M