d">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">e3211ea1-e90e-4299-82fe-c16d0522df98</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, when I first began dealing with this diabetes thing, I barely thought about how what I eat would mess with my sugar levels. I mean, I’d enjoy my same sadawi tea with too much sugar and the usual chapati with white flour, thinking, ‘It’s just food, hey?’ Then one morning, I had to help with a big family event, setting up chairs and serving food, but I felt all dizzy and weak halfway through. Can you believe it? I just didn’t get how those heavy carbs were hitting me like that. My small job of helping run the event nearly became a big mess because I wasn’t careful with food. So, what’s a girl to do when she wants to help but ends up feeling so off?\n\nLuckily, I got a chance to chat with Nurse Thandi from the clinic. I was a bit skeptical, thinking, 'This old advice again?' but she broke it down nice and easy. She said, 'Wanjiku, try swapping your white bread with whole grain, eat more veggies like spinach and carrots you can find at the market, and keep your fruit to small portions like pawpaw or guava – those won't spike your sugar too much.' She even told me that traditional ugali made from maize meal could be better if I ate smaller portions and paired it with my favourite beans. That was something I could do, right? The advice felt more like a friendly chat than a lecture, which made me think, maybe I can try this.\n\nSo, I started changing my meals bit by bit, but gee, it wasn’t easy. I kept forgetting to take smaller portions or worried about leaving some of my usual favorites behind. One time, I even got tempted back to my old sadawi with heaps of sugar – and, wah, my sugar climbed high again. But I told myself, there’s that big community gathering coming up next month, and now I want to be strong for it. If I let diabetes win, I might not keep up with my friends or help like I used to. So I kept trying, even when it wasn’t perfect, because that gathering means a lot to me.\n\nNow, things are looking brighter. I can feel my body working better, and those dizzy spells have shrunk a lot. At that big gathering last weekend, I was able to help without slowing down – even enjoyed a small plate of my favorite ugali and veggies without worry. It feels good to be in control, you know? I’m ready to keep learning how to eat right and stay strong, because if I can handle that gathering well, what can’t I do, right? This journey is just starting, but I’m feeling hopeful and ready.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e3211ea1-e90e-4299-82fe-c16d0522df98_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e3211ea1-e90e-4299-82fe-c16d0522df98_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e3211ea1-e90e-4299-82fe-c16d0522df98_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e3211ea1-e90e-4299-82fe-c16d0522df98_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e3211ea1-e90e-4299-82fe-c16d0522df98_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e3211ea1-e90e-4299-82fe-c16d0522df98_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e3211ea1-e90e-4299-82fe-c16d0522df98_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e3211ea1-e90e-4299-82fe-c16d0522df98_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:10:38.486868+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Wanjiku Njoroge is a 35-44 year old, \\nCulture: Kikuyu female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Commercial farmer, lives in a Suburban (near city or town) in Murang'a. Disability: she has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Horticulture. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or tr