ccess to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Tea farmer</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Volleyball</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">6d0d7e36-9dba-4f8c-80f5-61b01af2bb6b</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, at first I thought, 'I love working on the tea farm, and I walk a lot — isn’t that enough?' I’d be up early, picking and sorting the leaves, sometimes skipping breakfast, and then grabbing something quick and sweet to keep going. One day, we had a big delivery deadline, and I just pushed through without paying attention to how tired I was or if my legs felt shaky. I tried rushing more, thinking my body can handle it, but when the time came, I couldn’t finish the harvest on time. I asked myself, 'Why did this happen? Am I not strong enough anymore?' I started worrying if maybe my way of staying active wasn’t enough to keep me healthy."\n\nLuckily, there’s a clinic near my home where a nurse I trust told me something that made sense. She said, 'Wanjiku, walking on the farm is great, but if you want to keep your blood sugar steady, you need to exercise regularly — about 30 minutes, five days a week, but at a pace that keeps your heart happy, not tired.' She showed me some simple moves I could do before or after work, and reminded me how important it was to eat balanced meals. At first, I thought, ‘Thirty minutes? Five times? Can I really do that?’ But she explained it step by step, and said, ‘Start small, listen to your body, and before you know it, you’ll feel stronger.’ That made the idea less scary and more like something I could manage."\n\nSo, I tried. Early mornings, I took short walks around my neighborhood, sometimes just 10 minutes to start. But, I’ll be honest, some days I just wanted to sleep longer, or I felt too tired after farming. There was one morning when I skipped the walk and felt less energetic throughout the day. I almost gave up, thinking, 'Maybe this exercise plan isn’t for me.' But then I remembered the big tea harvesting coming up next month — I had to be ready, not weak. That was my push to keep trying, even when it was tough to make new habits."\n\nGradually, things started to change. I could walk longer without feeling tired, and my energy stayed up during workdays. When the big harvest came, I finished on time, even helped some of my friends when they were tired. That felt good — like I was doing something right. Now, I’m even thinking about joining a community dance group for more fun exercises. It’s nice feeling in control of my health and knowing I’m taking steps so this invisible enemy called diabetes won’t slow me down. So tell me, isn’t it better to move a little every day than to wait for problems to start?"	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6d0d7e36-9dba-4f8c-80f5-61b01af2bb6b_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6d0d7e36-9dba-4f8c-80f5-61b01af2bb6b_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6d0d7e36-9dba-4f8c-80f5-61b01af2bb6b_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6d0d7e36-9dba-4f8c-80f5-61b01af2bb6b_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6d0d7e36-9dba-4f8c-80f5-61b01af2bb6b_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6d0d7e36-9dba-4f8c-80f5-61b01af2bb6b_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6d0d7e36-9dba-4f8c-80f5-61b01af2bb6b_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6d0d7e36-9dba-4f8c-80f5-61b01af2bb6b_3.mp3}	How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:10:38.910845+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Wanjiku Njoroge is a 35-44 year old, \\nCulture: Kikuyu female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Commercial farmer, lives in a Suburban (near city or town) in Murang'a. Disability: she has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Horticulture. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes on