ue but persistence, 'Maybe this exercise plan isn’t for me,' followed by a pause, then determination in tone saying, '...I had to be ready.' Nearby sounds of children playing faintly in distance.", "Lively outdoor sounds: people talking softly and laughing, rustling tea leaves. Wanjiku’s voice warm and inviting, optimistic saying, 'It’s nice feeling in control of my health...' Background includes rhythmic tapping of feet and ambient village life noises, capturing a community in positive harmony."], "image_prompts": ["Medium shot of Wanjiku Mwangi, a young female tea farmer, looking tired and frustrated while sitting on a wooden crate in a modest urban farm courtyard. There are tea leaves scattered around, morning sunlight casting soft shadows, showing texture on her worn clothes and her slumped posture reflecting exhaustion.", "Medium shot of Wanjiku Mwangi sitting on a clinic bench listening attentively to a female nurse. They are in a small, bright clinic room with posters about health on the walls. Wanjiku leans forward, expression curious yet hesitant, as the nurse gestures with her hands explaining exercises. Natural daylight illuminates the scene with gentle warmth.", "Wide shot of Wanjiku Mwangi walking slowly on an urban dirt path early morning, wearing a simple exercise outfit. She looks slightly tired but determined, glancing toward the rising sun. Sparse grass and city buildings appear in the background as a soft breeze ruffles her hair. Her hands are loosely swinging, body posture showing effort to stay motivated.", "Medium shot of Wanjiku Mwangi smiling confidently while carrying a basket full of fresh tea leaves on her head during a busy harvest. She stands upright, sunlight highlighting her glowing face and solid figure, with fellow community members working in the background. The scene captures a joyful, proud moment of accomplishment."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 3.350223708152771, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some diabetes-friendly exercise options suitable for a 25-34 year-old female tea farmer living in urban Kiambu, Kenya?\\",\\n  \\"How can a working adult Muslim woman in Kenya incorporate physical activity into her daily routine without a current exercise habit?\\",\\n  \\"Are there Kikuyu cultural practices or community programs in Kiambu that support women with diabetes in starting physical activities?\\",\\n  \\"What types of group or community-based exercise sessions are available in urban Kiambu for Muslim women observing halal dietary restrictions?\\",\\n  \\"Are there safe volleyball clubs or womenâ€™s sports groups in Kiambu town for adults managing type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What modifications are recommended for volleyball training to ensure safety for a female tea farmer with type 2 diabetes who uses both insulin and oral medications?\\",\\n  \\"How can I manage my blood sugar levels during physically demanding tea farming activities in Kiambu while following a halal diet?\\",\\n  \\"Are there popular healthy Kenyan urban halal recipes suitable for young diabetic women in Kikuyu culture?\\",\\n  \\"What are the best times and safe locations for outdoor exercise for women in urban Kiambu, considering cultural and religious factors?\\",\\n  \\"How does rare alcohol consumption impact the health of a young female with type 2 diabetes in Kenyaâ€™s urban context?\\",\\n  \\"What local support groups or religious organizations in Kiambu help Muslim women stay active and manage diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there affordable diabetes education or fitness resources offered at Kiambuâ€™s local healthcare clinics for working adults?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "The parts of Wanjiku's life that really matter here are her job as a tea farmer in an urban setting, her regular exercise habits, and her strong connection to a community group. She's healthy otherwise, but she doesn't have much knowledge about exercise specific to managing blood sugar. Also, she doesn’t smoke or drink, which helps. What she really wonders is how often and how long she should exercise to keep her health in check and not let diabetes sneak up on her.", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "occupation", "activities", "health_conditions", "healthcare_frequency", "tobacco", "alcohol", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
cc035df1-0550-43dc-aaf0-3ed9e1b42437	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b817-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kiambu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Apostolic)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Wairimu Muthoni</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">18-24</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Suburban (near city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Coffee farmer</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Farming and Gardening</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1-2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Vegan</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">cc035df1-0550-43dc-aaf0-3ed9e1b42437</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I like to snackâ€”what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	One bright morning, I had a simple task—water the garden, just like any other day. It looked easy, right? But halfway through carrying my big blue watering can, my arms started shaking. My heart raced, I felt so tired, and I had to sit under our old mango tree. Sitting there, I asked myself, is it because I skip exercise? Maybe those extra ugali snacks aren’t helping me. I finished the job slowly, and the plants were not as green and happy as before. I knew that I had a bigger job—harvesting the ripe coffee beans next week.\n\nThe next day, I decided to speak to Nurse Waweru at our local clinic, since I go there often. She smiled kindly and listened, then said, 'Wairimu, you don’t need a gym to keep strong for farming! Use what’s around you. Try lifting small buckets when weeding, do short walks up your hill, and even make sweeping the path a fun exercise. And remember, snack on roasted nduma or fruits, not always bread.' I hesitated, feeling awkward—the garden tools were heavy! 'Will this really make a difference?' I asked. She nodded and patted my shoulder, 'Consistency is all, Wairimu. Little by little, you be stronger.'\n\nThat afternoon, I tried carrying small buckets for my garden routine and swept the path for longer. My muscles ached and sweat dripped down my back! Old ways are hard to change, and it was so tempting to collapse with a sugary snack. But I thought about the big harvest ahead—how will I manage heavy coffee baskets if I don’t build up today? I took a deep breath, grabbed some sweet potatoes, and kept going, one step at a time.\n\nDay by day, my arms felt less shaky. My energy started to grow, and I noticed I finished chores faster. The week passed quickly, and soon it was time for the coffee harvest. With my family around, I lifted baskets with a new confidence. I even showed my younger brother how to do stretches under the avocado tree! That evening, I laughed with my parents, feeling proud. Now, I believe I can keep getting healthier—even without a gym. Isn’t it funny how small changes lead to big things?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/cc035df1-0550-43dc-aaf0-3ed9e1b42437_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/cc035df1-0550-43dc-aaf0-3ed9e1b42437_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/cc035df1-0550-43dc-aaf0-3ed9e1b42437_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/cc035df1-0550-43dc-aaf0-3ed9e1b42437_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/cc035df1-0550-43dc-aaf0-3ed9e1b42437_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/cc035df1-0550-43dc-aaf0-3ed9e1b42437_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/cc035df1-0550-43dc-aaf0-3ed9e1b42437_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/cc035df1-0550-43dc-aaf0-3ed9e1b42437_3.mp3}	I don't have a gym—what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:10:41.232331+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Nia Wanjiru is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Kikuyu female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Commercial farmer, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Murang'a. Disability: she has none. Religion: Atheism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Farming and Gardening. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: Anxiety or depression. Diet restrictions: Lactose-free. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 1-2 days. Support network: Community or religious group. Health goals: Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?. her food access Limited access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Nia Wanjiru and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "iteration", "audio_prompts": ["There’s only Wairimu Muthoni nearby. You hear her breathing heavily, her sandals scraping against hard red soil as she gently sets down a sloshing watering can. The leaves above rustle softly in a Kiambu breeze. Wairimu mutters quietly to herself, her voice soft and slightly frustrated, ‘Eish, why am I so tired already? I just started.’ Her breath is unsteady and there’s a pause as a bird chirps overhead.", "Nurse Waweru, female, stands near Wairimu in a quiet, sunlit clinic room. Medium distance: Nurse Waweru’s voice is calm and reassuring, ‘Wairimu, you can get strong right here at home, no gym needed,’ she says with a gentle Kikuyu accent. Wairimu sits calmly, replying, ‘Will this really help, Nurse?’ There are soft echoes in the small room as chairs scrape faintly. The tone is warm and instructive, with a quiet hum from distant hospital activity.", "Wairimu Muthoni is outdoors, panting lightly with the effort of lifting buckets. The faint crunch of gravel is heard as she walks, sweat audible as she exhales between words. She whispers with determination, ‘Let me try again… just one more time,’ her steps steady, background chickens clucking, and the quiet ring of a metal bucket tapping the path. The sound is mid-range, persistent, honest with audible effort.", "Wairimu Muthoni is outside with her younger brother near the avocado tree. Wide stereo: Both are laughing cheerfully, their voices overlapping as Wairimu counts stretches aloud, 'One, two, three!', her brother echoing with giggles. Baskets are shuffled and there’s a soft thump of beans. The garden birds chirp and distant family voices carry through the evening air, giving a happy, natural atmosphere."], "image_prompts": ["Wairimu Muthoni, a young Kikuyu woman, sits under a tall, old mango tree in a suburban Kiambu garden, her body slightly slumped and a half-filled blue watering can resting beside her. The sunlight filters through green leaves, making patterns on the rich red soil and her work clothes. Around her, the plants look slightly wilted. She has sweat on her brow and stares at her hands, unsure and slightly frustrated.", "Nurse Waweru, a middle-aged Kikuyu woman, stands in a tidy local clinic room with Wairimu Muthoni. Medium shot: Nurse Waweru brings a gentle smile, one hand on Wairimu’s shoulder, while Wairimu sits straight on a plastic chair, hands folded, looking uncertain but hopeful. Natural light streams in from a single window. Clinic posters line the plain walls, and a stethoscope hangs nearby. The scene is warm and supportive.", "Wairimu Muthoni, dressed in casual farming clothes, is outdoors in her tidy suburban garden. Medium shot: She carries two small water buckets in both hands, arms tense but determined, sweat visible on her forehead. Behind her, the freshly swept path winds between green plants. She stands in the sunlight, biting her lip with effort but focused on her task.", "Wairimu Muthoni, with her younger brother (teenage Kikuyu boy), is in their garden during the late afternoon. Wide shot: Both are stretching playfully under a broad avocado tree, baskets and freshly picked coffee beans nearby on the ground. Wairimu laughs, arms raised above her head, while her brother copies her pose. The fading sunlight adds a golden glow to their happy faces and rustic suburban home in the background."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 2.2845958173274994, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some gluten-free snack options using local foods available in Kiambu, Kenya for diabetic women?\\",\\n  \\"How often can someone with type 2 diabetes snack while managing blood sugar through both medication and lifestyle changes?\\",\\n  \\"What traditional Kikuyu snacks are naturally gluten-free and suitable for a young female coffee farmer with diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there recipes for healthy, easy-to-prepare snacks that fit a busy working schedule for a coffee farmer in suburban Kiambu?\\",\\n  \\"How can physical activity be incorporated into the daily routine of a female coffee farmer with type 2 diabetes in Kiambu province?\\",\\n  \\"What local community programs or clinics in Kiambu offer diabetes-friendly nutrition counseling, especially for young women?\\",\\n  \\"What fruits or vegetables grown in the Kiambu area are best for gluten-free, diabetic-friendly snacks?\\",\\n  \\"Does Apostolic Christian practice in the Kikuyu community influence traditional snacking habits for women with diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What are the best ways to involve my family in healthy snacking habits that support my diabetes management in a Kikuyu household?\\",\\n  \\"How do weather and farming seasons in Kiambu affect access to healthy, gluten-free snack options for local farmers?\\",\\n  \\"Are there support groups for young women with diabetes in the Kikuyu community near Kiambu that focus on healthy eating habits?\\",\\n  \\"How can I balance frequent coffee farming tasks with eating healthy snacks without disrupting blood sugar control?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "My name is Wairimu Muthoni. I’m a young coffee farmer in Kiambu, living in a nice house just a matatu-ride from town. My days are always busy—farming, gardening, and making sure my insulin stays cool in the fridge. I get support from family, and I visit the clinic every year. Still, even with all that, I sometimes forget that small health choices can make my work easier—or harder! And truth be told, I’m always snacking, even when I’m not sure if it’s best for my health. I really love to be outside, but I wonder, what exercise can I do if I don’t have a gym?", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
