nt-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1-2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Lactose-free</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">f4c83734-f972-4328-ae1f-1cff387b6349</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, every morning I wake up ready to teach the young ones in our small village school near Murang'a. But sometimes, with the little food we have and the busy days, I don’t pay much mind to my diabetes until later. I remember once, I had to show the children some math exercises outside, but because I hadn’t eaten well and was a bit tired, I couldn’t keep up. Felt like I let them down, but I thought, can exercise really help me if I’m this exhausted all the time?\n\nThen one day at the local clinic, Nurse Wanjiku explained to me that simple exercises like walking or easy stretches can actually help my blood sugar stay steady. She laughed and told me, 'Kamau, you don’t have to run a marathon. Just start with small steps.' She gave me tips that fit into our daily life here—like walking slowly to visit neighbors or doing gentle movements before I begin teaching. I was unsure at first, wondering if I could keep up, but her smile gave me hope.\n\nI tried to add those small walks after school, but sometimes I felt sleepy or rushed, and I’d skip them. Once, after a late supper that I wasn’t supposed to have, my sugar was high, and I felt weak during class again. But I remembered what Nurse Wanjiku said about not giving up. The bigger task was coming: preparing all my classes without feeling tired or dizzy. That thought kept me going, even when the routine felt tough to change.\n\nNow, after a few weeks, I feel a little stronger. I can walk slowly with my friends in the afternoon and even do some stretches before school starts. The children notice I’m more alert, and I’m proud I finished the term well. I still have more to learn, but maybe, just maybe, I can keep improving my health while teaching the young ones. Who would’ve thought that simple steps would make such a difference?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/f4c83734-f972-4328-ae1f-1cff387b6349_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/f4c83734-f972-4328-ae1f-1cff387b6349_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg