I head outside to water the small sukuma wiki garden behind my flat. One day, I rushed through my garden work to help my neighbour trim their fence, but after a few minutes, I started feeling tired and dizzy. "Eh, why am I sweating like this when others are still working?" I wondered. I realized maybe I underestimated how my health affects my daily work.\n\nAfter lunch, I went to the local clinic for my regular checkup. The nurse, Fatuma, greeted me and asked, "Mwangi, have you been keeping active?" I shrugged and admitted, "Sometimes, I just walk when I feel like it." She smiled and explained, "With diabetes, walking is a good start. But adding easy exercises like gentle stretching, cycling, or even gardening can help you control your sugar and keep your energy up. Try twenty minutes each day. Are you willing to try?" I laughed shyly, unsure if I could change, but at least now I knew where to start.\n\nThe next morning, I put on my sneakers and decided to walk a bit longer before watering the garden. I tried some gentle squats and stretches Fatuma showed me, but it felt strange and slow. After two days, I skipped because it felt awkward, and my legs were sore. But I remembered that next week, my mosque was hosting a big community clean-up, and I wanted to be strong for that work. So I pushed through, even if my movements were funny.\n\nDay by day, the walks grew easier and my energy started to last longer. The community clean-up day arrived, and I was ready. I worked alongside my friends—lifting, raking, and chatting. I didn’t get tired so quickly or feel dizzy. Afterwards, I felt proud and even thought, "Maybe I’ll try cycling next!" I am still learning, but now, I believe I can keep taking small steps for my health, and my work will not hold me back.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/bc31eba8-d32e-4a02-862d-fc509af8e6fe_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/bc31eba8