scrape the ground, kids shout nearby, and someone offers chai in the background."], "image_prompts": ["Mwangi Kamau, a young Kikuyu male in his early twenties, stands in his modest urban backyard garden in Kiambu, pouring water from a plastic jug onto bright green sukuma wiki plants. Morning sunlight glows through the concrete wall. Mwangi is sweating lightly, a puzzled look as he clutches his side for support, with modest apartment flats and wire fencing in the background (medium shot).", "Female nurse Fatuma, mid-thirties, stands beside Mwangi Kamau inside a small, clean local clinic consultation room. Fatuma is pointing at a colorful exercise poster on the wall, while Mwangi sits on a plastic chair, holding his chin and nodding with a shy, unsure smile. Sunlight filters through barred windows, casting soft shadows (medium shot).", "Mwangi Kamau in sportswear steps outside the entrance of his apartment building early in the morning. He’s mid-stretch, gently touching his toes, with awkward posture but trying hard. His face shows determination despite being a little uncomfortable. Laundry lines and parked boda-bodas are visible behind him (medium shot).", "Mwangi Kamau and his friend, Joseph, a male in his twenties, are captured sweeping a dusty curb with community members in the background, during a sunny clean-up day. Mwangi leans forward with confident posture, smiling broadly, broom in hand. Joseph laughs, clapping Mwangi’s back, as laughter and energy fill the daylight streets of urban Kiambu (wide shot)."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 2.2845958173274994, "task_parent_id": "37438852-4d69-4e1f-9027-3741a99069b0", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are the most accessible and safe places for a young male horticulture farmer with diabetes to exercise in urban Kiambu, Kenya?\\",\\n  \\"What types of physical activities besides walking are culturally popular and suitable for Kikuyu Muslims living in Kiambu's urban areas?\\",\\n  \\"Are there group exercise programs in Kiambu specifically for young adults with type 2 diabetes who attend local clinics?\\",\\n  \\"What are some recommended exercise routines for horticulture farmers with type 2 diabetes who can only exercise 1-2 days per week?\\",\\n  \\"How does being a Kikuyu Muslim in Kiambu affect access to community-based fitness initiatives or support for diabetes management?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find healthy food markets or nutrition support groups in urban Kiambu that cater to diabetes-friendly diets with no restrictions?\\",\\n  \\"Are there storytelling groups or cultural activities in Kiambu that promote physical well-being for young Muslim men?\\",\\n  \\"What are the best exercise options for diabetics living in urban Kenyan areas with easy access to healthy foods?\\",\\n  \\"How can I integrate brief exercise routines into my daily work as a horticulture farmer in Kiambu town?\\",\\n  \\"Does the local mosque or Muslim community in Kiambu offer any sports or physical wellness activities for young adults?\\",\\n  \\"What success stories exist for young male Kikuyu horticulture farmers in Kenya who manage diabetes through a mix of exercise types?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any local traditional herbal practices in Kiambu that support diabetes management when combined with modern exercise and medication?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "I’m Mwangi Kamau, a young horticulture farmer, living in Kiambu town. I have type 2 diabetes and follow a Halal diet. I use insulin, metformin, and herbal medicine, but sometimes get confused about what exercises are safe for me. As a working adult, I go for clinic checkups, try to eat healthy, and exercise once or twice a week. I’m active in my mosque and community, and my big health question is: is walking enough, or should I do other exercises for my diabetes?", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
a1a4a0f5-23c3-41dd-8880-1d0f5526be4a	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b817-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Murang&#x27;a</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Agnosticism</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Wanja Mwangi</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">18-24</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Rural (small community or countryside)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Limited access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Coffee farmer</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Farming and Gardening</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1-2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No Restrictions</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">a1a4a0f5-23c3-41dd-8880-1d0f5526be4a</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	The sun comes up early here, you know? Just before the roosters really start crowing. I get up, same as always, and make some ugali with a little bit of sukuma wiki – it’s the cheapest way to fill my belly before heading out to the coffee bushes. My husband, David, he’s already out there, checking on the seedlings. We need to get a good harvest this year, you see, to pay the bills and get enough medicine for Mama. It’s a tough life, but it’s our life. I don’t really think much about how tired I get, or how my legs ache by the end of the day. I just keep going. It’s what we do.\n\nToday, I was supposed to help David carry a heavy load of fertilizer up to the new patch. He’s got a bad back, you know, and needs all the help he can get. But I was feeling a bit slow, a bit dizzy actually. I told him I’d catch up later, but I just… I couldn’t quite manage the weight. It slipped right through my hands and spilled all over the ground. David was disappointed, of course, but he didn’t yell. He just sighed and said, ‘Wanja, you need to take care of yourself.’ I knew he was right, but it was just so hard to stop and rest, to slow down.\n\nI went to the clinic last week, you know, just for a check-up. Dr. Okeke was really kind. He looked at my blood sugar and said I need to be more careful with my diet and maybe do a little bit of exercise. He suggested walking, something easy. But walking? Here? It feels like a lot when you’re used to just carrying things all day long. And honestly, after a long day in the fields, I just want to sit down and rest.  I told him it was hard, that I didn't have time, but he just smiled and said, 'Little steps, Wanja. Little steps are still steps.'\n\nSo, I started small. I walk to the market with Mama every morning, even if it's just for a few minutes. And I try to take a short break after weeding the garden, just to stretch my legs. David’s been helping too, he’s been encouraging me.  Yesterday, we finished the fertilizer delivery without a single spill! It felt… good, you know? Like I was actually making a difference.  I’m still learning, and it’s not always easy, but I feel a little bit stronger, a little bit more sure of myself. Maybe, just maybe, I can actually get through this year’s harvest and keep taking care of my family.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a1a4a0f5-23c3-41dd-8880-1d0f5526be4a_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a1a4a0f5-23c3-41dd-8880-1d0f5526be4a_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a1a4a0f5-23c3-41dd-8880-1d0f5526be4a_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a1a4a0f5-23c3-41dd-8880-1d0f5526be4a_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a1a4a0f5-23c3-41dd-8880-1d0f5526be4a_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a1a4a0f5-23c3-41dd-8880-1d0f5526be4a_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a1a4a0f5-23c3-41dd-8880-1d0f5526be4a_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a1a4a0f5-23c3-41dd-8880-1d0f5526be4a_3.mp3}	What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?	exercise	female	consistency	2026-03-23 13:10:43.76229+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gemma-3-4b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Wanjiku Mwangi is a 35-44 year old, \\nCulture: Kikuyu female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Business owner, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Murang'a. Disability: she has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Storytelling. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resourc