<td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5-7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Vegetarian</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Close friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">a5c62ec2-17d7-4b79-ab94-7308092b1187</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I don&#x27;t have a gymâ€”what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Every morning in Kiambu town starts for me, Wanjiku Mwangi, with a hot cup of tea and a mountain of emails from the office. You know how it is? Everyone thinks being a young MP is all about speeches and TV, but it’s mostly meetings and reports. I just grab whatever is near for breakfast, like two slices of white bread with margarine and a banana, because time never waits. That day, I was meant to lead a small committee workshop, but halfway through, my energy disappeared, and my hands started shaking during my notes. Was it just the tight schedule, or was I not doing something right with my food? I blamed stress, but deep down, I wondered if my skipping proper meals was the problem.\n\nLater that afternoon, I visited the clinic as usual for my diabetes follow-up. Nurse Muthoni, always smiling, looked me straight in the eye and asked, 'Wanjiku, did you eat a real breakfast?' I shrugged, not sure what counted as a real breakfast anymore. She shook her head like a mum and gave me clear advice: 'Just try a bowl of porridge with groundnuts, or sukuma wiki with avocado and brown bread. Drink water, not only tea. It’s easy, and you can prep ahead. Your committee will wait, but your health cannot.' It sounded simple but also new, and honestly, did I really need such a big change?\n\nThe next morning, I forced myself to wake up a bit earlier and made the porridge Nurse Muthoni suggested. It tasted plain, almost different from what I liked, and I nearly tossed it for my usual bread. Even as I got ready, the old habits pulled me back—who has time for slow breakfast? But then I remembered the big community clean-up event planned for Saturday, where I’d be leading, and I told myself: This new routine is for that day too, not just today. I pushed on, again and again.\n\nLittle by little, things started changing. I began to feel steadier through each Parliament session, and my energy lasted even through long walks during community volunteer days in Kiambu estate. On clean-up day, I was able to chat, laugh, and work beside everyone—no shaky hands, just strength. And as I saw how a small change in my morning grew big rewards in my work, I felt ready to try more healthy steps. I guess sometimes, you just need a push—and maybe a nurse who believes you can change more than you thought.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a5c62ec2-17d7-4b79-ab94-7308092b1187_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a5c62ec2-17d7-4b79-ab94-7308092b1187_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a5c62ec2-17d7-4b79-ab94-7308092b1187_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a5c62ec2-17d7-4b79-ab94-7308092b1187_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a5c62ec2-17d7-4b79-ab94-7308092b1187_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a5c62ec2-17d7-4b79-ab94-7308092b1187_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a5c62ec2-17d7-4b79-ab94-7308092b1187_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a5c62ec2-17d7-4b79-ab94-7308092b1187_3.mp3}	Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?	diet	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:10:42.190685+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Nia Wanjiru is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Kikuyu female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Commercial farmer, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Murang'a. Disability: she has none. Religion: Atheism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Farming and Gardening. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: Anxiety or depression. Diet restrictions: Lactose-free. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 1-2 days. Support network: Community or religious group. Health goals: Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?. her food access Limited access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Nia Wanjiru and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "social_support", "audio_prompts": ["Ambient sounds: Distant city traffic muffled by closed windows, the gentle tap of computer keys, and intermittent beeps of incoming emails. Wanjiku Mwangi’s voice is slightly hurried, with an audible sigh and faint short breaths as she mutters, 'Eh, why today now—my hands are even shaking.' The rustle of a notebook page as she quickly turns it, her speech trails off uncertainly and is interrupted by the soft scrape of a chair as she shifts. Her tone is slightly strained but brightens with a quiet chuckle. She pauses, then whispers her own name aloud, 'Wanjiku, just push through.'", "Nurse Muthoni, female, stands a few feet from Wanjiku Mwangi, who sits on an exam bed. Nurse Muthoni’s voice is calm, clear, and motherly, with gentle Kiswahili undertones in her South African English. 'Wanjiku, did you eat a real breakfast?' she asks, slightly tilting her voice for emphasis. Wanjiku replies with a soft, self-conscious laugh and hesitant words, her tone rising at the end as she shrugs. Paper rustles and a pen clicks lightly as Nurse Muthoni offers a practical suggestion, her tone warm and instructive. A muffled hallway announcement plays in the background. The air feels reassuring.", "Early morning kitchen ambiance: the electric kettle’s gentle heat hum, a light spoon tapping rhythmically in a ceramic bowl, and Wanjiku Mwangi’s soft exhale as she mutters doubtfully to herself. Her words are accented by a deliberate pace, nearly whispering, 'Is this really better than bread?' A moment’s silence, then her chair legs scrape as she stands, the promise of activity hanging in the air. The radio plays gospel music softly from another room. Her resolve surfaces in a firmer tone: 'This is for Saturday…' with a moderate, steady breath.", "Ruth, a young female friend, laughs heartily a few meters from Wanjiku Mwangi, who responds with genuine amusement, her voice confident and warm. The sounds of children playing, shoes rustling on grass, and soft wind in trees fill the park. Wanjiku’s laughter rings clearly, then she calls encouragement across the group, 'Tuwende, let’s clean Kiambu!' Her breathing is strong and steady, punctuated by bursts of energetic conversation with Ruth nearby. The cheerful background, easy pacing, and natural, open voices reflect accomplishment and support."], "image_prompts": ["Wanjiku Mwangi, a Kikuyu woman in her early twenties, is captured sitting at a basic office desk in a sunlit urban Kiambu apartment. She leans forward in her chair, her left hand pressed flat to the desktop for support as her right hand loosely holds a pen above cluttered official papers. Her facial expression is tense, eyebrows raised and mouth slightly open in confusion or worry. A half-eaten slice of white bread, a peeled banana, and a mug of tea sit nearby. Light streams through a sheer curtain, showing the textures of urban housing and modest furnishings in the background. Medium shot, candid, natural, unposed.", "Nurse Muthoni, a middle-aged woman wearing a white uniform and blue scarf, stands facing Wanjiku Mwangi in a tidy clinic examination room. Wanjiku sits on the edge of a simple bed, hands folded in her lap, looking up at Nurse Muthoni with a half-smile and raised eyebrows. Nurse Muthoni points gently toward a printout meal plan and an avocado on the desk, her stance steady but warm. Sunlight falls across the tiled floor, highlighting medical posters about nutrition on the wall. Medium shot, candid, natural, unposed.", "Wanjiku Mwangi stands in a small kitchen in her Kiambu town apartment, holding a steaming bowl of porridge with both hands. She gazes down at it with a skeptical frown, mouth pinched, and her body leans slightly away as if considering swapping it for something else. The kitchen is neat but simple, with a window casting soft morning light onto plain cabinets and a loaf of brown bread. The countertop holds groundnuts and a ripe avocado. Wide shot, candid, natural, unposed.", "Wanjiku Mwangi, wearing casual jeans and a committee T-shirt, stands laughing and talking with another young woman (her friend, Ruth, early twenties, female) during a park community clean-up in Kiambu. Wanjiku holds a large rubbish bag upright with one hand and gestures animatedly with the other, her posture relaxed and energetic. Ruth leans slightly toward Wanjiku, smiling with a rake in hand. The park background is urban but green, with sunlight dappling over grass, trees, and scattered volunteers. Wide shot, candid, natural, unposed."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 2.2845958173274994, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some effective home exercise routines for young women with type 2 diabetes living in urban Kiambu, Kenya, who do not have access to a gym?\\",\\n  \\"Which locally available foods in Kiambu can help manage type 2 diabetes while following a kosher and healthy diet?\\",\\n  \\"Are there community volunteering opportunities in urban Kiambu that also involve physical activity suitable for young adults?\\",\\n  \\"How can a female Baptist Christian in Kiambu incorporate her faith and Kikuyu culture into her daily wellness routine?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find support groups in Kiambu for young diabetic professionals who smoke occasionally?\\",\\n  \\"What creative ways can I use common household items in a Kiambu apartment for strength training exercises?\\",\\n  \\"How does being a member of parliament in Kenya impact my ability to manage regular physical activity and healthy eating habits?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any urban outdoor parks or safe walking trails in Kiambu suitable for regular physical activity for women?\\",\\n  \\"What are the best local herbal or traditional medicines in Kikuyu culture for managing type 2 diabetes, and how do they interact with insulin or Metformin?\\",\\n  \\"How do most urban young women in Kenya balance a demanding political career and self-care routines for diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any local resources or clinics in Kiambu offering group fitness or diabetes management classes for young women?\\",\\n  \\"What are the potential challenges for Baptist Christian women in Kiambu looking to maintain a kosher diet and how can these be addressed locally?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "Relevant fields: Working adult, Member of Parliament, Urban living in Kiambu, Type 2 Diabetes, Vegetarian with easy access to healthy foods, Regular healthcare access, Physical activity 5-7 days, Support from close friends, No gym access (seeking home activity ideas), Medication use, Busy public duties and volunteering, Diet knowledge gaps.", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
d6206666-8d71-4c33-ae49-ca53385c69fc	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b817-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Murang&#x27;a</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Methodist)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Wanjiku Mwangi</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">25-34</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Urban (city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Commercial farmer</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Farming and Gardening</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1-2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Vegan</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">d6206666-8d71-4c33-ae49-ca53385c69fc</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	The sun’s already hot when I wake up, even before six. I stretch, yawn, and head straight to the kitchen. Mama always makes uji – maize porridge – for breakfast. It’s thick and sweet, a little bit of sugar in it, just how I like it. Then it’s off to the farm. I help my husband, Joseph, with the maize. We’re planting for the next harvest, and it's hard work, bending down all day. I usually just take a small piece of roasted maize as a snack, nothing fancy. Honestly, I don’t really think about much else besides the crops and keeping things running smoothly. It's just how things are, you know? I always tell myself I'll rest later, after the planting is done. [Image Prompt: Wanjiku, 28, Kikuyu female, stands in a field of freshly planted maize, wearing a simple blue cotton dress and a woven headscarf. Sweat glistens on her forehead, and she’s bent over, carefully placing maize seeds in the earth. The field stretches out behind her, bathed in the golden light of the morning sun. Her posture is focused, determined, and slightly strained. Background: Rows of green maize plants, a traditional mud-brick farmhouse in the distance.]\n\nToday, I was supposed to check the irrigation system – Joseph asked me to, he knows I’m good with fixing things. But I was so tired, just finished planting, and I skipped it. I told myself I'd do it tomorrow.  Later, when I went to turn on the pump, it wouldn’t work!  It just sputtered and stopped. Joseph was frustrated. He said, 'Wanjiku, you need to take care of yourself too.  You can’t just keep working without looking after your health.  The clinic told you about the importance of drinking plenty of water and eating balanced meals.' He pointed to the small, worn pamphlet they gave us – it had pictures of fruits and vegetables.  I felt a bit embarrassed, to be honest. It all sounded so complicated.  I just nodded and said, 'Yes, Joseph, I’ll try.’ [Image Prompt: Wanjiku, 28, Kikuyu female, sits on a wooden stool in a small, shaded kitchen. She’s holding a worn pamphlet from the clinic, showing images of fruits and vegetables. Joseph, 32, her husband, stands behind her, gesturing with his hand towards the pamphlet. The kitchen is simple, with a clay stove and basic utensils. The lighting is warm and slightly diffused, with dust motes dancing in the sunlight. Wanjiku’s expression is a mix of slight embarrassment and a tentative acceptance.]\n\nI tried to drink more water, I really did. I kept a bottle by my side all day, but I kept forgetting to take sips. And I tried to eat some of the vegetables Mama cooked, but I always ended up reaching for a piece of uji instead.  It’s just… so sweet!  Yesterday, I felt a bit dizzy after helping with the weeding, and my legs started to ache.  Joseph said, 'Wanjiku, you need to slow down.  Maybe walk a little each day, just to get your body moving.’  Walking?  I haven’t really walked much in ages.  It feels like a lot of effort.  I thought, ‘Tomorrow, I’ll walk after the maize harvest,’ but then the harvest kept going on and on.  [Image Prompt: Wanjiku, 28, Kikuyu female, walks slowly along a dusty path in a field of maize. She is wearing a simple cotton dress and carrying a small woven basket. Her pace is deliberate and somewhat labored, with a slight limp in her stride. The sun beats down on her back. Background: Rows of tall maize plants stretching into the distance, a small, weathered fence line.]\n\nToday, I actually did walk. Just for about twenty minutes, after finishing the last of the planting. It wasn’t easy, my legs still ached, but it felt… good.  I walked to the river and back, and I even collected some water in a bucket. It wasn't much, but it felt like a big step.  I helped Joseph repair the irrigation system, and this time, I didn’t skip a beat.  I remembered what the health worker said about checking the pipes and making sure everything was working properly.  We finished it just in time for evening prayers.  It feels… lighter, somehow.  I’m still going to enjoy my uji, of course, but maybe, just maybe, I can start taking care of myself a little more.  It’s a start, isn’t it? [Image Prompt: Wanjiku, 28, Kikuyu female, stands proudly beside a repaired irrigation pump. Joseph, 32, is beside her, smiling. They are both wearing simple clothes. The setting is the farm, with the sun beginning to set, casting long shadows across the field. Wanjiku’s posture is upright and confident, and her expression is one of quiet satisfaction and hope. Background: The farm, with a backdrop of rolling hills and a clear blue sky.]	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d6206666-8d71-4c33-ae49-ca53385c69fc_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d6206666-8d71-4c33-ae49-ca53385c69fc_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d6206666-8d71-4c33-ae49-ca53385c69fc_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d6206666-8d71-4c33-ae49-ca53385c69fc_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d6206666-8d71-4c33-ae49-ca53385c69fc_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d6206666-8d71-4c33-ae49-ca53385c69fc_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d6206666-8d71-4c33-ae49-ca53385c69fc_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d6206666-8d71-4c33-ae49-ca53385c69fc_3.mp3}	Can I just walk for exercise, or should I do other types too?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:10:44.153831+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gemma-3-4b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Wanjiku Mwangi is a 35-44 year old, \\nCulture: Kikuyu female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Business owner, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Murang'a. Disability: she has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Storytelling. Activities: Socialising with family or f