arm labor. Wanjiku’s voice, 28, Kikuyu female, slightly hesitant, a little muffled, replies, 'Yes, Joseph, I’ll try.’", "Sound of Wanjiku’s labored breathing as she walks, accompanied by the crunching of dry earth under her feet.  A faint, rhythmic sigh.  She says, ‘It feels… lighter, somehow. I’m still going to enjoy my uji, of course, but maybe, just maybe, I can start taking care of myself a little more. It’s a start, isn’t it?’ Her voice is softer, a little more hopeful.", "Sound of cheerful conversation between Wanjiku and Joseph, 32, Kikuyu male, their voices warm and relaxed. They are laughing softly.  Background: Sounds of the farm – birds singing, the gentle breeze, and the distant sounds of other farmworkers. Wanjiku’s voice, 28, Kikuyu female, is clear and confident, reflecting a sense of accomplishment and renewed energy. She says, 'We finished it just in time for evening prayers.'"], "image_prompts": ["Wanjiku, 28, Kikuyu female, stands in a field of freshly planted maize, wearing a simple blue cotton dress and a woven headscarf. Sweat glistens on her forehead, and she’s bent over, carefully placing maize seeds in the earth. The field stretches out behind her, bathed in the golden light of the morning sun. Her posture is focused, determined, and slightly strained. Background: Rows of green maize plants, a traditional mud-brick farmhouse in the distance.", "Wanjiku, 28, Kikuyu female, sits on a wooden stool in a small, shaded kitchen. She’s holding a worn pamphlet from the clinic, showing images of fruits and vegetables. Joseph, 32, her husband, stands behind her, gesturing with his hand towards the pamphlet. The kitchen is simple, with a clay stove and basic utensils. The lighting is warm and slightly diffused, with dust motes dancing in the sunlight. Wanjiku’s expression is a mix of slight embarrassment and a tentative acceptance.", "Wanjiku, 28, Kikuyu female, walks slowly along a dusty path in a field of maize. She is wearing a simple cotton dress and carrying a small woven basket. Her pace is deliberate and somewhat labored, with a slight limp in her stride. The sun beats down on her back. Background: Rows of tall maize plants stretching into the distance, a small, weathered fence line.", "Wanjiku, 28, Kikuyu female, stands proudly beside a repaired irrigation pump. Joseph, 32, is beside her, smiling. They are both wearing simple clothes. The setting is the farm, with the sun beginning to set, casting long shadows across the field. Wanjiku’s posture is upright and confident, and her expression is one of quiet satisfaction and hope. Background: The farm, with a backdrop of rolling hills and a clear blue sky."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 10.488683498822725, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What exercise facilities or group fitness programs are available for adults with type 2 diabetes in urban areas of Murangâ€™a, Kenya?\\",\\n  \\"Are there culturally relevant diet plans for Kikuyu women with type 2 diabetes in Murangâ€™a who have easy access to healthy foods?\\",\\n  \\"How does high alcohol consumption affect blood sugar management for commercial farmers living in Murangâ€™a?\\",\\n  \\"What local support groups or diabetes education resources exist in Murangâ€™a for young female commercial farmers?\\",\\n  \\"What are effective ways to fit physical activity into a busy farming routine for Kikuyu women aged 25â€“34 with diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What types of farming activities in central Kenya count as moderate physical activity for diabetes management?\\",\\n  \\"How can a working adult in urban Murangâ€™a balance insulin injections and oral medications with an active farming schedule?\\",\\n  \\"Which traditional Kikuyu foods are recommended or should be avoided for those managing type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there local herbal or traditional remedies in Murangâ€™a that interact with diabetes medication like metformin or insulin?\\",\\n  \\"What Kikuyu cultural practices support better diabetes self-management for women in their 30s?\\",\\n  \\"How can a Methodist Christian incorporate faith-based community events to support a healthier lifestyle with type 2 diabetes in Kenya?\\",\\n  \\"What health check-up routines are recommended for young women commercial farmers with diabetes who visit local clinics regularly in Murangâ€™a?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
9c6264f2-bc3b-4191-96ee-19f04f5cba84	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b817-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kiambu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Baptist)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Wambui Mwangi</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">45-54</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Rural (small community or countryside)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Limited access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Government officer</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Storytelling</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Socialising with family or friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Hypertension (high blood pressure)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No Restrictions</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">9c6264f2-bc3b-4191-96ee-19f04f5cba84</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Wambui Mwangi. I work at a small shop near the market, and every day I walk to work and back. I don’t know much about how my body works, especially how my blood sugar and my tiredness are linked. I just thought, ‘If I eat bread and drink tea, I’ll be fine.’ But when I feel weak, I can’t even lift a basket. I wonder—why do I feel tired when I’m working so hard? And why does my body feel like it’s running on empty? I tried to skip meals, but that made me worse. I didn’t know my body was telling me something. I just kept doing what I always did. What if I could feel strong again, not just for one day, but for the whole week?\n\nOne day, at the clinic, a nurse named Thandi asked me, ‘Wambui, how have you been feeling lately?’ I said, ‘Tired, and I can’t sleep well.’ She didn’t rush me. She just said, ‘Your blood sugar is high, and that’s why you feel weak. But it’s not your fault. You just need to eat better and move your body a little every day.’ I said, ‘But I don’t have time. I have to work.’ She smiled and said, ‘You don’t need to go far. Just walk for five minutes after your meal. And eat food that’s not too sweet. Try beans and greens. That’s all.’ I thought, ‘Is this real? Can walking help?’ I didn’t say yes right away. I just nodded, like I was trying to believe it.\n\nI tried walking after my meal. But on the first day, I sat down and fell asleep. My feet were sore, and I felt like I had no energy. I thought, ‘Maybe I’m not meant to do this.’ But then I remembered—I have to prepare for the big event at the community centre next month. I have to help set up the health fair. If I don’t feel strong, how can I help people? So I got up. I walked again. This time, I walked with my neighbour, Nandi, who is a teacher. We walked slowly, and I felt my legs warm. My breathing got easier. I didn’t do it perfectly, but I kept going. I felt like I was building something inside me.\n\nAfter a week, I started to feel better. I didn’t need to take a long break. I could walk to the shop and back without feeling dizzy. I even started to eat beans and spinach instead of sweet bread. My blood sugar didn’t spike. I felt more awake. And when I saw the community fair coming up, I didn’t feel scared anymore. I said to myself, ‘I can do this. I can help others feel strong too.’ I now walk every day. I don’t just walk for me—I walk to teach others. I show them how small steps can change a life. And I know—my body is not broken. It just needed a little care, a little love, and a little movement. And now, I feel strong again.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9c6264f2-bc3b-4191-96ee-19f04f5cba84_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9c6264f2-bc3b-4191-96ee-19f04f5cba84_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9c6264f2-bc3b-4191-96ee-19f04f5cba84_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9c6264f2-bc3b-4191-96ee-19f04f5cba84_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9c6264f2-bc3b-4191-96ee-19f04f5cba84_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9c6264f2-bc3b-4191-96ee-19f04f5cba84_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9c6264f2-bc3b-4191-96ee-19f04f5cba84_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9c6264f2-bc3b-4191-96ee-19f04f5cba84_3.mp3}	Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:10:46.162936+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Nia Mwangi is a 25-34 year old, \\nCulture: Kikuyu female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Horticulture farmer, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Kiambu. Disability: she has none. Religion: Atheism. Daily routine: Worki