ould you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Tea farmer</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Horticulture</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exploring nature</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1-2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Gluten-Free</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">9c8225a0-4476-46c8-9dc0-03eceeb474b2</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Every morning in Kiambu town, I wake up to rush and get ready for my tea farm and check on my little vegetable garden outside the apartment. Some days, I just eat githeri quickly and skip any stretching or walking. One time, our tea buyers were coming to check the leaves and I wanted to show off my neat farm rows—but by midday, I was so tired and sweaty, my hands were shaking, and I had to sit down under a sukuma wiki shade. I wondered, was it only because I didn’t eat ugali today, or is my diabetes catching up? My heart sank when the buyers shook their heads and left, and all I could think was, maybe I’m just not strong enough for this work.\n\nThat afternoon, Nurse Chege at the clinic called my phone. You know that strict but caring tone? She always tells me, Wanjiku, even strong women need strong habits. I tried to say, I’m just too busy, but she didn’t let me finish. She explained that short walks around the shamba, squats when watering the plants, and gentle stretches as I pick sukuma wiki are all safe, good exercises for someone like me. She said, "No need for running clubs, just move more as you farm or garden, even if it’s ten minutes!" She reminded me, muscle work helps the insulin, my sugars, and even my sleep. Didn’t our mothers always garden and stay healthy? She finished by saying, "Start small, Wanjiku. Your body will thank you."\n\nThe next morning, I felt shy at first, stretching near the commuter matatus. I tried a few squats as I watered the spinach and walked longer in the shamba rows. But after twenty minutes, I got bored and wanted to go inside for a cigarette. My phone buzzed with a picture of the neighbour’s proud tomato harvest. I huffed and nearly gave up, but then I remembered, tea evaluation day is next week—if I cannot walk well and feel strong, what will my buyers or family think? So, I told myself I will keep trying, even one new small exercise every morning.\n\nWith each day, moving became a little easier. My legs ached less, and when the big tea delivery day came, I walked side by side with the buyers, explaining my garden and showing the neat rows. I noticed my body felt lighter and my mood was bright. I even showed the ladies my new stretches! That day, I sold more tea, got a smile from the buyers, and felt hope rise in me. Now, I look forward to making more space for garden walks and stretches. Who knew small changes could make a woman’s heart strong again?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9c8225a0-4476-46c8-9dc0-03eceeb474b2_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9c8225a0-4476-46c8-9dc0-03eceeb474b2_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9c8225a0-4476-46c8-9dc0-03eceeb474b2_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9c8225a0-4476-46c8-9dc0-03eceeb474b2_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9c8225a0-4476-46c8-9dc0-03eceeb474b2_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9c8225a0-4476-46c8-9dc0-03eceeb474b2_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9c8225a0-4476-46c8-9dc0-03eceeb474b2_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9c8225a0-4476-46c8-9dc0-03eceeb474b2_3.mp3}	What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?	exercise	female	consistency	2026-03-23 13:10:42.440451+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Nia Wanjiru is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Kikuyu female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Commercial farmer, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Murang'a. Disability: she has none. Religion: Atheism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Farming and Gardening. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: Anxiety or depression. Diet restrictions: Lactose-free. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 1-2 days. Support network: Community or religious group. Health goals: Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?. her food access Limited access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Nia Wanjiru and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "social_support", "audio_prompts": ["In this scene, Wanjiku Mwangi’s voice sounds mid-range, clear, and slightly weary, with slow measured speech as she sighs and mutters about feeling tired. Occasional birds chirp softly and distant city boda boda engines are faintly audible. She pauses, breathing deeply, as she says quietly, \\"Is it just my body or my diabetes today?\\" The shuffle of her feet on the garden soil is heard as she sits down.", "Nurse Chege, a middle-aged Kenyan woman, stands beside Wanjiku Mwangi near the busy clinic entrance. Nurse Chege’s firm, warm voice is clear and confident, gently rising at the end of advice, as she says, \\"Wanjiku, just ten-minute walks or squats in your garden will help you.\\" Wanjiku’s hesitant \\"mmm...\\" and short laugh are heard. In the background, clinic doors close and children play distantly. Nurse Chege ends supportively, \\"Your body will thank you, Wanjiku.\\"", "Wanjiku Mwangi’s quiet voice grumbles to herself as she tries stretching, with a breathless little laugh and short pauses of effort. The gentle splash of water from a watering can and the distant radio playing gospel music from a neighbour are heard. She mutters under her breath, \\"Eish, this is hard, but I’ll try again tomorrow.\\" The city sounds are a soft backdrop as she catches her breath.", "In the garden, Wanjiku Mwangi’s voice is energetic and singsong as she greets a male tea buyer standing nearby. Their footsteps crunch softly on the dry path as Wanjiku explains, \\"See, my rows are even better after all that stretching!\\" The tea buyer laughs approvingly in a deep, friendly tone. Birds chirp, and Wanjiku’s laughter rings clear and genuine, her confidence shining as she ends, \\"We are ready for market today!\\""], "image_prompts": ["Wanjiku Mwangi, a young Kikuyu woman in her early twenties, sits on a low wooden stool under a leafy sukuma wiki plant at the edge of her small farm plot in urban Kiambu. She is dressed in old but clean work trousers, a patterned shirt, and an apron that shows faint green stains. Her shoulders are slumped, and she wipes sweat from her brow with the back of her hand, a pensive look on her face as she glances toward withering tea rows. Sunlight filters between city flats in the background, highlighting the rich textures of soil and vegetables. (medium shot)", "Nurse Chege, a middle-aged Kenyan woman wearing a neat white coat and blue scarf, stands just outside the glass doors of a busy Kiambu clinic with Wanjiku Mwangi beside her. The nurse points encouragingly toward the small garden patch across the road, smiling with gentle authority, while Wanjiku stands arms crossed, a hesitant but curious expression on her face. The afternoon sun casts soft shadows and highlights the bright signage of the clinic entrance. (medium shot, 2 people: Wanjiku Mwangi & Nurse Chege)", "Wanjiku Mwangi stretches her arms above her head in her apartment’s small backyard garden just after sunrise, with a green plastic watering can by her feet. Her brow is furrowed, and her lips are pursed with effort. Urban flats rise in the background, and a neighbour’s laundry sways on a balcony rail above. Her posture is determined but slightly awkward, framed by dew-covered leaves and reddish soil. (medium shot)", "Wanjiku Mwangi, with a wide bright smile, stands straight next to a male tea buyer in navy overalls. She gestures proudly towards a lush row of tea bushes and recently dug horticulture beds in her urban plot. The buyer looks impressed, holding a notepad. In the background, Kiambu town rooftops shimmer under late morning sunlight, and nearby, bags of freshly plucked tea leaves sit ready for market. Textures of sunlit leaves and soil are vivid. (wide shot, 2 people: Wanjiku Mwangi & male tea buyer)"], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 2.2845958173274994, "task_parent_id": "ff616b14-e624-456e-a500-e3e40fbedff4", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are the best gluten-free traditional Kikuyu meals available in urban areas of Kiambu for someone with Type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How does being a tea farmer in Kiambu province affect blood sugar management for young Methodist Christian women with high alcohol and tobacco consumption?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find diabetes support groups or clinics for young women in Kiambu town who prefer local, culturally relevant care?\\",\\n  \\"What are safe late-night snack options common in Kenyan urban towns that won't spike blood sugar for gluten-intolerant women?\\",\\n  \\"Are there Kikuyu herbal or traditional remedies in Kiambu that can complement Type 2 diabetes management?\\",\\n  \\"How does the high availability of healthy foods in Kiambu urban areas impact the recommended diet for a gluten-free female tea farmer with diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What tailored physical activity routines are suitable for busy working women in Kiambu with limited time, diabetes, and low exercise frequency?\\",\\n  \\"Does exploring local nature spots in Kiambu offer any specific health benefits for managing blood sugar and stress in young female diabetic farmers?\\",\\n  \\"How do late-night eating habits affect Type 2 diabetes for young women who smoke and drink alcohol in Kenya's urban areas?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any Methodist church programs in Kiambu that help young women address alcohol and tobacco use as part of diabetes care?\\",\\n  \\"What are the local barriers in Kiambu for young female farmers accessing regular diabetes care besides local clinics?\\",\\n  \\"How can horticulture as a hobby be adapted to promote better physical activity for a 20-something Kikuyu woman living with Type 2 diabetes in urban Kenya?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "Relevant persona fields: Working adult routine (tea farmer, busy schedule, low physical activity), limited health knowledge (unclear on exercise for diabetes), regular healthcare access, smallholder urban living, diabetes medication (insulin, pills, herbal), hobbies in horticulture, sometimes eating late at night, support from local clinic/counsellor, easy access to healthy local foods, moderate alcohol and smoking habits.", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
345a533a-839f-453f-a7a5-55da8c74b903	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b817-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kiambu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (other)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Wanjiku Mwangi</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">45-54</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Rural (small community or countryside)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Limited access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Coffee farmer</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Farming and Gardening</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Socialising with family or friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Hypertension (high blood pressure)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3-4 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kosher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Close friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">345a533a-839f-453f-a7a5-55da8c74b903</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Ag, my life is busy, you know? I'm Wanjiku, and I look after our coffee farm here in the highlands. It's good work, but it keeps you going all day! Usually, I wake up and just grab something quick – maybe a piece of sweet bread with a bit of tea. Lunch is whatever my daughter, Njeri, packs for me, sometimes it's ugali and sukuma wiki, sometimes just a mandazi. But late at night, after checking on the drying beans, I get hungry again. A little something sweet, maybe a biscuit or two with tea, just to keep me going. Last week, I promised old Man Kamau I'd sort fifty kilos of beans for him before market day. I thought it would be easy, but halfway through, I felt so tired and shaky, I had to stop. I just couldn't concentrate, my hands were trembling! What's happening to me, I wondered?\n\nNjeri, she's a clever girl, she told me I needed to go to the clinic. I didn't want to bother the nurses, but she insisted. Sister Agnes, she’s a good woman, she listened to me and asked about what I eat. She said, 'Wanjiku, that late-night sweet stuff isn't helping. It's making your sugar levels go up and down, that's why you feel tired.' She explained it like I was a child! She gave me a simple plan: more vegetables with my meals, smaller portions of sweet things, and to try and eat my last meal earlier. She even wrote down a little menu: porridge with fruit for breakfast, beans and greens for lunch, and maybe some grilled fish and a small portion of sweet potatoes for supper. It sounded…different. I wasn't sure I could manage, but I really wanted to help Man Kamau.\n\nTrying to change things is hard, I tell you! The first night, I really wanted that biscuit with my tea. My hand just reached for it without me even thinking! But then I remembered Man Kamau and those fifty kilos of beans. I forced myself to eat an orange instead. It wasn't the same, but it was something. The next day, I tried to follow Sister Agnes’s menu, but the portions felt so small! I was still hungry after lunch. I almost gave up, but then I thought about how disappointed Man Kamau would be. I added a little more sukuma wiki to my plate and drank a big glass of water. It’s not easy, but I'm trying, for Man Kamau, and maybe, just maybe, for myself.\n\nSlowly, things started to get better. I found I wasn’t craving the sweet things as much. The porridge with fruit in the morning actually gave me energy that lasted. And when I went back to sorting Man Kamau’s beans, I felt…stronger. I finished the fifty kilos, even had enough energy to help him load them onto his truck! He was so grateful, he gave me a beautiful scarf for Njeri. Seeing him so happy, and feeling good myself, it made me think. Maybe Sister Agnes was right. Maybe taking care of my body isn’t just about feeling good today, but about being able to do the things I need to do, and the things I *want* to do, for a long time to come. I think I'll ask Sister Agnes for more ideas, you know, just to be sure.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/345a533a-839f-453f-a7a5-55da8c74b903_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/345a533a-839f-453f-a7a5-55da8c74b903_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/345a533a-839f-453f-a7a5-55da8c74b903_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/345a533a-839f-453f-a7a5-55da8c74b903_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/345a533a-839f-453f-a7a5-55da8c74b903_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/345a533a-839f-453f-a7a5-55da8c74b903_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/345a533a-839f-453f-a7a5-55da8c74b903_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/345a533a-839f-453f-a7a5-55da8c74b903_3.mp3}	Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?	diet	female	control	2026-03-23 