 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Lactose-free</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I live alone or have limited support</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">a1d3652b-4b43-471c-b0af-e62bdd300000</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Ai, life as a tea farmer in Kiambu, it's not easy, you know? Up before the sun, fingers freezing as I pick the leaves. I love my work, it's what my family has always done. But lately… lately, I've been feeling tired, proper tired. Like someone's stolen all my energy. I usually start my day with chai, sweet, sweet chai, and maybe a mandazi if there’s any left from yesterday. Lunch is usually ugali and sukuma wiki, good and filling. But I'll be honest, I don't always get around to making it myself. Sometimes it's just a quick bite from the shop. Yesterday, I promised Mama Njeri I’d help her sort through a big batch of tea leaves before market. Seemed simple enough, right? But halfway through, my hands started shaking, and I just couldn't concentrate. I had to stop, leaving Mama Njeri to finish it herself. Shameful, that was. What good am I if I can't even help a friend with her work?\n\nI finally went to the clinic, you see. Old Man Kamau, the nurse, he took one look at me and said, 'Wambui, you need to look after yourself.' He explained about the sugar in my blood, this 'diabetes' he called it, and how it makes you tired. I'd heard of it, of course, but I thought it was something that happened to old people, not me! He said the sweet chai and not moving enough wasn't helping. He didn’t yell or anything, just explained it like I was a little child. He said, 'Start with a walk, Wambui. Just 30 minutes every day. And maybe swap some of that sugar for a little more milk.' It sounded…doable, I suppose. But thirty minutes? Every day? I’ve never been one for exercise. But he mentioned the upcoming tea harvest, a really big one this season, and how I’d need my strength. That got me thinking. Maybe he’s right.\n\nThe first few days were…rough. Waking up early to walk before starting work? It felt like torture! My legs ached, and I kept wanting to stop. One morning, I just skipped it altogether and had an extra mandazi with my chai. I felt guilty all day. But then I remembered the harvest. If I wanted to earn a good amount of money this year, I needed to be strong. So, I pushed myself. I started small, walking around my shamba, then slowly ventured further. It wasn’t easy, but I kept telling myself, 'Just a little further, Wambui. For the harvest.' Sometimes, I’d even ask old Man Njoroge, the neighbor, to walk with me. He’s always full of stories, which helped distract me from the aching.\n\nSlowly, things started to change. I didn't feel quite as tired all the time. The shaking in my hands wasn't happening as often. I even started enjoying the walks! The fresh air, the birds singing… it’s quite peaceful. And the harvest? Well, I worked harder than I have in years. I sorted more tea leaves, carried heavier loads, and didn't feel nearly as exhausted at the end of the day. Mama Njeri even thanked me, saying my help made all the difference. I felt…proud. Really proud. Old Man Kamau, he smiled when I told him. He said, 'See, Wambui? Taking care of yourself isn’t just about feeling good, it's about living a good life.' He’s right, isn’t he? I think I'll ask him about some other ways to stay healthy. Maybe there's more to this than just walking.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a1d3652b-4b43-471c-b0af-e62bdd300000_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a1d3652b-4b43-471c-b0af-e62bdd300000_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a1d3652b-4b43-471c-b0af-e62bdd300000_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a1d3652b-4b43-471c-b0af-e62bdd300000_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a1d3652b-4b43-471c-b0af-e62bdd300000_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a1d3652b-4b43-471c-b0af-e62bdd300000_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a1d3652b-4b43-471c-b0af-e62bdd300000_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a1d3652b-4b43-471c-b0af-e62bdd300000_3.mp3}	What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?	exercise	female	consistency	2026-03-23 13:10:45.143455+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gemma-3-27b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Wanjiku Kamau is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Kikuyu female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Shop owner, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Murang'a. Disability: she has none. Religion: Agnosticism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Storytelling. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Vegan. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 3-4 days. Support network: Close friends. Health goals: What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?. her food access Limited access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Wanjiku Kamau and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "default", "audio_prompts": ["Wambui Njuguna, a Kikuyu woman, sighs deeply while describing her tiredness. Sounds of birds chirping faintly in the background, mixed with the distant clinking of metal pots. Her voice is slightly strained and breathy, with a slow pacing. A pause after mentioning the failed task with Mama Njeri, followed by a questioning tone. 'What good am I…if I can't even help a friend?'", "Old Man Kamau, a male voice, speaks with a gentle, reassuring tone, explaining diabetes to Wambui Njuguna. His voice is slightly raspy but clear. Wambui's voice is heard intermittently, hesitant and questioning, 'But…me? I thought it was for older people.' Background sounds of a quiet clinic, distant chatter, and the occasional rustle of papers. Wambui is nearby.", "Wambui Njuguna's labored breathing and the sound of her feet hitting the dirt path. Her voice is initially strained and complaining, 'Oh, my legs…they ache so much.' Gradually, her breathing becomes more regular, and her voice gains a slight determination. Old Man Njoroge's voice is heard in the background, telling a story in Kikuyu, providing a gentle distraction. Wambui is walking ahead of Old Man Njoroge.", "Sounds of busy activity in a tea plantation – women chatting, leaves rustling, the rhythmic thud of tea being sorted. Wambui Njuguna's voice, now stronger and more confident, as she laughs with a colleague. 'We finished a whole heap today!' A clear tone of accomplishment and satisfaction. The overall soundscape is vibrant and energetic, reflecting a sense of collective effort. Wambui is across the room."], "image_prompts": ["Medium shot of Wambui Njuguna, a Kikuyu woman in her early 30s, sitting on a wooden stool in a simple kitchen, looking down at a half-empty mug of milky tea. Her expression is weary. The kitchen is small, with corrugated iron walls and a dirt floor. A few utensils are hanging on the wall. Soft, diffused light filters through a small window. Wambui's hands are clasped loosely in her lap, showing slight tremors. ", "Wide shot of Wambui Njuguna, a Kikuyu woman in her early 30s, sitting across from Old Man Kamau, a weathered-looking nurse, in a sparsely furnished clinic. Kamau is gently explaining something, gesturing with his hands. Wambui is looking at him with a mixture of skepticism and curiosity, her posture slightly tense. The clinic walls are painted a pale green, and a small calendar hangs on the wall. Natural light streams in from a large window.", "Medium shot of Wambui Njuguna, a Kikuyu woman in her early 30s, walking along a dirt path through a tea plantation. She's wearing a colorful headscarf and carrying a small basket. Her face is flushed with exertion, and her brow is furrowed in concentration. Old Man Njoroge, a senior man, is walking beside her, gesturing as he speaks. The tea bushes stretch out in neat rows, bathed in warm sunlight.", "Wide shot of Wambui Njuguna, a Kikuyu woman in her early 30s, working alongside other women in a lush tea plantation. She is sorting tea leaves with a determined expression, her movements efficient and strong. The sun is shining brightly, casting long shadows across the field. Wambui's posture is upright and confident, her hands moving quickly and skillfully. Mama Njeri is nearby, smiling at Wambui."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 83.15678292665726, "task_parent_id": "4b2f1ba5-bfb1-4cab-9318-2ff4b276db92", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What types of exercise are recommended for women aged 25â€“34 with type 2 diabetes living in urban Kiambu, Kenya?\\",\\n  \\"Are there Kikuyu traditional storytelling groups or events in Kiambu that I can join to combine my hobby with social support?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find lactose-free healthy foods in urban areas of Kiambu and which local stores or markets offer these options?\\",\\n  \\"How can I balance tea farming work with an effective weekly physical activity routine given my diabetes and busy schedule?\\",\\n  \\"What are the best practices for reducing alcohol intake among working adults with type 2 diabetes in Kenyan urban settings?\\",\\n  \\"Are there diabetes support groups or counseling services in Kiambu County specifically for women who live alone?\\",\\n  \\"How does tobacco use, even if rarely, affect blood sugar management for Kikuyu women with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What local healthcare clinics or resources in Kiambu offer diabetes education that incorporates Kikuyu dietary habits and culture?\\",\\n  \\"Can you suggest lactose-free Kikuyu recipes that are suitable for people with diabetes aiming for better blood sugar control?\\",\\n  \\"Is there a risk of interactions between herbal or traditional Kikuyu medicines and insulin or oral diabetes medications?\\",\\n  \\"What strategies can a tea farmer in urban Kenya use to stay physically active despite having 0 exercise days per week?\\",\\n  \\"Are there government or NGO programs in Kiambu that provide support for individuals with type 2 diabetes who have limited social networks?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
8a60b937-dfd7-46ed-a87b-e341f7f86c02	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b817-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Murang&#x27;a</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Atheism</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Nia Wanjiru</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">18-24</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Urban (city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Limited access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Dairy farmer</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Storytelling</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Socialising with family or friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1-2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">8a60b937-dfd7-46ed-a87b-e341f7f86c02</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Eating healthy can get expensiveâ€”how can I manage a nutritious diet on a tight budget? And can I still enjoy some traditional foods? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	I wake up early like every day, before the sun is high. I go to my farm and start milking the cows. My hands are always dirty, my clothes are worn, but I feel strong. I think, 'Why should I stop working just because I feel tired?' I eat maize porridge with a little salt and a bit of milk — that’s what I can afford. But I don’t know if this is good for my body. I don’t feel strong after work, and I get dizzy sometimes. I wonder — if I don’t eat well, will I still be able to take care of my farm? I don’t know. So I keep going, even when my legs hurt. My small work task was to walk to the market to buy eggs. I didn’t take time to rest. I didn’t drink water. I didn’t eat fruit. And when I got home, I felt weak. I dropped my basket. I didn’t even notice. That’s when I started to think — maybe my body needs more than just hard work.\n\nAt the clinic, I met Mrs. Njeri, a kind woman with glasses and a warm smile. She looked at me and said, 'Nia, you’re doing a lot, but your body is telling you to rest. You need to move every day — even if it’s just walking for ten minutes.' I said, 'But I have to work. My farm depends on me.' She said, 'You don’t have to stop working. Just add a little movement. Walk after your milking. Walk to the river to wash your hands. Walk with your cousin when you go to the market.' I didn’t believe it at first. I thought, 'Ten minutes? What’s that?' But she showed me a picture of a girl walking and laughing, and I saw how happy she looked. I said, 'I’ll try.' I didn’t say yes with a big plan. I just said, 'I’ll try.' That was enough.\n\nI started walking after milking. I walked to the edge of the farm, past the old tree, and I counted the steps. I did it for five minutes. Then I felt my legs burning. I thought, 'I can’t do this.' I went back to the house and sat on the mat. I didn’t want to move. My cousin came by and said, 'Nia, we’re going to the market today. You can walk with me.' I said, 'I’m not feeling well.' She said, 'Just walk with me. It’s only ten minutes.' I agreed. We walked together. I felt the sun on my face, the wind in my hair. I didn’t feel dizzy. I felt light. But after that, I forgot to walk again. I thought, 'I don’t have time.' Then I remembered — I have a big task coming. In two weeks, I need to help build a new cow shed. I can’t fail. I need to be strong. So I said, 'I’ll walk again. I’ll walk every day.'\n\nNow, I walk every morning. I walk for ten minutes after milking. I walk with my cousin to the market. I walk to the river and back. I drink water. I eat fruit when I can. I feel stronger. I don’t feel dizzy anymore. I wake up and I smile. I know my body is listening. And when I go to build the cow shed, I don’t feel weak. I feel ready. I tell my cousin, 'I can do this.' And she says, 'You already did. You just didn’t know it.' I feel happy. I know that small steps — like walking — can change my life. I don’t need a big change. I just need to start. And I did. I’m not just a farmer. I’m a woman who knows how to take care of herself. And that’s the best thing of all.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/8a60b937-dfd7-46ed-a87b-e341f7f86c02_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/8a60b937-dfd7-46ed-a87b-e341f7f86c02_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/8a60b937-dfd7-46ed-a87b-e341f7f86c02_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/8a60b937-dfd7-46ed-a87b-e341f7f86c02_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/8a60b937-dfd7-46ed-a87b-e341f7f86c02_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/8a60b937-dfd7-46ed-a87b-e341f7f86c02_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/8a60b937-dfd7-46ed-a87b-e341f7f86c02_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/8a60b937-dfd7-46ed-a87b-e341f7f86c02_3.mp3}	How often and how