g tea, and grabbed a slice of brown bread with a little margarine. I had to sweep the shop before opening time – it’s a small space, nothing fancy. I bent down to pick a broom, but eh, my back was sore and my legs felt heavy as if small stones were tied to them. Did I not just rest yesterday? I thought it was only age or maybe I was just tired. Instead of sweeping well, I sat on my low stool and left the corners dirty. Customers noticed, and the shop didn’t look its best. That day, sales were slow. It made me wonder, is it my shop or is it my body holding me back?\n\nThe next day, Nurse Janet from the clinic stopped by, as she sometimes does to check on us older ones. She saw me limping and asked, "Wanjiku, have you been keeping active at home?" I shook my head and said, "Ah, nurse, without a gym here, what can I really do?" She smiled and said, "You don’t need a gym. You can use a water bucket, carry your groceries, walk around your compound, or stretch your arms when the kettle boils. Think of it like working in the shamba." At first, her advice sounded too simple, even a little funny. Was just moving around really exercise?\n\nBut I remembered that next weekend is market day—a big event where I sell more goods and need more energy. I tried the exercises Nurse Janet suggested; I used my broom as a support for stretching and walked slowly around my house, swinging my arms. Hmmm, at first, it felt out of place and I was short of breath quickly. My neighbour, Mama Njeri, even laughed seeing me march on the spot! Still, the thought of market day pushed me to try again, so I did the movements each morning, even when it felt strange and slow.\n\nBy the time market day arrived, my body didn’t feel so stiff. I opened the shop early, swept all the corners well, and carried the big bags with less stopping. Customers noticed my new energy. It gave me hope, and now I want to keep trying these home exercises. Who knew just moving around could make so much difference in my life? Maybe small changes really do bring big, happy endings after all.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/99f5e16c-2502-40c0-ae19-7e128c9fd41b_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/99f5e16c-2502-40c0-ae19-7e128c9fd41b_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/99f5e16c-2502-40c0-ae19-7e128c9fd41b_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/99f5e16c-2502-40c0-ae19-7e128c9fd41b_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/99f5e16c-2502-40c0-ae19-7e128c9fd41b_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/99f5e16c-2502-40c0-ae19-7e128c9fd41b_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/99f5e16c-2502-40c0-ae19-7e128c9fd41b_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/99f5e16c-2502-40c0-ae19-7e128c9fd41b_3.mp3}	I don't have a gym—what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:10:40.95548+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Wanjiku Mwangi is a 65+ year old, \\nCulture: None female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Business owner, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in None. Religion: None. Hobbies: Storytelling. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Halal. Smoking: Yes. Support network: Close friends. Health goals: Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?. Tone of the output: classic_change.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Wanjiku Mwangi and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I don't have a gym—what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?\\"), identify t