 of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kosher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Close friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">08408b10-7cdd-4d66-b5f4-e1e2abed18e0</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Mwangi Kamau, and I work for the government, you know, like helping people with paperwork. I live out near Kiambu, in a small house with my mama and my little sister, Aisha. It’s a nice place, but sometimes I feel a bit…slow. Like a tortoise, you know? We mostly eat ugali and sukuma wiki, sometimes with a bit of fish if we can get it. Mama says it’s good for us, but I don’t always *feel* it.  I used to think if I just kept working, kept pushing, everything would be okay. But lately, I’ve been feeling tired, even after a full night’s sleep.  Yesterday, Mr. Njagi asked me to file ten reports by lunchtime, and I just couldn't do it. My head felt like it was full of mud. I just sat there, staring at the papers, and couldn’t even write a single line. It made me feel really bad, like I was letting everyone down.  Did I really need to be this slow?\n\nToday, I went to the clinic – it’s not far, just a bumpy drive in Papa’s old matatu. Dr. Wambui listened to my heartbeat and looked at my feet. She asked me about how I’ve been feeling, and I told her about the reports and the tiredness. She smiled and said, ‘Mwangi, your body is telling you something. It needs a little bit of looking after.’ She explained that if I didn't move around a bit, and ate better, my body wouldn't work as well. She said something about ‘exercise’ and ‘sugar’ – words I didn’t really understand. She gave me a little booklet with pictures of healthy foods, like bananas and spinach. She said, ‘Even a short walk each day can make a big difference, son. Just 30 minutes, maybe.’ It sounded like a lot, but she said it was important.  It was a bit confusing, but I felt a little bit better just knowing someone understood.\n\nI tried to walk after work today. I told myself, ‘Just 30 minutes, Dr. Wambui said.’ But then I remembered I had to help Mama with the laundry. And then Aisha needed help with her homework. Before I knew it, the afternoon was gone, and I hadn’t walked at all! I felt a bit frustrated, like I’d let myself down again. I told Mama I was tired, and she just patted my head and said, ‘You’re a good boy, Mwangi. You’ll get to it tomorrow.’ But tomorrow felt like a long way away. I was thinking about the big project Mr. Njagi told me about – sorting all the land records for the whole district. It's a really important job, and I want to do a good job, but I can't do it if I’m always feeling this slow.  It’s hard to change things, isn’t it?\n\nToday, I took a walk with Aisha after she finished her homework. We walked to the market and back – about 20 minutes. It wasn’t easy at first, my legs felt a little wobbly, but Aisha kept encouraging me.  I even bought a banana! It tasted really good.  When I got back, I felt…lighter.  I finished filing Mr. Njagi’s reports, and they were neat and tidy. He even said, ‘Good job, Mwangi! You seem to have more energy today.’  It’s still a little bit of a struggle sometimes, but I’m starting to see that even small steps can make a big difference. Maybe, just maybe, I can be a bit quicker, a bit stronger.  And that’s good, isn't it?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/08408b10-7cdd-4d66-b5f4-e1e2abed18e0_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/08408b10-7cdd-4d66-b5f4-e1e2abed18e0_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/08408b10-7cdd-4d66-b5f4-e1e2abed18e0_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/08408b10-7cdd-4d66-b5f4-e1e2abed18e0_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/08408b10-7cdd-4d66-b5f4-e1e2abed18e0_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/08408b10-7cdd-4d66-b5f4-e1e2abed18e0_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/08408b10-7cdd-4d66-b5f4-e1e2abed18e0_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/08408b10-7cdd-4d66-b5f4-e1e2abed18e0_3.mp3}	How often and how long should I exercise to actually i