u describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Limited access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Shop owner</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Storytelling</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Socialising with family or friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3-4 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Vegan</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Close friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">77a03fd6-b997-429a-b743-f04e3f91a73f</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Ai, life, neh? I'm Wanjiku, and I run a small shop here in Murang'a town, selling beautiful Kikuyu baskets and carvings. It’s hard work, you know? Especially when I’m tired. Most days, I'm up before the sun, sorting stock, arranging things nicely. But by evening, after all the customers are gone, I'm famished! I usually grab something quick – maybe some mandazi and chai – because who has time to cook a proper vegan meal when you're exhausted? Yesterday, I had a big order to finish – weaving fifty small baskets for a tourist group coming next week. I thought I could manage, but I was so sluggish, my fingers just wouldn't move fast enough. I stayed up late, snacking on leftover sweet potatoes, trying to get it done, but honestly? The weaving was messy. It wasn’t my best work, not at all. I wondered, could my late-night eating have something to do with how tired I was?\n\nMy friend, Mama Njeri, she noticed I was looking a bit peaky. She’s a nurse at the local clinic, and she’s always telling me to look after myself. She insisted I come with her during her lunch break. She listened patiently as I explained about the late-night snacks and the slow weaving. Then she explained, so gently, about my sugar. 'Wanjiku,' she said, 'when you eat sweet things late at night, your body struggles to use the energy. It's like trying to fill a full pot – it just overflows!' She explained that it affects my diabetes, and that’s why I was feeling so tired. She told me to try and eat my last meal earlier, and if I *must* have something before bed, maybe a small piece of fruit. She said, 'It’s not about stopping everything you enjoy, just being mindful.' It sounded…possible, but honestly, I thought, 'Can I really change my habits?'\n\nTrying to eat earlier was harder than I thought! Old habits die hard, you know? The first night, I found myself craving mandazi around ten o'clock. I told myself, 'Just one won't hurt,' and then, well, one turned into two. The next day, I felt even more sluggish. The baskets…they still weren’t weaving themselves! But then I remembered that big order. The tourist group was coming, and I *needed* to deliver. I remembered Mama Njeri saying even a small change helps. So, I swapped the mandazi for a small apple and a cup of herbal tea. It wasn't the same, but it was better. It didn’t give me that immediate sugar rush, but I didn’t crash quite as hard later. I kept reminding myself, 'These baskets *need* to be perfect.'\n\nSlowly, things started to improve. I started finishing my dinner around seven, and if I felt hungry later, I'd have a small bowl of sukuma wiki or a piece of avocado. I still slipped up sometimes, of course, but I didn’t beat myself up about it. I just tried again the next day. And you know what? The weaving got easier! My fingers moved faster, the patterns were cleaner, and I felt…lighter, somehow. I finished the order with days to spare, and the baskets looked beautiful. When the tourist group arrived, they were so pleased with them! I even got a repeat order. Mama Njeri was so proud of me, and I realised, taking care of my health isn’t just about feeling good, it's about being able to do the things I love, and providing for my family. Maybe, just maybe, this is a good thing. Maybe I should ask Mama Njeri about some exercises that might help too…	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/77a03fd6-b997-429a-b743-f04e3f91a73f_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/77a03fd6-b997-429a-b743-f04e3f91a73f_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/77a03fd6-b997-429a-b743-f04e3f91a73f_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/77a03fd6-b997-429a-b743-f04e3f91a73f_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/77a03fd6-b997-429a-b743-f04e3f91a73f_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/77a03fd6-b997-429a-b743-f04e3f91a73f_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/77a03fd6-b997-429a-b743-f04e3f91a73f_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/77a03fd6-b997-429a-b743-f04e3f91a73f_3.mp3}	I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? 	diet	female	consistency	2026-03-23 13:10:45.398277+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gemma-3-27b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Wanjiku Kamau is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Kikuyu female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Shop owner, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Murang'a. Disability: she has none. Religion: Agnosticism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Storytelling. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Vegan. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 3-4 days. Support network: Close friends. Health goals: What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?. her food access Limited access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Wanjiku Kamau and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "default", "audio_prompts": ["Wanjiku Kamau sighs deeply, a slight rasp in her voice, as she describes her tiring day. The sound of hand weaving – the rustling of reeds – is faintly audible in the background, punctuated by the distant sounds of Murang'a town traffic. Her tone is initially frustrated, then shifts to a questioning murmur. 'Ai, life, neh? I wonder if my late-night eating has something to do with it...' A short pause follows.", "A nearby conversation between Wanjiku Kamau and Mama Njeri. Mama Njeri's voice is warm and reassuring, speaking with a clear, steady tone. Wanjiku's voice is softer, hesitant, with a slight tremble. Mama Njeri: 'When you eat sweet things late at night, your body struggles...' Wanjiku: 'But I'm so tired! Can I really change?' The sounds of a busy clinic – muffled voices, the rustling of papers – create a subtle background ambiance.", "Wanjiku Kamau is speaking to herself, a quiet, determined murmur. The sound of weaving is prominent, but slower and more deliberate. 'Okay, just the apple tonight. No mandazi.' A brief pause, followed by a sigh. 'It's not the same…but it's better.' The audio conveys a sense of internal struggle and gradual acceptance. A faint sound of crickets chirping in the distance.", "Wanjiku Kamau's voice is bright and energetic, filled with genuine amusement. The sounds of chatter and laughter from tourists are audible in the background, along with the clinking of money. 'They loved them! They really loved them!' A clear indication of success and happiness. The sound of a cash register 'dinging' is heard. Wanjiku's breathing is relaxed and even, reflecting her newfound energy and confidence."], "image_prompts": ["A candid, natural photo of Wanjiku Kamau, a young Kikuyu woman, sitting amidst a colourful display of woven baskets in her small shop in Murang'a town. She looks frustrated, her brow furrowed, as she attempts to weave a basket, her fingers fumbling with the reeds. The shop is modestly furnished, with simple wooden shelves and a dirt floor. Sunlight streams in through the open doorway, illuminating dust motes dancing in the air. Wanjiku's expression is one of quiet desperation.", "A medium shot, candid, natural photo of Wanjiku Kamau, a young Kikuyu woman, sitting across from Mama Njeri, a woman in her 50s, in a brightly lit but basic healthcare clinic waiting area. Wanjiku is listening intently, her hands clasped in her lap, with a slightly uncertain expression on her face. Mama Njeri is speaking with a gentle, encouraging tone, gesturing with her hands. The clinic walls are painted a pale green, and a simple chart hangs on the wall behind them.", "A candid, natural photo of Wanjiku Kamau, a young Kikuyu woman, in her small shop. She's holding a half-eaten apple, looking thoughtfully at a basket she's partially woven. The shop is dimly lit, with shadows lengthening as evening approaches. Her posture is slightly slumped, reflecting a struggle with willpower, but her gaze is determined. A partially finished pile of baskets is visible in the background.", "A wide shot, candid, natural photo of Wanjiku Kamau, a young Kikuyu woman, smiling brightly as she presents a finished basket to a group of tourists in her shop. The shop is bustling with activity, and the tourists are admiring the colourful handicrafts. Wanjiku appears confident and energetic, her posture upright and her face radiating joy. The baskets are neatly displayed, and the shop is well-lit and inviting. Wanjiku's face is beaming."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 83.15678292665726, "task_parent_id": "0cc828bd-19e2-4bb8-8141-047cde4999af", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some vegan-friendly traditional Kikuyu dishes available in Murangâ€™a urban areas that are suitable for managing type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How can an 18â€“24 year-old female shop owner in Murangâ€™a city balance work and regular physical activity to control blood sugar levels?\\",\\n  \\"What culturally relevant storytelling events or clubs exist in Murangâ€™a for young adults who are interested in socializing and storytelling?\\",\\n  \\"Are there specific urban spaces or community centers in Murangâ€™a where young women can safely exercise 3â€“4 times a week?\\",\\n  \\"What challenges do urban-based vegans in Murangâ€™a face with limited access to healthy foods, and how can local shop owners source affordable options?\\",\\n  \\"What support groups or networks exist in Murangâ€™a for young women with type 2 diabetes who are also high-frequency tobacco smokers?\\",\\n  \\"How does the Kikuyu culture in Murangâ€™a view herbal or traditional medicine for diabetes management, and what are the most recommended local practices?\\",\\n  \\"Which local clinics or hospitals in Murangâ€™a are best rated for type 2 diabetes care for young adults?\\",\\n  \\"What are the best strategies for quitting or reducing tobacco use for young women with diabetes in an urban Kenyan context?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any government or NGO programs in Murangâ€™a that support shop owners with diabetes to access healthy food supplies or information?\\",\\n  \\"How can someone agnostic in the Kikuyu community find social support for their health journey without religious affiliations?\\",\\n  \\"What are some popular places in Murangâ€™a town where young adults with limited food access can find affordable vegan meals?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
104abadd-8066-428a-bbcd-d49b2e2c21d6	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b817-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kiambu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Methodist)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Wambui Njoroge</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">25-34</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Suburban (near city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Teacher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Storytelling</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Socialising with family or friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Gluten-Free</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">104abadd-8066-428a-bbcd-d49b2e2c21d6</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	I’m Wambui, a teacher in Kiambu. Every morning, I walk to school with my students, and I love the way the sun hits the school gates. My lunch is always githeri with sukuma wiki — it’s cheap, it’s traditional, and I don’t think about sugar or blood sugar. I don’t even know what that means. My health worker once told me I should eat more greens and less maize, but I just said, ‘Ma’am, I’ve been doing it this way for years.’ I didn’t know it was wrong. My work is busy, and I don’t have time to think about food. So I just keep eating what I know — and it feels normal.\n\nOne day, I went to the clinic with my teacher friend, and the nurse asked me about my energy. I said, ‘I’m fine, I just work hard.’ She said, ‘Wambui, your blood sugar is high. You need to eat more vegetables, less maize, and eat meals at regular times.’ I said, ‘But I don’t have time — I have classes, I have meetings, I have children at home.’ She smiled and said, ‘You can do it in small steps. Try eating one green vegetable with every meal. Start with sukuma wiki, and add a little more every day. No sugar, no oil, just fresh food. And when you feel tired, walk for five minutes.’ I didn’t believe it at first. I thought, ‘Can I really walk five minutes? I have so much to do.’ But she said, ‘It’s not about doing it all at once. It’s about doing it every day, even if it’s just a little.’\n\nI tried it. I started with sukuma wiki every day. But on Fridays, I was tired and I forgot. I went to the market and bought githeri with beans — I didn’t think about greens. I felt weak and dizzy that afternoon. My friend said, ‘Wambui, you didn’t eat the green part.’ I said, ‘I didn’t know it mattered.’ I was sad. But then I remembered — next week, I have to lead a group teaching session on healthy food for other teachers. I said to myself, ‘If I don’t do this, I won’t be able to teach others. I must do it right.’ So I made a plan: I will eat greens with every meal, and I will walk after class — even if it’s just to the corner shop. I started again, slowly, and it felt better.\n\nNow, I eat sukuma wiki every day. I even make a small pot of green stew with beans and sweet potato. I walk after school — five minutes, then ten. My energy is better. I don’t feel tired. When I teach, I feel strong. And when I talk to other teachers, I say, ‘I used to not know about my body, but now I do. I started small — just one green, just five minutes — and now I can help others.’ I don’t feel like I need to change everything. I just know I can do it. And I know I can keep going.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/104abadd-8066-428a-bbcd-d49b2e2c21d6_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/104abadd-8066-428a-bbcd-d49b2e2c21d6_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/104abadd-8066-428a-bbcd-d49b2e2c21d6_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/104abadd-8066-428a-bbcd-d49b2e2c21d6_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/104abadd-8066-428a-bbcd-d49b2e2c21d6_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/104abadd-8066-428a-bbcd-d49b2e2c21d6_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/104abadd-8066-428a-bbcd-d49b2e2c21d6_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/104abadd-8066-428a-bbcd-d49b2e2c21d6_3.mp3}	I don't have a gym—what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?	exercise	female	consistency	2026-03-23 13:10:47.606549+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Nia Mwangi is a 25-34 year old, \\nCulture: Kikuyu female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Horticulture farmer, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Kiambu. Disability: she has none. Religion: Atheism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Farming and Gardening. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currentl