nt-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Luo</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5-7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Vegetarian</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Close friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">90a4b098-0af0-4d84-86cd-d45d48db0e80</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I like to snackâ€”what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Achieng, and I live in Homa Bay, near the lake. It’s a busy place, always people rushing about. I work at the fish market, sorting and cleaning the fish for people to buy. It’s hard work, but it pays the bills. I love to dance to Ohangla music, especially when I’m tired after a long day – it makes me feel good. But sometimes, I don’t think about *how* good it makes me feel, you know? I just want to move, to feel the beat. I mostly eat what’s available at the market – ugali with a little fish stew, maybe some sukuma wiki if I’m lucky. Sometimes, I grab a sweet potato, just because it’s there. Do you think it’s okay to eat a little bit of everything, even if it makes my tummy feel a bit funny sometimes?\n\nYesterday, I was helping Mr. Okoth pack up the fish for a big order – a truck was coming in from Nairobi. I had to be super fast, making sure everything was clean and piled neatly. But I was so busy, I forgot to drink water. My head started to spin, and I felt really weak. I dropped a whole basket of fish! Mr. Okoth was so angry, he said, ‘Achieng, you need to listen to your body! You can’t work like this if you’re not feeling well.’ He told me I needed to eat properly and take care of myself, but I just felt frustrated. I didn’t really understand what he meant. It was embarrassing, dropping all the fish like that. It felt like my body just wasn't cooperating.\n\nToday, I went to the clinic. Nurse Nyabera was very kind. She asked me about my diet and how I was feeling. She explained that my blood sugar sometimes goes up when I eat too much sugar and starchy food, like the ugali and sweet potatoes. She said I need to eat more vegetables like the sukuma wiki, and to drink plenty of water. She gave me a little booklet with pictures of healthy foods and told me how much I should eat. It felt a bit confusing, trying to remember all of it, but she said I could try small changes. She also said that because I'm working so hard, I need to make sure I'm eating enough protein to keep my energy up. She told me to eat some beans or chicken. It’s a lot to think about, isn’t it? I just want to be able to do my job properly, but it feels like I'm trying to do too much at once.\n\nI’ve been trying to follow Nurse Nyabera’s advice. I still eat ugali, but I’m adding more sukuma wiki to my plate. I drink water all day long, even when I don’t feel thirsty. And I’ve started eating a handful of roasted peanuts after work – they give me a bit of energy. Mr. Okoth noticed the difference. He said I looked brighter and I was working faster. Tomorrow, I have to help load a whole truck of fish - a really big job! But now, I feel stronger and more confident. It’s still hard, but I know I can do it, and I’m taking care of myself, one bite at a time. I think I'm starting to get it, you know? Maybe I can even dance a little longer after work now.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/90a4b098-0af0-4d84-86cd-d45d48db0e80_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/90a4b098-0af0-4d84-86cd-d45d48db0e80_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/90a4b098-0af0-4d84-86cd-d45d48db0e80_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/90a4b098-0af0-4d84-86cd-d45d48db0e80_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/90a4b098-0af0-4d84-86cd-d45d48db0e80_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/90a4b098-0af0-4d84-86cd-d45d48db0e80_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/90a4b098-0af0-4d84-86cd-d45d48db0e80_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/90a4b098-0af0-4d84-86cd-d45d48db0e80_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:20:02.227042+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gemma-3-4b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Akinyi Odhiambo is a 55-64 year old, \\nCulture: Luo female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Public health officer, lives in a Suburban (near city or town) in Kisumu. Disability: she has none. Religion: Christianity (Zion Christian Church). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Ohangla dancing. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: Hypertension (high blood pressure). Diet restrictions: Vegan. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: Yes. Physical activity: 1-2 days. Support network: I live alone or have limited support. Health goals: I don't have a gymâ€”what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?. her food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Akinyi Odhiambo and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "default", "audio_prompts": ["Sound of a bustling fish market in Homa Bay – the shouts of vendors, the splashing of water, the chatter of customers. Achieng’s voice, slightly weary and casual, narrates: ‘My name is Achieng… it’s always busy here. I mostly eat what’s available… ugali and a little fish stew.’  Subtle background music of traditional Ohangla rhythm fades in and out.  Achieng sighs softly, a slight pause before the rhetorical question.", "Sound of frantic activity – the clatter of fish baskets, hurried footsteps, Mr. Okoth’s raised voice. Achieng’s voice, slightly breathless and frustrated: ‘I was so busy… I dropped the fish! Mr. Okoth was angry… he said I needed to listen to my body.’  Sound of a brief, sharp exclamation from Mr. Okoth – 'Achieng, you need to listen!'.  Achieng’s voice trails off, a hint of embarrassment.", "Gentle sounds of a clinic – the quiet hum of a fan, the rustle of paper, Nurse Nyabera’s calm voice explaining dietary advice. Achieng’s voice, hesitant and slightly confused: ‘She explained about my blood sugar… and how to eat more vegetables… sukuma wiki. She gave me a booklet with pictures. It’s… a lot to think about.’  Nurse Nyabera’s voice calmly reiterates key points.  Achieng’s voice expresses a slight sense of relief and hope.", "Sound of a truck engine starting", "I’m loading the truck! Mr. Okoth is so proud of me.’  Mr. Okoth’s voice", "Good job, Achieng! You’re doing great.’  The sounds of activity in the market return, but with a more positive and upbeat tone.  Achieng’s voice expresses a feeling of accomplishment and renewed energy.”"], "image_prompts": ["Achieng, a young Luo woman, stands amidst a bustling fish market in Homa Bay, sorting through fish. She wears a simple, brightly colored headscarf and a worn apron. Her expression is focused and slightly tired. The scene is filled with vendors, crates of fish, and the smells of the sea. Soft, diffused light illuminates the market, highlighting the textures of the fish scales and the worn wooden stalls. Achieng’s hands are stained with fish scales, and she holds a small bucket of water.", "Medium shot of Achieng, 18, kneeling on the ground, surrounded by scattered fish. Her face is flushed with embarrassment and frustration. She has a hand to her forehead, looking dazed. Mr. Okoth, a middle-aged man with a stern expression, stands over her, gesturing with his hands. The market scene is blurred in the background, conveying a sense of chaos and urgency. The lighting is harsh, emphasizing the mess and Achieng’s distress. Her clothes are slightly rumpled.", "Close-up of Achieng’s hands holding a small booklet with colorful pictures of vegetables and fruits. Nurse Nyabera, a kind-faced woman in a white clinic uniform, is gently pointing to a picture of sukuma wiki. The background is slightly blurred, showing the interior of a simple, well-lit clinic. The lighting is warm and inviting. Achieng’s hands are slightly trembling, and her eyes are focused on the booklet.", "Wide shot of Achieng, smiling confidently, loading a large truck with fish. Mr. Okoth stands nearby, clapping her on the back with a supportive expression. The truck is parked in a dusty road outside the market. The sky is a bright blue, and the surrounding landscape is a mix of rural farmland and the shimmering lake. The scene is filled with activity and a sense of accomplishment. Achieng’s posture is upright and proud, and her clothes are clean and pressed."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 9.96361825405023, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some traditional Luo gluten-free snacks available in urban areas of Homa Bay for someone with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How does daily alcohol consumption affect blood glucose levels for young Luo women who are physically active and use both medication and traditional medicine for diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find fresh and gluten-free fish-based dishes suitable for diabetics in Homa Bay town?\\",\\n  \\"What are the recommended healthy snack frequencies for Kenyan young adults with type 2 diabetes who exercise 5-7 days a week?\\",\\n  \\"Are there support groups or community programs in Homa Bay that focus on diabetes management for young female fish processors?\\",\\n  \\"What impact does daily smoking have on blood sugar management for women with type 2 diabetes in urban Kenya?\\",\\n  \\"Which local Luo herbs or traditional remedies are commonly used alongside insulin and oral medication for diabetes, and are they safe?\\",\\n  \\"How can I balance my work as a fish processor with my need to eat regular, healthy, gluten-free snacks while on shift?\\",\\n  \\"What culturally relevant types of Ohangla dance or physical activities are suitable for someone managing type 2 diabetes in urban Homa Bay?\\",\\n  \\"Are there gluten-free snack options at urban Homa Bay markets that are affordable and nutritious for someone avoiding wheat?\\",\\n  \\"What's the best way to involve close friends in supporting diabetes-friendly snacking habits within the Luo community?\\",\\n  \\"How do local healthcare clinics in Homa Bay help urban young adults manage type 2 diabetes with both modern and traditional approaches?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
