n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3-4 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">6970357d-762c-491e-8fe5-61f3a831dcef</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouragedâ€”how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Ag, man, life is busy, you know? I'm Nariyuki, and I sell spices down at the market in Johannesburg. Good spices, mind you! But these days, just walking to the shop feels… heavy. I start my day with a strong, sweet tea and a vetkoek with polony, gotta have something quick. Yesterday, Old Man Themba asked me to deliver a big bag of chilli powder to his shop across town. Just a little walk, I thought. But halfway there, I was sweating like a pig, my chest tight, and I had to stop three times to catch my breath. I left the chilli powder with a neighbour and promised Themba I’d bring it later. Honestly, I just felt… tired. Like someone switched off the lights a little bit inside me. Is this just getting old, or what?\n\nMy wife, Aisha, she saw me struggling. She’s a smart woman, Aisha. She took me to the clinic down the road. Sister Nomusa, she’s a good nurse, always smiling. She checked my blood sugar and said it was a bit high. 'Nariyuki,' she said, 'you need to move your body more, and maybe watch the sweet things a little.' I told her, 'But Sister, I *work* all day! And a man needs his tea!' She just chuckled and said, 'Even a little walk each day, maybe 30 minutes, can make a big difference. Start small. And try swapping the polony for some beans sometimes, hey?' It sounded… doable, I guess. But thirty minutes? Every day? That's a long time!\n\nOkay, so I tried. First day, I walked around the block after work. Thirty minutes felt like thirty years! My legs were burning, and I kept wanting to stop for a cool drink. I even forgot to walk with Themba’s chilli powder! He gave me a proper telling-off, he did. But I remembered the big order coming up – a restaurant wanted a whole crate of my special Durban curry blend. It’s a big deal, a lot of money. I couldn't be puffing and panting trying to carry it. So, I tried again the next day. Shorter walk, slower pace. I even managed to walk with the chilli powder to Themba's shop this time. It was still hard, but a little bit easier. A little…hopeful.\n\nAfter a week, something strange started happening. The walks weren’t so bad anymore. I started feeling… lighter. I even started adding a few extra minutes each day. I swapped the polony for baked beans most mornings, and Aisha started making me more salads with dinner. When the day came to deliver the curry blend, I didn’t even break a sweat! I carried the crate all the way to the restaurant, chatting with the chef like it was nothing. He was so impressed, he ordered another one for next week! I felt proud, strong. Sister Nomusa was right. Maybe this health thing isn't so bad after all. Maybe I'll even try a little jog next week. What do you think?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6970357d-762c-491e-8fe5-61f3a831dcef_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6970357d-762c-491e-8fe5-61f3a831dcef_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6970357d-762c-491e-8fe5-61f3a831dcef_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6970357d-762c-491e-8fe5-61f3a831dcef_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6970357d-762c-491e-8fe5-61f3a831dcef_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6970357d-762c-491e-8fe5-61f3a831dcef_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6970357d-762c-491e-8fe5-61f3a831dcef_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6970357d-762c-491e-8fe5-61f3a831dcef_3.mp3}	How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?	exercise	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:29:36.534784+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "gemma-3-27b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Amina Bello is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Hausa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Trader, lives in a Suburban (near city or town) in Kano. Disability: she has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Storytelling. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Halal. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 0 days. Support network: Community or religious group. Health goals: Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?. her food access Limited access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Amina Bello and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prom