man in his 60s, speaks in a warm, reassuring tone from nearby. He’s advising Wanja Mwangi, who responds with hesitant agreement. ‘You need to go see Sister Noma at the clinic, Wanja.’ Wanja Mwangi’s voice is slightly muffled, expressing her initial reluctance. ‘Oh, I don’t know, Old Man Dlamini…clinics are always so busy.’ The sounds of distant traffic and chatter fill the background.", "Wanja Mwangi, speaking with a slightly strained voice, describes her struggles with the new diet. A subtle sound of a spoon scraping against a bowl is heard. ‘I tried the bean stew, but I kept adding a little extra salt, just a pinch, you understand?’ A pause, followed by a frustrated sigh. The sound of a radio playing soft traditional music in the background. Wanja Mwangi.", "Wanja Mwangi, speaking with a clear and joyful tone, describes Thabo’s reaction to the finished jersey. Thabo, a young boy, giggles excitedly in the background. ‘When he put it on, his face lit up like a Christmas tree!’ Her voice is filled with warmth and pride. The sound of happy chatter and laughter fills the room. Wanja Mwangi."], "image_prompts": ["A medium shot of Wanja Mwangi, a 70-year-old woman, sitting in a dimly lit living room with floral patterned wallpaper, surrounded by balls of colourful yarn. She is looking down at a partially knitted blue jersey with a frustrated expression, her hands holding the knitting needles awkwardly, with dropped stitches visible. Soft, natural light filters through a window, highlighting the texture of the yarn and the weariness in her face. Wanja Mwangi.", "A wide shot of Wanja Mwangi sitting in a brightly lit, but slightly worn, clinic waiting room, looking towards Sister Noma, a healthcare worker in a white coat, who is smiling reassuringly and holding a colourful booklet. The room is filled with other patients, creating a sense of community. The light is slightly harsh, emphasizing the clinical environment. Wanja Mwangi.", "A medium shot of Wanja Mwangi standing in a bustling local market, looking slightly overwhelmed by the colourful display of fruits and vegetables. She is holding a shopping basket and hesitantly reaching for a bunch of spinach, a conflicted expression on her face. The lighting is dappled, creating shadows and highlighting the textures of the produce. Wanja Mwangi.", "A medium shot of Wanja Mwangi beaming with pride as her grandson, Thabo, a young boy around 8 years old, models a brightly coloured blue jersey with a lion design. They are standing in a modest living room with warm lighting, and Thabo is looking up at her with a huge smile. Wanja Mwangi’s hands are gently adjusting the jersey, showing a sense of accomplishment and affection."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 83.15678292665726, "task_parent_id": "a9230200-a30e-4a02-8a3b-a5e2fcbdc338", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some common Kikuyu vegetarian recipes that are suitable for someone with diabetes and hypertension living in Kiambuâ€™s urban areas?\\",\\n  \\"Where in urban Kiambu can I find social groups or community centers for retired Kikuyu women over 65 to meet and socialize?\\",\\n  \\"How can I safely increase my physical activity at home as a 65+ year-old retired female dairy farmer with diabetes, given I donâ€™t have access to a gym?\\",\\n  \\"What traditional Kikuyu herbal remedies are commonly used to help manage high blood pressure and are they safe to use alongside insulin and Metformin?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any local health clinics or hospitals in urban Kiambu that offer diabetes management programs tailored for elderly women?\\",\\n  \\"What types of farming or gardening activities can I do in a small urban space to stay physically active without straining myself, considering my age and health conditions?\\",\\n  \\"How can I balance my Methodist faith practices with recommendations for healthy living with type 2 diabetes and hypertension in central Kenya?\\",\\n  \\"Is there government or NGO support in Kiambu for senior women dairy farmers living alone who need help with health or social support services?\\",\\n  \\"What practical tips do Kikuyu elders in Kiambu follow to reduce sodium in a vegetarian diet to manage hypertension?\\",\\n  \\"Are there online or radio programs in Kiambu specifically for retired elderly women that focus on diabetes management and physical activity at home?\\",\\n  \\"How can I reduce tobacco use and what local resources in Kiambu support rare smokers who are also diabetic?\\",\\n  \\"What indoor exercises can I do in my apartment using everyday household items, suitable for a retired 65+ woman with limited mobility but no disability?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": ["city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_frequency", "tobacco", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
5c9e2814-42cb-4aa4-a6bd-f12b51003588	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b817-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kiambu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Agnosticism</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Wanjiku Mwangi</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">45-54</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Urban (city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Dairy farmer</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Storytelling</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Socialising with family or friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3-4 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kosher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I live alone or have limited support</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5c9e2814-42cb-4aa4-a6bd-f12b51003588</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I don&#x27;t have a gymâ€”what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Every morning, I wake up at five, just like I did when I was a young girl. I go to my small kitchen, pour milk from the fridge, and eat a slice of bread with butter. I say to myself, 'Wanjiku, you’re strong, you’re a farmer, you don’t need to move much.' But then I feel my legs heavy, like they’re made of stones. I go to the field and walk with my cow, but I don’t walk fast. I just walk slowly, like I’m trying to save my energy. I think, 'If I walk more, will I still be able to take care of the milk and the farm?' I don’t know. So I keep walking little, and my body feels tired by the end of the day. That’s when I remember: I’ve been told I need to move more. But I don’t know how much. Is it just a little? Or do I need to walk for hours? I don’t know. And that’s why I keep doing the same thing — just walking with the cow, not really moving my body. I wonder if this is enough.\n\nOne day, I go to the clinic. The nurse, a kind woman named Amina, asks me how I’ve been. I tell her I feel tired and that my legs don’t feel strong. She listens and says, 'Wanjiku, you’re doing good by going to the clinic, but your body needs movement. Try walking for ten minutes every day — just ten minutes — in the morning or after dinner. No need to go far. Just walk around the block, or near your house. You don’t need a gym. You don’t need special shoes. Just walk. And if you feel dizzy or weak, stop. That’s it. And after a week, if you feel better, you can walk longer.' I nod. I say, 'But I smoke, and I don’t want to feel weak. Will walking help me stay strong?' She smiles and says, 'Yes, Wanjiku. Walking helps your body feel better. It helps your blood go to your legs, and your mind feel calm. You can start small. Just ten minutes. And it will help your milk come out better too — that’s how strong you are.' I feel a little hopeful. I say, 'Okay, I’ll try.'\n\nI start walking every morning after I finish my milk. I walk for ten minutes — just around the corner, past the old tree, near the market. But after two days, I feel my legs stiff. I think, 'I didn’t walk fast, I just walked slow like before.' I stop. I sit on the bench near the gate and just breathe. My body feels heavy. I think, 'Maybe I need to walk more than ten minutes?' But then I remember Amina’s words — 'Start small, don’t rush.' So I go back to ten minutes. I walk slowly, with my hands on my knees. I feel tired, but I keep going. I think, 'If I don’t do this, I won’t be ready for the big work — the milking of the new cow next week.' That’s when I decide: I will walk every day. Even if I feel tired. Even if I don’t feel strong. Because I know the big task is coming — and I need to be ready.\n\nAfter a month, I walk for ten minutes every day. I don’t feel tired anymore. My legs feel lighter. I can carry the bucket without dropping it. I even start to smile when I walk. I go to the market and see my neighbour, Njoroge, and say, 'Wanjiku, you look strong today!' He laughs. I say, 'Yes, I walked more. I didn’t stop.' I try walking for fifteen minutes one day — just after dinner. I walk past the school, past the church, and stop at the corner. I feel my heart beating steady. I feel happy. I know now that walking is not just for health — it’s for strength, for peace, for being ready to take care of my farm. I tell myself: I can do more. I can walk longer. I can move my body every day. And one day, I’ll walk the whole block and feel proud. That’s how I know I’m getting better. And that’s how I know I’m not just surviving — I’m living.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5c9e2814-42cb-4aa4-a6bd-f12b51003588_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5c9e2814-42cb-4aa4-a6bd-f12b51003588_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5c9e2814-42cb-4aa4-a6bd-f12b51003588_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5c9e2814-42cb-4aa4-a6bd-f12b51003588_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public