ow, a cow tied to a post outside the open door, dust floating in the air, low natural light, slightly tired posture, eyes half-closed, one foot resting on a stool.", "Wanjiku Mwangi sits on a plastic chair at a clinic counter, a nurse named Amina in a white uniform, medium shot, Amina smiling gently, Wanjiku looking down at her hands, fingers slightly trembling, the clinic walls painted with faded red and yellow, a poster of a healthy cow on the wall, soft morning light from a window behind them, both women in quiet conversation, Wanjiku’s face showing a mix of hope and doubt.", "Wanjiku Mwangi walks slowly on a dusty path near a small market, wearing a worn-out red dress, one hand on her knee, the other holding a bucket, her body leaning slightly forward, her face slightly downcast, sunlight filtering through trees, a small cow grazing in the background, wind blowing her hair, the ground covered in dry leaves and dust, her shoes worn and scuffed, looking tired but determined.", "Wanjiku Mwangi walks confidently past a school building, wearing a clean blue dress, her arms swinging naturally, smiling slightly, sunlight bright and warm, the path lined with flowering plants, a small child running in the background, her face open and relaxed, her hands relaxed at her sides, the sky blue with soft clouds, her posture straight and strong, looking ahead with pride."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 62.298312187194824, "task_parent_id": "dd6ed27e-583a-41dc-b69b-ca87a563319b", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are effective home-based physical activities for a 45-54 year old vegan female dairy farmer living in urban Kiambu with type 2 diabetes and no gym access?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any urban parks or safe outdoor walking trails in Kiambu suitable for moderate exercise for women with diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What vegan meal options that use locally available foods in Kiambu support good blood sugar control for Kikuyu adults with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How can I build a diabetes-friendly exercise routine using items typically found at home in a Kenyan city apartment?\\",\\n  \\"Are there diabetes support groups or wellness programs specifically for middle-aged women in Kiambu who live alone and manage their health independently?\\",\\n  \\"What culturally appropriate Kikuyu vegan recipes are recommended for managing type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How can storytelling and socializing with family be integrated into a self-care routine for diabetes management in urban Kenya?\\",\\n  \\"What are the best strategies for dairy farmers with irregular work hours to maintain regular physical activity without attending a gym in Kiambu town?\\",\\n  \\"Are there resources or clinics in Kiambu offering education on balancing diabetes, vegan diet, and rare tobacco use?\\",\\n  \\"What is the impact of vegan dietary patterns among urban Kikuyu women with diabetes compared to traditional Kikuyu diets?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find affordable vegan sources of protein in Kiambu's city markets for a diabetic-friendly diet?\\",\\n  \\"How can I reduce the risks of isolation's effect on diabetes self-management as a single middle-aged woman in urban Kenya?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
ff9e8b4d-3444-4ef5-b92c-3f4a6a19124f	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b818-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Siaya</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Anglican)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Akinyi Odhiambo</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">25-34</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Urban (city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Limited access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Fisher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Swimming</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Hypertension (high blood pressure)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Luo</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3-4 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Vegetarian</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Close friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">ff9e8b4d-3444-4ef5-b92c-3f4a6a19124f</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouragedâ€”how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, it’s tricky. I’d eat whatever was easy or cheap, like white ugali with some fried greens or maybe fried potatoes, hoping it would give me enough energy to work long hours. But I kept feeling tired at the fish market, and once I fainted just before selling my catch. I thought, why bother changing foods when work is the priority? I tried to ignore the tiredness, thinking I could push through. But it wasn’t long before a small task of arranging the fish for sale fell apart because my hands were shaking and I felt too weak to help a customer properly.\n\nThen, I visited the local clinic in Siaya. The nurse was patient and told me, 'Akinyi, let’s talk about food that doesn’t make your sugar spike and still keeps your strength.' She said, 'Try to eat more leafy greens like sukuma wiki, and combine them with fish or beans. Swap white ugali for brown maize ugali sometimes, and avoid too much frying.' I was hesitant, thinking, 'Will I really keep up at work if I eat like that?'. But the nurse broke it down simple and said, 'Small changes matter. You don’t have to be perfect overnight. And keep up with your swimming and walking too – exercise helps your sugar and blood pressure.' I wasn’t sure I could do all that, but I left feeling a bit less overwhelmed.\n\nI started swapping some meals, like making brown ugali with beans and sukuma wiki. But it wasn’t easy. Some days I slipped back to my usual fried potatoes because they were quicker and cheaper. One afternoon, after a long morning of fishing, I ate more than I should and my sugar went high again. That made me doubt if changing was worth it. But then I remembered the bigger task coming up – helping at the fish cooperative’s big auction next week, where I needed to be alert and strong. So I kept trying, slowly making those new meal choices and squeezing in walks along the shore before dawn.\n\nBy the time the auction day arrived, I felt more energy than before. My sugar was steadier, and I wasn’t as tired midday. I managed the fish sales confidently and even helped a new fisherwoman learn how to prepare omena without frying it too much. Though I’m still learning, I’m starting to see how these small food and exercise changes matter. I’m open now to keep improving, not just for work but for me, so I can fish and swim for many more years to come.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ff9e8b4d-3444-4ef5-b92c-3f4a6a19124f_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ff9e8b4d-3444-4ef5-b92c-3f4a6a19124f_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ff9e8b4d-3444-4ef5-b92c-3f4a6a19124f_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ff9e8b4d-3444-4ef5-b92c-3f4a6a19124f_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ff9e8b4d-3444-4ef5-b92c-3f4a6a19124f_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ff9e8b4d-3444-4ef5-b92c-3f4a6a19124f_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ff9e8b4d-3444-4ef5-b92c-3f4a6a19124f_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ff9e8b4d-3444-4ef5-b92c-3f4a6a19124f_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:20:00.520684+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Owiti Odhiambo is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Luo male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Lecturer, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Siaya. Disability: he has none. Religion: Christianity (other). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Ohangla dancing. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Vegetarian. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 3-4 days. Support network: Healthcare provider or counsellor. Health goals: How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?. his food access Limited access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Owiti Odhiambo and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "resistance", "audio_prompts": ["Sounds of distant vendors calling at the urban Siaya fish market, ambient chatter and seagulls. Akinyi Odhiambo’s voice, mid-range natural pitch with slight weariness, speaks slowly and hesitantly about feeling tired and unsure about what food to eat, small sighs punctuating the speech.", "Quiet clinic room ambiance with muffled footsteps outside. Nurse, a female voice nearby, calm and clear, gently explains dietary advice to Akinyi Odhiambo who responds with soft, hesitant acknowledgements and occasional pauses reflecting uncertainty but growing interest.", "Outside in a small urban yard, children playing faintly in the background; Akinyi Odhiambo’s voice sounds a bit strained, expressing mild frustration and regret over struggling to adjust meals. Breathing is measured but the tone reveals determination beneath the momentary discouragement.", "Lively bustling auction sounds with people greeting and negotiating in the background. Akinyi Odhiambo’s voice is warm, confident, speaking with clear articulation and a natural cadence as she happily shares how she has managed the big day and helps a younger woman, laughter lightly fills the scene."], "image_prompts": ["Akinyi Odhiambo, a Luo woman in her late 20s, stands by the lakeside fish market in urban Siaya, holding a small basket of fried potatoes, wearing well-worn casual clothes, the morning sun casting soft light on her tired face; medium shot capturing her hesitant, weary posture as she looks at her hands nervously.", "Akinyi sits attentively on a plastic chair inside a simple local clinic room in Siaya, listening to a female nurse explaining dietary advice; the nurse is mid-30s, holding a leaflet with pictures of sukuma wiki and brown ugali; warm natural light filters through the window, illuminating Akinyi’s thoughtful and slightly uncertain expression; medium shot.", "Akinyi stands outside her modest urban Siaya home courtyard, wearing a fish-print headscarf and simple work clothes, holding a plate with brown ugali, beans, and greens; a small frown shows mild frustration as she looks away, reflecting on the challenge of changing habits; late afternoon light creates deep shadows; medium shot.", "Akinyi laughs warmly, steady and confident, helping a younger Luo woman at the busy fish cooperative auction site in urban Siaya; both women are engaged and smiling naturally, with baskets of fresh fish and leafy greens visible; bright daylight highlights the energy and camaraderie in the scene; wide shot capturing the lively environment."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 3.10124871134758, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are recommended exercise routines for women in their late 20s to early 30s with type 2 diabetes and hypertension living in urban Siaya, Kenya?\\",\\n  \\"How can a Luo woman working as a fisher in Siaya manage diabetes and limited access to healthy foods?\\",\\n  \\"Are there local support groups in Siaya for women with diabetes to stay motivated with physical activity?\\",\\n  \\"Which traditional Luo herbal medicines are commonly used for diabetes and hypertension, and are there known interactions with insulin or Metformin?\\",\\n  \\"What are affordable, healthy meal options using local Siaya ingredients for someone with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How does regular swimming impact blood sugar control, especially for Kenyan women with both diabetes and hypertension?\\",\\n  \\"Are there Anglican church-based health programs in urban Siaya that support healthy lifestyles for young women?\\",\\n  \\"What local clinics or hospitals in Siaya offer diabetes education and motivation workshops?\\",\\n  \\"What strategies do other female fishers in Kenya use to stay consistent with exercise and manage diabetes at work?\\",\\n  \\"How does limited healthy food access in urban Siaya affect diabetes management and what community resources can help?\\",\\n  \\"Are there Luo cultural beliefs that support or discourage women from exercising regularly for health?\\",\\n  \\"What are the best practical ways to overcome discouragement in managing diabetes for working adults in urban Kenya?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "I am Akinyi Odhiambo, a Luo woman working as a fisher in urban Siaya. My days start early: fishing, selling fish by the lake, and rushing between markets. I have type 2 diabetes and hypertension, but sometimes it feels like I don't really know which foods help me stay strong or which ones spike my blood sugar. I smoke and sometimes drink, even though I know it's not good for my health. I swim and try to exercise a few times a week, but it’s hard to keep everything balanced while working and managing my medicines.", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
