 <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Luo</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Gluten-Free</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">7f57e126-108f-4d5c-be81-8dacf89e4dbe</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Anyango, and I live near Homa Bay, where we grow sugarcane. It’s a good life, mostly. My days start early, before the sun even thinks about waking up. I walk to the fields, carrying a little basket with some tea and a banana – that’s all I can usually afford for breakfast. Then, I work until the sun is high, pulling the cane, sweating under the heat. Sometimes, I get a bit hungry, and I’ll grab a piece of cassava or a small portion of ugali with some beans. It’s enough to keep me going, right? But sometimes, after a long day, I just want to relax and have a sweet soda or a piece of mandazi – you know, a little treat. It feels good, doesn’t it? But then, my tummy starts to feel funny, and I don’t really think about it too much. I just keep watching the Ohangla on TV, dancing until I feel sleepy.\n\nYesterday, I had to help my brother, Joseph, with his harvest. He needed me to carry a big sack of sugarcane from the field to the truck. It was a heavy sack, heavier than usual. I was so focused on getting it done quickly, I didn’t even stop to drink water. I just kept walking and walking, and my legs started to ache. By the time I got the sack to the truck, I felt really weak and dizzy. I had to sit down right there, on the dusty ground. It was embarrassing, really. Everyone was looking at me, and Joseph was frowning. I felt terrible, not just because I couldn't carry the sack, but because I knew I shouldn’t have pushed myself so hard without taking care of myself. It just made me feel tired and sluggish, didn’t it?\n\nToday, I went to the clinic. Nurse Aisha asked me about how I was feeling, and I told her about yesterday. She smiled and said, ‘Anyango, your body is telling you something. You need to listen to it. Walking is good, yes, but sometimes you need to do other things too, like eating healthy foods and drinking plenty of water. It’s like planting a field – you can’t just plant the seeds and hope for the best. You need to water them and give them sunlight, too.’ She showed me pictures of fruits and vegetables – mangoes, sweet potatoes, spinach. I’d never really thought about them being good for me, just food to eat. She explained that my sugar needs to stay steady, and that eating too much sweet stuff can make it go up and down, which makes me feel bad. She also told me to try walking for at least 30 minutes every day, and to talk to her if I felt unwell again. It sounded like a lot of work, but she said it would help me feel stronger and have more energy.\n\nI tried to follow her advice. This morning	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7f57e126-108f-4d5c-be81-8dacf89e4dbe_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7f57e126-108f-4d5c-be81-8dacf89e4dbe_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7f57e126-108f-4d5c-be81-8dacf89e4dbe_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7f57e126-108f-4d5c-be81-8dacf89e4dbe_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7f57e126-108f-4d5c-be81-8dacf89e4dbe_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7f57e126-108f-4d5c-be81-8dacf89e4dbe_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7f57e126-108f-4d5c-be81-8dacf89e4dbe_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7f57e126-108f-4d5c-be81-8dacf89e4dbe_3.mp3}	Can I just walk for exercise, or should I do other types too?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:20:02.610228+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gemma-3-4b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Akinyi Odhiambo is a 55-64 year old, \\nCulture: Luo female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Public health officer, lives in a Suburban (near city or town) in Kisumu. Disability: she has none. Religion: Christianity (Zion Christian Church). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Ohangla dancing. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: Hypertension (high blood pressure). Diet restrictions: Vegan. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: Yes. Physical activity: 1-2 days. Support network: I live alone or have limited support. Health goals: I don't have a gymâ€”what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?. her food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Akinyi Odhiambo and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "default", "audio_prompts": ["Sound of crickets chirping softly.  Then, a woman’s voice, Anyango, 30, slightly raspy, speaking in Luo English. ‘My name is Anyango… I live near Homa Bay… sugarcane… breakfast… banana… tea… a little treat… soda… mandazi…’  A faint, repetitive rhythmic drumming sound in the background – Ohangla music.  Her voice is slightly weary, with a hint of resignation.", "Sound of heavy footsteps on dirt, mixed with labored breathing.  Anyango, 29, voice strained, ‘Ugh… so heavy… my legs… dizzy…’  Joseph’s voice, a young man, in the background, ‘Anyango, are you alright? Let me help you.’  The sound of distant sugarcane being loaded onto a truck. The overall tone is one of exertion and slight distress.", "Sound of a clinic room: a gentle, calming voice – Nurse Aisha, 45, speaking in clear, measured tones. ‘Anyango, your body is telling you something… listen to it… water… healthy foods… walking…’  Subtle background sounds of medical equipment – a stethoscope, a printer.  Anyango’s voice, slightly hesitant, ‘I don’t know… it’s a lot of work…’  A quiet, reassuring tone from Nurse Aisha.", "Sound of Anyango, 31, walking along a dirt path, her footsteps steady and purposeful.  The sound of birds chirping and the wind rustling through the grass.  She’s humming a simple Ohangla melody.  Her voice is brighter and more confident now, with a hint of joy.  ‘It feels good… walking… strong…’  A faint, distant sound of market chatter in the background.”"], "image_prompts": ["Anyango, a 28-year-old Luo woman, stands in a sun-drenched sugarcane field in Homa Bay, Kenya. She is wearing a worn, brightly patterned kanga dress and a straw hat. Her face is etched with the lines of hard work and a slight frown as she pulls a heavy cane stalk. The background shows rows of vibrant green sugarcane stretching into the distance.  Dust motes dance in the sunlight. She looks determined, but slightly exhausted.", "Medium shot of Anyango, 30, sitting on a dusty ground near a sugarcane truck in Homa Bay. She is slumped forward, her head resting on her hand, her expression one of disappointment and slight dizziness. Joseph, a young man in his early 20s, stands nearby looking concerned. The truck is partially visible in the blurred background.  The lighting is harsh and direct, emphasizing her fatigue.", "Close-up of Anyango, 29, sitting in a small, brightly lit clinic room in Homa Bay. Nurse Aisha, a kind-faced woman in her 40s, is pointing to a colorful chart displaying fruits and vegetables. Anyango is looking at the chart with a thoughtful expression, a slight furrow in her brow. The room is clean and organized, with basic medical equipment visible in the background. A gentle, warm light illuminates the scene.", "Wide shot of Anyango, 31, walking along a dirt path outside her home near Homa Bay. She is wearing a simple cotton dress and carrying a woven basket. The path leads towards a small market stall in the distance. She is smiling, her posture is upright and confident, and the sun is casting long shadows. The landscape is rural and beautiful, with rolling hills in the background."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 9.96361825405023, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some Luo traditional meals that are lactose-free and suitable for someone with type 2 diabetes living in suburban Homa Bay?\\",\\n  \\"As a sugarcane farmer in Homa Bay, what are the best times of day to eat to manage my blood sugar levels, especially when my work days start early?\\",\\n  \\"How does late-night eating affect blood glucose in women aged 25-34 who are managing diabetes with both insulin and oral medication?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any community Ohangla dance events or safe physical activities in Homa Bay where someone with limited free time and diabetes can participate?\\",\\n  \\"What exercise routines can a working Luo woman in her 20s or 30s incorporate into a busy farming schedule to help manage type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Where in suburban Homa Bay can I find healthy, lactose-free food options or restaurants that accommodate dietary restrictions for diabetics?\\",\\n  \\"How do beliefs of African Independent Churches in Kenya influence the approach to diabetes management, specifically regarding herbal or traditional medicine?\\",\\n  \\"What are some family-centered support groups for diabetes in Homa Bay, especially those for young women farmers?\\",\\n  \\"How can regular check-ups at local clinics in Homa Bay help with early signs of diabetes complications for someone with my profile?\\",\\n  \\"Are there Luo cultural practices or evening routines that can help prevent late-night eating and promote better blood sugar control?\\",\\n  \\"What is the average HbA1c level for young female sugarcane farmers with diabetes in Homa Bay, and how can I improve mine?\\",\\n  \\"Does the physical activity from sugarcane farming count towards my weekly exercise for diabetes, or should I add specific workouts on top of my daily work?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
