iting room. Wanjiku looks apprehensive, slightly slumping in her chair, while Mama Zawadi gestures gently with her hands, explaining something. The walls are painted a calming light blue.", "Wide shot of Wanjiku Mwangi, 50, walking home from the market, carrying a shopping bag with vegetables. She's glancing at a half-eaten mandazi wrapper in her other hand with a conflicted expression. The background shows a dusty road and simple houses in a suburban Kiambu neighborhood. The sun is setting, casting long shadows.", "Medium shot of Wanjiku Mwangi, 51, confidently taking notes during a meeting in a slightly more modern office setting. She's sitting upright, focused, and smiling slightly. A colleague, X, a man in his 30s, is visible in the background, looking at her with approval. The room is brightly lit with natural light."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 83.15678292665726, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some healthy, diabetes-friendly snack options that are commonly available in Kiambu's suburban markets and also meet Halal dietary requirements?\\",\\n  \\"How can a 45-54 year old Kikuyu woman with Type 2 diabetes and hypertension safely incorporate farming and gardening into her daily exercise routine in Kiambu?\\",\\n  \\"What local Kikuyu herbal or traditional medicines are often used to manage diabetes or hypertension, and are they safe to use along with insulin and metformin?\\",\\n  \\"Are there culturally appropriate support groups or diabetes education programs for Christian women in suburban Kiambu?\\",\\n  \\"Which nearby clinics or hospitals in Kiambu provide comprehensive care for women with both Type 2 diabetes and hypertension?\\",\\n  \\"How can I reduce high alcohol consumption safely while maintaining family and community ties in Kikuyu culture?\\",\\n  \\"What air quality and environmental factors in Kiambu suburbs might affect the health of a woman with diabetes who spends a lot of time gardening?\\",\\n  \\"How does the availability of healthy foods in Kiambuâ€™s suburban areas influence snacking habits for diabetic individuals, and what local food brands are recommended?\\",\\n  \\"Are there weekend community exercise groups in Kiambu where I can participate given my health conditions and interest in physical activity?\\",\\n  \\"What are examples of Halal-friendly, local Kikuyu snacks suitable for someone with Type 2 diabetes and hypertension?\\",\\n  \\"How do local Kikuyu family structures support women managing chronic illnesses like diabetes, especially for those who are working adults?\\",\\n  \\"What are the risks of continuing weekly tobacco smoking and high alcohol consumption for a middle-aged woman on insulin and hypertension medication in Kenya?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
e563ffd3-3dd0-4fc6-ab22-ebd0d24c64b9	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b817-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Murang&#x27;a</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Anglican)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Wanjiku Mwangi</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">18-24</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Suburban (near city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Business owner</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Storytelling</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Socialising with family or friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5-7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Lactose-free</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">e563ffd3-3dd0-4fc6-ab22-ebd0d24c64b9</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	I'm Wanjiku, and I run a small shop near the market in Murang'a. Every morning, I open early and start selling fresh items—like beans, maize, and fruits. My family says I should eat more healthy food, but I just grab whatever is easy to carry. I don’t know much about how my body works, especially with diabetes. I thought if I work hard, my body will just get stronger. But I didn’t know that my feet feel tired and my hands shake when I’m not eating. I tried to walk after work, but I didn’t do it long or often. One day, I forgot to check my blood sugar before walking, and I felt dizzy. I fell on the floor. My friend said, 'Wanjiku, you’re not strong because you’re not moving your body.' I didn’t know what to say. That’s when I started to think—maybe my work and my health are not separate. Maybe I need to move more, not just work harder.\n\nI went to the local clinic with my mother, and the nurse, Mrs. Njeri, asked me gently, 'Wanjiku, how are you feeling today?' I said, 'I’m tired and I don’t know why.' She smiled and said, 'You don’t have to be strong all the time. Just move your body a little each day. Even walking for 10 minutes helps. You don’t need to run or jump. Just walk. And check your blood sugar before and after. You can do this with your shop hours—walk before opening, walk after closing. Your body will thank you. I saw a young girl like you in the clinic last week—she walked every day and her sugar stayed normal. You can do it too. Just start small.' I said, 'But I have so much to do at the shop!' She said, 'Yes, but your shop is your life. You can still run it and walk too. Just pick one day to start. Maybe walk with your sister after the market closes.' I nodded. I didn’t say no. I just said, 'Okay, I’ll try.'\n\nThe next day, I tried to walk with my sister after closing. But the shop was full of customers, and I was tired. I walked only 5 minutes and then sat down to rest. My feet hurt. I thought, 'Maybe I’m not strong enough.' I didn’t want to fail. But then I remembered—next week, I have a big event at the village hall. I have to prepare food and help with the dance. I can’t be weak then. So I said to myself, 'I’ll walk for 10 minutes every day, even if it’s just around the shop. I’ll do it before I start selling. I’ll check my blood sugar. I’ll carry a small glucose tablet just in case.' I did it. The next day, I walked a little longer. My legs didn’t hurt as much. I felt lighter. I didn’t know it then, but my body was learning to listen.\n\nNow, I walk every day—before opening, after closing, and on weekends. I walk with my sister, and sometimes with my cousin. We talk about the market, about the weather, and about how my body feels. I check my blood sugar every time. I feel better. My hands don’t shake as much. My feet feel warm. I even started making meals with more vegetables and no sugar. My doctor said, 'Good job, Wanjiku. You’re doing it right.' I smiled and said, 'I didn’t think I could do it. But I did. And now, I know—my health is not something I hide. It’s part of my work, my life, my family. I’m not just a shop owner. I’m a woman who moves, who listens, who cares. And that makes me strong.'	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e563ffd3-3dd0-4fc6-ab22-ebd0d24c64b9_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e563ffd3-3dd0-4fc6-ab22-ebd0d24c64b9_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e563ffd3-3dd0-4fc6-ab22-ebd0d24c64b9_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e563ffd3-3dd0-4fc6-ab22-ebd0d24c64b9_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e563ffd3-3dd0-4fc6-ab22-ebd0d24c64b9_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e563ffd3-3dd0-4fc6-ab22-ebd0d24c64b9_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e563ffd3-3dd0-4fc6-ab22-ebd0d24c64b9_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e563ffd3-3dd0-4fc6-ab22-ebd0d24c64b9_3.mp3}	How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:10:47.86346+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Nia Mwangi is a 25-34 year old, \\nCulture: Kikuyu female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Horticulture farmer, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Kiambu. Disability: she has none. Religion: Atheism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Farming and Gardening. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Halal. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: Yes. Physical activity: 0 days. Support network: Close friends. Health goals: I don't have a gymâ€”what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?. her food access Limited access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Nia Mwangi and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task 