    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Only when necessary</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1-2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Close friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">763c17c5-cc1b-4992-aa81-a60cb4091de9</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Sadi Salisu and I always thought walking around my kasi in Johannesburg was enough to keep me healthy. After all, I buy fresh tomatoes and spinach from the spaza down the road, and sometimes I help carry heavy water buckets for Gogo. The other day, though, when my boss asked me to carry a big box of files to the upstairs office, I struggled to get up without stopping to catch my breath. Hey, isn’t walking everywhere enough? Clearly, I was missing something.\n\nLater that week, I popped into the clinic where Nurse Lerato, with her bright green uniform, called me aside. She smiled and shook her head when I told her about my ‘exercise programme’. She said, 'Sadi, walking is a great start, but your body needs different moves to stay strong. Do a bit of stretching, try lifting small things, or even join us for aerobics outside the centre on Wednesdays. It doesn’t have to be fancy. You can start small, neh?' I nodded, not sure if I was ready, but Nurse Lerato always talks sense.\n\nBack home, I tried stretching before my evening walk to the local shop, but I felt stiff and a bit silly. One uncle passing by laughed and asked if I was training for Comrades marathon! For a moment, I wanted to give up and just stick to my usual route. But, remembering that next week I had to help set up big chairs for our neighbour’s wedding, I kept going. Maybe a little bit of stretching wouldn’t hurt, right?\n\nAfter a few days, I started noticing my legs felt less heavy and climbing stairs wasn’t such a mission anymore. On Saturday, when it was time to arrange all those chairs at the wedding hall, I finished the job without huffing and puffing. My friends even teased, 'Sadi, you look fresh today!' Eish, who knew a bit of extra exercise could help like this? Now, I actually listen out for new tips. Next time, maybe I’ll try those aerobics on Wednesday!	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/763c17c5-cc1b-4992-aa81-a60cb4091de9_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/763c17c5-cc1b-4992-aa81-a60cb4091de9_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/763c17c5-cc1b-4992-aa81-a60cb4091de9_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/763c17c5-cc1b-4992-aa81-a60cb4091de9_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/763c17c5-cc1b-4992-aa81-a60cb4091de9_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/763c17c5-cc1b-4992-aa81-a60cb4091de9_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/763c17c5-cc1b-4992-aa81-a60cb4091de9_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/763c17c5-cc1b-4992-aa81-a60cb4091de9_3.mp3}	Can I just walk for exercise, or should I do other types too?	exercise	male	control	2026-03-23 13:29:32.805499+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Chinonso Okafor is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Hausa male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Trader, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Kano. Disability: he has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional wrestling. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: Anxiety or depression. Diet restrictions: Halal. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 0 days. Support network: I live alone or have limited support. Health goals: Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?. his food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Chinonso Okafor and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "classic_change", "audio_prompts": ["Background: The gentle buzz of a Johannesburg kasi street, Sadi Salisu’s mid-range natural voice panting lightly as he lifts a box, footsteps scuffing on dusty ground, and occasional greetings from neighbours. He mutters quietly to himself, 'Hai, isn’t walking enough?' with a genuine note of surprise and mild frustration.", "Nurse Lerato (female, clear South African English but softer and encouraging tone) is sitting near Sadi Salisu in a quiet clinic, gentle echo in the small room. Nurse Lerato’s voice is warm and authoritative, explaining with friendly clarity. Sadi responds hesitantly with soft 'Eh...' and little laughs, sound of chairs creaking and distant soft clinic activity. Nurse Lerato says Sadi’s name, 'Listen, Sadi, just a bit extra will help you, mhm.'", "The environment is outdoors, with faint township street noises—children playing far off, birds, the sound of Sadi stretching (fabric rustling, hands brushing over arms). Sadi’s breathing is slightly strained, followed by a shy chuckle. Uncle passes by near Sadi, his older male voice low and teasing, 'Heh, Sadi, you training for Comrades?' Both share a brief quiet laugh.", "Inside a lively but echoing community hall, Sadi Salisu’s voice is cheerful with a clear, upbeat South African accent. He laughs lightly, with friends joking in the background, clapping and whistling. Chairs scraping, Sadi’s relieved sigh, words like, 'Eish, it’s not so bad after all, hey...' with the sound of his steps as he walks past the neatly arranged chairs."], "image_prompts": ["A candid medium shot of Sadi Salisu, a middle-aged Black male, wearing a simple t-shirt and shorts, holding a plastic bag filled with fresh spinach and tomatoes from a spaza shop. He's pausing and holding his lower back as he tries to lift a cardboard box. Around him, township scenery is visible with corrugated fencing and a dust road, warm midday sunlight, scattered shadows.", "Nurse Lerato, a Black female in her 40s, stands next to Sadi Salisu in a small urban clinic. Lerato is in a bright green nurse uniform, gesturing with open hands while smiling at Sadi, who sits on a bench looking a bit uncertain but attentive. The room has faded painted walls, motivational posters, and sunlight filtering through dusty windows. Medium shot.", "Sadi Salisu is outside his modest home, stretching his arms above his head in the late afternoon. A curious uncle, older Black male, passes nearby, glancing with an amused expression. The pavement is lined with wild grass and laundry is strung across a small, tin fence. Sadi’s posture is awkward but determined. Wide shot.", "Sadi Salisu stands confidently in a community hall filled with rows of white plastic chairs. He has just finished arranging them and wipes sweat from his brow, smiling as he leans back with hands on hips. Sunlight streams through open windows, and a few friends in casual clothes cheer for him in the background. Medium shot."], "language_code": "en-NG", "avg_time_taken": 1.9408850491046905, "task_parent_id": "0c2d4d32-ad0c-4988-a79d-0a0984d9e46c", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are traditional Hausa exercises or physical activities in Kano that are safe for young adult Muslim males with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there urban parks or gyms in Kano city suitable for men with limited time who want to increase weekly exercise for diabetes control?\\",\\n  \\"Which affordable Halal foods are easily available in Kano urban markets that help manage blood sugar for people with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How do farmers in Kano balance physically demanding work with insulin use and oral medication for type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What are some popular Hausa storytelling gatherings in Kano that also incorporate light physical activity or walking?\\",\\n  \\"Are there local diabetes support groups in Kano city for young male adults where I can share experiences and socialize?\\",\\n  \\"How do religious practices in Kano, especially during Ramadan, affect exercise routines and insulin management for Muslim men with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What herbal or traditional medicines used in Kano are considered safe alongside insulin and metformin for diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What is the availability of diabetes education programs focused on diet and exercise at healthcare clinics in Kano?\\",\\n  \\"How can I create a weekly exercise routine in Kano that fits within my work as a farmer and my social commitments?\\",\\n  \\"What are some culturally acceptable ways for a Hausa Muslim male to exercise in public urban spaces in Kano?\\",\\n  \\"Are there specific fruits or vegetables from Kano's local markets that are both Halal and diabetes-friendly?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "Relevant fields: Male. Storytelling hobby. Close friends as support network. No disability. Easy access to healthy foods. No smoking or alcohol. Lacks basic health knowledge about types of exercise for his routine and work. No specific routine awareness connecting exercise and work. No formal health guidance informally received.", "selected_profile_info": [null, "culture", "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "hobbies", "tobacco", "alcohol", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
fad58dac-8bdf-4e1b-afbc-6ede33badb5d	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b817-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kiambu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Lutheran)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Wanjiku Mwangi</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">55-64</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Urban (city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Agro-business owner</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Farming and Gardening</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Hypertension (high blood pressure)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kikuyu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1-2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kosher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">fad58dac-8bdf-4e1b-afbc-6ede33badb5d</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Eating healthy can get expensiveâ€”how can I manage a nutritious diet on a tight budget? And can I still enjoy some traditional foods? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Ai, life as a farmer, even a small one like me, is never easy. I’m Wanjiku, and my days start before the sun even thinks about waking up. I have a small plot where I grow kales, spinach, and some beans – enough to sell at the market and feed my family. Truth be told, I love a good snack. A little sweet tea with mandazi in the morning, maybe some roasted groundnuts while I’m weeding…it gives me energy, doesn’t it? I thought so. Yesterday, though, I was supposed to deliver a big order of kales to Mama Njeri’s hotel. A proper order, it was! But halfway through loading the cart, I felt…woozy. Like my legs were going to give way. Had to sit down right there, feeling foolish and letting the kales sit in the sun. My blood sugar, the nurse told me last year, was a bit high, but I just thought it meant I needed a little something sweet sometimes. Now, Mama Njeri wasn’t pleased, and I was worried about the bigger order next week – the one for the wedding.\n\nI finally went to the clinic, grumbling a bit. I don’t usually like going to doctors, feels like they just tell you what you already know. But Nurse Agnes, she’s a good woman. She didn’t scold me. She just sat me down and explained, slowly, how the sugary tea and too many groundnuts weren’t giving me energy, they were making things *harder*. She said, ‘Wanjiku, your body is like a good field. You need to give it the right things to grow strong. Too much sugar is like weeds, it chokes everything.’ She showed me a list of foods – githeri, ndengu, sweet potatoes – things I already grow! – and said to focus on those. ‘And a small handful of groundnuts, not the whole bag, after your meal,’ she said. ‘And water, plenty of water.’ She even wrote down a simple snack plan: a small piece of fruit, or some sliced cucumber, if I felt hungry between meals. It sounded…doable, I suppose. Especially if I wanted to be able to deliver that wedding order.\n\nTrying to change things is harder than it looks, let me tell you. The first day, I tried to skip the sweet tea. Ai! My head was pounding. I almost ran back for the sugar, but then I remembered Mama Njeri’s face and the wedding order. I forced myself to eat some ndengu with a little bit of sukuma wiki for breakfast. It wasn’t the same, not at all. And then, yesterday, I forgot completely and grabbed a handful of groundnuts while I was sorting the beans. Felt guilty all afternoon. But I kept at it. I started carrying a small bottle of water with me, and I’m trying to swap the mandazi for a slice of sweet potato. It’s slow going, but I’m telling myself, if I can get through this, I can handle anything. That wedding order is looming, and I *need* to be strong enough to fill it.\n\nIt’s been a week now, and I can honestly say I feel…better. Not perfect, but better. The wooziness hasn't come back, and I have more energy throughout the day. I'm even sleeping better. I delivered the wedding order today – a huge pile of kales and spinach, enough to feed a whole village! – and I didn’t even feel tired. I still allow myself a tiny treat sometimes, a small orange or a few groundnuts after lunch, but I’m much more careful. Nurse Agnes was right – it’s about balance. I’m starting to think maybe this ‘healthy eating’ isn’t so bad after all. Maybe, just maybe, I can even grow some of those other vegetables she mentioned, like the green grams. It’s a good feeling, knowing I’m taking care of myself, and I'm starting to think this is something I can keep up, long after the wedding is over.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/fad58dac-8bdf-4e1b-afbc-6ede33badb5d_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/fad58dac-8bdf-4e1b-afbc-6ede33badb5d_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/fad58dac-8bdf-4e1b-afbc-6ede33badb5d_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/fad58dac-8bdf-4e1b-afbc-6ede33badb5d_3.png}