s her diabetes, but her diet is mainly fish and ugali. She sometimes eats sukuma wiki and osuga, but not enough to make a difference. Her job is physically demanding, but she doesn't know how to handle fatigue. She has never been to a doctor about her diabetes, and her medication is not consistent. She is worried about her health but doesn't know where to start.\n\nOne day, Mkena's doctor told her to start eating more vegetables to help control her diabetes. The doctor explained that vegetables are low in carbohydrates and high in fiber, which can help regulate blood sugar levels. Mkena was skeptical at first, but the doctor assured her that it would make a big difference. Mkena decided to give it a try and started cooking more vegetables for herself. She started with sukuma wiki and osuga, and soon added other vegetables like tomatoes and onions.\n\nMkena's new diet was not easy to maintain. She was used to eating a lot of ugali and fish, and it was hard to give up her favorite foods. She also had to adjust to cooking new vegetables, which took time and effort. One day, Mkena was feeling tired and struggled to finish her work. She realized that she needed to make changes to her diet and exercise routine. She began to make small changes, like taking a short walk during her lunch break and eating more vegetables. She also started to listen to her body and take breaks when she needed to.\n\nOver time, Mkena noticed that her energy levels were improving. She was able to finish her work without feeling tired, and she even started to enjoy her new diet. She realized that eating vegetables was not just good for her diabetes, but also for her overall health. She started to make bigger changes, like exercising regularly and taking care of her mental health. Mkena felt more confident and in control of her health, and she was grateful for the advice from her doctor.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/78eb7797-c8ce-4988-a6b4-2c82