rk at the post office, and I walk every day to go from home to the office — just a short walk, but I didn’t know it was helping my blood pressure. My doctor said, "Keep walking, but don’t skip it," and I thought, "I’m already walking — why change?"\n\nI used to eat bread and beans every day, and sometimes a piece of cake after work. I didn’t know that walking helps my heart, and that eating too much sugar can make my blood pressure go up. One day, I had to deliver a package to a shop near the market — a small job — but I didn’t walk properly. I walked fast, and my legs felt tired. I felt dizzy. I stopped and sat down. My heart was beating too fast. I thought, "Is this normal?"\n\nThen, Mrs. Nkosi, the nurse at the clinic, came to me. She said, "Akinyi, walking is good — just do it slowly and for 20 minutes every day. No need to run. Just walk like you’re talking to your neighbour." She didn’t say anything about food or medicine — just walking. I said, "But I have a lot of work to do!" She said, "Work is good — but your body needs rest too. Walking helps your body feel strong." I didn’t say no — I just nodded.\n\nI started walking after work — first 10 minutes, then 15. My legs felt heavy at first. I forgot to take my insulin one day because I was tired. I felt weak. But then I remembered: I have to deliver a big parcel to the hospital next week — it’s a big job, and I need to be strong. So I walked more. I walked with my shoes on, I walked in the morning, I walked in the park near my house. My blood pressure didn’t go up. I felt calm. I felt happy. Now, I say to myself, "Walking is enough — it’s good enough."	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9091d263-2951-4eef-80fe-fd0791e71585_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9091d263-2951-4eef-80fe-fd0791e71585_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9091d263-2951-4eef-80fe-fd0791