p the football on the field for the community game, but halfway through I was so tired, I just had to sit down. I figured maybe I’m just not cut out for much exercise, or maybe it’s just me getting old or weak. Is it normal to feel that way so quick?\n\nOne day at the clinic, the nurse asked me about how I move around and eat. I told her I play football sometimes but mostly just watch or walk a little. She smiled and said, "Chinonso, you don’t have to run a marathon. Even walking for 30 minutes a day, five times a week can really help with your sugar." She explained that slow, steady moves matter more than quick bursts. She also suggested adding some beans or spinach when I can find them, even if it’s small. It made sense; simple things can change a lot without needing fancy gyms or special food.\n\nSo I started walking after breakfast, but it’s not easy. Some days my legs feel heavy, and sometimes I forget or feel lazy. Last week, I missed two days in a row and wondered if it was all just useless. Then I remembered the big community tournament coming up—if I want to play better and not get tired so fast, I need to keep at it. Little by little, I changed. I asked some mates to join my morning walks, and that made it easier and less lonely.\n\nNow, after a few weeks, I notice I’m less tired when helping set up football or even joining the game. I finished talking at the last church group meeting without needing to sit. I didn’t think a small thing like walking and changing a little food could make a difference, but it’s showing in my body. I think this is just the start; maybe with more steps, I’ll feel even stronger and ready for whatever comes next.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a9d50c4e-6e02-4bf6-89d4-43cc4a424fbd_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a9d50c4e-6e02-4bf6-89d4-43cc4a424fbd_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/imag