y counter and work long hours without much movement. Sometimes, I just grab a quick rice and stew lunch from the pot, not really thinking about what my body needs. Last week, I tried to rearrange my shelves for a new stock arrival. But halfway, tiredness hit me, and my hands started shaking a bit. I could not finish that task. I just sat there, confused, wondering if it is from my sugar or maybe I am just not strong like before.\n\nLater, I went to the local clinic nearby for my yearly check-up. The nurse, Mrs. Esther, looked at me and asked, 'How do you feel these days, Hanako?' I told her honestly, 'A bit tired, and I’m just not sure what I’m doing wrong.' She smiled kindly and said, 'You know, a little walking every day—even around your shop—will help your sugar and give you more strength. It does not need to be hard. Just 10 or 15 minutes in the morning, then again in the evening. Can you try?' I was not sure at first—it sounded too simple. But something about her gentle way made me nod.\n\nSo, the next day, I took her advice and tried to walk around my shop’s grounds in the morning before opening. Eh, my feet felt so heavy! I walked in my simple slippers, feeling awkward, wondering if my neighbours were watching. I almost gave up. But then, I remembered I had a big stock delivery coming next week, and if small tasks made me weak, how could I handle bigger ones? I took a deep breath, wiped sweat from my forehead, and promised myself to try each day.\n\nDays passed, and I noticed small changes. My legs felt less tired, and I did not need to sit as much during the day. When the big delivery truck came, I surprised myself. I moved boxes with energy I thought I had lost. As I wiped my hands and looked at my arranged shelves, I smiled and thought, 'Maybe, little by little, exercise really helps.' Now, I feel ready to keep going, one day at a time, stronger for my shop and myself.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/806b8