           <td class="p-2">Urban (city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Civil servant</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Storytelling</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Socialising with family or friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">â€” None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1-2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">58983660-b4b1-44ff-863e-7a2345d3d532</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Wallahi, life as a civil servant in Kano keeps a man busy. Every day is the same – wake up, pray, a quick bowl of tuwo shinkafa with miyan kuka for breakfast, rush to the office. Lunch is usually something small from a roadside vendor, maybe some suya and a drink. I like my food, you see? It’s what we grew up with. My wife tries to get me to eat more vegetables, but…well, a man needs his fill! Today, the head of department asked me to prepare a report on new land allocations. A small task, really. But I couldn't concentrate. My head was foggy, and my legs felt heavy. I kept rereading the same sentences, making silly mistakes. I just couldn’t get the numbers to add up right. I blamed it on the heat, but deep down, I knew something wasn’t quite right.\n\nMy friend, Malam Musa, noticed I wasn't myself. He’s a retired teacher and always tells me to take care of myself. He insisted I visit the clinic at the local government office. I was hesitant, you know? Doctors… always telling you what to do. But Musa was firm. At the clinic, the nurse, Sister Fatima, was very kind. She listened patiently as I explained my tiredness and the trouble with the report. She checked my blood sugar, and her face became serious. 'Alhaji Nura,' she said, 'your sugar is high. You have diabetes. It’s affecting your energy and concentration. You need to watch your diet – less tuwo shinkafa, more vegetables, and a good walk every day. Even 30 minutes can make a difference.' She gave me a simple chart with pictures of healthy foods and a walking schedule. It didn't seem so bad when she explained it like that, and she assured me it wouldn't interfere with my prayers.\n\nTrying to change a lifetime of habits is harder than it looks, I tell you! The first few days were tough. I missed my big bowl of tuwo shinkafa. My wife tried making me more vegetable-based soups, but it just didn’t feel satisfying. I tried to walk after work, but I was always too tired. One evening, I gave in and had a plate of fried akara with a sugary drink. The next day, I felt even worse. But then I remembered the bigger report, the one the head of department was waiting for. If I couldn't even manage a small task when I was feeling unwell, how would I handle something important? So, I forced myself to walk, even if it was just around the block, and I started swapping tuwo shinkafa for more beans and vegetables. It wasn't easy, but I kept reminding myself of the report.\n\nSlowly, things started to improve. The headaches became less frequent, and I found I could concentrate for longer periods. The walking, surprisingly, started to feel good. I even started joining Malam Musa for his evening strolls. I finished the land allocation report, and the head of department was pleased. He even praised my attention to detail! It wasn’t just the report, though. I felt…lighter, more energetic. Sister Fatima was happy when I went back for a check-up, and my blood sugar was down. She encouraged me to keep up the good work and suggested I join a community walking group. I’m still getting used to the changes, but I’m starting to realize that taking care of my health isn’t about denying myself pleasures, it’s about finding a balance. Maybe, just maybe, this is a good thing. I’m even thinking about trying to learn how to make a proper moringa salad from my wife. Who knows what else I might discover?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/58983660-b4b1-44ff-863e-7a2345d3d532_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/58983660-b4b1-44ff-863e-7a2345d3d532_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/58983660-b4b1-44ff-863e-7a2345d3d532_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/58983660-b4b1-44ff-863e-7a2345d3d532_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/58983660-b4b1-44ff-863e-7a2345d3d532_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/58983660-b4b1-44ff-863e-7a2345d3d532_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/58983660-b4b1-44ff-863e-7a23