ou describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Cattle trader</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Storytelling</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Socialising with family or friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Hypertension (high blood pressure)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), D â€” Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Luo</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3-4 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Vegan</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I live alone or have limited support</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">508cb285-6277-48d9-ab65-b75746c6e56d</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	I'm Akinyi Otieno, and I work at a small shop in Nairobi. Every day, I wake up early, go to work, and I'm always tired by afternoon. My blood pressure is high, and I know it's not good — but I don't know what to eat that won't make my body feel worse. I used to eat bread and beans, and I even drink tea with sugar. I thought, 'Why would food hurt me?' But then I saw my boss, James, sitting with a headache and looking pale. He said he had to go to the clinic. I thought, 'Oh no — is it me?' I didn't know. I just kept eating the same things. One day, I had to fix a big problem at work — a customer was angry because the shop didn't have enough stock. I had to go to the market and buy things fast. I went to a shop that sold maize and beans, and I bought a lot of them. But I didn’t know — maize and beans are not good for blood pressure. I ate them, and I felt worse. I was dizzy, my head hurt, and I couldn’t focus. I thought, 'Maybe I should stop eating these things.' That night, I went to the clinic. The nurse, Amina, looked at me and said, 'Akinyi, you need to eat more greens and fresh fruits — like spinach, carrots, and apples. No sugar, no fried food. And you can have a little plant-based protein like lentils or tofu.' I said, 'But I don’t know how to cook that.' She smiled and said, 'Start small. Try one dish a day. And if you feel better, you’ll feel stronger at work.' I didn’t believe it at first. I said, 'But I’ve always eaten like this.' She said, 'Try it for one week. And if you feel good, you’ll know.' I agreed. I started with a spinach and tomato salad. I made it with olive oil and lemon juice. I didn’t like it at first — it tasted different. I even threw it away once. But I remembered — I have a big project next week. I have to help my team set up a new shop in a new area. I need to be strong. So I tried again. I cooked a lentil soup with carrots and onions. I ate it slowly. I felt better. My head didn’t hurt. I even smiled when I saw the customer. The next day, I went to the clinic again. Amina said, 'Good job, Akinyi. You're doing better.' I said, 'Yes, I feel stronger. I can work now.' I now eat more greens, no sugar, and I drink water with lemon. I still go to the market, but I pick fresh things. I even share recipes with my friends. I know now — small changes help my body. And my work is better too. I’m not just surviving — I’m growing.\n\nI'm Akinyi Otieno, and I work at a small shop in Nairobi. Every day, I wake up early, go to work, and I'm always tired by afternoon. My blood pressure is high, and I know it's not good — but I don't know what to eat that won't make my body feel worse. I used to eat bread and beans, and I even drink tea with sugar. I thought, 'Why would food hurt me?' But then I saw my boss, James, sitting with a headache and looking pale. He said he had to go to the clinic. I thought, 'Oh no — is it me?' I didn't know. I just kept eating the same things. One day, I had to fix a big problem at work — a customer was angry because the shop didn't have enough stock. I had to go to the market and buy things fast. I went to a shop that sold maize and beans, and I bought a lot of them. But I didn’t know — maize and beans are not good for blood pressure. I ate them, and I felt worse. I was dizzy, my head hurt, and I couldn’t focus. I thought, 'Maybe I should stop eating these things.' That night, I went to the clinic. The nurse, Amina, looked at me and said, 'Akinyi, you need to eat more greens and fresh fruits — like spinach, carrots, and apples. No sugar, no fried food. And you can have a little plant-based protein like lentils or tofu.' I said, 'But I don’t know how to cook that.' She smiled and said, 'Start small. Try one dish a day. And if you feel better, you’ll feel stronger at work.' I didn’t believe it at first. I said, 'But I’ve always eaten like this.' She said, 'Try it for one week. And if you feel good, you’ll know.' I agreed. I started with a spinach and tomato salad. I made it with olive oil and lemon juice. I didn’t like it at first — it tasted different.