 don’t always think about how this keeps my sugar steady. One day, I got so tired while bargaining with customers, my legs felt weak, and I had to close early. How many times have you thought, 'I can just push through'? Yet, my body was telling a different story.\n\nThen during a clinic visit, the nurse kindly explained how exercise helps with my blood sugar and blood pressure. I was hesitant because I thought going for walks or moving more would make me more tired. But she gave me clear tips: start small, like short walks after work, and how this could actually give me more energy. She reminded me to keep my halal diet and stay on my insulin but add small moves to my day. I thought, can this really change how I feel?\n\nI tried the nurse's advice, but at first, it was hard. After a busy day trading and with little support, finding the energy to walk was a challenge. One afternoon, I skipped my walk and felt a bit down, like maybe this wasn’t for me. But then I remembered the bigger task coming—a big family gathering where I wanted to show everyone I’m healthy and strong. That kept me going, even when it was tough to change habits.\n\nWith time, walking became easier, and I noticed my energy was better during busy market days. My sugar levels got steadier, and I felt proud to have managed my health while still working hard. When the family event came, I was confident and full of joy. Now I see exercise not as a burden, but a way to keep me moving and happy. Maybe I’ll try more ways to care for myself from here on.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/352e3e74-dd93-4bfa-a943-ba3ca480cf55_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/352e3e74-dd93-4bfa-a943-ba3ca480cf55_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/352e3e74-dd93-4bfa-a943-ba3ca480cf55_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/35