   </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Online information or digital apps</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Close friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">b1c0cf24-1819-45ed-9196-e6ab8021e739</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Every morning I wake up early, before the call to prayer, and I walk among the green rice fields. I always pack my lunch in a simple plastic bowl, sometimes it is rice, sometimes bread spread with margarine. I never really thought much about how my food affects how I move or work. One day, I tried to clear the small canal for water in my own field. You ever feel so weak in your arms even though you lift things every day? My legs nearly gave way, and the spade dropped. I just sat down, sweating and breathing fast. Why can't I even finish this small job?\n\nLater, I had to go for my normal checkup at the clinic in town. The nurse, Aisha, she asked what I ate that day. I told her about my rice and my bread. She shook her head gently, not angry, just concerned. She explained, 'Kenji, for your diabetes, some foods like white rice and bread can make your blood sugar go high fast. Try more brown rice, beans, vegetables, grilled fish. Add fresh fruit, like guava or orange, not the sweet drinks. And never skip your food, even when you are busy on the farm.' I laughed softly. Why must it sound so easy when my body wants old habits?\n\nBack home, I tried. That night, I swapped my white rice for brown and made a salad with tomato and cucumber, just like Nurse Aisha said. But, my hands wanted to scoop the old rice, my mouth missed the soft bread. Still, I finished my meal and washed my hands. The canal needed fixing the next morning, and I promised myself to try again, just to be able to do a bigger part of the farm.\n\nDays passed, and I kept mixing new foods into my plates, sometimes beans, sometimes grilled chicken. I felt less shaky, sweat did not fall down into my eyes so quick, and my feet stayed firm even when the sun beat harder. At last, I finished clearing the big canal with my brother watching, both of us surprised. I started thinking, maybe I can teach my friends too. It is not easy, but if my body can feel lighter and stronger, then I am sure I am on the right path.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/b1c0cf24-1819-45ed-9196-e6ab8021e739_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/b1c0cf24-1819-45ed-9196-e6ab8021e739_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/b1c0cf24-1819-45ed-9196-e6ab8021e739_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/b1c0cf24-1819-45ed-9196-e6ab8021e739_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/b1c0cf24-1819-45ed-9196-e6ab8021e739_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/b1c0cf24-1819-45ed-9196-e6ab8021e739_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/b1c0cf24-1819-45ed-9196-e6ab8021e739_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/b1c0cf24-1819-45ed-9196-e6ab8021e739_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:29:33.312108+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Chinonso Okafor is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Hausa male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Trader, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Kano. Disability: he has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional wrestling. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: Anxiety or depression. Diet restrictions: Halal. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 0 days. Support network: I live alone or have limited support. Health goals: Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?. his food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Chinonso Okafor and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), ide