         </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3-4 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">30bf73a3-1cd9-4688-8f99-2832eb30d5d3</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Chinonso, and life here in Kano is…well, it’s work. Every day is about the goats, the little farm, making sure we have enough to sell at the market. I don’t think much about feeling tired, or what I eat. If there’s food, I eat it. Usually, that’s tuwo shinkafa with maybe some vegetable soup if my wife, Aisha, has been to the market. Sometimes, late at night, after everyone is asleep, I have a little fura da nono – it’s sweet, and I like it. Today, I was supposed to repair the fence around the goat pen, a small job, really. But I just…couldn't get going. Felt heavy, sluggish. Kept sitting down, saying I'd start in a minute. The goats, they took their chance and wandered into old man Bello's yam field. That wasn’t good. He wasn't pleased.\n\nAisha, she noticed I was dragging myself around. She insisted I go to the clinic in Wudil. I didn't want to, thought it was a waste of time. What would they tell me that I didn’t already know? But she was firm. The nurse, a kind woman named Fatima, she spoke Hausa, thank goodness. She checked my blood sugar – high, she said. She explained, slowly, that the late-night snacks, even something small like fura, were messing with my sugar. She said if I wanted to have the strength to build a good, strong fence for the goats, and to be ready for the bigger work of preparing the field for the rainy season, I needed to eat more regularly during the day and avoid sugar at night. She said small changes, like swapping the fura for a handful of groundnuts before bed, could make a difference. It sounded…possible, I suppose.\n\nTrying to change things is harder than it sounds. Old habits, you know? I still found myself craving something sweet after dinner. The first night, I gave in and had a little fura. The next day, I felt that same sluggishness. The goats seemed to sense it too, testing the weak spots in the fence. I had to rush to fix it before they caused more trouble. But I remembered Fatima’s words about the field. Preparing that field is important. It’s the biggest job of the year, and if I’m not strong, we won’t have a good harvest. So, I forced myself to eat a handful of groundnuts instead. It wasn't the same, but it quieted the craving… a little.\n\nIt’s been a week now, and things are…better. I’m eating more tuwo and vegetables during the day, and Aisha is helping me. We're even adding more moringa leaves to the soup – she says it’s good for everything! The late-night cravings are still there sometimes, but the groundnuts help. And I have more energy. I started working on the field yesterday, and I didn't feel as tired as I expected. I managed to clear a good patch before the sun got too high. Old man Bello even complimented me on the new, stronger fence. Maybe this health business isn’t so bad after all. I might even ask Fatima about other ways to stay strong. It seems looking after myself isn't just for me, but for my goats, my family, and the harvest to come.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/30bf73a3-1cd9-4688-8f99-2832eb30d5d3_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/30bf73a3-1cd9-4688-8f99-2832eb30d5d3_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/30bf73a3-1cd9-4688-8f99-2832eb30d5d3_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/30bf73a3-1cd9-4688-8f99-2832eb30d5d3_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/30bf73a3-1cd9-4688-8f99-2832eb30d5d3_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/30bf73a3-1cd9-4688-8f99-2832eb30d5d3_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/30bf73a3-1cd9-4688-8f99-2832eb30d5d3_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/30bf73a3-1cd9-4688-8f99-2832eb30d5d3_3.mp3}	I don't have a gym—what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?	exercise	male	consistency	2026-03-23 13:29:37.738087+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gemma-3-27b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile