hat apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Rural (small community or countryside)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Limited access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Farmer</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Farming-related activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">â€” None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1-2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Vegan</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">47b937fa-e3af-4b38-b3ab-11351927e4f1</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Akio, and I live out here near Katsina town. Life is good, mostly. My work is farming – helping my father with the millet and maize. We wake up before the sun, and it’s hard work, always. Breakfast is usually just a bit of koko – that’s groundnut milk – and maybe some bread if we’re lucky. Lunch is often just rice and stew, maybe with some vegetables if we can find them. Sometimes, if we have a good harvest, we’ll have some meat, but mostly it’s just staples. I don’t really think about how healthy it is, you know? It’s just what we eat. Yesterday, I was helping my father repair the irrigation channel – a long, hard job, digging and moving rocks. I was feeling okay at first, but then my legs started to ache, and I felt really tired. I didn't stop to rest, just kept going, wanting to finish the job quickly. It was a mistake, I know. \n\nLater that afternoon, my father took me to the clinic in town. Dr. Bello, he’s a good man, listened to my chest and checked my blood sugar. He said I was a bit low, and that my legs were hurting because I hadn’t rested enough. He explained that if I keep pushing myself without taking care of my body, it will only get worse. He showed me pictures of my heart and blood vessels, and how diabetes can damage them. It was a little scary, but he told me it wasn't too late to change things. He said I need to eat more vegetables and fruits, and that I should take short breaks while I’m working, even just a few minutes to sit down and catch my breath. He gave me a simple plan – a few minutes of walking each day, and to make sure I drink plenty of water. It seemed like a lot, but he said it was important to start small.\n\nI tried to follow his advice, I really did. I started walking for ten minutes after lunch, but then my younger brother needed help with his schoolwork, and I just ended up helping him instead. And then my mother asked me to fetch water from the well, and I felt too tired to stop. It’s hard to change habits, you know? We always work, always working.  Yesterday, I tripped over a rock while carrying a bucket of water, and I twisted my ankle a little. It hurt, and I felt frustrated. It felt like I was making things worse, not better. I almost gave up on the walking, thinking it wasn't worth the effort. But then I remembered my father saying I needed to be strong for the harvest – we have to repair the fence before the rains come, and that’s a big job.\n\nToday, I walked for twenty minutes after lunch, and I felt…better. Not perfect, but better. My ankle still aches a little, but it’s not as bad. I even helped my father fix the fence – I was careful to take breaks and drink plenty of water. It wasn't easy, but I finished it, and it felt good. I realize now that taking care of myself isn’t just about avoiding sickness; it’s about being able to do the things I need to do, like helping my family and farming our land. Dr. Bello was right. Small steps, every day, can make a big difference. Maybe tomorrow, I'll try to eat a little more of the okra my mother grows – it looks good and healthy.  It’s a start, and that’s enough for now.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/47b937fa-e3af-4b38-b3ab-11351927e4f1_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/47b937fa-e3af-4b38-b3ab-11351927e4f1_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/47b937fa-e3af-4b38-b3ab-11351927e4f1_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/47b937fa-e3af-4b38-b3ab-11351927e4f1_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/47b937fa-e3af-4b38-b3ab-11351927e4f1_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/47b937fa-e3af-4b38-b3ab-11351927e4f1_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/47b937fa-e3af-4b38-b3ab-11351927e4f1_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/47b937fa-e3af-4b38-b3ab-11351927e4f1_3.mp3}	I don't have a gym—what can I do at home or with everyday