              </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1-2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I live alone or have limited support</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">495a23bc-eb31-48f2-ba26-df254af05537</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I like to snackâ€”what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Aisha, and I’m a transport operator here in Dutse. It’s a tough job, always on the road, picking up passengers and trying to make enough to get by. Most days, I start with a big plate of Tuwo Shinkafa – that’s like a really thick rice pudding – and some fried plantains. It’s filling, and it keeps me going until lunchtime. Honestly, I don’t really think about anything else. I grab some roasted corn from the roadside vendors, maybe a few biscuits if I’m feeling fancy, and that’s usually it. My work keeps me busy, and I just… eat when I’m hungry. It’s simple, it’s easy, and it’s what I’m used to. I don't really have time to think about things like ‘healthy’ or ‘diabetes’ – I just need to get to the next pickup.\n\nYesterday, I had a really important trip – I was supposed to take a group of students to a school outside of town. I was running late, and I’d grabbed a quick plate of Tuwo Shinkafa and some moi moi from a vendor near the station. But halfway through the journey, I started feeling really shaky and dizzy. My head was spinning, and I couldn’t even focus on the road. I pulled over, completely panicked. The students were worried, and I felt terrible. That’s when I realized something was seriously wrong. I'd never really felt like this before, and it was scary. I knew I needed help, so I called the clinic in town, and a nurse came to check me out. She said my blood sugar was dangerously low, and I needed to eat something immediately.\n\nThe nurse, Fatima, was really kind. She explained that my diet – all that rice and fried food – was probably the problem. She told me about the importance of eating smaller, more frequent meals and choosing foods that wouldn’t spike my blood sugar. She gave me a little card with some pictures of healthy options – things like boiled beans, fruits, and some vegetables. I tried to follow her advice, but it was hard. I’m used to eating big meals, and changing my habits felt overwhelming. I tried to eat a handful of roasted peanuts for a snack, but I kept craving that Tuwo Shinkafa. It was frustrating, and I almost gave up. I was worried about missing my next trip, and honestly, I just didn't know how to do it.\n\nBut then I remembered the big trip I was supposed to take – a field trip for the students. I knew I needed to be strong and focused for that. So, I kept trying. I started taking a small bag of boiled beans with me on my trips, and I made sure to drink water instead of sugary drinks. It wasn’t easy, and I still slipped up sometimes, but slowly, I started to feel better. The dizziness went away, and I felt more energetic. I even managed to make it through that field trip without any problems. It wasn’t perfect, but I was proud of myself for taking care of my health. Now, I'm thinking about making small changes, one step at a time, and I realize that taking care of myself is important, even if it’s just a little bit at a time.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/495a23bc-eb31-48f2-ba26-df254af05537_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/495a23bc-eb31-48f2-ba26-df254af05537_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/495a23bc-eb31-48f2-ba26-df254af05537_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/495a23bc-eb31-48f2-ba26-df254af05537_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/495a23bc-eb31-48f2-ba26-df254af05537_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/495a23bc-eb31-48f2-ba26-df254af05537_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/495a23bc-eb31-48f2-ba26-df254af05537_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/495a23bc-eb31-48f2-ba26-df254af05537_3.mp3}	I like to snack—what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?	diet	female	consistency	2026-03-23 13:29:39.385306+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gemma-3-4b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Nari Kazehara is a 18-24 year old, \\nCu