       </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">82c7c837-de43-45dc-9aac-158c058086ec</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I don&#x27;t have a gymâ€”what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Every morning, Adisa wakes up and makes a small breakfast. She has a banana and a glass of milk. She thinks, 'This is enough. I’m not hungry.' But after eating, she feels heavy. Her legs feel like they’re made of sand. She walks to her little garden, where she plants herbs. She picks a few thyme leaves and puts them in a bowl. She smiles and says, 'I’m doing good.' But by afternoon, she feels weak. She sits on the back step and just watches the birds. She thinks, 'Why can’t I just stay still?' Then she remembers—her health worker said, 'Walking helps your blood sugar go down. Even a little walk helps.' She didn’t believe it at first. 'I’m not strong enough,' she said. But the health worker just smiled and said, 'Try it. Just five minutes. No pressure.' And that’s when she decided to try.\n\nSo Adisa walks. First, she walks to the corner shop to buy a loaf of bread. She walks slowly, her feet touching the ground like a slow dance. She feels her legs move, and she thinks, 'This is new.' But after two days, she forgets. She eats a chocolate bar and sits down. She feels worse. Her hands shake. She says, 'I can’t do this.' But then she remembers: next week, she has to teach a new group. She has to speak clearly. She can’t be tired. So she tries again. This time, she walks to the park. She walks with her shoes on, her arms swinging. The sun is warm on her skin. She feels the breeze. She says to herself, 'I can do this. Just for the lesson.' She doesn’t run. She doesn’t race. She just walks. And slowly, she feels better.\n\nAfter a week, Adisa walks every day. She walks after her lesson, before she eats. She walks to the neighbour’s house to say hello. She walks past the old tree where the children play. She feels strong. Her legs don’t feel heavy. Her blood sugar feels balanced. She doesn’t need sugar or sweets to feel happy. She says to her friend, 'I didn’t think walking would help so much.' And the friend says, 'Maybe it does.' Adisa smiles. She knows now that small things matter. She has a new habit. And when she teaches her lesson, she speaks with confidence. The group says, 'You are so calm and strong.' She feels proud. She knows she can keep going. And she knows—walking is not just for the body. It’s for the heart too. And maybe, one day, she’ll teach a lesson about movement, too.\n\nAdisa walks every day now. She walks with her hands in her pockets, her head up. She doesn’t rush. She watches the birds. She listens to the wind. She knows her body is learning. She knows that small steps can change everything. And when she feels tired, she doesn’t stop. She just walks a little more. She knows that her diabetes is not a problem. It’s a part of her life. And with care, it can be managed. She says to herself, 'I can do this. I am strong. I am healthy. I am Adisa.' And she smiles. And she walks.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/82c7c837-de43-45dc-9aac-158c058086ec_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/82c7c837-de43-45dc-9aac-158c058086ec_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/82c7c837-de43-45dc-9aac-158c058086ec_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/82c7c837-de43-45dc-9aac-158c058086ec_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/82c7c837-de43-45dc-9aac-158c058086ec_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/82c7c837-de43-45dc-9aac-158c058086ec_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/82c7c837-de43-45dc-9aac-158c058086ec_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/82c7c837-de43-45dc-9aac-158c058086ec_3.mp3}	Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:29:39.646499+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Aisha Bello is a 35-44 year old, \\nCulture: Hausa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Trader, lives in a Suburban (near city or town) in Kano. Disability: she has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Storytelling. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Halal. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 1-2 days. Support network: Close friends. Health goals: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . her food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Aisha Bello and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in Eng