     \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Luo</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3-4 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Vegan</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">c86ef9c1-7ff3-40c0-9b71-8027af29c3d4</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	I wake up every morning, get ready for work, and head to the office. My day is always busy, and I often find myself eating late at night, usually something quick and easy like fried chips or a pie from the street vendor. I love dancing, especially Ohangla, and I try to fit in a few sessions during the week. But lately, I've been feeling tired and sluggish, and my blood sugar levels have been all over the place. I've been taking my insulin injections and oral medication, but I'm not sure if I'm doing enough. I have a small task at work coming up, a report that's due soon, and I'm worried that I won't be able to finish it on time if I don't get my energy levels up.\n\nI decided to visit my local clinic to get some advice on how to manage my blood sugar levels. The nurse was very helpful and explained to me that eating late at night was not good for my health, and that I should try to eat more balanced meals throughout the day. She also told me that I should aim to do at least 30 minutes of physical activity per day, and that walking was a great way to start. But she also suggested that I try other types of exercise, like swimming or cycling, to mix things up and keep my body guessing. I was a bit resistant at first, I mean, who has time to go swimming or cycling? But the nurse was very encouraging and gave me some practical tips on how to fit it into my busy schedule.\n\nI tried to follow the nurse's advice, but it wasn't easy. I started walking every day during my lunch break, but I found it hard to resist the temptation of fried foods and sugary snacks. I had a minor setback when I ate a whole pizza by myself one night, and my blood sugar levels skyrocketed. But I didn't give up. I knew I had a bigger task coming up at work, a project that required me to be at my best, and I was determined to get my health back on track. So I persisted, and I started to notice small changes, like having more energy and feeling more focused.\n\nAs the days went by, I started to feel like myself again. I was walking every day, and I had even started taking swimming lessons at the local pool. I was eating more balanced meals, and I had cut back on the fried foods and sugary snacks. My blood sugar levels were under control, and I was feeling more confident. I finished my report at work, and it was a huge success. My boss was impressed, and my colleagues were all congratulating me. I realized that taking care of my health was not just about avoiding crises, but about living a better life. I was open to continued health improvement, and I was excited to see where this new journey would take me.\n\nNow, I'm not saying it's easy. There are still days when I feel like giving up, when the temptation of fried foods and sugary snacks is too strong. But I know that I have the tools and the knowledge to make better choices. I know that I can do more than just walk for exercise, and that I can try new things like swimming and cycling. I'm excited to see where this journey takes me, and I'm grateful for the support of my healthcare team. I'm Akinyi Odinga, and I'm taking control of my health, one step at a time.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c86ef9c1-7ff3-40c0-9b71-8027af29c3d4_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c86ef9c1-7ff3-40c0-9b71-8027af29c3d4_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c86ef9c1-7ff3-40c0-9b71-8027af29c3d4_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c86ef9c1-7ff3-40c0-9b71-8027af29c3d4_3.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c86ef9c1-7ff3-40c0-9b71-8027af29c3d4_4.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/c86ef9c1-7ff3-40c0-9b71-8027af29c3d4_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/c86ef9c1-7ff3-40c0-9b71-8027af29c3d4_1.mp3,https://prxqua