iambo and let me tell you, sometimes my work just takes over my whole day, you know? I get home late, and the only food in my cupboard is pap and a few beans from the street market—nothing fancy. I’m always tired but I still go to the kitchen, cook quick and eat well after sunset. I used to think, 'It’s just food, hey, what harm can a late meal do?' But the other night, I had to finish a small report for work before I slept. My mind just started drifting, I couldn’t focus, my eyes were heavy, and my hands felt shaky. By the time I was typing, the words didn’t even make sense. Can you imagine? I failed to finish, and it all started with that late supper.\n\nThe next day at the clinic, Nurse Faith, the diabetes sister, called me in. She just shook her head and asked, 'Akinyi, why do you keep eating so late?' I tried to explain but she wasn’t cross, just firm. She said, 'When you eat late, especially heavy foods, your blood sugar goes up while you sleep. You wake up more tired, harder for your medicine to work, and you can’t perform your best the next day.' She told me to try eat earlier, plan small meals, and maybe keep some boiled sweet potatoes or fruits on hand. She even wrote it down for me—simple, clear, like she knew my struggles.\n\nEish, changing my eating habits was not easy! I found myself hungry after a long day, reaching for whatever was left, fighting old habits. One night I nearly went back to my late cooking, but then I remembered I have to finish a bigger project at work—a talk I’m supposed to give next week. I made a cup of rooibos tea, munched on a banana early, and forced myself to wait out the hunger till morning. It was hard. Ag, old choices can pull you back, ne?\n\nLittle by little I started to feel the change. I woke up with a clearer head and less shakiness in my hands. When the big work presentation came, I felt steady—my words flowed, and my boss even smiled. Now I’m thinking, maybe this is how I can keep my diabetes steady, one small choice at a time. Who knew skipping that late-night chow could make such a difference? I’m not perfect, but I’m moving—one step closer to my healthy self.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/858b7f69-5fca-4d13-9cc6-a8dba0db4954_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/858b7f69-5fca-4d13-9cc6-a8dba0db4954_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/858b7f69-5fca-4d13-9cc6-a8dba0db4954_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/858b7f69-5fca-4d13-9cc6-a8dba0db4954_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/858b7f69-5fca-4d13-9cc6-a8dba0db4954_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/858b7f69-5fca-4d13-9cc6-a8dba0db4954_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/858b7f69-5fca-4d13-9cc6-a8dba0db4954_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/858b7f69-5fca-4d13-9cc6-a8dba0db4954_3.mp3}	I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? 	diet	female	control	2026-03-23 13:19:57.815335+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Owino Odhiambo is a 45-54 year old, \\nCulture: Luo male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Journalist, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Homa Bay. Disability: he has none. Religion: Christianity (Methodist). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Football (soccer). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healt