raph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "crisis_avoidance", "audio_prompts": ["Akinyi Odhiambo exhales heavily as she moves slowly in her kitchen. There are quiet background sounds of cutlery clattering, a kettle whistling, and the city night faint through a thin window. She mutters to herself with tired, slightly slurred speech, 'Shoo, maybe I’ll just warm this up quick.' Her voice thins out near the end as she yawns softly. The tone is weary, and the silence is broken only by the gentle scrape of a pot.", "Nurse Faith (female) sits nearby, her shoes shuffling lightly on the tiled floor. Nurse Faith speaks with gentle authority, 'Akinyi, tell me, what time did you eat last night?' Her pen clicks on the desk as Akinyi Odhiambo hesitates, clearing her throat and answering softly, 'Eish, it was late, sister.' Papers shuffle, and Faith writes while Akinyi breathes unevenly, showing mild embarrassment. The hum of the clinic’s old fan underlies their soft, conversational tones.", "Akinyi Odhiambo alone, sitting in her lounge. The ticking of a cheap wall clock mixes with the faint buzz of a night insect outside. She sighs deeply, shifting in her seat. Her voice, husky and deliberate, speaks aloud as if encouraging herself, 'Akinyi, just hold on, you don’t need to eat now.' The spoon clinks softly against her mug. There’s a restless tapping of her foot and a subtle, hopeful hum near the end.", "A coworker, a young man, sits across the room as Akinyi Odhiambo stands and begins her presentation. The quiet shuffling of papers and occasional chair creaks mix with her steady, clear voice: 'So, let’s talk about our new project...' Her words are fluent, her breathing even. There’s a hint of pride in her tone, and soft murmurs of agreement from the small group echo across the bright, echoing room."], "image_prompts": ["Akinyi Odhiambo, a young Black woman, stands in her modest kitchen at night, slouching tiredly against a small plastic table cluttered with a chipped enamel plate and a tin of beans. Harsh fluorescent light highlights her tired eyes and worn work clothes. The narrow kitchen walls reveal peeling paint. Texture of the wooden cupboard is rough, and the tin plate shines under the single lightbulb, with Akinyi distractedly stirring her food.", "Nurse Faith, a middle-aged Black woman in a bright clinic uniform, sitting across a plastic desk from Akinyi Odhiambo in a small public clinic consulting room. Wide shot. The sunlight through the barred window casts soft natural light over Akinyi, who sits scratching her arm, shoulders hunched in mild discomfort, while Nurse Faith speaks clearly, pen poised over a pamphlet. The colorful, slightly faded posters about diabetes and healthy eating line the wall behind them.", "Akinyi Odhiambo, alone, sits on a small worn sofa at night in her sparse lounge, medium shot. She holds a cup of rooibos tea close to her chest, her body leaning forward, eyes on her phone and a banana peel at her side. The dim yellow lamp creates shadows, her posture tense but with a hopeful set to her brow. Beside her, a worn laptop rests unopened, hinting at the task she needs to prepare.", "Akinyi Odhiambo stands at the front of a small community office meeting room, wide shot. She gestures confidently with an open hand as she presents, her body upright, lips curved into a gentle smile. Her work clothes are neat. One coworker, a young Black man, watches her with interest from a cracked office chair. Bright overhead lighting reflects off the polished table. Akinyi’s eyes are clear and focused."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 1.799616140127182, "task_parent_id": "cc064760-db4d-47ce-ac50-f36ef998c275", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"Which affordable, gluten-free traditional Luo foods can I find in Kisumu that help manage type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What are locally available vegetables or grains in Kisumu urban markets that are both budget-friendly and suitable for a gluten-free diet?\\",\\n  \\"How do other Luo Christian women my age with type 2 diabetes balance traditional dishes like kuon (ugali) and healthy eating on a tight budget?\\",\\n  \\"Are there community gardens or subsistence farming groups in Kisumu that support young female farmers with limited access to healthy foods?\\",\\n  \\"What are some simple Luo recipes for type 2 diabetes that avoid gluten but still let me enjoy familiar tastes from home?\\",\\n  \\"How can I incorporate physical activities suitable for women in their late twenties and early thirties who work as farmers in Kisumuâ€™s urban areas?\\",\\n  \\"What support programs or clinics in Kisumu offer nutrition counseling for diabetic patients who prefer both lifestyle changes and herbal or traditional medicine?\\",\\n  \\"Is there guidance from local Lutheran churches in Kisumu for healthy eating during church gatherings or festive seasons, especially for members with dietary restrictions like gluten-free?\\",\\n  \\"How do Luo communities in Kisumu address the rising cost of healthy foods, and are there any initiatives to improve access for subsistence farmers?\\",\\n  \\"What are the pros and cons of using herbal or traditional medicine for diabetes management along with insulin and oral medication, according to local Kisumu healthcare providers?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I buy gluten-free substitutes for common Luo staples in Kisumu city that wonâ€™t greatly increase my food expenses?\\",\\n  \\"What are common challenges faced by Luo women farmers in Kisumu who need to balance work, healthy eating, and regular diabetes care, and how do they overcome them?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "Relevant fields: Limited access to healthy foods, Working adult, Type 2 Diabetes status, Insulin injections, Oral medication, Lifestyle changes, Regular healthcare access, Gluten-Free diet, No physical activity, Sometimes eats late at night, Health knowledge gap: Not sure how late eating affects blood sugar.", "selected_profile_info": ["city_town", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "daily_routine", "medication", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
a540d0cd-b3c8-499e-9501-00383fe260e2	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b818-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Migori</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Atheism</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Ogola Odhiambo</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Male</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">18-24</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Suburban (near city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Sorghum farmer</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Ohangla dancing</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), D â€” Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community support groups</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Luo</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5-7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kosher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">a540d0cd-b3c8-499e-9501-00383fe260e2</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouragedâ€”how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, farming sorghum in Migori keeps me on my toes every day. I’m always busy in my fields before sunrise, then back again after. But I didn’t really think much about what I ate. I’d snack on some fried maize or sweet mandazi thinking it’s just fuel, but my blood sugar felt like a rollercoaster, and sometimes I was just plain tired in the middle of work. One day, I tried to finish planting early so I could see my friends dance Ohangla that evening, but I just got dizzy and had to stop. Who knew that my snacking was messing me up so bad?\n\nLuckily, the nurse at the local clinic explained things to me in a way that made sense. She said, 'Ogola, choose foods that won’t spike your sugar, like your sorghum porridge, green vegetables, and nuts instead of the fried stuff.’ She reminded me that even though you like your chai tea with sugar, cutting back slowly helps too. I was a bit unsure at first—could I really change all that? But she said, ‘Take small steps. Your diabetes is something you can manage.’ Hearing it this way made me think, maybe I can do this.\n\nChanging my habits wasn’t easy. I tried swapping my mandazi for some boiled sweet potatoes, but sometimes the smell of the fried foods from the market was too tempting, and I gave in. Plus, I still wanted to join my mates after work for drinks sometimes, which didn’t help. But then I reminded myself—if I want to master planting the new plot this season and stay strong for dancing Ohangla, I need energy that won't let me down. So, I kept on trying, even when it was tough.\n\nNow, I feel stronger and less tired in the fields. I’m more careful with what I eat, sticking to my sorghum porridge in the morning and fresh veggies from the market. When I dance Ohangla with my friends, I have the energy to keep up till late. That big planting task I was worried about? I finished it this season faster than before. It feels good to take care of myself and still enjoy life. I’m starting to believe the little things really do make a difference.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a540d0cd-b3c8-499e-9501-00383fe260e2_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a540d0cd-b3c8-499e-9501-00383fe260e2_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a540d0cd-b3c8-499e-9501-00383fe260e2_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a540d0cd-b3c8-499e-9501-00383fe260e2_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a540d0cd-b3c8-499e-9501-00383fe260e2_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a540d0cd-b3c8-499e-9501-00383fe260e2_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a540d0cd-b3c8-499e-9501-00383fe260e2_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a540d0cd-b3c8-499e-9501-00383fe260e2_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:19:59.774911+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Owiti Odhiambo is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Luo male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Lecturer, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Siaya. Disability: he has none. Religion: Christianity (other). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Ohangla dancing. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Vegetarian. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 3-4 days. Support network: Healthcare provider or counsellor. Health goals: How often and how long shou