-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Clinical officer</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Ohangla dancing</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Luo</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1-2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kosher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3071dc79-21ed-476d-98b6-9309ec6089c3</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I don&#x27;t have a gymâ€”what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Akinyi, and I live in a small village, far from the big city. It’s quiet here, really quiet. Most days, I’m busy at the clinic, helping the doctor and looking after all the people who come for sick. We mostly eat maize porridge and sometimes, if we’re lucky, a bit of fish from the river. It’s good, but sometimes my legs get tired, you know? Like, really tired, especially after a long day at work. I don't really think much about it, just keep going.  Do you think tiredness is just because I work too hard?\n\nToday, I had to deliver a very important message to the chief about a sick child. It was a big job, carrying the message all the way across the fields. But I was rushing, and I didn’t stop to drink water. And I skipped my usual walk.  The message was heavy, and my legs felt like lead. I dropped it! Right there in the dust! The chief was very disappointed. He said, 'Akinyi, you must be careful! You need to take care of yourself if you want to do your job properly.'  It made me feel bad, really bad. It was embarrassing, and I knew it wasn't just tiredness.\n\nThe nurse at the clinic, Mama Rose, she talked to me. She said, ‘Akinyi, your body is like a car. If you don’t look after it, it won’t run properly. With your diabetes, you need to be extra careful about what you eat and how you move.’ She showed me pictures of simple exercises – just walking, stretching, maybe even dancing to Ohangla music! She said a little bit every day can make a big difference. It sounded a bit scary, but she told me it was okay to start slowly. She said, ‘Think about the message, Akinyi. You want to deliver it without dropping it, don’t you?’\n\nSo, I started walking a little bit each day. Just around the village. And I tried to stretch my legs when I was waiting for patients. It was hard at first, like my legs remembered how to be tired. But then, I remembered the chief’s message. I wanted to be strong enough to carry it without stumbling. And you know what? It’s getting easier! My legs don’t feel quite so tired, and I feel a bit more… brighter. I even tried a little bit of Ohangla dancing in the kitchen – Mama Rose said that’s good too!  I think I can do this. I think I can be strong.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3071dc79-21ed-476d-98b6-9309ec6089c3_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3071dc79-21ed-476d-98b6-9309ec6089c3_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3071dc79-21ed-476d-98b6-9309ec6089c3_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3071dc79-21ed-476d-98b6-9309ec6089c3_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3071dc79-21ed-476d-98b6-9309ec6089c3_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3071dc79-21ed-476d-98b6-9309ec6089c3_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3071dc79-21ed-476d-98b6-9309ec6089c3_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3071dc79-21ed-476d-98b6-9309ec6089c3_3.mp3}	What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:20:01.537833+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "gemma-3-4b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: \\nCulture: None female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Clinical officer, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in None. Disability: she has none. Religion: None. Hobbies: Ohangla dancing. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Alcohol drinking: Yes. Health goals: I don't have a gymâ€”what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?. Tone of the output: crisis_avoidance.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Akinyi Odhiambo and understanding the persona's needs (\\"What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?\\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?. \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a crisis_avoidance tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "crisis_avoidance", "audio_prompts": ["Akinyi’s voice, slightly tired and hesitant, speaking in a South African English accent. ‘My name is Akinyi, and I live in a small village… quiet, really quiet.  I’m busy at the clinic… maize porridge… sometimes fish… my legs get tired… just keep going. Do you think tiredness is just because I work too hard?’ Background sounds: distant birdsong, gentle wind rustling through leaves, faint chatter of villagers.", "Akinyi’s voice, showing frustration and embarrassment, a slightly rushed pace. ‘Today, I had to deliver a message… a big job… across the fields… I dropped it! Right there in the dust! The chief was disappointed… you must be careful… take care of yourself.’  Sound effects: the sound of a scroll falling to the ground, a brief, sharp gasp from Akinyi, a distant shout from the chief.", "Mama Rose’s voice, calm and reassuring, a slightly lower pitch and a soothing tone. ‘Akinyi, your body is like a car… if you don’t look after it… it won’t run properly… with your diabetes… you need to be extra careful… walking… stretching… Ohangla music!’ Background sounds: gentle clinic sounds – a ticking clock, the rustle of paper, the quiet murmur of other patients.", "Akinyi’s voice, reflecting a growing sense of determination and hope, a moderate pace and a slightly brighter tone. ‘So, I started walking… stretching… it was hard… but I remembered the message… I wanted to be strong… my legs don’t feel quite so tired… I feel a bit more… brighter.  I even tried a little Ohangla dancing…’ Background sounds: footsteps on dirt, faint music from an Ohangla song playing softly in the distance, a happy sigh from Akinyi."], "image_prompts": ["Akinyi Odhiambo, a young woman with dark skin, stands in a rural village, holding a small, rolled-up message scroll. She looks weary, with a slightly slumped posture and a concerned expression. The background is a dusty path leading to a small, thatched-roof building – the clinic. Sunlight is warm and golden, casting long shadows. Texture: rough fabric of the scroll, dusty ground, weathered wood of the clinic.", "Akinyi Odhiambo, mid-stride, struggling to carry a heavy message scroll across a dirt path in a rural village. She’s sweating slightly, her face showing determination mixed with effort. A small, brown dog runs alongside her, playfully nipping at her heels. The background is a field of maize stalks swaying gently in the breeze. Lighting: harsh sunlight, creating strong shadows. Texture: rough dirt path, dry maize stalks, worn leather straps of the scroll.", "Akinyi Odhiambo sitting across from Mama Rose, a kind-looking older woman in a white clinic uniform, at a simple wooden table in the clinic. Mama Rose is pointing to a colorful picture book showing simple exercises. Akinyi is looking at the picture with a slightly hesitant expression, her hands clasped together.  The background is a brightly lit, sparsely furnished clinic room. Texture: worn wooden table, colorful picture book, smooth fabric of Mama Rose's uniform.", "Akinyi Odhiambo, smiling slightly, is walking slowly down a village path, a relaxed posture. She's wearing a simple cotton dress. The background shows colorful houses and people going about their daily lives. A small, playful dog bounds alongside her. Lighting: soft, diffused sunlight. Texture: worn dirt path, brightly colored fabrics of the houses, smooth fur of the dog."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 86.09775114059448, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some home-based physical activities suitable for a female clinical officer living in rural Homa Bay, Kenya, without gym access?\\",\\n  \\"Are there Luo-specific traditional dances or Ohangla dance routines that can help manage Type 2 diabetes through exercise in a rural setting?\\",\\n  \\"What healthy, Halal meal options are commonly available in rural Homa Bay for someone with easy food access and Type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How do community or religious groups in Homa Bay support Baptist women in maintaining an active lifestyle and diabetes care?\\",\\n  \\"What creative ways can I use household items for strength training or aerobic exercise at home in rural Kenya?\\",\\n  \\"Which outdoor or group physical activities are popular among rural Luo women aged 25-34 in Homa Bay that fit a busy working schedule?\\",\\n  \\"What are some evidence-based strategies for balancing insulin management and work responsibilities as a clinical officer in Kenya?\\",\\n  \\"Are there local support programs or diabetes education resources in Homa Bay County tailored to healthcare workers?\\",\\n  \\"How can Baptist faith practices in rural Kenya be incorporated into daily physical activity and wellness routines?\\",\\n  \\"What barriers do women clinical officers face when engaging in physical activity in rural Kenyan communities, and how can these be addressed?\\",\\n  \\"Are there local stories or role models among Luo women who successfully manage Type 2 diabetes through non-gym physical activities?\\",\\n  \\"What are the risks and benefits of exercising 1-2 days per week for rural women with Type 2 diabetes, and how can I safely increase activity given my work schedule?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": ["city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "describe_location", "disability", "occupation", "hobbies", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_frequency", "t2d_status", "alcohol", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
ff203d88-88fb-4653-b870-a076c73e47a3	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b818-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kisumu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Roman Catholic)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Achieng Odhiambo</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">25-34</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Urban (city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Sugarcane farmer</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Ohangla dancing</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Luo</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5-7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Lactose-free</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Close friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">ff203d88-88fb-4653-b870-a076c73e47a3</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Eating healthy can get expensiveâ€”how can I manage a nutritious diet on a tight budget? And can I still enjoy some traditional foods? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Ai, my life, it's always busy, busy, busy! I’m Achieng, and I work at the market, selling vegetables. It's good money, but standing all day… whew! I love my chakalaka with pap in the mornings, gives me strength, and a nice bit of polony on a roll for lunch. Sometimes I forget to drink enough water, you know? Just yesterday, Old Man Themba asked me to sort a big pile of tomatoes for him – a small job, right? But by lunchtime, my back was killing me and my hands were shaking so much I couldn't even pick up the tomatoes properly. I just felt… weak. Honestly, I thought maybe it was just a bad day. But was it, really?\n\nMy neighbour, Mama Zanele, she saw me struggling. She's a nurse at the clinic, bless her. She said, 'Achieng, you need to look after yourself! All this hard work, it needs a strong body.' I told her, 'But I'm fine, just tired.' She just smiled and said, 'Tired is one thing, but your sugar is a bit high, and you need to move more. Walk for 30 minutes every day, Achieng, and drink more water. Even just around the block. It will help your back and your hands, I promise.' Thirty minutes? I thought, 'That's a long time! Where will I find the time?' But Mama Zanele explained it simply, 'Small steps, Achieng. Start with 15, then build up. It's for you.'\n\nTrying to walk every day was hard, I won't lie. The first few days, I’d walk for ten minutes and then just… stop. My legs would ache, and I'd think about all the things I needed to do at the market. One day, I promised a big customer I'd have a special order of spinach ready for her, but I was so tired from trying to walk that I forgot to go to the supplier! She wasn’t happy, and I felt terrible. But then I remembered Old Man Themba’s tomatoes, and the bigger order I have next week - a wedding! I *had* to get stronger. I pushed myself a little harder each day, even when it felt like my feet were glued to the ground.\n\nSlowly, slowly, things started to change. I started feeling less tired at the end of the day. My back didn't ache as much. And believe it or not, I actually started enjoying my walks! I found a route through the park, and it was so peaceful. When the day came to sort the spinach for the wedding, I did it quickly and easily. My hands didn’t shake, and my back felt strong. The customer was happy, and I was proud of myself. Mama Zanele was right. Maybe looking after myself isn't just about feeling good, but about being able to do the things I need to do. Maybe I should ask her about other things I can do to stay strong, eh?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ff203d88-88fb-4653-b870-a076c73e47a3_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ff203d88-88fb-4653-b870-a076c73e47a3_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ff203d88-88fb-4653-b870-a076c73e47a3_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ff203d88-88fb-4653-b870-a076c73e47a3_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ff203d88-88fb-4653-b870-a076c73e47a3_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ff203d88-88fb-4653-b870-a076c73e47a3_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ff203d88-88fb-4653-b870-a076c73e47a3_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ff203d88-88fb-4653-b870-a076c73e47a3_3.mp3}	How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:20:04.372712+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "gemma-3-27b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: \\nCulture: None female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. Religion: None. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol d