                  <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Luo</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5-7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kosher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">59c4474f-46f6-4e72-98ba-33686cfddffa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouragedâ€”how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Now, let me tell you, life as a sugarcane farmer in Siaya isn't easy, you know? Every morning, before the sun even thinks about waking up, I’m out there with my panga, chopping. Strong work, it is. I usually grab a quick bite - maybe some ugali and fish, sometimes with a bit of sukuma wiki if Mama Rosa at the market has a good batch. But honestly? I don't really think much about what I eat, just that it fills my belly. Yesterday though? Disaster. Old Man Omondi needed a small section of cane cleared for replanting, a little job, I thought. But halfway through, I just...couldn’t. My legs felt like lead, and I was sweating like a boiled potato. I had to stop, and Omondi, well, he wasn't pleased. He just shook his head and said, 'Otieno, what's happening to you, boy? You used to be strong as a bull!' It got me thinking, though… what *is* happening?\n\nI decided to go to the clinic, you see. Sister Agnes, she’s a good woman, always has a kind word. I told her about feeling tired, about the cane, about Omondi’s words stinging my ears. She checked me over, took some blood, and then she explained… diabetes. Said my sugar was a bit high. I wasn't too worried, honestly. I thought, 'Is this why I get so thirsty?' She explained it all slowly, like I was a small child. 'Otieno,' she said, 'you need to move your body more, and be careful with the sweet things. Even the mangoes, enjoy them, but not too many!' She told me to walk for at least thirty minutes most days, and to try and eat more greens. Thirty minutes? That didn't sound so bad, but changing what I eat? That felt… difficult. But then I remembered Omondi's big field needing clearing next month. A proper job, that one. I needed to be ready.\n\nTrying to walk thirty minutes every day? Whew! It's harder than it sounds, let me tell you. The first few days, I barely made it ten minutes before needing to rest. My body felt so heavy. And the ugali… oh, the ugali! It's so good with fish stew, but Sister Agnes’s words kept ringing in my ears. I tried swapping some of it for more sukuma wiki, but my mum, she looked at me like I’d grown a second head! 'What kind of son refuses ugali?' she asked, laughing. But I stuck with it, mostly. That big clearing job for Omondi was looming, and I knew if I didn't get my strength back, I'd be letting him down. Plus, I didn't want him thinking I was becoming weak. One day, I skipped my walk because a friend invited me to a little Ohangla dancing, and I felt guilty all evening. I knew I had to choose.\n\nSlowly, slowly, things started to change. The walks got easier. I found myself actually enjoying them, especially when I walked along the river. I even started joining in with the younger guys for a quick jog sometimes! And I got used to eating more greens, even though I still sneak a little extra ugali when my mum isn't looking. When it came time to clear Omondi’s field, I was ready. I worked the whole day, from sunrise to sunset, and didn't feel that heavy, tired feeling like before. Omondi was impressed, he really was. He even gave me a big bunch of bananas as a thank you! Now, I'm thinking… maybe Sister Agnes was right. Maybe taking care of my health isn’t just about feeling better, it’s about being able to do the things I need to do, the things I love to do. Maybe I should ask her about some more ways to stay strong, eh?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/59c4474f-46f6-4e72-98ba-33686cfddffa_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/59c4474f-46f6-4e72-98ba-33686cfddffa_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/59c4474f-46f6-4e72-98ba-33686cfddffa_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/59c4474f-46f6-4e72-98ba-33686cfddffa_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/59c4474f-46f6-4e72-98ba-33686cfddffa_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/a