td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">bddd541f-3036-45cd-987e-3ac18d3424d7</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouragedâ€”how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Achieng Odhiambo, and every morning I wake up before the sun, make a cup of strong tea, and prepare for my show at the radio station here in Homa Bay. I have always loved telling stories on air, so my job hardly feels like work. But sometimes, I just grab whatever food is easy—ugali, white bread, or mandazis with sugar—because I am always rushing! The other day, I was trying to write a funny story for my afternoon show, but I felt so weak and tired I couldn’t even finish the script. Eish! Isn’t it strange how a small thing like food can spoil your plans?\n\nLater that week, Sister Jane, a nurse from our local clinic, came to the station for a health talk. She saw me eating chapati and sodas in the staff kitchen and shook her head, joking, 'Mama Achieng, are you feeding your sugar or your stories?' I laughed, but deep down I felt worried. She sat with me and explained kindly—'You see, you can still enjoy most foods! Just eat more traditional vegetables like sukuma wiki, tilapia fish, brown ugali, and fruits like papaya. Reduce sugar and white bread. You’ll feel stronger on air, Mama!' I wanted to believe her but wondered, can simple changes really help me work better?\n\nThe next few days, I tried to listen to Sister Jane. Instead of sugary snacks, I carried roasted groundnuts and sweet potato to work. Ha! Temptation was everywhere—my friends were enjoying mandazis and sodas in the studio. I felt left out and almost gave up. But I remembered my special Sunday radio show was coming up, and I wanted energy for my stories. So I pushed on, promising myself, 'Just one more healthy lunch before I quit...'\n\nWith time, things started to change—slowly, but they did. My afternoons became brighter, my voice more lively, and my mind was sharper, even after a whole morning of storytelling. That Sunday, I told three stories on air without feeling tired, and the listeners called in to say, 'Mama Achieng, you sound younger today!' I smiled, thinking, who knew that changing my lunch could help me love my work again? Now, I am more open to learning new things for my health, one day at a time.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/bddd541f-3036-45cd-987e-3ac18d3424d7_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/bddd541f-3036-45cd-987e-3ac18d3424d7_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/bddd541f-3036-45cd-987e-3ac18d3424d7_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/bddd541f-3036-45cd-987e-3ac18d3424d7_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/bddd541f-3036-45cd-987e-3ac18d3424d7_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/bddd541f-3036-45cd-987e-3ac18d3424d7_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/bddd541f-3036-45cd-987e-3ac18d3424d7_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/bddd541f-3036-45cd-987e-3ac18d3424d7_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:19:58.078167+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Owino Odhiambo is a 45-54 year old, \\nCulture: Luo male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Journalist, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Homa Bay. Disability: he has none. Religion: Christianity (Methodist). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Football (soccer). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Vegetarian. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: Yes. Physical activity: 1-2 days. Support network: Close friends. Health goals: Sometimes I feel tired or discouragedâ€”how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?. his food access Limited access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Owino Odhiambo and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "iteration", "audio_prompts": ["Quiet interior kitchen sounds: Achieng Odhiambo’s soft sighs, the rustling of paper as she turns her radio script, the gentle clink of a teaspoon against a mug. Her voice, clear and mid-range with a slightly breathless texture, mutters to herself, 'Why am I feeling so tired every morning?' The background is filled with muffled bird song through the window.", "Female voice of Sister Jane, energetic and clear, laughs nearby and says, 'Eh, Mama Achieng, are you feeding your sugar or your stories?' Achieng Odhiambo chuckles, her reply a little uncertain: 'Maybe both…' Chairs scrape softly on the tile; plates clink as Sister Jane gently explains about sukuma wiki and brown ugali. The staff kitchen buzzes quietly with distant radio static.", "Quiet soundscape of Achieng Odhiambo’s footsteps in the radio corridor, her steady breathing and the rustle of her lunch bag. In the background, a colleague’s cheerful voice calls, 'Achieng, mandazi?' Achieng pauses, her own moderate, clear voice says softly, 'No, not today.' The hum of equipment and distant voices from the studio.", "Warm, lively studio ambience: Achieng Odhiambo’s voice is strong, animated, and confident as she delivers a story on air. She laughs with genuine amusement, her words flowing smoothly in South African-accented English. Listeners’ call-in lines beep faintly in the background. Her microphone pops slightly, and papers shuffle as she moves energetically during the live show."], "image_prompts": ["Candid, natural, unposed photo of Achieng Odhiambo, a 65+ year old Luo woman, in her modest but neat suburban Homa Bay kitchen. She is wearing a colorful kitenge dress, standing with a half-eaten mandazi in one hand and her radio script in the other, looking frustrated at the kitchen table cluttered with bread, sugar, and tea. Soft morning light comes through the window, expressing fatigue through her slightly hunched shoulders and slow posture. Textures of wooden table, metal teapot, and simple kitchen items visible.", "Female nurse named Sister Jane (about 50 years old) sits next to Achieng Odhiambo in the vibrant staff kitchen at a small Homa Bay radio station. Wide shot. Achieng is holding a chapati in one hand, looking puzzled but engaged, while Sister Jane, in a neat nurse's uniform, laughs and gestures towards typical Luo healthy foods on the table (tilapia, sukuma wiki, papaya). Scene lit by bright midday light, subtle posters about diabetes in the background.", "Medium shot. Achieng Odhiambo walks into her radio studio carrying a reusable container with roasted groundnuts and sweet potato slices. In the background, a younger female colleague smiles and waves, holding a soda and a bag of mandazis. Achieng's face is determined but a little wistful, showing resolve in her stride. Detailed view of classic painted radio mic, wooden desks, and bright colored kitenge head wrap.", "Medium shot of Achieng Odhiambo at the radio station microphone, eyes bright, smiling widely as she tells a story. A notepad and plate with brown ugali and vegetables are at her elbow. The studio has posters and traditional Luo decorations. Afternoon sunlight slants across her face, and she is sitting upright and animated, one hand lifted as she speaks with enthusiasm."], "language_code": "en-KE", "avg_time_taken": 1.799616140127182, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are culturally relevant ways for a Luo woman in Homa Bay to stay motivated to manage type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What exercise routines are popular among older women (65+) in suburban areas of Homa Bay, Kenya, especially those with diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there community or church support groups near Homa Bay for Christian women managing type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How can storytelling and socialising with family help with emotional well-being and motivation for elderly Luo women with diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Which healthy foods commonly available in Homa Bay's suburban markets are recommended for Luo elders with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How do African Independent Churches in Homa Bay support health, wellness, and diabetes education for their elderly female members?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any traditional Luo herbal remedies that can be safely used with insulin and metformin for type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What advice do Homa Bay healthcare clinics provide women over 65 for staying active and managing blood sugar?\\",\\n  \\"How does regular physical activity help control blood sugar in older Luo women, and are there local exercise groups in Homa Bay for seniors?\\",\\n  \\"What are some Luo recipes that are both traditional and suitable for someone with type 2 diabetes and no dietary restrictions?\\",\\n  \\"Are there radio programs in Homa Bay focused on diabetes education for older adults, and how can a presenter get involved?\\",\\n  \\"What are the challenges and motivational strategies for older women in Homa Bay who manage diabetes but rarely consume alcohol and do not smoke?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "Relevant fields: Daily routine (working adult, radio presenter), Health gaps (diet knowledge for diabetes), Access to health resources (local clinic), Motivation (work performance, upcoming work task), Community support (family, church, social groups), Dietary habits (gluten-free, easy access to healthy foods), Diabetes management (struggles with motivation/tiredness, regular exercise, medication).", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
23ae908a-1046-4d9c-bd6c-0d96531388f0	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b818-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kisumu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Methodist)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Odhiambo Okello</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Male</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">18-24</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Suburban (near city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Civil servant</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Ohangla dancing</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Once or twice a week</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), D â€” Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Luo</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Gluten-Free</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">23ae908a-1046-4d9c-bd6c-0d96531388f0</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Eating healthy can get expensiveâ€”how can I manage a nutritious diet on a tight budget? And can I still enjoy some traditional foods? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, sometimes I wonder if I'm doing enough to look after myself, you know? I’m Odhiambo, a civil servant here in Kisumu, just trying to make a life. Every day, I’m working hard but when it comes to moving around or exercise, I just don’t do much. My diet’s mostly okay, but sometimes I eat more ugali and fried fish than what’s good for my blood sugar, especially since I have this diabetes thing. One day, I had this small task at work where I had to stand for hours, but I felt tired and dizzy halfway through. I thought, 'Ah, maybe that’s just how things are,' so I didn’t think much about moving more or managing my diet better."\n\nThen one afternoon, I visited the local clinic for my regular check-up. The nurse, a kind woman from our community, asked how I was feeling and I told her about the tiredness at work. She smiled and said, ‘Odhiambo, you don’t need a gym to stay active. Have you tried simple things like walking around your compound or using empty water bottles as weights? Oh, and dancing Ohangla is a great way to move too!’ At first, I wasn’t so sure. How could such small things help with my diabetes? But she explained it in a way that felt right for me—simple steps I could do at home without any costs, even while keeping my food gluten-free and traditional.”\n\nSo, I started trying these small exercises at home. Walking around the house, lifting those bottles, even a little Ohangla in the evenings. It wasn’t easy at first. Some days, I’d forget or just feel lazy, and I wanted to give up. Once, I even skipped all movement days because I was tired from work, and my sugars felt off. But I kept going because I knew there was a bigger task coming—a church event where I’d be performing Ohangla. I wanted to be strong and not feel tired in front of everyone. That pushed me to keep at it, even when habits made it hard."\n\nWeeks later, I could feel the change. I wasn’t as tired standing for long hours at work, and my blood sugar was steadier. At the church event, I danced my heart out with confidence and energy. It felt good, more than just the dance—knowing I could take care of myself with small steps. Now, I’m thinking about what other small changes I can make, and I’m open to keep improving because, hey, if I can do this with just bottles and some dancing, what’s next?"	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/23ae908a-1046-4d9c-bd6c-0d96531388f0_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/23ae908a-1046-4d9c-bd6c-0d96531388f0_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/23ae908a-1046-4d9c-bd6c-0d96531388f0_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/23ae908a-1046-4d9c-bd6c-0d96531388f0_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/23ae908a-1046-4d9c-bd6c-0d96531388f0_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/23ae908a-1046-4d9c-bd6c-0d96531388f0_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/23ae908a-1046-4d9c-bd6c-0d96531388f0_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/23ae908a-1046-4d9c-bd6c-0d96531388f0_3.mp3}	I don't have a gym—what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?	exercise	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:20:00.02079+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Owiti Odhiambo is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Luo male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Lecturer, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Siaya. Disability: he has none. Religion: Christianity (other). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Ohangla dancing. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine