n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Luo</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5-7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Lactose-free</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">cc887af1-6d26-4bad-b407-75309372e6c2</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I don&#x27;t have a gymâ€”what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Ai, my life, it's always busy, busy! I'm Achieng, and I farm sugarcane near Kisumu. Every morning, I wake up before the sun, drink strong tea with a little milk – I can't do without my tea, can I? – and maybe a mandazi if there's one left from yesterday. Then it's straight to the shamba. I love my work, truly, but these days, getting through a full day is… hard. Just yesterday, I was trying to fix the watering system, a small thing, really. But halfway through, I felt so tired, so weak, I had to sit down. Old Man Oloo walked by and said I was looking pale. I just thought it was the heat. I didn’t think much of it, you know? Just a little tired. But now we have to harvest a whole field, a big one, and I'm worrying how I’ll manage. It feels like a mountain!\n\nI finally went to the clinic, pushed by my daughter, Atieno. She worries too much, that one! But the nurse, Sister Agnes, she was kind. She listened when I told her about the tiredness and the pale feeling. She checked my sugar, and said it was a bit high. 'Achieng,' she said, 'you need to move more. Walking is good, very good. Start with small walks around the shamba, then longer ones. No need for fancy gyms! And try to eat more greens, less sweet things.' I was a little confused. Just walking? I thought I needed something…more. But Sister Agnes smiled. 'Walking strengthens your heart, Achieng. It helps your sugar come down. It gives you energy.' She even showed me how to check my feet for sores, because of the sugar. It all seemed so simple, but could it really make a difference?\n\nTrying to walk more was harder than I thought! I'm used to working, not just walking for the sake of it. The first few days, I barely made it around the sugarcane field once before I was breathless. And then, the day before we started preparing for the harvest, I forgot to take my evening medicine. I felt awful – shaky and dizzy. I nearly gave up on the walking altogether. 'What's the point?' I grumbled to Atieno. 'I'm still tired!' But then I remembered the harvest. That big field, waiting to be cut. I couldn’t let my tiredness ruin it. So, I took my medicine, and forced myself to walk, even if it was just a short distance. I told myself, 'Achieng, you can do this. For the sugarcane, for your family!'\n\nSlowly, slowly, things started to change. I began walking a little further each day. I started swapping some of the mandazi for fruits like mangoes and avocados from our tree. It wasn't easy, believe me! But I found myself feeling a little stronger, a little less tired. And when the harvest came, well, I was surprised. I still got tired, of course, but I didn't collapse like I feared. I worked alongside my family, cutting and loading the sugarcane, and I actually enjoyed it! Old Man Oloo even said he hadn't seen me so energetic in ages. He asked me what my secret was, and I just smiled and told him about my walks. Now, I’m thinking, maybe Sister Agnes was right. Maybe just walking *is* enough, for now. But maybe… maybe I could try a little more. Perhaps some exercises Atieno showed me? It's a good feeling, this strength, and I want to keep it.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/cc887af1-6d26-4bad-b407-75309372e6c2_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/cc887af1-6d26-4bad-b407-75309372e6c2_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/cc887af1-6d26-4bad-b407-75309372e6c2_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/cc887af1-6d26-4bad-b407-75309372e6c2_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/cc887af1-6d26-4bad-b407-75309372e6c2_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/cc887af1-6d26-4bad-b407-75309372e6c2_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/cc887af1-6d26-4bad-b407-75309372e6c2_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryi