I don’t know why. I eat my porridge with beans and a little meat, like my mother used to make. I think, 'Why am I so tired?' I go to the garden to pick vegetables—carrots, onions, cabbage—because they’re cheap and easy. But I don’t eat them with meals. I just eat them when I’m hungry. I play ajua with my neighbour, Mwenda, after lunch. We laugh and move our stones. But after that, I feel weak. I don’t walk much. I just sit. I think, 'If I walk, will my blood sugar go down?' I don’t know. I just don’t feel like doing anything. Then one day, I promised Mwenda I’d help him teach a group of kids how to play ajua. But I can’t even walk to the village hall. My legs feel heavy. I don’t want to fail him. But I don’t know how to start.\n\nOne Sunday, I go to the local clinic. The nurse, Amina, is kind. She says, 'Jared, your blood sugar is high because you’re not moving much. When you walk, your body uses sugar better. Even a little walk helps.' I say, 'But I’m old. I don’t feel strong.' She smiles and says, 'You don’t need to run. Just walk for five minutes after your meal. You can do it after ajua. It’s like a small thank you to your body.' I don’t believe it at first. I say, 'But I’m tired. I don’t have energy.' She says, 'Try it. And when you feel better, you can teach the kids. That’s your reason.' I nod. I say, 'Okay, I’ll try.' I feel a little hope. I don’t know if it will work, but I want to help Mwenda.\n\nI try walking after ajua. First day, I walk to the corner shop. I feel dizzy. My legs are weak. I sit on a stone and rest. I think, 'I can’t do this.' But then I remember Mwenda’s face when he said, 'Jared, you taught me how to win.' I want to show him I can still teach. So I walk again—just five steps. Then I walk to the water pump. I feel better. But the next day, I skip it because I’m busy. I cook dinner and eat too fast. My blood sugar goes up again. I feel sad. But I think, 'I have to do it again. The kids are waiting. I must be strong for them.' So I try again. This time, I walk slowly, with my hands on my knees. I don’t run. I just move. It feels good.\n\nAfter two weeks, I walk every day after ajua. I walk to the church, then back. I feel lighter. My blood sugar is not as high. I don’t feel tired as much. I start to smile when I walk. I tell Amina, 'It’s working.' She says, 'Good. Now you can teach the kids with confidence.' I go to the village hall. I show them how to play ajua. I say, 'When you move, your body feels better. It helps your blood sugar too.' The kids laugh and play. One girl says, 'I want to learn how to walk like you.' I feel proud. I don’t need to be strong to help. I just need to move. And now, I know—I can do it. I will keep walking. I will keep teaching. And I will stay healthy for my family.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a3f98731-e1fb-4bfe-b293-e39416e32f32_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a3f98731-e1fb-4bfe-b293-e39416e32f32_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a3f98731-e1fb-4bfe-b293-e39416e32f32_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/a3f98731-e1fb-4bfe-b293-e39416e32f32_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a3f98731-e1fb-4bfe-b293-e39416e32f32_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a3f98731-e1fb-4bfe-b293-e39416e32f32_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a3f98731-e1fb-4bfe-b293-e39416e32f32_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/a3f98731-e1fb-4bfe-b293-e39416e32f32_3.mp3}	Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?	exercise	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:20:06.666181+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Akinyi Odhiambo is a 55-64 year old, \\nCulture: Luo female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Fish processor, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Kisumu. Disability: she has none. Religion: Christianity (other). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Ohangla dancing. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Gluten-Free. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: Yes. Physical activity: 5-7 days. Support network: Close friends. Health goals: What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?. her food access Limited access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Akinyi Odhiambo and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<