     \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3-4 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Close friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">8aeda51c-2bdd-4651-b771-de9ce2c6a3a0</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, sometimes I wonder if I really eat the right things for my body. Every morning, I rush to the farm and I tuck into whatever's quick – like plain bread with sugary tea or fried snacks. I thought it was enough for energy. But last week, I had to carry heavy sacks, and I felt dizzy, like my sugar was out of balance. How could this happen when I eat every day? I think I just didn’t know how my foods affect my blood sugar, and that small task ended up harder than I thought.\n\nLast time I went to the clinic, Nurse Grace asked about my meals, and I said, 'I eat what I can on the farm.' She smiled and told me, 'Amina, there are everyday foods that won’t spike your sugar — like fresh tomatoes, okra, fish, and millet.' She said, 'Try to eat small portions often and avoid heavy fried things.' I was a bit unsure at first, thinking, 'Can this work here?' But her advice felt real and simple, even for someone like me who raises animals and works hard every day.\n\nSo, I decided to try changing my diet. I bought millet and fresh veggies at the market and stopped buying fried snacks for a bit. But, oh man, it wasn’t easy to cook differently or skip my usual tea with sugar. One day, I felt a bit weak and almost gave up. But then I remembered my big work coming next week – planting new seeds around the city farm. I wanted to be strong for that. So even though it was tough, I kept going with the new foods, hoping it would help.\n\nNow, after a few weeks, I notice I have better energy for work. Carrying sacks and feeding my animals feels less tiring. The planting work went well. I’m happy, and I even talk to my friends about the healthy meals I’m trying. Maybe this is the start of something good, you know? I’m ready to keep learning and eating better so my body can keep up with everything I love doing.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/8aeda51c-2bdd-4651-b771-de9ce2c6a3a0_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/8aeda51c-2bdd-4651-b771-de9ce2c6a3a0_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/8aeda51c-2bdd-4651-b771-de9ce2c6a3a0_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/publi