f your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I live alone or have limited support</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">efcf4a24-5611-4062-952b-d3026e0363aa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouragedâ€”how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, every morning I wake up to the sounds of roosters and the sweet smell of sorghum coming through my window. As a sorghum farmer in Homa Bay, my days are busy with work on the shamba. I always make sure to eat my ugali and some sukuma wiki—nothing too oily because I know my diabetes needs that. But sometimes, I just feel tired. Maybe it's because I'm not moving as I should, or maybe because the fields seem smaller when I am working alone. There was a day I tried to clear just a small corner of weeds, but I got so breathless and sweaty, I just left it for tomorrow. Did I know that my body is trying to tell me something, or am I just ignoring the signs?\n\nLater that week, Fatuma, the nurse from our local clinic, came by for a checkup. She sat me down and asked, 'Akinyi, how are you moving these days?' Honestly, I told her, 'I have no gym and after work, I just want to rest.' Fatuma smiled and said, 'You do not need a gym, my sister! Start small—use your jembe as a weight. Dance Ohangla for ten minutes in your sitting room. Even stepping around your compound helps.' I thought, are those small things enough? Fatuma nodded, 'Yes, as long as you move, you help your sugar. And you do not have to change everything at once. Try today and see.' It sounded simple, so why am I still unsure?\n\nThe next day, I tried dancing Ohangla in the kitchen before making tea. At first, I felt shy—even though I live alone! My feet did not move the way they used to. I tried swinging my arms with the jembe after lunch, but ah, my muscles complained a bit. I almost stopped, saying to myself, what will this change really do? But next week, I have to weed the whole big field alone. That big work is waiting for me. Can I let tiredness win, or will I just try again tomorrow?\n\nAfter a few tries, my body felt lighter—just a little, but it was something. Sweating from Ohangla steps felt like winning, and my sugar readings looked good. When the time came for the bigger field work, I noticed my hands were stronger and my breathing was not so heavy. I finished before the hot sun stood high. I laughed out loud. Why did I doubt these small daily moves? Now, I even think next time I can add a longer walk. Maybe, with every little thing I try, my health will be ready for any big task—one step at a time.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/efcf4a24-5611-4062-952b-d3026e0363aa_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/efcf4a24-5611-4062-952b-d3026e0363aa_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/efcf4a24-5611-4062-952b-d3026e0363aa_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/efcf4a24-5611-4062-952b-d3026e0363aa_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/efcf4a24-5611-4062-952b-d3026e0363aa_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/efcf4a24-5611-4062-952b-d3026e0363aa_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/efcf4a24-5611-4062-952b-d3026e0363aa_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/efcf4a24-5611-4062-952b-d3026e0363aa_3.mp3}	I don't have a gym—what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:19:58.363052+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Owino Odhiambo is a 45-54 year old, \\nCulture: Luo male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Journalist, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Homa Bay. Disability: he has none. Religion: Christianity (Methodist). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Football (soccer). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a 