the township yard fills the background.", "Other person: Nurse Dudu, female. At the taxi rank, background noise includes distant taxi horns, street vendors shouting, and footsteps. Nurse Dudu speaks first with a warm, clear South African English accent, ‘Sibongile, you’re so quiet today, is everything all right?’ Sibongile responds with a slightly hesitant, thoughtful voice, her breathing a bit heavier, followed by a quiet laugh. There is sincerity and hope in their voices, and Sibongile’s name is mentioned naturally.", "Sibongile hums softly and stirs a pot, audible bubbling of vegetable stew rising. Her voice is now more upbeat as she sings a piece of a traditional song, her feet shuffling in a dance rhythm on the worn kitchen floor. The fridge hums in the background, and the sound of a wooden spoon tapping against a pot creates a comforting, steady pattern. She says her own name with pride while speaking to herself."], "image_prompts": ["Candid medium shot of Sibongile Mvula, an African woman in her late forties, wearing a simple headscarf and well-worn dress, standing in a small, sunlit township kitchen. She is leaning on the counter, looking thoughtful and worried, as she packs basic homemade pap and cabbage into plastic containers. Old pots, faded tiles, and a single window let in warm late-morning light, highlighting the worn, clean surfaces and limited ingredients. The overall texture shows humble living with muted colors.", "Candid wide shot showing Sibongile Mvula sitting tiredly on a wooden bench outside a lively crèche, her posture slumped, hands resting on her knees, with kids playing in the dusty yard background. Bright midday sun casts sharp soft shadows; half-filled snack containers sit on the table beside her. Her face shows worry mixed with a small, gentle smile as she looks at the children. The scene reveals modest resources and genuine community spirit.", "Other person: Nurse Dudu, female. Medium shot of Sibongile Mvula and Nurse Dudu standing together at a busy township taxi rank. Sibongile is listening, arms folded, with a questioning expression, while Nurse Dudu leans forward, speaking with a caring gesture, holding a reusable shopping bag. The background captures taxis, street vendors with fruit, and neighbors moving. Afternoon light casts clear, natural shadows, with both women in everyday attire.", "Medium shot of Sibongile Mvula in her kitchen, stirring a steaming pot of vegetable stew over an electric stove. She pauses, holding the spoon with determination, her body leaning forward in resolve. Sunlight streams through a curtain, highlighting the colorful chopped vegetables on the counter. The wall clock and a small shopping list can be seen in the background, showing real, everyday township life."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 1.9909880101680755, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What vegetarian snack options are available in rural areas of the Eastern Cape with limited access to healthy foods?\\",\\n  \\"How can isiXhosa traditional foods fit into a vegetarian diet for someone with Type 2 diabetes in a rural South African community?\\",\\n  \\"What are some affordable and healthy snacks suitable for someone who has hypertension and diabetes, considering I live alone and work full-time as a civil servant in the Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find local community programs or support groups in rural Eastern Cape for women with diabetes who want help with meal planning?\\",\\n  \\"What traditional snacks from isiXhosa culture are considered healthy for someone with high blood pressure and diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there rural healthcare resources in Eastern Cape that provide dietary guidance for vegetarian diabetics who also smoke tobacco?\\",\\n  \\"How often should a middle-aged woman with Type 2 diabetes and hypertension include healthy snacks in her diet if she exercises most days of the week?\\",\\n  \\"What are some easy-to-prepare, diabetes-friendly vegetarian snacks using ingredients commonly found in small Eastern Cape rural stores?\\",\\n  \\"Can you suggest healthy snacks that support traditional IsiXhosa dancing and exercise routines for a 45–54-year-old woman with diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What is the link between tobacco use and snack cravings for diabetic women aged 45–54 living in rural South Africa?\\",\\n  \\"Which local fruits or vegetables available in the Eastern Cape are best for managing blood sugar for vegetarians with limited market access?\\",\\n  \\"What practical meal and snack strategies help rural single women manage diabetes and hypertension while maintaining an active volunteer job?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "I always try to keep active, even though balancing my diabetes and high blood pressure means I take medication and watch my diet. My life is full of moving—dancing umxhentso at gatherings, helping neighbours, and getting by with what I have. Vegan cooking on a budget is tough, and sometimes, I wonder if I’m doing things right. I know I should eat healthy, but figuring out what’s good when healthy food is so expensive around here? Eish, that confuses me. My main work is helping at the local crèche, but even small things like setting up snacks for kids sometimes gets hard for me.", "selected_profile_info": ["city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_frequency", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
0ae047ac-aabf-4e15-bda3-fc44fb9d3463	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b816-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Eastern Cape</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Presbyterian)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Siphokazi Ntloko</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">35–44</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Suburban (near city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Teacher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Storytelling (iintsomi) and praise poetry (izibongo)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Socialising with family or friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Anxiety or depression</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5–7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I live alone or have limited support</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0ae047ac-aabf-4e15-bda3-fc44fb9d3463</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Can I just walk for exercise, or should I do other types too?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Every morning, I grab my usual tea with sugar and a slice of white bread with jam or sometimes a samosa, you know, quick and easy. I don’t really think about how that might be messing with my blood sugar, especially when I have to stand in front of a classroom all day teaching. One time, I tried to do a quick quiz with my class after a sugary breakfast, but I felt shaky and distracted. Maybe I didn’t eat right, but I just thought it was normal tiredness or nerves. I didn’t realise my food could be the problem until I almost bungled that lesson.\n\nLuckily, I went to the clinic for a check-up, and the nurse explained things nicely. She said eating foods that don’t spike blood sugar means choosing things like brown bread instead of white, and replacing sugary snacks with fruits like apples or pears. She reminded me to watch portion sizes and stick to my halal diet, which was reassuring. At first, I wasn’t sure if I could do all that – changing habits isn’t easy, right? But the way she explained it made sense – small changes that fit my life and my culture.\n\nSo, I gave it a shot the next week. I switched to brown bread and kept fruit in my bag instead of sweets. At first, it was tough – I missed my usual sugary tea and those samosas. One day, I accidentally grabbed the wrong bread, and I felt that familiar crash again. But I kept telling myself I had a bigger goal – I need to be sharp for the big parent-teacher conference coming up soon. That kept me going, even when it felt like a struggle to break old habits.\n\nNow, I’m feeling much better. My energy lasts longer, and I’m not as shaky during lessons. The parent-teacher meeting went well, and I was calm and confident. It’s funny, but sticking to these food changes hasn’t been as hard as I thought. I’m even thinking about trying out other healthy options to keep my blood sugar steady. It feels good to be in control, especially knowing I can do this for myself and my work.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/0ae047ac-aabf-4e15-bda3-fc44fb9d3463_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/0ae047ac-aabf-4e15-bda3-fc44fb9d3463_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/0ae047ac-aabf-4e15-bda3-fc44fb9d3463_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/0ae047ac-aabf-4e15-bda3-fc44fb9d3463_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/0ae047ac-aabf-4e15-bda3-fc44fb9d3463_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/0ae047ac-aabf-4e15-bda3-fc44fb9d3463_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/0ae047ac-aabf-4e15-bda3-fc44fb9d3463_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/0ae047ac-aabf-4e15-bda3-fc44fb9d3463_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:24.061768+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Zanele Mabhena is a 25–34 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Teacher, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: she has none. Religion: Christianity (Methodist). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Vegan. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: Yes. Physical activity: 5–7 days. Support network: Family (parents, siblings, children). Health goals: How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?. her food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Zanele Mabhena and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "crisis_avoidance", "audio_prompts": ["Sounds of classroom murmurs and faint chalk scrapes, Siphokazi Ntloko’s moderate paced South African English voice with a slightly concerned tone as she talks quietly to herself about her breakfast choices, distant children's chatter, subtle background hum of fans, audio perspective nearby.", "Clinic room ambiance with soft footsteps and quiet medical equipment noises, female nurse speaking warmly and clearly to Siphokazi Ntloko, who responds with hesitant yet attentive voice, occasional deep breaths hinting mild anxiety, spatial cues place voices across a desk, audio crisp and clear.", "Kitchen sounds of rustling plastic and fruit being handled, Siphokazi Ntloko’s voice slow and slightly frustrated as she tries to adjust her food routine, soft sighs and footsteps on tiled floor, natural morning light audible through window sounds (birds), close audio perspective.", "Room filled with soft conversations and occasional laughter during parent-teacher meeting, Siphokazi Ntloko’s voice steady and confident, moderate speed and warm tone in South African English as she interacts with a parent female nearby, low ambient noise of paper shuffling and chair movements, candid audio capture."], "image_prompts": ["Siphokazi Ntloko sitting in her modest classroom, wearing casual teacher clothes, holding a sugary tea cup and a white bread sandwich, looking thoughtful with slight worry, late morning soft sunlight streaming through windows, chalkboard and desks in background, candid medium shot.", "At a local clinic consultation room, Siphokazi Ntloko listening attentively to a female nurse in uniform who is explaining diet advice using simple charts, both seated across a small desk, subtle trust and mild hesitation visible in Siphokazi's posture, clinical lighting clear and warm, candid medium shot.", "Siphokazi Ntloko in her small kitchen at home holding a brown bread package with a slight frown, looking at a table with fruit and a misplaced white bread loaf, morning light casting soft shadows, candid natural medium shot.", "Siphokazi Ntloko confidently standing beside a classroom board during a parent-teacher meeting, smiling and interacting calmly with a parent, middle-aged female, warmly lit room with windows, both relaxed and engaged, candid wide shot."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 5.38300901055336, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some popular physical activity groups or walking clubs for women in their 30s or 40s in suburban areas near Mthatha, Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"How can I incorporate isiXhosa storytelling or praise poetry (izibongo) into my social gatherings or as a form of cultural community wellness activity?\\",\\n  \\"Are there lactose-free traditional Xhosa dishes suitable for someone managing type 2 diabetes available at local supermarkets in the Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"What are the best diabetes-friendly walking routes or safe neighborhoods for outdoor physical activity in suburban areas of the Eastern Cape near major towns?\\",\\n  \\"What support resources exist for female Presbyterian Christians in the Eastern Cape dealing with anxiety or depression and diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How can a female teacher with limited social support effectively manage daily insulin injections and oral medication in a South African school setting?\\",\\n  \\"Are there local healthcare clinics in the Eastern Cape that offer mental health support tailored for adults with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What strategies are recommended for quitting daily tobacco smoking for adults with type 2 diabetes in South Africa, especially in suburban, isiXhosa-speaking communities?\\",\\n  \\"What lifestyle changes apart from walking are culturally relevant for isiXhosa women with diabetes in South Africa?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find in-person or online support groups for suburban women living alone with type 2 diabetes and anxiety in the Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"Are there local church groups or Presbyterian community initiatives in the Eastern Cape that provide health, nutrition, or wellness support?\\",\\n  \\"What are some effective ways to maintain social connections and reduce stress for suburban isiXhosa-speaking women with anxiety who live alone?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "So, I’m Siphokazi Ntloko, a teacher living in a comfy suburban area near town. Every day, I’m busy with lessons and making sure my little learners stay focused. I love chatting with friends and family regularly, which keeps me going. Lately, I’ve been kind of worried about my blood sugar, but honestly, I’m not too sure what foods I can eat without it spiking. Like, I know I need to watch my health because of my anxiety, but fitting healthy food into my busy routine feels tricky. Plus, I’m still smoking sometimes and moving about every day to keep fit.", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "describe_location", "occupation", "activities", "health_conditions", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "tobacco", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
