          <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Luo</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3b68a53e-861f-4f97-aed5-99e3122092ac</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I like to snackâ€”what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	I wake up early, as usual, when the light is just beginning to touch the rooftops here in Homa Bay. My small farm outside town is waiting for me, and the chickens are already calling. Most days, I grab some chapati or leftover ugali before heading out, hoping it will keep me going till noon. But you know how it is—by 10 o'clock, I always want to snack. So I find some leftover mandazi, or maybe I nibble on sweet fruits, not minding what is really good for my sugar levels. This morning, I tried to fix one fence on the goat kraal, but before I finished, I felt dizzy and needed to sit down. Eish, is it possible that these sweet snacks are making me weak instead of strong?\n\nThat same day, I walked slowly to the local clinic after feeling those shakes. Sister Mary, our nurse, looked at me with those caring eyes. 'Jared, you know, your body is telling you something,' she said softly, her Luo accent just like mine. I just shook my head—me, change how I eat? Really? But she wrote down some options. 'Try some nduma, roasted groundnuts, or plain yoghurt when you need a snack—not mandazi or those too-sweet fruits,' she warned. 'Small portions, ati three times between meals if you're hungry, and not every moment your mouth feels lonely.' I left nodding, but wondering, can I really give up my mandazi?\n\nThe next morning, I prepared a handful of groundnuts and cut slices of watermelon, like Sister Mary suggested. But as I worked fence posts into the rocky soil, I kept thinking of sugar. My fingers wanted to reach for the sweet buns, not nuts! My mind told me to just eat quick sugar so I can finish the job fast. But then I remembered—the next day, relatives were coming to help clear a big part of the farm, and I needed to be strong. So I drank water, chewed groundnuts, and even told my cousin, 'No mandazi today, brother—we must try this new way.' Oh, was it easy? No, I nearly gave up!\n\nBy the time the relatives arrived, I had tried those snacks every day, little by little. My hands were more steady, and I stood longer with the jembe without stopping for sugar breaks. We finished clearing the big patch under the hot Homa Bay sun, and in the evening, I was tired, but not shaking or dizzy. Sitting with my wife, eating grilled maize with avocado and fresh tea, I thought, maybe there is something in this healthy snacking after all. I smiled and said, 'Next week, let us try this again.'	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3b68a53e-861f-4f97-aed5-99e3122092ac_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3b68a53e-861f-4f97-aed5-99e3122092ac_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3b68a53e-861f-4f97-aed5-99e3122092ac_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3b68a53e-861f-4f97-aed5-99e3122092ac_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3b68a53e-861f-4f97-aed5-99e3122092ac_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3b68a53e-861f-4f97-aed5-99e3122092ac_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3b68a53e-861f-4f97-aed5-99e3122092ac_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3b68a53e-861f-4f97-aed5-99e3122092ac_3.mp3}	I like to snack—what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?	diet	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:19:58.64557+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Owino Odhiambo is a 45-54 year old, \\nCulture: Luo male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Journalist, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Homa Bay. Disability: he has none. Religion: Christianity (Methodist). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Football (soccer). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Vegetarian. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: Yes. Physical activity: 1-2 days. Support network: Close friends. Health goals: Sometimes I feel tired or discouragedâ€”how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?. his food access Limited access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Owino Odhiambo and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The imag