r>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No Restrictions</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1c9e8e89-22e1-4b73-b6fc-43aeed25cb0a</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I don&#x27;t have a gym—what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	That morning, I was unpacking a delivery, and I felt heavy, my legs like bricks and my eyes blurry. I pushed myself to finish, but then boom, I dropped a whole carton of bottles. My boss shook his head, aaai Luzuko, but he asked if I was okay. I just wiped my face and said, "Ja, just tired." Deep down, I wondered, "Is it really just tired, or is something wrong? Maybe my blood sugar again?" But I didn’t know, so after mopping the mess, I just grabbed a cheap samoosa and a fizzy drink for lunch like always.\n\nLater, after work, I went to the clinic because my body was still feeling weird. The nurse there sat me down and asked about what I eat. She looked at me with those kind eyes and said, "Luzuko, you must try to swap that soda for water and eat brown bread instead of white. Put maybe some beans, cabbage, and chicken (not fried) in your lunch box. Even if you don’t have much, these foods are right at home, and they won’t push your sugar up and down like a minibus taxi on the highway, you know?" I almost laughed at her example but felt shy too. Can eating these simple things really help?\n\nSo, the next day, I tried the nurse’s advice. My lunch was brown bread, baked beans, and a bottle of water. Yoh, it felt odd, hey. No Coke, no chips. My friend Sipho even joked, 'Luzuko, are you on a diet now?' It wasn’t easy, and I kept thinking about that big stock take coming next week. I nearly gave up and bought a koeksister, but I thought, 'Let me give these small changes a chance.' The nurse said it might help, so why not, neh?\n\nA week later, the stock take came. This time, I felt lighter and clearer. My hands didn’t shake, no blurry eyes—nothing. I worked fast, kept cool, and even the boss smiled. I thought, 'Is this because of the beans and brown bread?' Maybe, hey. I felt proud, and I started to think: if these foods make work easier, maybe they can make life a bit sweeter too, without needing all that sugar. Now, I look for more ways to eat smart and show others that us kasi boys can stay strong the healthy way.	{http