Support network: Healthcare provider or counsellor. Health goals: Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?. his food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Lwazi Nkosi and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "classic_change", "audio_prompts": ["Medium shot audio, ambient of a township bottle store storeroom. Luzuko Makhubela moves crates, his breath is audible and slightly laboured. Footsteps shuffle, bottles clink. Luzuko sighs quietly, his voice muttering, 'Eish, I’m just tired,' with a rising intonation of uncertain self-questioning. Muffled radio music plays softly in the background.", "A senior nurse, Black female, sits across from Luzuko Makhubela in a crowded clinic room. Nurse’s voice is clear, warm, slightly higher pitch, explaining calmly about healthy food choices. Luzuko’s reply is a hesitant, thoughtful murmur, 'Brown bread? Beans and cabbage?' Chairs creak as Luzuko shifts. Clinic bustle fills the background with low voices and distant children’s laughter.", "Medium shot audio, outdoor township shop. Luzuko Makhubela unwraps his lunch while Sipho, young Black male, stands nearby. Sipho teases light-heartedly, 'Are you on a diet, boy?' Luzuko laughs softly, then answers with slightly embarrassed but steady tone. Sound of traffic, birds, and wrappers crinkling in the breeze.", "Wide shot audio in storeroom. Luzuko Makhubela’s energetic footsteps and pen tapping on a clipboard, boxes sliding on concrete. Manager stands a short distance away, softly saying, 'Good work, Luzuko.' Luzuko smiles, breathing steady and proud. Ambient background: echo of workers talking, phone ringing, and sunlight filtering in through open backdoor."], "image_prompts": ["Medium shot of Luzuko Makhubela, young Black male, standing in a busy bottle store storeroom with crates and soft drink bottles. He is holding a carton, looking fatigued, his shoulders slightly slumped, eyes looking downward. The scene captures everyday textures: scuffed tiled floor, faded posters on the wall, natural early morning light coming in through a dusty window.", "A senior nurse, Black female, sits across from Luzuko Makhubela in a small township clinic consulting room. It is a medium shot, nurse pointing gently at Luzuko's cheap lunch in a plastic bag while making a kind face. Luzuko is sitting upright, arm resting on a simple wooden table, looking uncertain but listening closely. The fluorescent light is slightly harsh, there is a worn green file on the desk, and community health posters decorate the wall.", "Medium shot of Luzuko Makhubela on lunch break, sitting on a low concrete step outside the shop, unpacking brown bread with baked beans from an old plastic ice cream tub. His friend Sipho, young Black male, stands nearby joking, holding a Coke can, while Luzuko looks embarrassed but determined. Their uniforms are slightly dusty. Bright sunlight with harsh midday shadows.", "Wide shot of Luzuko Makhubela in the store storeroom, ticking off boxes for a stock take. He is energetic, arms lifted, smile small but confident. His manager, older Black male, stands nearby watching approvingly. The room is filled with stacked crates, boxes, and the afternoon sun lights up patches of the concrete floor."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 1.9909880101680755, "task_parent_id": "c499a664-3dd1-4c4b-9d62-8013015da7db", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are effective home exercises for a young male teacher with type 2 diabetes living in an urban area of Eastern Cape, South Africa, who doesn't have access to a gym?\\",\\n  \\"What IsiXhosa traditional dances like umxhentso or intlombe can be adapted as physical activity for someone needing more exercise but has no equipment?\\",\\n  \\"Are there locally available gluten-free foods in urban Eastern Cape that fit a South African, IsiXhosa-influenced diet for managing type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What culturally relevant support groups or resources exist in the Eastern Cape for Methodist Christians living with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How can I create a diabetes-friendly physical activity routine at home in Eastern Cape when I currently do no exercise days per week?\\",\\n  \\"What are the best ways to quit or reduce tobacco use in young urban South African males with type 2 diabetes, especially with high smoking frequency?\\",\\n  \\"How do Eastern Cape city-dwellers with easy access to healthy foods manage a gluten-free diet when coping with type 2 diabetes and a busy teaching schedule?\\",\\n  \\"What role can traditional Xhosa music and dance play in maintaining physical activity for men in their early twenties with type 2 diabetes in urban areas?\\",\\n  \\"How does being a Methodist and part of the IsiXhosa community influence approaches to type 2 diabetes management in the Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"What herbal or traditional medicines used in the Eastern Cape are safe to use alongside insulin and metformin for managing type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there local clinics or programs in Eastern Cape cities that support young teachers with diabetes who have rare alcohol use and high tobacco use?\\",\\n  \\"What practical household items do urban South Africans in the Eastern Cape use for resistance training or cardio when they can't access a gym?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "I’m Luzuko Makhubela, I’m 22, and my life is pretty busy. I work hard—always on my feet at the bottle store, stacking crates and sometimes helping with the till. I used to think, \\"Ag man, as long as I’m young, can drink a Coke and slap chips, nothing will touch me.\\" My health and what I eat? Eish, that felt far from my mind. Gym? Who has money for gym? I just did whatever, until that one day at work.", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "daily_routine", "activities", "medication", "healthcare_frequency", "tobacco", "physical_activity", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
