lass="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Close friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5f36f53c-6569-4aeb-85e8-0ff06e9087e4</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Which everyday foods can I eat that won&#x27;t make my blood sugar spike?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, it wasn't so long ago when I thought a bit of rest was best for me, especially with this diabetes and high blood pressure. I still had my old habits – sitting most of the day, eating the same foods, and hardly moving around much. When a little chore came up at the house – like fetching firewood or helping a neighbour – I’d feel tired quickly. I blamed my age, but maybe I didn’t see how my health was getting the better of me. It was like my body didn’t want to play along, and I was missing the signs. Do we sometimes forget just how much our bodies tell us, if only we listen?"\n\nOne day at the clinic, nurse Thandi helped me understand a bit more about moving safely with diabetes. At first, I was unsure – ‘How can a person my age start exercising without overdoing it?’ I asked. She gently explained that walking slowly, doing some traditional dance moves like umxhentso, and even light stretching are good ways to start. She said these exercises help lower blood sugar and keep our hearts strong, which is great for hypertension too. Best part, she told me to listen to my body and stop if I felt pain. It felt good to have clear advice, not too complicated, and linked to my culture and life. Could this be the fresh start I needed?"\n\nI tried following nurse Thandi’s advice. Some mornings I walked slowly around the block, and other days I practiced simple dance steps in the yard. But I must admit, changing my habits wasn’t easy. Some days I felt restless, some days my feet hurt, and I wanted to skip the exercises altogether. I even wanted to sneak a smoke instead, like old times. But then, I remembered there’s a bigger task coming—helping my grandchildren prepare for a big school event. If I want to be active and strong for them, I need to keep going. Sometimes we struggle before we find our rhythm, don’t we?"\n\nSlowly but surely, I felt my body getting stronger and my sugar levels more steady. The walking and dance started to feel natural, like they belonged in my day. When the school event came, I could move with joy and energy, not like the tired woman of before. My friends noticed my change and even asked me to join their morning walks. I feel hopeful now, thinking, ‘Maybe I can keep improving bit by bit, and not let diabetes rule my life.’ It’s like a new dance has begun — one where I lead, step by step."	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5f36f53c-6569-4aeb-85e8-0ff06e9087e4_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5f36f53c-6569-4aeb-85e8-0ff06e9087e4_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5f36f53c-6569-4aeb-85e8-0ff06e9087e4_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5f36f53c-6569-4aeb-85e8-0ff06e9087e4_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/5f36f53c-6569-4aeb-85e8-0ff06e9087e4_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/5f36f53c-6569-4aeb-85e8-0ff06e9087e4_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/5f36f53c-6569-4aeb-85e8-0ff06e9087e4_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/5f36f53c-6569-4aeb-85e8-0ff06e9087e4_3.mp3}	What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?	exercise	female	consistency	2026-03-23 13:02:24.364228+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Zanele Mabhena is a 25–34 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Teacher, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: she has none. Religion: Christianity (Methodist). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine.