ou describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Teacher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Storytelling (iintsomi) and praise poetry (izibongo)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Socialising with family or friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Anxiety or depression</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Gluten-Free</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">c39eca08-7324-4d37-a0fd-db63b6698cdd</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I like to snack—what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	I wake up every morning, get ready for work, and head to the kitchen to make some breakfast. Usually, it's something quick like scrambled eggs or oatmeal with fruit. As a teacher, I need something to keep me going throughout the day. But lately, I've been feeling really tired, even after eating. I just can't seem to understand why. Maybe it's because I've been snacking on whatever is convenient, like chips or cookies, instead of taking the time to prepare something healthy. Today, I had a big meeting at school, and I was supposed to give a presentation, but I ended up feeling so sluggish that I stumbled over my words. What's going on with me?\n\nAfter that embarrassing meeting, I decided to visit our local healthcare clinic to see if they could help me figure out what's going on. The nurse there, Sister Nomsa, was very kind and asked me a lot of questions about my diet and exercise habits. She explained to me that as someone with Type 2 Diabetes, it's really important for me to be mindful of what I eat and to get some physical activity every day. She suggested that I start by taking short walks around my neighborhood and doing some bodyweight exercises at home. At first, I was a bit resistant, thinking that I didn't have time for this, but Sister Nomsa assured me that even small changes could make a big difference. She also reminded me that I have easy access to healthy foods, so there's no excuse for not eating well.\n\nSo, I decided to take Sister Nomsa's advice and start making some changes. I began by taking a 10-minute walk around my block every morning and doing some push-ups and squats at home. It wasn't easy at first - my body felt stiff, and I missed my old snacking habits. But I knew I had to keep going, especially since our school's annual cultural festival is coming up, and I've been asked to perform a praise poem in front of the whole community. I didn't want to feel embarrassed like I did during that meeting, so I persisted. However, I did have a minor setback when I slipped up and had a bag of chips one day. But I didn't let that discourage me. I just got back on track the next day.\n\nIt's been a few weeks now, and I can already feel a difference. My energy levels are up, and I don't feel as tired all the time. I've also started to enjoy my daily walks and exercises - it's a great way to clear my head before work. And the best part is, I feel more confident about performing at the cultural festival. I've been practicing my praise poem, and I'm excited to share it with everyone. I realize now that taking care of my health isn't just about me; it's also about being able to do my job well and participate in the things I love, like storytelling and socializing with my community. I'm open to continuing to make healthy changes and seeing where this journey takes me.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c39eca08-7324-4d37-a0fd-db63b6698cdd_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c39eca08-7324-4d37-a0fd-db63b6698cdd_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c39eca08-7324-4d37-a0fd-db63b6698cdd_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c39eca08-7324-4d37-a0fd-db63b6698cdd_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/c39eca08-7324-4d37-a0fd-db63b6698cdd_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/c39eca08-7324-4d37-a0fd-db63b6698cdd_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/c39eca08-7324-4d37-a0fd-db63b6698cdd_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/c39eca08-7324-4d37-a0fd-db63b6698cdd_3.mp3}	I don't have a gym—what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:26.567433+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "Llama-3.3-70B", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Siyabulela Nkosi is a 18–24 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Teacher, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Eastern Cape. Disability: he has none. Religion: Christianity (Presbyterian). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Storytelling (iintsomi) and praise poetry (izibongo). Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Kosher. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 1–2 days. Support network: Community or religious group. Health goals: Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?. his food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Siyabulela Nkosi and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end m