 like birds chirping and leaves rustling in the background, \\"I'll start by taking short walks around my neighborhood and doing some bodyweight exercises at home.\\"", "Lindiwe Mkhonza's voice, sounding confident and happy, with some traditional music playing in the background, \\"I'm excited to share my praise poem with everyone, and I feel more confident about performing at the cultural festival, Lindiwe Mkhonza.\\"}"], "image_prompts": ["Lindiwe Mkhonza, a middle-aged woman, sitting at her kitchen table with a plate of scrambled eggs and fruit, looking tired and confused, with a clock in the background showing it's early morning, medium shot.", "Sister Nomsa, a female nurse, standing in a clinic room with Lindiwe Mkhonza, who is sitting on an examination table, looking concerned, with a whiteboard in the background showing a diagram of a healthy plate, wide shot.", "Lindiwe Mkhonza, walking alone in her neighborhood, looking determined, with a few houses and trees in the background, and a sunny sky, medium shot.", "Lindiwe Mkhonza, standing in front of a mirror, practicing her praise poem, looking confident and happy, with a few traditional clothing items and accessories in the background, medium shot, Lindiwe Mkhonza."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 7.321289109020698, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some Kosher and healthy snack options that are easily available in urban areas of the Eastern Cape for someone with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How can a female isiXhosa teacher in her late 40s living in a South African city incorporate physical activity into a busy daily routine despite having anxiety or depression?\\",\\n  \\"What are the best relaxation techniques recommended at local healthcare clinics in the Eastern Cape for working adults managing type 2 diabetes and rare tobacco use?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any isiXhosa storytelling or praise poetry (izibongo) groups or workshops in the Eastern Cape cities where I can connect with others and reduce stress?\\",\\n  \\"Which community or religious support networks in urban Eastern Cape cities cater to agnostic women managing diabetes and mental health concerns?\\",\\n  \\"What local resources, recipes, or stores provide Kosher foods suitable for someone with easy access to healthy foods in Eastern Cape urban areas?\\",\\n  \\"Are there culturally relevant diet plans or snack guidelines for isiXhosa adults with diabetes who do not exercise regularly but want to start?\\",\\n  \\"What impact does rare tobacco smoking have on anxiety and diabetes management in women aged 45–54, specifically in the South African urban context?\\",\\n  \\"How do local healthcare clinics in the Eastern Cape support teachers with type 2 diabetes who are on insulin, oral medication, or lifestyle changes?\\",\\n  \\"What family or social activities in urban Eastern Cape towns encourage healthy snacking and physical movement for agnostic, middle-aged women?\\",\\n  \\"Are there examples of urban isiXhosa women successfully managing both type 2 diabetes and anxiety/depression, and what strategies do they use?\\",\\n  \\"What local city-based mental health programs in the Eastern Cape are available for teachers experiencing anxiety or depression while managing chronic health conditions?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
ef2b5ddc-b726-41b2-a2b4-cdffa32782ba	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b816-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Eastern Cape</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Non-religious spirituality</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Anele Makhanda</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">25–34</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Rural (small community or countryside)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Teacher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Vegetarian</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">ef2b5ddc-b726-41b2-a2b4-cdffa32782ba</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Eating healthy can get expensive—how can I manage a nutritious diet on a tight budget? And can I still enjoy some traditional foods? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Hayi, life as a teacher in this village, it keeps you on your toes, doesn't it? Every morning, it's straight to school, helping the little ones with their numbers and letters. I love my job, truly. But lately, I've been feeling… sluggish. Just tired, you know? Breakfast is usually a piece of kota with polony – quick and easy. Lunch? Often a pap and chakalaka from the shebeen across the road. It fills you up, and it’s cheap. Yesterday, I promised to help Old Man Themba mend the fence around the school veggie garden before the parents arrived for the meeting. I figured, easy enough. But after just a few poles, I was completely out of breath, leaning on the fence itself! Honestly, it was embarrassing. I had to ask young Sipho to finish it for me. What does it say about me, if I can’t even fix a fence?\n\nI felt so ashamed after that fence business, I finally went to the clinic. Sister Nandi, she's a good woman. She checked my sugar, and well, it wasn’t looking good. She explained about the diabetes, how the sweet things and not moving enough were causing problems. I told her, 'But Sister Nandi, kota is life! And I don’t have time for fancy exercise!' She just smiled gently. She said, ‘Anele, we’re not asking you to run a marathon. Just a little walk each day, maybe around the village. And instead of polony every day, try adding some beans or eggs to your kota. Small changes, that’s all.’ She even showed me some simple stretches I could do while I'm marking papers. It sounded…doable, I guess. Still, it felt like a lot to think about.\n\nTrying to walk every day is harder than it looks! I started, I really did. But the first few days, my legs felt like lead. And the kota… oh, the kota! It calls to me! I slipped up on Tuesday and had a double polony kota, and the next day I felt even worse than usual. My feet were aching, and I felt so frustrated. But then I remembered the school play. We’re preparing the children for the Heritage Day celebrations, and I’m supposed to choreograph the umxhentso dance. If I can't even keep up with a simple walk, how am I going to teach those energetic little ones a whole dance routine? That thought pushed me to get moving again, even if it was just a slow walk around the school grounds.\n\nSlowly, things started to change. I started adding beans to my kota most days, and I even tried making a simple vegetable stew a couple of times. The walking got easier, and I started feeling…lighter, somehow. I even managed to do a few of Sister Nandi’s stretches while watching TV. The day of the school play arrived, and I was nervous, but I wasn’t breathless. I danced with the children, showing them the steps, and I didn’t have to stop and catch my breath once! The parents were so impressed, and Old Man Themba winked at me. It felt good, really good. Maybe, just maybe, taking care of myself isn’t so hard after all. Maybe I can even learn to enjoy it. And who knows, maybe next year I'll be able to fix that fence all by myself.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ef2b5ddc-b726-41b2-a2b4-cdffa32782ba_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ef2b5ddc-b726-41b2-a2b4-cdffa32782ba_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ef2b5ddc-b726-41b2-a2b4-cdffa32782ba_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/ef2b5ddc-b726-41b2-a2b4-cdffa32782ba_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ef2b5ddc-b726-41b2-a2b4-cdffa32782ba_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ef2b5ddc-b726-41b2-a2b4-cdffa32782ba_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ef2b5ddc-b726-41b2-a2b4-cdffa32782ba_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/ef2b5ddc-b726-41b2-a2b4-cdffa32782ba_3.mp3}	What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:28.499357+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "gemma-3-27b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Akhona Ndlovu is a 45–54 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Teacher, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: she has none. Religion: Agnosticism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: No Restrictions. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: Yes. Physical activity: 0 days. Support network: I live alone or have limited support. Health goals: Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?. her food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Akhona Ndlovu and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona 