ightly breathless and tired, with a slow pacing. Sounds of chickens clucking nearby.", "Sister Nandi, a 45-year-old female, speaks in a calm and reassuring tone, positioned nearby Anele Makhanda. ‘Anele, we’re not asking you to run a marathon. Just a little walk each day.’ Anele Makhanda, 30 years old, responds with a hesitant tone, ‘But Sister Nandi, kota is life!’ A slight pause before her response, conveying uncertainty. Background sounds of a quiet clinic waiting room.", "Anele Makhanda, 27 years old, is heard breathing heavily as she walks, her footsteps crunching on gravel. She mutters to herself, 'My legs feel like lead.' Her voice is strained and slightly raspy. A distant sound of a car passing by. The audio fades slightly as she moves further away.", "Anele Makhanda, 33 years old, is laughing heartily, her voice filled with genuine amusement. The sound of children cheering and clapping in the background. 'It felt good, really good!' Her voice is energetic and clear. Faint sounds of traditional South African music playing in the distance."], "image_prompts": ["A candid, natural medium shot of Anele Makhanda, a 30-year-old woman, leaning against a wooden fence, looking slightly frustrated and out of breath. The fence is partially repaired, with some poles still leaning. The background shows a rural South African schoolyard with children playing in the distance. Warm, golden hour lighting. Dust motes in the air.", "A candid, natural medium shot of Anele Makhanda, 32 years old, sitting across from Sister Nandi, a middle-aged healthcare worker, in a small, brightly lit clinic room. Anele looks slightly hesitant, her hands clasped in her lap. Sister Nandi is smiling gently, holding a chart. The room is sparsely decorated with health posters. Soft, diffused lighting.", "A candid, natural wide shot of Anele Makhanda, 28 years old, walking slowly along a dusty road in a rural South African village. She is wearing comfortable clothes and looks determined but tired. In the background, small houses and trees are visible. The lighting is overcast and slightly muted. ", "A candid, natural medium shot of Anele Makhanda, 31 years old, laughing joyfully while dancing with a group of young children during a school play. She is wearing traditional South African attire, and the children are dressed in colorful costumes. The background shows a school hall decorated for a celebration. Bright, vibrant lighting."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 84.23266913340642, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are affordable traditional IsiXhosa dishes suitable for someone with type 2 diabetes living in rural Eastern Cape with easy access to fresh foods?\\",\\n  \\"How can I adapt umxhentso or intlombe traditional dance as a form of low-impact exercise for diabetes management if I currently don't exercise?\\",\\n  \\"Are there community-led programs in rural Eastern Cape that support working adults like teachers managing diabetes with both medication and lifestyle changes?\\",\\n  \\"What local support groups exist in the Eastern Cape for non-religious spiritual people managing chronic conditions like type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find fresh, healthy foods at lower cost near my rural community in Eastern Cape and are any local markets recommended for this?\\",\\n  \\"How can I reduce daily alcohol consumption safely as a woman with type 2 diabetes in a small rural IsiXhosa community?\\",\\n  \\"Are there IsiXhosa cultural practices or beliefs around health and nutrition that support or conflict with diabetes-friendly diets?\\",\\n  \\"What specific dietary advice is recommended for South African women aged 25–34 with type 2 diabetes who have no dietary restrictions but want to enjoy traditional foods?\\",\\n  \\"Can participating in local traditional dancing events in my rural community be counted as sufficient physical activity for diabetes management?\\",\\n  \\"How might my role as a teacher in a rural area influence my ability to stick to a healthy daily routine with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there cost-effective ways or government subsidies for medication and diabetes care in rural parts of the Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"What are some practical strategies to balance traditional social drinking customs and diabetes management in an IsiXhosa rural community?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": ["city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "hobbies", "healthcare_resources", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "physical_activity", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
3482f041-52ef-43f2-8e83-97ab2638055b	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b816-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Eastern Cape</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Apostolic)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Nomvula Qamata</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">55–64</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Urban (city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Teacher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Hypertension (high blood pressure)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3482f041-52ef-43f2-8e83-97ab2638055b</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I like to snack—what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Nomvula, and I teach young ones here in Dispatch. It’s a busy life, you know? I live in a little house near the market, not fancy, but it’s home. My days are full – lesson plans, making sure the children eat, and then helping my mama with the garden. We mostly eat what we can grow: maize porridge for breakfast, sometimes a bit of cabbage if we’re lucky, and a meaty stew for supper. I take my insulin and my pills, like I’m supposed to, but sometimes, I just…forget. It’s easy to do, isn’t it?  Do you ever just get so busy you forget to take care of yourself?  I was grading papers yesterday, a big stack of them, and I just kept eating biscuits. I felt a bit tired, but I told myself it was just the heat.  Then, my head started to feel funny, and I had to sit down.  It was a proper struggle to finish the lesson, and the children looked a bit confused. It made me think, maybe I need to pay more attention to what my body is telling me.\n\nToday, I had to go to the clinic. Mr. Dlamini, the nurse, he’s a good man. He always asks about my blood sugar, and he tells me to walk a little bit every day. He says it helps with the sugar, and keeps my heart strong. I told him I don’t have time, that teaching takes up all my days. He just smiled and said, ‘Nomvula, your health is like a small seed. If you don’t water it, it won't grow.  Just a little bit each day, that’s all it takes.’ He gave me a pamphlet with pictures of healthy food – bright red tomatoes, green beans, and lovely oranges.  I took it home, but honestly, I just put it on the table and forgot about it. My mama was making umleqiso, and I was hungry, so I had a piece of that, too. It’s hard to change, you know? Especially when you're used to things one way.\n\nI tried to walk today, like Mr. Dlamini said. I started slowly, just around the yard. But then, my sister, Thandi, she called and asked me to help her with the laundry.  It's always something! So, I folded clothes for a good hour, and by the time I finished, I felt really tired. My feet were aching, and I forgot all about my walk.  I told myself, ‘Tomorrow, tomorrow,’ but then I started planning the next day’s lessons.  It’s like, my job is important, and my family needs me, so I just keep going, keep going. But I know, deep down, that if I don’t take care of myself, I won’t be able to do anything for anyone. My boss, Mr. Nkosi, he's expecting a big report next week, and I need to be sharp to finish it.\n\nToday, I walked for fifteen minutes, just after my lesson. It wasn't much, but it felt good. I even ate an orange, a real juicy one!  Mr. Dlamini will be pleased. The report for Mr. Nkosi? I finished it, and it was good, really good. I didn’t feel that funny head, and I had plenty of energy. I’m starting to see that taking care of myself isn’t just about medicine; it’s about being strong for my family and for my work.  It’s like planting that little seed – a little bit of water, a little bit of sunshine, and it grows.  And maybe, just maybe, I’ll even have time to dance a little umxhentso with my grandchildren soon.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3482f041-52ef-43f2-8e83-97ab2638055b_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3482f041-52ef-43f2-8e83-97ab2638055b_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3482f041-52ef-43f2-8e83-97ab2638055b_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/3482f041-52ef-43f2-8e83-97ab2638055b_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3482f041-52ef-43f2-8e83-97ab2638055b_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3482f041-52ef-43f2-8e83-97ab2638055b_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3482f041-52ef-43f2-8e83-97ab2638055b_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/3482f041-52ef-43f2-8e83-97ab2638055b_3.mp3}	What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?	exercise	female	consistency	2026-03-23 13:02:30.74534+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gemma-3-4b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Lwando Mlambo is a 35–44 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Teacher, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: he has none. Religion: Atheism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: No Restrictions. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 3–4 days. Support network: Community or religious group. Health goals: What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?. his food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Lwando Mlambo and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "default", "audio_prompts": ["Nomvula Qamata, 55, female voice, slightly raspy, speaking in a South African accent. Background sounds of children's laughter and distant classroom chatter. She begins with a weary tone, listing her daily tasks – ‘teaching, helping mama, gardening…’. There’s a slight pause as she considers her health, followed by a questioning tone: ‘It’s easy to do, isn’t it? Do you ever just get so busy you forget to take care of yourself?’ The audio fades slightly as she describes her tiredness and the strange feeling in her head.", "Nomvula Qamata, 60, female voice, slightly hesitant, speaking in a South African accent. A gentle, reassuring tone.  The voice of Mr. Dlamini, a male voice, is heard nearby, offering advice. ‘Nomvula, your health is like a small seed…’.  Subtle background sounds of a clinic environment – the quiet hum of equipment, the rustle of papers.  A slight pause as Nomvula processes the information.", "Nomvula Qamata, 58, female voice, a slightly rushed tone, with a hint of apology.  The sounds of fabric being folded, a rhythmic rustle.  A brief, cheerful voice of her sister, Thandi, in the background: ‘Nomvula, you’re helping so much!’  The audio fades slightly as she describes her fatigue and the temptation to postpone her walk.", "Nomvula Qamata, 62, female voice, a calmer, more confident tone.  Gentle background sounds of birds singing and a light breeze rustling through leaves.  A subtle, contented sigh.  The audio fades out slowly, leaving a sense of peace and quiet accomplishment."], "image_prompts": ["Nomvula Qamata, 58, South African woman, sitting at a wooden table in a small, cluttered classroom. She wears a patterned dress and a simple headscarf. Sunlight streams through the window, illuminating dust motes in the air. She’s deeply focused on grading papers, a furrow in her brow, clutching a pencil tightly.  The classroom is filled with colorful children’s drawings on the walls. A sense of quiet determination and slight fatigue.", "Nomvula Qamata, 59, standing in a small, brightly lit clinic room. She's speaking to Mr. Dlamini, a kind-looking man in a white coat.  Mr. Dlamini is gesturing towards a pamphlet with pictures of fruits and vegetables. Nomvula looks slightly hesitant, holding the pamphlet with a questioning expression.  The clinic is filled with the gentle sounds of medical equipment and hushed conversations.", "Nomvula Qamata, 60, bending over a washing line filled with colorful clothes. Her sister, Thandi, a younger woman with a warm smile, is nearby, helping her. The scene is bathed in the golden light of the afternoon sun.  Nomvula is concentrating on folding a piece of cloth, her expression one of slight weariness and a touch of guilt.", "Nomvula Qamata, 62, walking slowly along a paved path in a small town. She is smiling slightly, holding an orange in her hand. A few trees line the path, and the sky is a clear blue. The photograph captures a sense of gentle movement and quiet contentment."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 9.697208734659048, "task_parent_id": "237bba40-16a7-4bdb-af77-c705b2ce45df", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What healthy snack options are available in urban areas of Eastern Cape for someone following a Kosher diet and living with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How can I incorporate traditional Xhosa foods from Eastern Cape into a diet suitable for managing hypertension and type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there community programs or exercise groups in Eastern Cape cities for women over 55, especially those who enjoy traditional dancing like umxhentso?\\",\\n  \\"What are effective ways for Christian Apostolic women in South Africa to balance spiritual practices with health goals like lowering blood pressure?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find local resources or support groups in Eastern Cape for older adults managing both type 2 diabetes and hypertension?\\",\\n  \\"How does weekly tobacco smoking and daily alcohol consumption affect diabetes management for women in their late 50s in South Africa?\\",\\n  \\"What are some low-impact physical activities suitable for women over 55 who haven’t exercised recently and have hypertension?\\",\\n  \\"Are there cultural events or social groups in urban Eastern Cape where I can participate in traditional dancing safely with my health conditions?\\",\\n  \\"What advice do local healthcare clinics in Eastern Cape provide about combining herbal/traditional medicine with prescription medication for diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How often should I consult my healthcare provider about adjusting my diabetes medication if I change my diet or physical activity level?\\",\\n  \\"What are some family-friendly snack recipes that fit Kosher guidelines and are appropriate for Xhosa households in South Africa?\\",\\n  \\"Where in Eastern Cape urban areas can I buy affordable, healthy snacks that are suitable for people with diabetes and hypertension?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
