    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1–2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">99d0dba7-21f7-4f9e-b165-c9e8b675b37a</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I like to snack—what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Yho, namhlanje I woke up feeling a bit lazy, but I had lesson plans to finish for my Grade 4 class. Usually, I like to snack while I work, so I grabbed amagwinya and sweet rooibos tea, thinking it would help me focus. As I worked on the lesson outline, my eyes started feeling heavy, and I just felt tired. Can you imagine? I couldn’t even finish my class poster! I wondered, 'Is this from age, or maybe from the food I ate?' I just didn’t know which foods were good for my sugar.\n\nLater in the week, I went to our local clinic for my regular check-up. The nurse, Sister Zanele, noticed my blood sugar was a bit high and asked about my meals. I told her about my snacking. She gave me friendly advice, saying, 'Mama Lulama, rather try snacking on things like unsalted nuts, fresh fruit like apples, and wholegrain crackers. These won’t spike your sugar too much. Also, small portions are best—don’t eat all at once. You’ll have more energy at work and for your dancing!' I wanted to argue, but honestly, who am I to question a nurse who knows these things?\n\nThat night, I packed a small container of apple slices and a handful of peanuts to take to class the next day. Eish, old habits die hard! I found myself craving something sweet during my tea break, and I almost reached for a scone in the staffroom. But I remembered, next week was the big parent-teacher meeting, and I needed all my energy. Holding my apple, I sighed, and took a slow bite instead. Was this really going to help?\n\nBy the time the meeting came, I had stuck to my new snacks for a week. I didn’t get tired, not even once! I stood up in front of all the parents, speaking with confidence and answering questions. I even planned a small dance with the learners afterward. Now I see—changing a snack isn’t so hard if you decide to try, especially when you notice the difference. I’m ready to learn more step by step. Who says old ladies can’t change?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/99d0dba7-21f7-4f9e-b165-c9e8b675b37a_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/99d0dba7-21f7-4f9e-b165-c9e8b675b37a_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/99d0dba7-21f7-4f9e-b165-c9e8b675b37a_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/99d0dba7-21f7-4f9e-b165-c9e8b675b37a_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/99d0dba7-21f7-4f9e-b165-c9e8b675b37a_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/99d0dba7-21f7-4f9e-b165-c9e8b675b37a_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/99d0dba7-21f7-4f9e-b165-c9e8b675b37a_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/99d0dba7-21f7-4f9e-b165-c9e8b675b37a_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:22.287917+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Lwazi Nkosi is a 18–24 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Entrepreneur, lives in a Suburban (near city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: he has none. Religion: Agnosticism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: Anxiety or depression. Diet restrictions: Vegetarian. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 5–7 days. Support network: Healthcare provider or counsellor. Health goals: Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?. his food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Lwazi Nkosi and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "default", "audio_prompts": ["In this scene, Lulama Ngxeke’s mid-range natural, slightly breathy South African English voice sounds slightly tired as she mutters to herself while flipping lesson papers on a laminated kitchen table. There is a soft clatter of mug against table, gentle background hum of the fridge, and the shuffling of paper. Her sighs are audible between words, and her voice slows as she wonders, 'Why am I so tired today?' The atmosphere is quiet with ambient traffic faintly audible through a window.", "The scene includes Sister Zanele, a middle-aged female nurse. From across a small clinic room, Sister Zanele's clear, friendly South African English mingles with Lulama Ngxeke’s hesitant responses. Chairs squeak on tiled floor as Zanele explains, 'Try fruit like apples, not too much, Mama Lulama.' Lulama’s voice rises with mild protest then lowers. A blood pressure monitor beeps softly in the background; the room is softly echoey, filled with calm professional warmth.", "In the school staffroom, Lulama Ngxeke is heard breathing out slowly as she opens a plastic container of apples. Her quiet, self-spoken words have a South African English accent; her voice is hesitant and slow, as she almost whispers, 'Maybe just one bite.' Behind her, a faint mug clink and a colleague’s laugh are heard at a distance. The room carries the dull hum of conversation, footsteps, and Lulama’s deliberate, thoughtful chewing.", "The scene captures Lulama Ngxeke standing in front of a classroom, her confident, mid-range clear South African English voice projecting warmly as she speaks to a group of parents. There’s the soft rustle of notebooks, an enthusiastic child’s giggle from the side, and the subtle shuffle of chairs. Lulama’s voice is strong and steady, rising as she addresses parents, pausing to allow a question, then continuing with relaxed, genuine warmth. The classroom acoustics add gentle reverb to her voice."], "image_prompts": ["Lulama Ngxeke, a 65+ year old IsiXhosa female, sits at her small kitchen table, surrounded by neatly stacked lesson papers, holding a mug of sweet rooibos tea in one hand and an amagwinya in the other. The kitchen is bright with morning sunlight streaming through a curtain, and her face is tired, her shoulders slouched, eyes half-closed, the untouched class poster rolled beside her. Her body posture is resigned and weary, showing frustration and fatigue. Highly detailed, with clear skin texture and the textured cloth of her dress visible.", "A middle-aged woman named Sister Zanele, female nurse, stands beside Lulama Ngxeke in the local clinic consultation room. The scene is a medium shot with Lulama sitting upright on a plastic clinic chair, looking thoughtfully at Sister Zanele who gestures warmly with her hand. Details include clinical posters on pale green walls, fluorescent lighting, both women in clear focus, showing supportive body language. Lulama’s hands rest calmly in her lap, and her attentive posture displays mild resistance but openness.", "Lulama Ngxeke stands near the staffroom countertop at her school, holding a clear container of apple slices in one hand and hesitant eyes glancing towards a box of scones on the counter. The scene is a candid medium shot, with the staffroom’s fluorescent lights overhead, a kettle and mugs in the background, and Lulama pausing mid-step, her body caught between habit and determination. Her lips are pursed, showing struggle to change habits.", "Lulama Ngxeke stands in front of a school classroom with sunlight pouring through tall windows, confidently addressing a few seated parents. The scene is a wide shot, capturing Lulama gesturing with open hands as she speaks, shoulders relaxed and head high. Some learners at the side smile, and colorful posters hang on the walls. Lulama’s posture is upright, full of energy, reflecting new confidence and readiness. Her dark traditional dress adds detail."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 1.9909880101680755, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some popular vegan snack options available in urban areas of Eastern Cape, South Africa, especially near local markets or stores?\\",\\n  \\"How can a 65+ year-old isiXhosa woman with type 2 diabetes manage healthy snacking while participating in traditional dances like umxhentso?\\",\\n  \\"Are there vegan-friendly snack recipes inspired by isiXhosa culture that support blood sugar control for teachers who are physically active?\\",\\n  \\"What are the guidelines on how often someone with type 2 diabetes should snack if they are doing physical activities only 1–2 days per week?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find affordable, healthy vegan snacks in Eastern Cape cities with easy access to healthy foods?\\",\\n  \\"How does regular tobacco and daily alcohol use affect snack choices and diabetes management for older women in South Africa?\\",\\n  \\"Are there local support groups or community programs in urban Eastern Cape that provide advice on vegan snacking and diabetes control for women?\\",\\n  \\"What are the healthiest vegan snack options that are quick to prepare for a busy teacher working in an urban environment in the Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"How can women over 65 in urban Eastern Cape integrate traditional isiXhosa herbs or foods into vegan snacks that help manage diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there isiXhosa traditional dances or gatherings in Eastern Cape that offer vegan snack options suitable for someone managing diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What strategies can I use to avoid unhealthy snacking at work as a female school teacher with type 2 diabetes living in an urban area?\\",\\n  \\"How can my healthcare provider or local clinic in Eastern Cape support me with recommendations for healthy vegan snacks for diabetes, considering my lifestyle and dietary preferences?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "Relevant persona fields: Lulama is a 65+ year old IsiXhosa female living in an urban Eastern Cape setting. She works as a teacher and takes both insulin and oral medication for type 2 diabetes. Her hobbies include traditional dancing. She has easy access to healthy foods, receives regular health care, has support from health workers, and wants to learn about everyday foods that won't spike her blood sugar.", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
2312a2a6-507d-4099-83b4-ef0ce7ec5b45	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b816-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Eastern Cape</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Lutheran)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Lihle Mthembu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">45–54</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Urban (city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Trader</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Anxiety or depression</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3–4 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No Restrictions</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">2312a2a6-507d-4099-83b4-ef0ce7ec5b45</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, every morning I push myself to the market, standing long hours, selling things, thinking that’s enough exercise, right? But then I start feeling tired, and my sugar levels go all over the place. One day, I was asked by my boss to help carry heavy boxes early in the morning, and I nearly had to stop. I thought, 'Is this how my body wants to behave?' I hadn’t really thought much about the kind of exercise that would be good for me. I just moved as I always did, thinking that was enough for diabetes. Turns out, it wasn’t, and I struggled to finish that small task. I guess I was missing something important about taking care of my body for work."\n\nThen I went to my regular check-up at the local clinic, telling the nurse about my tiredness and trouble with the boxes. She smiled kindly and said, ‘Lihle, exercising doesn’t have to be hard or heavy. Walking for 30 minutes, gentle dancing like our umxhentso, or even stretching can help keep your blood sugar steady.’ I wasn’t sure at first, thinking, ‘Can dancing really be exercise for my diabetes?’ But she explained how moving your body helps insulin work better, and how it fits nicely with our own culture. She even said to start slow and listen to my body. That advice sounded doable and made me feel hopeful.”\n\nSo I started trying — a little walk here, a few dance steps there. But, oh, it was tough changing old habits. Sometimes I forgot or felt lazy after a long day at the market. There was a day when my sugar dropped a bit too low because I walked too fast without eating properly first. That scared me a bit, and I almost gave up. But I remembered there’s a bigger task coming up — a community festival where I’d join the dance group to celebrate our culture. I wanted to be strong for that day, so I kept going, learning bit by bit how to balance rest, food, and movement."\n\nNow, I feel a little lighter and more able to carry my things at work without feeling drained. The festival came, and I danced with joy, not worrying about my sugar spikes or crashes. I learned that small, safe exercises can make a real difference, and I’m even planning to keep it up because it helps me with my diabetes and my spirit. Who would have thought that embracing our traditional dances and easy walks would give me not just strength, but more hope and happiness? I’m ready for whatever comes next, knowing I can take care of myself while doing what I love."	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/2312a2a6-507d-4099-83b4-ef0ce7ec5b45_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/2312a2a6-507d-4099-83b4-ef0ce7ec5b45_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/2312a2a6-507d-4099-83b4-ef0ce7ec5b45_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/2312a2a6-507d-4099-83b4-ef0ce7ec5b45_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/2312a2a6-507d-4099-83b4-ef0ce7ec5b45_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/2312a2a6-507d-4099-83b4-ef0ce7ec5b45_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/2312a2a6-507d-4099-83b4-ef0ce7ec5b45_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/2312a2a6-507d-4099-83b4-ef0ce7ec5b45_3.mp3}	What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:24.669967+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Zanele Mabhena is a 25–34 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Teacher, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: she has none. Religion: Christianity (Methodist). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication