ork responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "social_support", "audio_prompts": ["Sipho Mkize's voice, sounding worried and slightly out of breath, 'Oh no, not again. I forgot my insulin. Okay, stay calm, Sipho. You can't let this ruin your day.'", "Sister Nomsa's voice, sounding calm and reassuring, 'Don't worry, Sipho. We can work together to get your diabetes under control. Let's start with small changes, okay?'", "Sipho Mkize's voice, sounding determined, 'I can do this. I just need to take it one step at a time. Okay, Sipho, let's get moving.'", "Sipho Mkize's voice, sounding happy and relieved, 'I did it. I'm taking control of my health. I feel proud of myself, and I'm grateful for the support of my loved ones.'"], "image_prompts": ["Sipho Mkize, a middle-aged man, sitting on his couch, looking concerned and rubbing his temples, with a clock on the wall showing 7:30 am, and a bottle of insulin and a glucometer on the coffee table in front of him.", "Sister Nomsa, a middle-aged woman with a kind smile, sitting at her desk, with Sipho Mkize, a middle-aged man, sitting across from her, looking concerned, and a notebook and pen on the desk between them, in a medium shot.", "Sipho Mkize, a middle-aged man, walking in a park, looking determined, with a water bottle in his hand, and a few trees and birds in the background, in a wide shot.", "Sipho Mkize, a middle-aged man, sitting in his living room, looking happy and relaxed, with a book in his hand, and a few plants and a vase with flowers on the coffee table in front of him, in a medium shot."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 5.6488824540918525, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some IsiXhosa traditional foods in Eastern Cape urban areas that are suitable for someone with type 2 diabetes and hypertension?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I buy affordable, fresh kosher food options in urban centres of the Eastern Cape for a diabetic-friendly diet?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any Apostolic Christian community health support groups in Eastern Cape cities that focus on managing diabetes and high blood pressure?\\",\\n  \\"Which traditional games popular among urban IsiXhosa men aged 45–54 can help increase my physical activity for diabetes management?\\",\\n  \\"How can I adapt popular South African urban dishes to keep my blood sugar stable while following a kosher diet?\\",\\n  \\"What lifestyle tips do Eastern Cape teachers use to manage stress and hypertension while living with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there recommendations for low-sugar, low-salt recipes commonly eaten by the IsiXhosa community that fit a daily routine with family and social gatherings?\\",\\n  \\"What urban spaces or groups in Eastern Cape are available for adults looking to socialize through traditional activities that also support healthy living with diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Which herbal or traditional medicines used in IsiXhosa culture are considered safe as complementary care for type 2 diabetes alongside standard medication?\\",\\n  \\"How does daily alcohol consumption impact blood sugar and blood pressure for men in their 50s with type 2 diabetes in South Africa?\\",\\n  \\"Are there Eastern Cape healthcare clinics with expertise in managing both diabetes and hypertension for middle-aged male patients who prefer regular checkups?\\",\\n  \\"How can I quit smoking in a culturally supportive way as an IsiXhosa-speaking Christian in an urban part of Eastern Cape while balancing work as a teacher?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
d020a4e0-e68f-4259-87ab-54e52d569074	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b816-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Eastern Cape</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Apostolic)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Thandekile Mzamane</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">25–34</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Urban (city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Teacher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Storytelling (iintsomi) and praise poetry (izibongo)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Socialising with family or friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3–4 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Gluten-Free</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">d020a4e0-e68f-4259-87ab-54e52d569074</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Hayi, let me tell you now. My name is Thandekile, and I’m a teacher at a primary school here in East London. It’s a good job, I love the children, but sometimes… sometimes it’s hard to keep up! I always feel tired, you see? And I like a little something sweet with my tea, maybe a biscuit or two, or even a nice piece of koeksister after supper. It just helps me relax after a long day of marking papers and trying to get little ones to understand their numbers. Last week, I promised to help Miss Dlamini with the school play decorations, painting all the backdrop scenes. I thought, easy peasy! But halfway through, I felt so weak and shaky, I had to stop. I just sat there, staring at the half-finished tree, wondering what was wrong with me. It was embarrassing, really. What good is a teacher who can't even paint a tree?\n\nMy neighbour, Mama Nomusa, she noticed I was looking a bit down. She’s like my second mother, always looking out for me. She told me I should go to the clinic, just to check things. I wasn’t keen, honestly. Clinics, they always make you wait, and I thought, I’m just a little tired. But Mama Nomusa, she insisted. So I went. The sister, she was very kind. She checked my blood sugar and explained that my sweet tooth was causing trouble. She said I have something called ‘diabetes’ and that I need to watch what I eat. ‘Thandekile,’ she said, ‘you need to swap those biscuits for something healthier. A handful of peanuts, maybe, or a piece of fruit. And don’t wait until you’re starving to eat, have small snacks throughout the day.’ She even gave me a list of foods I could eat and told me about gluten-free options. It sounded… different. But she explained it would help me have more energy for my work, and for my children at school. Maybe she was right.\n\nTrying to change my habits wasn't easy, I tell you! I'm used to my tea and biscuits, it’s a comfort. The first few days, I kept forgetting to pack the peanuts, and then I’d end up grabbing a biscuit from the shop anyway. One afternoon, I was supposed to meet with another teacher to plan a lesson, and I completely forgot my snack. I felt so grumpy and couldn’t concentrate. But then I remembered the school play decorations. Miss Dlamini was counting on me! I couldn’t let her down again. So I quickly ran to the shop and bought an apple. It wasn't a koeksister, but it helped a little. It’s a slow process, this changing, but I knew I had to keep trying. It’s like learning a new izibongo – it takes practice.\n\nSlowly, slowly, things started to get better. I started packing a small bag with apples, peanuts, and sometimes even biltong for a protein boost. I still have my tea, but without the mountains of sugar. And you know what? I started feeling stronger! I finished the backdrop for the school play, the tree looks beautiful, and Miss Dlamini was so pleased. I even had enough energy to help with the costumes. I still get cravings for something sweet, but now I can usually resist. The sister at the clinic said I need to keep checking my blood sugar, and I am. It’s not perfect, but I’m learning. I’m starting to think maybe, just maybe, taking care of myself isn’t so bad after all. Maybe it’s even… a good thing. Who knew a little change in my snacks could make such a big difference?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d020a4e0-e68f-4259-87ab-54e52d569074_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d020a4e0-e68f-4259-87ab-54e52d569074_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d020a4e0-e68f-4259-87ab-54e52d569074_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d020a4e0-e68f-4259-87ab-54e52d569074_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d020a4e0-e68f-4259-87ab-54e52d569074_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d020a4e0-e68f-4259-87ab-54e52d569074_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d020a4e0-e68f-4259-87ab-54e52d569074_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d020a4e0-e68f-4259-87ab-54e52d569074_3.mp3}	I like to snack—what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?	diet	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:28.883541+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gemma-3-27b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Akhona Ndlovu is a 45–54 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Teacher, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: she has none. Religion: Agnosticism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: No Restrictions. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: Yes. Physical activity: 0 days. Support network: I live alone or have limited support. Health goals: Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?. her food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Akhona Ndlovu and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, a