d">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Teacher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Storytelling (iintsomi) and praise poetry (izibongo)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5–7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Vegetarian</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">62f94371-8b50-46b9-bd21-5ec1a69bd6a7</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Sipho, and I teach at the school in Buffalo City. It’s a good school, lots of kids, but sometimes, it feels like I’m running around like a headless chicken! I usually have a sadza and beans for breakfast – it’s quick, you know? And then maybe some veggies if I have time. Lunch is usually something from the tuck shop – a bunny chow, or maybe a boerewors roll. It’s yummy, but sometimes my tummy feels a bit funny afterwards. I don't really think much about it, though. I just get on with the day, teaching my little ones about history and reading. It’s important work, but honestly, sometimes I forget to take care of myself. I think, ‘Later, Sipho, later.’\n\nToday, I had to prepare a presentation about the history of the Xhosa people for the school’s heritage day. I promised Mr. Dube, the principal, I’d have it ready by lunchtime. I planned to make some pap with beetroot for my lunch, thinking that would give me energy for the afternoon. But I got so busy with the kids, helping them with their writing and explaining the stories, I completely forgot to check my blood sugar. By the time I realised, it was almost 3 pm and the presentation was a disaster! My hands were shaking, and I couldn’t remember half of what I wanted to say. Mr. Dube looked a little disappointed. I felt terrible. Why didn’t I just stop for a minute and check myself? It’s like, I was so focused on the kids, I forgot about me.\n\nNurse Nomusa at the clinic told me a lot of things. She said my blood sugar was a little high, and that eating too much of the sugary things from the tuck shop was making it worse. She showed me pictures of good foods – lots of vegetables, like spinach and cabbage, and fruits like apples and bananas. She said I could have porridge with milk instead of just sadza, and that it would help keep my energy up. She also gave me a little booklet with recipes – simple things, like a bean stew and a carrot and tomato soup. It felt a bit strange, trying to change my eating habits. I still wanted my bunny chow sometimes! But she said, ‘Sipho, you need to think about the school presentation, and all the other things you need to do. You need to be strong.’\n\nI tried making the bean stew last night. It wasn’t as good as my mama’s, but it was still tasty. I even ate some of the carrots and tomatoes! And today, I actually checked my blood sugar before lunch. It was a little lower than yesterday. Mr. Dube noticed my presentation was better – much better! He said he was proud of my effort. It felt good to have done well, and it made me want to keep trying. I’m going to try to eat a bit healthier every day, just like Nurse Nomusa said. Maybe tomorrow, I'll even make a big pot of porridge. It's a slow change, but it feels good. I’m starting to realize that taking care of myself is just as important as teaching my kids.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/62f94371-8b50-46b9-bd21-5ec1a69bd6a7_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/62f94371-8b50-46b9-bd21-5ec1a69bd6a7_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/62f94371-8b50-46b9-bd21-5ec1a69bd6a7_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/62f94371-8b50-46b9-bd21-5ec1a69bd6a7_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/62f94371-8b50-46b9-bd21-5ec1a69bd6a7_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/62f94371-8b50-46b9-bd21-5ec1a69bd6a7_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/62f94371-8b50-46b9-bd21-5ec1a69bd6a7_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/62f94371-8b50-46b9-bd21-5ec1a69bd6a7_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:30.993324+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gemma-3-4b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Lwando Mlambo is a 35–44 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Teacher, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: he has none. Religion: Atheism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: No Restrictions. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 3–4 days. Support network: Community or religious group. Health goals: What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?. his food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Lwando Mlambo and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "iteration", "audio_prompts": ["Sipho Mhlontlo, 19, speaks with a slight South African accent, his voice slightly hurried and a little breathless. ‘Umgcine, umgcine! I’m so busy today. The kids are always asking questions. I just need to get through the day, you know?’ Background sounds of children’s voices and classroom activity are faintly audible in the background. A faint, distant sound of a school bell ringing.", "Sipho Mhlontlo, 20, sighs deeply, his voice lower and more subdued. ‘It’s just… I forget sometimes. I get so caught up in helping the children that I don’t even think about myself. It’s like a bad habit, I guess.’ Nurse Nomusa, a woman’s voice, is speaking calmly in the background, ‘Remember, Sipho, your health is just as important as their education.’", "Sipho Mhlontlo, 21, speaks with a deliberate pace, his voice slightly strained but determined. ‘Okay, so the beans… I’ll chop them up really small. And lots of vegetables. Maybe some spinach and carrots. It’s not so bad, really. Nurse Nomusa said it would help.’ The sound of sizzling vegetables in a pot is prominent, accompanied by the gentle clinking of utensils. A quiet, reassuring voice – Nurse Nomusa – is explaining something in the background.", "Sipho Mhlontlo, 22, speaks with a lighter, more confident tone. ‘Yes, it’s… it’s getting easier. I checked my sugar this morning, and it was better. Mr. Dube was pleased. I’m starting to feel… stronger. Like I can actually do this.’ A faint, cheerful background sound of children’s laughter and the distant sound of a school bell ringing."], "image_prompts": ["Sipho Mhlontlo, a young man, stands in a classroom filled with children, looking slightly overwhelmed and stressed. He’s holding a textbook and pointing at a map of South Africa, surrounded by a sea of young faces. The classroom is brightly lit with natural sunlight streaming through the windows, showing worn wooden desks and colorful posters. He has a slightly furrowed brow and a hesitant expression.", "Sipho Mhlontlo, 19, sits at his desk in the principal's office, his head in his hands, a look of frustration and disappointment on his face. Mr. Dube, a middle-aged man with a kind face, is standing beside him, gesturing gently. The office is small and cluttered with papers, with a view of the school grounds through a window. The lighting is warm and slightly dim.", "Sipho Mhlontlo, 20, is carefully stirring a pot of bean stew on a stove in a simple kitchen. The kitchen is modest, with worn linoleum flooring and basic appliances. He’s wearing a slightly stained apron, and a thoughtful expression on his face. A small booklet with recipes is open on the counter beside him.", "Sipho Mhlontlo, 21, stands confidently in front of the school’s heritage day display, a colorful collage of photographs and historical artifacts. He is smiling and gesturing to a group of children who are gathered around, listening intently. The display is set up in the school hall, which is decorated with traditional Xhosa patterns and colors. The lighting is bright and celebratory."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 9.697208734659048, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some common isiXhosa-inspired, Kosher, diabetes-friendly meals suitable for a male working as a teacher living in a suburban area of Eastern Cape, South Africa?\\",\\n  \\"Are there local Eastern Cape food markets or grocery stores near suburban areas offering fresh and healthy options suitable for type 2 diabetes and Kosher dietary needs?\\",\\n  \\"How can I integrate traditional iintsomi (storytelling) and izibongo (praise poetry) sessions into group physical activities for young adults managing type 2 diabetes in South African suburbia?\\",\\n  \\"What easy meal prep ideas are popular among young isiXhosa Christians in the Eastern Cape who follow Kosher dietary restrictions and need to manage type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there community exercise groups or safe jogging routes near suburban towns in Eastern Cape suitable for males aged 18–24 with regular physical activity and diabetes management needs?\\",\\n  \\"What are the best strategies for maintaining blood sugar levels during long teaching days for type 2 diabetics on insulin and oral medication in Eastern Cape schools?\\",\\n  \\"How does diabetes care or support differ in local healthcare clinics versus hospitals in suburban parts of Eastern Cape for young adults?\\",\\n  \\"Are there isiXhosa-language educational resources or support groups for young male teachers managing diabetes in the Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"How can I involve my family in supporting my dietary and exercise goals while respecting both Kosher and traditional isiXhosa food preferences?\\",\\n  \\"What local or regional events in Eastern Cape promote healthy lifestyles or diabetes awareness that align with Christian beliefs and the isiXhosa culture?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any smartphone apps or online resources recommended for tracking glucose levels, meals, and activity, tailored for South African conditions?\\",\\n  \\"How can I adapt traditional suburban Eastern Cape recipes to meet both Kosher and diabetes-friendly dietary requirements?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
