r support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">e5ecade1-ec1d-4576-8cf0-0bfc799635e6</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Every day for me, Otieno Odhiambo, starts with a quick mug of uji as I check emails before heading out to the office in Kisumu. You know, as an administrator, my work never waits. Most times I grab some mandazi at the gate and push on, thinking I can always skip a proper breakfast or snack if I'm too busy. Today, I had to prepare an important report for the council meeting, but, ah, midway my energy just crashed. My hands were shaky, and I snapped at a colleague for no reason. Who really thinks snacking is important when budgets are due?\n\nLater, when I went to the clinic for my insulin, the nurse, Sister Anne, called me aside. I wasn't ready for a lecture, but she only asked if I was feeling tired lately. I told her about the shakes and skipped snacks, and she smiled, saying, 'Otieno, even strong men need fuel. Try snacking on roasted groundnuts or a half-slice of sweet potato in between meals. You have diabetes, so it's not just about eating, but eating smart.' She even wrote a simple plan—ugali brown, omena, greens, and moderate fruit—straightforward, eh? Still, sometimes I wonder, must I really change all this for snacks?\n\nHonestly, the next day I packed some groundnuts for work, but at break time, I almost reached for mandazi again when the aroma floated in! Change is not easy, especially when everyone else is munching chapati or nyama choma. Still, the big council audit is next week, and I can't afford another midday crash. So, stubborn as I am, I pushed on with groundnuts even though it felt weird in front of colleagues. Why does healthy snacking have to feel like punishment?\n\nDay by day, I adjusted. The shakes got less, my mind cleared, and I nailed that audit report without barking at anyone. It's not magic, just small changes with realistic snacks, and I didn't even lose my Luo appetite. Now, when I step out to exercise after work, I feel lighter and more hopeful—who would have thought that learning to snack smart could help me win at work too? Maybe this healthy thing, it's not as bad as I feared.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e5ecade1-ec1d-4576-8cf0-0bfc799635e6_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e5ecade1-ec1d-4576-8cf0-0bfc799635e6_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e5ecade1-ec1d-4576-8cf0-0bfc799635e6_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/e5ecade1-ec1d-4576-8cf0-0bfc799635e6_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e5ecade1-ec1d-4576-8cf0-0bfc799635e6_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e5ecade1-ec1d-4576-8cf0-0bfc799635e6_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e5ecade1-ec1d-4576-8cf0-0bfc799635e6_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/e5ecade1-ec1d-4576-8cf0-0bfc799635e6_3.mp3}	I like to snack—what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?	diet	male	consistency	2026-03-23 13:19:58.902849+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Owino Odhiambo is a 45-54 year old, \\nCulture: Luo male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Journalist, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Homa Bay. Disability: he has none. Religion: Christianity (Methodist). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Football (soccer). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Vegetarian. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: Yes. Physical activity: 1-2 days. Support network: Close friends. Health goals: Sometimes I feel tired or discouragedâ€”how can I stay motiva