y meal prep ideas for someone with easy access to healthy foods and a halal diet in Kisumu?\\",\\n  \\"What local Kisumu initiatives focus on healthy living for urban young adults with diabetes who have a family support network?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
c829854d-c8d2-4029-9ecd-a089b3ab8f09	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b816-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Eastern Cape</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Roman Catholic)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Sithandiwe Mthimkhulu</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">25–34</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Suburban (near city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Teacher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family gatherings and celebrations</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Socialising with family or friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Anxiety or depression</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5–7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I live alone or have limited support</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">c829854d-c8d2-4029-9ecd-a089b3ab8f09</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Some mornings, I wake up early and smell tea brewing in my own kitchen. I pack my lunch—rice with chicken stew, and sometimes an apple if I remember. I give myself an insulin injection and swallow my other medication with water. These things are like brushing my teeth, neh? Sometimes, I don’t even think about what they do. That morning, I was preparing lesson plans for my Grade 4’s, hoping to finish the handouts before school so I don’t have to worry later. Suddenly, halfway into my planning, my hands started to shake, and I felt tired, but I ignored it, thinking, 'Maybe I’m just hungry? Should I push through?' I told myself to finish the simple handout task quickly, but I ended up forgetting some important things, and my head just couldn’t work right. That small task became a mess. How am I supposed to teach when I feel so tired?\n\nLater, I went to the local clinic near my school. The nurse called me in, saying, 'Sithandiwe, are you alright?' I tried to explain, but she seemed to just know I was struggling with my sugar and my busy routine. She smiled gently and said, 'Take it slow, sisi. Try walking even just ten minutes before work. It helps your sugar, and makes you feel stronger for your learners.' Part of me wanted to argue, because who has time? But I nodded, because the nurse seemed to understand exactly how tired teachers always feel. I needed simple advice, not a big lecture. She wrote a small plan for me—'Start with one walk, then see if your handouts go better.' I thought, 'Maybe, just maybe, I can try.'\n\nThe next morning, I put on my sweatpants and walked down my quiet street before sunrise. Ai, the first five minutes, I wanted to turn back. My legs felt heavy, and I kept thinking of all the work waiting. I worried, was this really going to help? I almost skipped my walk, but remembered I have to help plan the school’s Heritage Day celebration—it’s a big job! I told myself, 'If I can do this small walk, maybe I’ll have energy for my learners and the big event.' I finished my walk slowly, feeling a little proud. Did I fix everything? Not yet, but I tried.\n\nOver the next few weeks, my short walks slowly turned into a normal thing. My mornings didn’t feel so hard, and my lesson handouts made more sense. The Heritage Day event came, and as I handed out prizes to the best-dressed learners, I realised, 'Hey, I didn’t feel so tired this year.' My principal even smiled and said, 'Well done, Sithandiwe!' I know I still get discouraged sometimes, but now I understand: just starting with one small change can help me and my learners. Maybe next time, I won’t wait until I’m tired to look after myself.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c829854d-c8d2-4029-9ecd-a089b3ab8f09_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c829854d-c8d2-4029-9ecd-a089b3ab8f09_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c829854d-c8d2-4029-9ecd-a089b3ab8f09_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c829854d-c8d2-4029-9ecd-a089b3ab8f09_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/c829854d-c8d2-4029-9ecd-a089b3ab8f09_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/c829854d-c8d2-4029-9ecd-a089b3ab8f09_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/c829854d-c8d2-4029-9ecd-a089b3ab8f09_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/c829854d-c8d2-4029-9ecd-a089b3ab8f09_3.mp3}	Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:22.578589+00	{"label": "GOOD", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Lwazi Nkosi is a 18–24 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Entrepreneur, lives in a Suburban (near city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: he has none. Religion: Agnosticism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: Anxiety or depression. Diet restrictions: Vegetarian. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 5–7 days. Support network: Healthcare provider or counsellor. Health goals: Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?. his food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Lwazi Nkosi and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "default", "audio_prompts": ["The setting is Sithandiwe’s kitchen. The gentle whistle of a kettle, the clink of a teaspoon in a mug, and quiet typing on a laptop are heard. Sithandiwe’s voice is soft, breathing a little heavier as she mutters, 'Just finish this, just finish…' There’s a wavering tone at the end, signaling tiredness and frustration. Paper rustles as she fidgets with her handouts, and there is a quiet sigh as she drops her pen.", "The nurse, a middle-aged woman, sits close to Sithandiwe in the clinic. Chairs scrape softly. The nurse speaks gently in measured English: 'Sithandiwe, walking helps your sugar, sisi. Just ten minutes in the morning.' Sithandiwe replies, voice uncertain but polite, 'Okay… I can try.' There’s a subtle pause, the background is filled with distant coughing, murmured conversations, and echoes off tiled floors, adding to the busyness of the clinic.", "Sithandiwe’s footsteps crunch on gravel as she breathes steadily, occasionally puffing out air through her lips. Birds chirp faintly in the early morning. Sithandiwe’s voice, softly spoken yet a bit breathless from exertion, says to herself, 'It's harder than I thought… But Heritage Day is coming, Sithandiwe.' Her voice trembles with resolve, slowing as she pauses briefly. Distant dogs bark and a car drives by slowly.", "The school hall buzzes with excited children’s voices, giggling and chattering about costumes. Wide shot sound perspective; Sithandiwe stands near the mic, her voice clear and confident: 'Congratulations, everyone! You all look beautiful!' Her laughter rings genuinely, then she calls a child’s name. Small hands clap, creating a round of applause, and a colleague’s voice shouts, 'Well done, Sithandiwe!' The atmosphere is festive, joyful, and energetic, with her voice calm and proud among the lively echoes."], "image_prompts": ["Sithandiwe, a Xhosa woman in her late twenties, stands in a modest, tiled suburban kitchen in the Eastern Cape at sunrise. She wears a simple blouse and skirt, brow furrowed, holding a cup of tea and looking tired while typing lesson plans on a neat, slightly cluttered dining table. A brown lunchbag, insulin pen, and pill bottles are visible beside her laptop. The soft golden morning light casts long, natural window shadows. Sithandiwe's hands are shaky, and her posture is hunched, showing fatigue and worry.", "A middle-aged female nurse sits across from Sithandiwe inside a bright, busy local Eastern Cape clinic. Wide shot. The nurse wears a white uniform, holding a medical chart, smiling warmly and pointing to a handwritten walking plan on paper. Sithandiwe, in casual work clothes, sits upright and attentive, a hint of doubt in her eyes and a school notebook loosely held. Sunlight pours through the window, highlighting posters about diabetes on the cream walls. Both women have open body language, the nurse showing encouragement, Sithandiwe listening uncertainly.", "Sithandiwe walks slowly along a quiet, narrow suburban street before sunrise. She is wearing sweatpants, trainers, and a light hoodie, arms folded across her chest against the morning cold. Her posture is slightly slumped with her head down, a determined but tired look on her face as she glances sideways at neat houses and trimmed gardens. The rising sun throws long blue shadows and warm light on dew-covered grass by the sidewalk.", "Wide shot. Sithandiwe stands in a decorated primary school hall filled with smiling children in colourful Heritage Day clothes. She wears a floral dress, standing tall and holding small prize bags. Her face shows genuine delight and surprise, shoulders back and eyes bright with confidence. Streamers, posters, and South African flags hang overhead. Morning sunlight filters through large school windows, catching the texture of the prizes and children’s neatly braided hair."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 1.9909880101680755, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some IsiXhosa-inspired, Halal-friendly recipes with low glycemic index ingredients available in Eastern Cape suburban areas?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I join group exercise or wellness support programs for women with type 2 diabetes in the suburbs near East London or Port Elizabeth?\\",\\n  \\"How do cultural family gatherings in the Eastern Cape typically impact my ability to manage type 2 diabetes and stick to my healthy eating plans?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any Roman Catholic churches in Eastern Cape offering mental health or emotional support groups for young adults with anxiety or depression?\\",\\n  \\"What activities or clubs in suburban Eastern Cape support social connections for female teachers living alone who want to stay active?\\",\\n  \\"Which local clinics in my area of Eastern Cape have specialists or support groups for managing both diabetes and mental health?\\",\\n  \\"What are some IsiXhosa family traditions that can be adapted to create healthier celebrations suitable for someone with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Where in Eastern Cape can I find fresh, healthy, Halal-certified foods close to residential suburban neighborhoods?\\",\\n  \\"As a young adult woman living alone in a suburban area, what are the best ways to build a diabetes management support system?\\",\\n  \\"How can regular exercise specifically impact blood sugar control and motivation for women with type 2 diabetes who also experience anxiety?\\",\\n  \\"Which exercise routines are popular among diabetic women in Eastern Cape and fit easily into a busy teacher’s daily schedule?\\",\\n  \\"Are there resources or apps in South Africa that track both physical activity and emotional wellbeing for managing type 2 diabetes?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "Relevant persona fields: Adult work routine as a teacher, regular access to healthcare clinic, daily medication (insulin, metformin), exercise routine, anxiety or depression, limited support network, feeling discouraged, lives in a suburban Eastern Cape environment, seeks ways to stay motivated and link between exercise and blood sugar.", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "g