">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Community or religious group</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">6daaf71b-b4a7-4d5e-a975-0ec93260adc9</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Which everyday foods can I eat that won&#x27;t make my blood sugar spike?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	One day, after a big lesson on isiXhosa history, I felt extra weak—my sugar was high, I think. I had eaten pap and meat earlier without the imifino, like I usually do. I wonder now, did I forget that little thing the clinic nurse said about portion sizes and foods to avoid? I thought as long as I eat something, I’m fine. But after class, I nearly skipped a meeting with my coworkers because of low energy. Was it just bad luck, or was I not managing my diet as I should? It felt like the small effort wasn’t enough to keep me sharp for work.\n\nLuckily, the nurse at the local clinic spotted me and offered some advice. She told me about traditional foods that are both good for diabetes and halal, like umngqusho made with sugar beans, and lots of wild greens—the imifino we often see growing nearby. She said I should balance my meals with those, and eat smaller amounts of pap. She explained it in a way I understood, even reminding me that these foods have been part of our culture for generations and can help keep sugar steady. I was a bit unsure at first, thinking, ‘How will I change habits now?’ But having the nurse show me simple meal ideas made it feel possible.\n\nI tried to change my lunch the next week, packing umngqusho and amasi instead of my usual pap and meat. At first, it was hard. I missed the heavier meals that fill my stomach fast and even slipped up one day, eating at a local braai with more pap and alcohol than I should have. But remembering the upcoming school sports day, where I needed to be energetic and alert, pushed me to keep trying. The changes felt slow, but I was determined not to let my health hold me back.\n\nOver time, I started feeling stronger during my teaching and even at home when playing diketo with my nephews. That sports day went well—I was alert, and I even helped organize the kids for one of the races. The little changes in my diet helped me manage my sugar better, and I feel hopeful. Now, I’m looking forward to learning more at the clinic and sharing what I’ve learned with friends and family, knowing I can live well with diabetes without giving up the things I love.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6daaf71b-b4a7-4d5e-a975-0ec93260adc9_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6daaf71b-b4a7-4d5e-a975-0ec93260adc9_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6daaf71b-b4a7-4d5e-a975-0ec93260adc9_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6daaf71b-b4a7-4d5e-a975-0ec93260adc9_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6daaf71b-b4a7-4d5e-a975-0ec93260adc9_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6daaf71b-b4a7-4d5e-a975-0ec93260adc9_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6daaf71b-b4a7-4d5e-a975-0ec93260adc9_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6daaf71b-b4a7-4d5e-a975-0ec93260adc9_3.mp3}	Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?	diet	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:24.957023+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Zanele Mabhena is a 25–34 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Teacher, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: she has none. Religion: Christianity (Methodist). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Vegan. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: Yes. Physical activity: 5–7 days. Support network: Family (parents, siblings, children). Health goals: How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?. her food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Zanele Mabhena and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of 