es around him.", "Near a homestead, Andile Mkhize laughs warmly and chats with his nephews playing diketo. His voice is joyous and relaxed with a crisp South African English accent. The boys’ laughter and the clinking of stones hitting the ground create lively natural sounds. Occasional calls from nearby neighbors and rustling grass add to the candid outdoor ambiance, highlighting a moment of happiness and connection."], "image_prompts": ["Medium shot of Andile Mkhize sitting at a rustic wooden desk in a modest rural classroom, mid-lesson, looking slightly tired with a reflective expression. The classroom has faded posters on the walls, sunlight streaming through window slats, and rough-textured wooden benches. Andile's posture shows slight slouch, one hand resting on a textbook, capturing the moment of concentration mixed with fatigue.", "Medium shot of Andile Mkhize sitting on a plastic chair at the local rural clinic. A female nurse, mid-40s, calm and professional, speaks gently beside him. The clinic room has plain white walls, a poster displaying healthy foods in isiXhosa, and a small wooden table with medical supplies. Andile leans in slightly, eyebrows raised in mild doubt but attentive, hands folded on his lap, showing natural, candid interaction.", "Wide shot of Andile Mkhize walking along a dirt road in his rural village, carrying a small container of food and a water bottle. The sun is low, casting warm golden light over simple thatched houses and green fields. Andile wears casual work clothes, his face showing a mix of determination and slight struggle. Nearby, children play traditional isiXhosa games in the background, adding a social, lively atmosphere.", "Medium shot of Andile Mkhize smiling and crouching beside his young nephews playing diketo outside a small homestead in Eastern Cape. The boys laugh while Andile encourages them, hands on knees, bright eyes, and straight posture. The natural light highlights the textured grass and worn fence posts around them, capturing a joyful, community moment that reflects improved health and spirit."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 5.38300901055336, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What traditional IsiXhosa foods available in rural Eastern Cape are safe for a gluten-free diet and diabetes management?\\",\\n  \\"How can I find healthy, gluten-free ingredients with limited access to food stores in my small rural community in the Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any low-sugar recipes for traditional Methodist or community events that fit a gluten-free, diabetes-friendly diet?\\",\\n  \\"Which outdoor or traditional games common in rural Eastern Cape can help me increase my physical activity if I haven't exercised in weeks?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I join local support groups or health programs for men managing diabetes in rural Eastern Cape IsiXhosa communities?\\",\\n  \\"How does daily smoking and drinking affect diabetes management for adults like me in South Africa's rural areas?\\",\\n  \\"What easy, affordable gluten-free meals can I prepare for my workday as a teacher in a rural school setting?\\",\\n  \\"Are there government or NGO programs in the Eastern Cape that help with accessing diabetes-friendly and gluten-free foods in small communities?\\",\\n  \\"Is there guidance for managing diabetes during Methodist religious gatherings with traditional foods in rural South African communities?\\",\\n  \\"How can I encourage my family or friends to support my healthier lifestyle changes while still participating in social activities in my community?\\",\\n  \\"What are the challenges other IsiXhosa men my age in rural areas face balancing teaching, diabetes management, and cultural traditions?\\",\\n  \\"Are there community-based exercise initiatives or activities suitable for adults with diabetes who have limited time and resources in rural Eastern Cape?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "I'm Andile Mkhize, a teacher in our small Eastern Cape village. Every day, I wake up early to get ready for school, I teach the kids, and then come home tired. I live with type 2 diabetes but never really thought much about how my food or habits affect my job. I smoke and sometimes drink, and my meals are mostly what we find at the local market or homegrown, but I guess I don’t always choose what’s best for my sugar levels. Sometimes I get dizzy or tired at school, but I try to push through for my learners.", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
