uld you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Administrator</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3–4 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Vegan</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">dbe6362e-b132-4fd7-a687-10d76acb7f0b</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Hayi, life, it keeps moving, doesn't it? I'm Bokamoso, and I work as an administrator at the hospital here in East London. It’s busy, always papers to sort, files to check. I love my job, but sometimes… sometimes my head just feels foggy. I usually start my day with a strong cup of tea and a vetkoek from Mama Nomsa’s stall down the road – so warm and tasty! And lunch? Usually a bunny chow, nice and filling. It’s quick, you see. I don’t have time to fuss with fancy food. Yesterday, I was supposed to prepare the patient admission reports for Dr. Ndlovu. Simple enough, right? But my mind just wouldn’t focus. I kept making mistakes, dates wrong, names misspelled. Dr. Ndlovu wasn’t happy, not at all. I just felt… sluggish, like my brain was swimming in mielie-meal.\n\nI eventually went to the clinic, feeling a bit silly, to be honest. I thought, 'What can a doctor do about a foggy brain?' But Sister Thandi was very kind. She explained about my sugar, how the vetkoek and bunny chow, as delicious as they are, can make it go up and down. She said that's why I was feeling tired and struggling to concentrate. 'Bokamoso,' she said, 'your body is like a car. You need the right fuel to make it run smoothly.' She showed me a list of foods - lots of greens, beans, some fruit, and even a little bit of brown rice. She said I could still have my tea, just not so much sugar. It sounded…different. A little bit difficult, maybe. But Dr. Ndlovu is giving me a big project next month, a whole new patient tracking system to set up. I *need* to be sharp for that.\n\nTrying to change things has been… a bit of a battle, I won't lie. Yesterday, I promised myself a salad for lunch, but the smell of the fish and chips from the takeaway next door… oh, it was strong! I ended up grabbing a small portion, telling myself it wouldn’t hurt. And it did, a little. I felt that sluggishness creep back in the afternoon. But I remembered the patient tracking system. I pictured myself, confident and organized, presenting it to Dr. Ndlovu. That thought pushed me to make a proper dinner - some spinach and beans with a little bit of butternut squash. It wasn't a vetkoek, but it wasn’t bad. I even danced a little umxhentso in the kitchen while I cooked, to keep my spirits up. \n\nSlowly, things are getting easier. I’ve started swapping my tea for herbal infusions and having a small piece of fruit instead of a vetkoek. I'm still having my tea, but with just a tiny bit of sugar. The brain fog is lifting, bit by bit. And you know what? I actually *enjoy* the new foods! Mama Nomsa even started making a spinach and bean pie for me, she said she wants me to be healthy. Last week, I finished the first draft of the patient tracking system, and Dr. Ndlovu was impressed! She said it was well-organized and easy to understand. I felt so proud. Maybe taking care of my body isn't just about feeling good, maybe it's about being able to do the things I love, and do them well. Now, I’m thinking… what else can I change to feel even better?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/dbe6362e-b132-4fd7-a687-10d76acb7f0b_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/dbe6362e-b132-4fd7-a687-10d76acb7f0b_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/dbe6362e-b132-4fd7-a687-10d76acb7f0b_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/dbe6362e-b132-4fd7-a687-10d76acb7f0b_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/dbe6362e-b132-4fd7-a687-10d76acb7f0b_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/dbe6362e-b132-4fd7-a687-10d76acb7f0b_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/dbe6362e-b132-4fd7-a687-10d76acb7f0b_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/dbe6362e-b132-4fd7-a687-10d76acb7f0b_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spi