<tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1–2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Gluten-Free</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I live alone or have limited support</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">d984af91-379b-4036-94e3-dac37e845212</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I like to snack—what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	The sun’s already beating down, even this early, and I’m already chasing after crates of mangoes for the market. It’s a good day for business, a busy one. I usually just grab a quick cup of maize porridge – nothing fancy, you know? – and a few peanuts before heading out. It’s all I can manage before the day really kicks off. Honestly, I don't think too much about what I’m eating, just need something to keep me going. Today, I rushed out, grabbed a handful of peanuts, and headed straight to the stall. But halfway through the morning, my stomach started feeling funny, and I just couldn’t seem to finish the day’s work. I felt weak and dizzy, and had to sit down for a while. It was frustrating, a real waste of time and money.\n\nLater, Dr. Zola called me in at the clinic. She listened patiently as I explained how I felt, and then she looked at my blood sugar readings. She said, ‘Liyabona, you’re not getting enough fiber in your diet, and those quick snacks aren’t helping your blood sugar stay stable.’ She explained that a balanced diet, with regular meals and snacks, is really important for keeping your energy levels up and preventing those dizzy spells. She suggested I start with a small bowl of cooked beans or a piece of fruit with a handful of nuts, and to drink plenty of water throughout the day. She also told me about a local support group that helps people manage diabetes – something I hadn’t even considered. It all sounded a bit overwhelming, but she was really calm and encouraging.\n\nI tried to follow her advice, honestly I did. I started adding a small bowl of beans to my lunch, and I kept a piece of apple with me during the afternoon. But it’s hard, you know? The market is busy, and I’m always running around. Sometimes, I just grab a sweetroll from the vendor – it’s quick and easy. And then my stomach feels awful again, and I feel sluggish. Yesterday, I completely skipped the beans and ended up feeling terrible for the rest of the day. It felt like I was fighting against my own habits, and it was exhausting.\n\nBut today, things felt different. I had the beans for breakfast, and I stuck with the apple and nuts during the afternoon. I even managed to finish my work without feeling weak or dizzy. The market was still busy, but I felt stronger and more focused. It’s not a miracle, of course, but it’s a start. I’m starting to realize that taking care of my health isn’t just about avoiding feeling sick; it’s about being able to do the things I love – running my business, spending time with my family, and even dancing with my community. And, honestly, it feels good to be in control, to be making a small change for a better future.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d984af91-379b-4036-94e3-dac37e845212_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d984af91-379b-4036-94e3-dac37e845212_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d984af91-379b-4036-94e3-dac37e845212_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/d984af91-379b-4036-94e3-dac37e845212_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d984af91-379b-4036-94e3-dac37e845212_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d984af91-379b-4036-94e3-dac37e845212_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d984af91-379b-4036-94e3-dac37e845212_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/d984af91-379b-4036-94e3-dac37e845212_3.mp3}	I like to snack—what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?	diet	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:31.899352+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gemma-3-4b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Lwando Mlambo is a 35–44 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Teacher, lives in a Urban (city or town)