         <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Gluten-Free</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0864248a-9a3a-4a1d-8ce6-d9849e6dd8b8</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	I’m Lulama, a nurse working in a busy clinic in the Eastern Cape. Every day I rush to work, and I often eat late at night — after shifts, when my body is tired. I know I have diabetes and high blood pressure, but I don’t really understand how eating late affects my blood sugar. My parents say I should eat better, but I just think, ‘I’ll eat tomorrow.’ I used to eat umxhentso with my friends after work, but now I eat chips and sweets because they’re easy. I don’t feel sick, so I don’t think it matters. That’s how I keep going — just because I don’t feel bad doesn’t mean it’s not hurting my body.\n\nOne night, after a long shift, I went to the local clinic for my check-up. The nurse, Nandi, asked me about my diet. I said I eat late, and she looked at me and said, ‘Lulama, when you eat late, your blood sugar goes up — it’s like a rollercoaster. You can’t just eat chips and sweets and expect it to be fine.’ I said, ‘But I don’t feel anything.’ She smiled and said, ‘That’s the problem — your body doesn’t tell you, but your blood does.’ She gave me a simple plan: eat a small portion of vegetables and grilled chicken, like in a stir-fry, and drink water. She said, ‘Start with one meal a week — just one. It’s not a big change, but it will help your body feel better.’ I was surprised, but I said yes. I didn’t know how to start, but I felt like I had a chance.\n\nI tried the stir-fry on my first night — chicken, spinach, and carrots — but I forgot to eat it before bed. I ate it with my friend Sipho after work, and we had chips and lemonade instead. I felt guilty, but I didn’t stop. I thought, ‘Maybe I just need to be patient.’ The next week, I tried again — this time I made it with my sister. She said, ‘Lulama, you’re doing it right — just one meal a week is enough to start.’ But I still didn’t feel strong. I missed the umxhentso dance with my friends. I was sad. But then I remembered: next month, I have to lead a health workshop for young nurses at the clinic. I can’t go in with a tired body or high blood sugar. That’s when I decided to keep going — even if it’s hard.\n\nAfter a few weeks, I started eating the stir-fry every Friday night. I added a little lemon and herbs, and I drank water before bed. My blood sugar stayed steady, and I felt more awake. I even taught my sister how to make it. At the health workshop, I showed the recipe and said, ‘This is how I changed my life — one small step at a time.’ The nurses smiled and said, ‘Lulama, you’re doing great.’ I didn’t just feel better — I felt proud. I now know that my body is not just a machine. It listens. And when I listen, I can dance, work, and live well. I still dance umxhentso — but now I do it after I eat the right food. That’s how I found peace.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/0864248a-9a3a-4a1d-8ce6-d9849e6dd8b8_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/0864248a-9a3a-4a1d-8ce6-d9849e6dd8b8_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/0864248a-9a3a-4a1d-8ce6-d9849e6dd8b8_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/0864248a-9a3a-4a1d-8ce6-d9849e6dd8b8_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/0864248a-9a3a-4a1d-8ce6-d9849e6dd8b8_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/0864248a-9a3a-4a1d-8ce6-d9849e6dd8b8_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/0864248a-9a3a-4a1d-8ce6-d9849e6dd8b8_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/0864248a-9a3a-4a1d-8ce6-d9849e6dd8b8_3.mp3}	I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? 	diet	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:34.380552+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Sizwe Mbeki is a 25–34 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Teacher, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: he has none. Religion: Christianity (Presbyterian). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Rugby. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: Anxiety or depression. Diet restrictions: Vegetarian. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 3–4 days. Support network: I live alone or have limited support. Health goals: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . his food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Sizwe Mbeki and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "social_support", "audio_prompts": ["Lulama speaks softly in a moderate pace, her voice slightly tired, breathing deeply as she says, ‘I eat late… after work… I just don’t feel sick.’ Background: distant traffic, faint hum of a refrigerator, a clock ticking. Her voice is mid-range natural, slightly low, with a soft texture, no laughter, calm but uncertain.", "Nandi speaks clearly and gently, in a moderate pace, her voice warm and calm, saying, ‘Lulama, your blood sugar goes up when you eat late.’ Background: soft clinic sounds — a beep from a monitor, distant voices, a gentle hum of a fan. Lulama’s voice is slightly hesitant, with a pause after Nandi finishes, showing surprise and listening.", "Lulama and Sipho laugh softly, Sipho says, ‘We had chips again!’ Lulama says, ‘I feel bad… I didn’t eat the stir-fry.’ Background: light chatter, a distant radio playing isiXhosa music, wind outside. Lulama’s voice is breathless, with a slight rise in pitch, showing guilt and sadness, natural and unposed.", "Lulama speaks confidently, in a slow, steady pace, her voice clear and warm, saying, ‘I now eat the stir-fry every Friday. I feel stronger. I can dance.’ Background: soft applause, a group of nurses smiling, distant music from a speaker. Her voice is rising slightly at the end, showing pride and joy, natural and emotional."], "image_prompts": ["Lulama sits on a plastic chair in a dimly lit kitchen at night, holding a bag of chips, her eyes tired, one hand resting on her stomach, the kitchen wall showing a faded poster of umxhentso dancers, a small bowl of sweets on the table, streetlight casting long shadows, ambient kitchen lighting from a small bulb, realistic and unposed.", "Lulama stands in a white clinic waiting room, wearing a nurse’s uniform, talking to a woman in a blue nurse’s uniform named Nandi, both facing each other in a medium shot, soft morning light through the window, Nandi pointing to a printed meal plan on a clipboard, Lulama’s face showing curiosity and slight hesitation, natural expression, realistic lighting from a side window.", "Lulama and Sipho sit on a wooden bench outside a community centre, both eating chips and drinking lemonade, Lulama’s hands are in her lap, her head slightly down, Sipho laughing, background showing a small street with a sign for ‘Umhlanga Health Day’, warm afternoon light, soft shadows, candid and unposed, Lulama’s posture shows regret and fatigue.", "Lulama stands in front of a group of young nurses in a clinic hall, wearing a simple blue dress and a smile, holding a small plate of grilled chicken and vegetables, one hand gesturing to the group, sunlight streaming through the windows, the background shows a poster of traditional dances, Lulama’s eyes are bright, confident, and open, natural expression, unposed and real."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 59.339460287338646, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some easy, quick-to-make, halal and diabetic-friendly meals using foods commonly found in suburban areas near cities in the Eastern Cape, South Africa?\\",\\n  \\"Are there traditional isiXhosa meal options suitable for someone with type 2 diabetes and hypertension that I can prepare at home?\\",\\n  \\"What sample daily menus fit a nursing professional's busy schedule, that are both halal and support blood sugar control for type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How can I balance my love for traditional dances like umxhentso with my need to increase physical activity to manage type 2 diabetes, given my current activity level is 0 days per week?\\",\\n  \\"Where in suburban Eastern Cape can I find affordable halal groceries or diabetic-friendly products near me?\\",\\n  \\"Which fruits and vegetables in season right now in the Eastern Cape are good choices for a diabetic-friendly, halal diet?\\",\\n  \\"Are there community groups or workshops in the Eastern Cape, especially in isiXhosa communities, that support women with diabetes and hypertension?\\",\\n  \\"What are the best meal prep strategies for a female nurse with a busy daily routine looking to manage her blood sugar and blood pressure?\\",\\n  \\"Are there examples of healthy isiXhosa-inspired snacks that are allowed for type 2 diabetics observing halal dietary restrictions?\\",\\n  \\"How can I involve my family in making meal planning and preparation easier for my diabetes and hypertension management?\\",\\n  \\"Which local clinics or hospitals near my suburb offer nutrition counseling or diabetes education specifically for African Independent Church communities?\\",\\n  \\"Are there online resources or mobile apps in South Africa that provide halal, diabetic-friendly menu ideas tailored to isiXhosa culture?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
