 filled with physical activity, but I never thought about how my diet affects my work. I love snacking on mandazi and chapati, but I've noticed that I get tired easily, especially after eating those foods. I've been having trouble finishing my tasks on time, and today was no different. I was supposed to fix a broken fence, but I didn't have the energy to do it. I thought to myself, "What's wrong with me? Why can't I even fix a simple fence?" \n\nI decided to visit the local healthcare clinic to see if they could help me. The health worker, Sister Akoth, asked me about my diet and exercise routine. I told her about my love for snacking and my physical activity. She explained to me that as a person with Type 2 Diabetes, I need to be mindful of what I eat and how often I exercise. She advised me to snack on healthy options like fruits and nuts, and to limit my intake of sugary foods. I was resistant at first, thinking that it would be too hard to change my habits. But Sister Akoth assured me that it's all about making small changes and being consistent.\n\nI tried to follow Sister Akoth's advice, but it wasn't easy. I found myself reaching for mandazi and chapati out of habit. I had a minor setback when I ate a whole bag of chips while watching TV. I felt frustrated and thought, "I'll never be able to do this." But then I remembered that I have a bigger task coming up - I need to prepare my farm for the upcoming harvest season. I knew I had to get my energy levels up if I wanted to succeed. So, I persisted and kept trying to make healthier choices.\n\nIt's been a few weeks now, and I've noticed a gradual improvement in my energy levels. I've been snacking on fruits and nuts, and I've even started drinking more water. I still have my moments of weakness, but I'm getting better at resisting temptation. I've also started walking more, and I've even incorporated some strength training exercises into my routine. I feel more confident now, and I'm excited to tackle the harvest season. I realized that taking care of my health is not just about me, but also about my family and my livelihood.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7e2d8355-3cef-44e1-98f3-142188316eee_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7e2d8355-3cef-44e1-98f3-142188316eee_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7e2d8355-3cef-44e1-98f3-142188316eee_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/7e2d8355-3cef-44e1-98f3-142188316eee_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7e2d8355-3cef-44e1-98f3-142188316eee_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7e2d8355-3cef-44e1-98f3-142188316eee_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7e2d8355-3cef-44e1-98f3-142188316eee_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/7e2d8355-3cef-44e1-98f3-142188316eee_3.mp3}	I like to snack—what are some healthy options and how often should I have them?	diet	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:19:57.27375+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "Llama-3.3-70B", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Achieng Odhiambo is a 65+ year old, \\nCulture: Luo female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Maize farmer, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Kisumu. Disability: she has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Farming and animal keeping. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare 