     </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">0 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I live alone or have limited support</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">42030472-13a9-45cb-9220-422dfc76cc2c</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Which everyday foods can I eat that won&#x27;t make my blood sugar spike?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Let me tell you how my normal day goes, eh? Every morning, I wake up just before sunrise, say my prayers, and get ready for the market. Sometimes my breakfast is just a small plate of tuwo shinkafa and left-over miyan kuka from last night—quick and filling, sha. By the time I finish sweeping my small flat, sorting out my fabrics, and rushing to the stall, I’m already tired before customers even start coming. The other day, I wanted to rearrange my stock shelves and make the table neat—simple task, bah? But by halfway, my head was spinning, my hands shaky, and I had to sit down. I just told myself, ‘Ah, maybe it’s the sun or small tiredness, it will pass.’ But deep down, I wondered, 'Is it not just me being lazy?' \n\nAfter that, I dragged myself to the clinic since my medication was almost finished. Nurse Halima saw me looking drained, and instead of shouting, she sat beside me. She asked, ‘Chiamaka, do you move your body at all when you’re not trading?’ I laughed, ‘Me, gym? Where do I get time for running around after work, eh?’ She smiled and shook her head, ‘You don’t need a gym, my sister. Just start with what you have—walk around your room, dance while you sweep, stretch your arms after every two customers. Even prayers add movement. Try and do these small things, then next week see if you feel better. Body like machine—it needs to move, even if it’s little.’ Her advice sounded easy but strange. Still, it’s from nurse, so maybe I should try.\n\nThe next morning, I started by stretching my arms before I got out of bed. Uhm—honestly, that one was not easy; everything stiff, as if my joints refused. I tried walking up and down my small passage after sweeping. My legs screamed, 'Why now?' and my mind fought, wanting to just rest before work started. But then I remembered—next week, I have to re-stock all my fabrics at Dawanu market, a big job needing strength. I sighed and told myself, ‘Chiamaka, even if it’s small, just do it, so you don’t disgrace yourself for market front.’ I counted my steps as motivation, even though sweat soaked my hijab under the hot room.\n\nKai, see as days passed, eh! My body slowly got used to the stretching and short walks. I started feeling less tired at the stall and, wallahi, didn’t rush to sit down so quickly. When Dawanu Market day finally came, I lifted my fabric bales with more ease and didn’t beg for help, even from the big men. It shocked me, y’know? Maybe small movement is not just wahala. Now, I think I could even try more—maybe some dancing in the kitchen while waiting for my moi moi to finish. Step by step, my confidence came back. I’m not there fully, but at least I know my health does small magic if I just listen and try every day.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/42030472-13a9-45cb-9220-422dfc76cc2c_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/42030472-13a9-45cb-9220-422dfc76cc2c_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/42030472-13a9-45cb-9220-422dfc76cc2c_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/42030472-13a9-45cb-9220-422dfc76cc2c_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/42030472-13a9-45cb-9220-422dfc76cc2c_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/42030472-13a9-45cb-9220-422dfc76cc2c_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/42030472-13a9-45cb-9220-422dfc76cc2c_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/42030472-13a9-45cb-9220-422dfc76cc2c_3.mp3}	I don't have a gym—what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?	exercise	female	consistency	2026-03-23 13:29:33.836188+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Chinonso Okafor is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Hausa male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Trader, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Kano. Disability: he has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional wrestling. Activiti