t polite. Occasional clinic sounds: muffled footsteps, faint fan whir, distant child crying, and soft paper rustling as Halima explains simple physical activities. Both voices clear, with pauses as Chiamaka processes, ‘You mean just walking around the house helps?’", "Home interior, morning echoes. Chiamaka Okonkwo’s slightly breathless, clear voice counts steps aloud, then mutters encouragement to herself in English, tempo irregular, pitch rising on ‘Why now?’ Her breathing is audible from exertion as sandals scuffle on tile floor. There is background noise of a broom brush, metal bucket clinking, and a distant radio announcing market prices. She ends phrase with a tired sigh and a determined, deliberate tone.", "Outdoor, market setting; Chiamaka Okonkwo’s voice is vibrant, with laughter and a confident lilt. Speech is clear and lively, occasionally greeting sellers in the background with Hausa phrases. Footsteps on gravel, street sounds—bicycle bells, customers bartering—blend in, and her breathing is steady, voice swelling with proud amusement after lifting her wares. The audio closes with her exclaiming, ‘I didn’t beg for help, ah!’ as laughter and market banter fade."], "image_prompts": ["Candid medium shot: Chiamaka Okonkwo, a young Hausa woman in her early 20s, is sitting on a wooden stool in her small, sparsely furnished flat in urban Kano, surrounded by stacks of folded fabric. She has a tired posture, one hand on her head, a half-eaten plate of tuwo shinkafa and miyan kuka on a low table. Dust particles hang in the morning sunlight filtering through a window with sheer curtains. Facial expression shows mild frustration and fatigue, eyes gazing toward the open market bag as she reconsiders standing up.", "There is one other person: Female health worker Nurse Halima. Wide shot: Nurse Halima, a middle-aged Hausa woman in a crisp white and blue nurse’s uniform, sits beside Chiamaka Okonkwo on a clinic wooden bench, sunlight spilling through hospital louvre windows. Halima leans forward with gentle concern, smiling as she gestures calmly, while Chiamaka slouches, arms folded, lips pursed skeptically, watching the nurse’s hand. Hospital posters are on pale green walls behind them, and an old weighing scale sits in the background.", "Candid medium shot: Chiamaka Okonkwo stands barefoot in her simple passageway at home. She is stretching her arms above her head, eyes partly closed, lips pressed in determination. The tile floor glows in the early light. A small wall clock, broom, and colorful Hijab hang by the door. Her fabric samples are stacked neatly by the wall, and she is mid-motion, sweat visible on her brow, mouth set in slight discomfort as she forces a reluctant smile.", "Candid wide shot: Chiamaka Okonkwo stands at Dawanu grains market, sunlight high overhead. She lifts a bundle of brightly colored fabrics onto a low wooden counter with both hands, posture upright, back straight, and face showing a proud, surprised smile. Traders and customers bustle in the blurred background among fabric and grain stalls. Her hijab is bright and tidy, and her stance radiates newfound confidence."], "language_code": "en-NG", "avg_time_taken": 1.9408850491046905, "task_parent_id": "2c8bdf11-e2a7-4665-aefc-acd942149696", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some common Hausa dishes available in Kano city that are suitable for someone with type 2 diabetes and hypertension?\\",\\n  \\"How can a female trader in urban Kano easily find affordable, healthy foods that fit kosher dietary restrictions?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any community support groups or diabetes education programs in Kano for young adults living alone with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find local markets in Kano that offer fresh produce and whole grains ideal for blood sugar control?\\",\\n  \\"What traditional herbal remedies are popular among Hausa women in Kano for managing high blood pressure or diabetes, and are they safe to use alongside prescribed medication?\\",\\n  \\"Which Nigerian foods and snacks commonly sold in urban markets wonâ€™t cause blood sugar spikes, especially for people who smoke and drink alcohol weekly?\\",\\n  \\"What simple, culturally accepted physical activities can someone in their early 20s, living in Kano, incorporate at home if they have zero days of physical activity presently?\\",\\n  \\"How can a working adult in Kano balance a busy trading schedule with meal planning for diabetes, especially when living alone?\\",\\n  \\"Are there local storytelling or social groups in Kano where I could meet others with similar health challenges for support?\\",\\n  \\"How does frequent tobacco use impact diabetes and hypertension management for urban Hausa women in Northern Nigeria?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any local Hausa recipes for blood pressure-friendly meals that adhere to kosher guidelines?\\",\\n  \\"Which hospitals or clinics in urban Kano provide the best regular care for young women with both type 2 diabetes and hypertension?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "Relevant persona details: Chiamaka Okonkwo is a young adult Hausa woman, working as a trader in urban Kano. She lives alone and has both type 2 diabetes and hypertension. Her routine is busy, she has no physical activity days, and she wonders how to stay active without a gym. She smokes but does not drink alcohol and eats mostly local foods. She regularly visits a clinic and is curious about which everyday foods keep blood sugar stable, but she isn’t sure how to balance her health and work tasks.", "selected_profile_info": [null, "culture", "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "s