      <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5–7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Vegan</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">6a45fc62-02de-4989-8767-484491582397</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Sometimes I feel tired or discouraged—how can I stay motivated, and how does exercise help my blood sugar?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Bongiwe Mntungwa and you know, I always wake up before dawn, make a quick cup of tea, and rush to school with nothing but a white bread sandwich in my bag. Sometimes I eat fried chips from the street vendor or a cheap magwinya during break, because who has time, neh? I was feeling a bit dizzy, but I decided I must mark my class tests before the bell rings. But eish, my hands shook so much I scribbled by mistake and had to redo everything. Don't you hate when you want to work hard but your own body just doesn't listen?\n\nLater that day, Sister Zinzi, the nurse from the clinic, caught me while I was leaving the staff room. She smiled and greeted, 'Bongiwe, sisi, can we talk quick-quick about your sugar?' At first, I felt tired and did not want another lecture. But she didn't judge me, just asked, 'What do you usually eat at school?' When I answered, she shook her head gently and told me, 'Next time, try umngqusho or umfino—samp and beans, or maize meal with spinach. Bring those from home in a lunchbox, you’ll see. They keep your energy and won’t spike your sugar. And if you feel tired, take a short walk.’ Simple advice, but I still was not sure it would really help.\n\nThe next morning, I packed umfino and chakalaka for lunch like she said, but I missed my magwinya already—these food changes feel strange, qha! After my first class, my hands reached for the old sugar doughnut in my drawer, then I remembered Sister Zinzi’s words. Heh, this healthy routine is difficult, but I have to try. The headmaster gave me news: I must lead the school play next week, so I need my strength. I didn't want to let anyone see me failing again.\n\nDay by day, as I kept bringing my home-packed foods and snuck in a few dizzying dance steps during breaks, things got better. I had more energy to practise umxhentso with the girls for the big Friday. Even when I wanted to give up, I thought of all the children counting on me. By opening my lunchbox and feeling strong, I finished marking test scripts and even got compliments from the headmaster. Now, when Sister Zinzi greets me, I laugh and say, ‘Sisi, your umfino advice is working. Maybe I can change, step by step.’	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6a45fc62-02de-4989-8767-484491582397_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6a45fc62-02de-4989-8767-484491582397_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6a45fc62-02de-4989-8767-484491582397_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/6a45fc62-02de-4989-8767-484491582397_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6a45fc62-02de-4989-8767-484491582397_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6a45fc62-02de-4989-8767-484491582397_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6a45fc62-02de-4989-8767-484491582397_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/6a45fc62-02de-4989-8767-484491582397_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:23.02548+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Lwazi Nkosi is a 18–24 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Entrepreneur, lives in a Suburban (near city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: he has none. Religion: Agnosticism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: Anxiety or depression. Diet restrictions: Vegetarian. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 5–7 days. Support network: Healthcare provider or counsellor. Health goals: Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?. his food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Lwazi Nkosi and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "resistance", "audio_prompts": ["Scene: Bongiwe Mntungwa alone in her classroom staff room, mid-morning. Bongiwe sighs loudly, the scratch of pen on paper and the faint sound of children playing echo in the corridor. Her stomach growls, she mutters, 'Yho, ndiyadinwa,' as she fumbles with papers, voice weary and slightly out of breath. The microwave hums in the background with the rattle of cups. Footsteps pass by outside the door. Bongiwe’s breathing is restless and a bit shallow.", "Other person X: Sister Zinzi, female nurse, stands close to Bongiwe in the hospital corridor. Bongiwe’s voice is hesitant, slightly defensive but polite, 'Eish, sisi, I just take whatever is quick.' Sister Zinzi’s tone is gentle but firm, with a soft IsiXhosa accent. She speaks slowly and clearly, 'Bongiwe, umfino and umngqusho—those will help you. Please try, you’ll feel the difference.' Bongiwe grunts in uncertainty, 'Mhm… I can try.' Office phones ring in the background, with soft hospital footsteps, creating a supportive but busy atmosphere.", "Medium scene: Bongiwe Mntungwa sighs as she opens her lunchbox, hands thumping softly on the table. She mutters quietly to herself, ‘Yhu, I wish I had my magwinya today.’ Her chair squeaks as she shifts uncertainly. In the background, muffled school announcements and the chatter of children filter in from the window, mixed with her slow, wondering breathing, each exhale carrying a hint of doubt and determination.", "Wide audio: Bongiwe’s voice is loud and full of laughter, 'Hayi, nani makhaya, let's keep going!' as she leads children’s singing and rhythmic clapping in the courtyard. The stomp of dancing feet, children’s shouts, and the occasional whistle from another teacher fill the space. Bongiwe’s words are confident and bright, her breathing steady despite exertion. Birds chirp and distant city traffic hums, the mood joyful and bustling with school energy."], "image_prompts": ["Medium shot of Bongiwe Mntungwa, a Xhosa woman in her late 30s, sitting at a busy classroom desk in a modest, urban Eastern Cape primary school staff room, wearing a simple blouse and long skirt. Bongiwe is frowning, slouched over stacks of marked student test papers, holding her head in one hand and a pen in the other, forehead creased in frustration. On the desk is a plastic lunchbox with a white bread sandwich and a polystyrene cup of tea, with urban life seen through the dust-streaked window. Harsh mid-morning sunlight filters in, casting detailed shadows.", "One person in scene: Sister Zinzi, a friendly Xhosa female clinic nurse, stands beside Bongiwe Mntungwa in a tiled hospital corridor with posters about diabetes on the wall. Wide shot showing Bongiwe, wearing her work clothes and slinging a worn handbag, pausing and listening with her body leaning away, uncertain, while Sister Zinzi gently points at Bongiwe’s lunch bag and gestures supportively. Both are captured in candid, unposed motion. Fluorescent overhead lights give a slightly cool hue with natural city street noise through an open window.", "Medium shot of Bongiwe Mntungwa alone, sitting on a basic classroom chair by her desk in a cluttered educator’s office at school, looking down at a colourful lunchbox with umfino and chakalaka inside, her hands resting uncertainly on the lid. Papers and textbooks are scattered on the table. Bongiwe glances sideways at a brown paper bag with a magwinya peeping out from under her desk, her body torn between choices. Early morning sunlight spills across the floor, revealing schoolbags and chalkdust.", "Wide shot: Bongiwe Mntungwa leads a small group of pupils dancing umxhentso in the sunny school courtyard paved with cracked cement. Bongiwe’s posture is upright, her smile genuine as she raises her arms and steps with the girls; confidence and energy show in her movements. A few teachers watch from the background, admiring. Children are laughing, and Bongiwe’s lunchbox with spinach traces visible in her hand. The afternoon light makes sharp, warm shadows, highlighting textures in school uniforms and bright skirts."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 1.9909880101680755, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are some popular traditional Xhosa dishes that fit into a Kosher and diabetes-friendly diet in Eastern Cape urban areas?\\",\\n  \\"How can I incorporate umxhentso and intlombe traditional dancing safely into my weekly exercise routine to help manage type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Where are the best healthcare clinics in Eastern Cape cities for diabetes management support tailored to women in their 30s and 40s?\\",\\n  \\"Are there local support groups in Eastern Cape urban areas for Xhosa-speaking Christian women with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What tips do Eastern Cape-based teachers with diabetes use to manage energy and motivation during busy workdays?\\",\\n  \\"How does regular physical activity like traditional dancing improve blood sugar levels for women who use both insulin 