    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">bbb786e2-dc07-4797-967f-45c1bc6c5cb6</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, I often find myself snacking late at night after studying or hanging out with my family. I used to think, 'It's just a little food, it won't matter much.' But one night, after eating some leftover pap and fried chicken at 10 PM, I woke up feeling dizzy and tired. I thought, 'Am I just tired from late nights, or is it something else?' It felt like my blood sugar was acting up, but I didn't really connect the dots. I tried to push through schoolwork and family plans, but I was drained. How was I supposed to focus on my studies and help with family celebrations if my body was like this?"\n\nI decided to go to the local clinic for some help, where the sister explained things clearly. She said eating late at night can cause your blood sugar to spike because your body isn't as active to use the energy. She told me that instead of big late meals, I could try lighter snacks like a small fruit or some nuts, and to avoid heavy foods close to bedtime. She also reminded me to keep up my insulin on time and encouraged me to walk more—like brisk walks around the neighborhood, about 30 minutes, five days a week. It sounded doable but also different from my usual routine. I felt a bit unsure but wanted to try it for my health and also to feel better at school and home."\n\nThe next few days, I tried to eat smaller meals earlier in the evening and go for walks after class. But sometimes, I got hungry late and reached for sweets or leftover food again because it was easy and comforting after a long day. One evening, I felt a bit shaky and realized my blood sugar had gone high. I felt frustrated with myself and wondered if I could keep this up. But then I remembered the big family gathering coming up next month where I wanted to enjoy the food and have energy to dance and laugh with everyone. That gave me reason to keep trying, even if I stumbled a little now and then."\n\nOver time, I started to notice things changing. When I kept up the walks and planned my meals better, I felt more awake during class and less tired at night. At the family gathering, I could join in the dancing and chat without feeling sick or weak. I realized that small changes like walking with my cousins or swapping late fried snacks for fruit helped me a lot. Now, I feel more confident about managing my diabetes and even look forward to finding new ways to keep fit and enjoy my family's celebrations without worry. Isn't it amazing how looking after yourself can make all the difference?"	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/bbb786e2-dc07-4797-967f-45c1bc6c5cb6_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/bbb786e2-dc07-4797-967f-45c1bc6c5cb6_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/bbb786e2-dc07-4797-967f-45c1bc6c5cb6_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/bbb786e2-dc07-4797-967f-45c1bc6c5cb6_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/bbb786e2-dc07-4797-967f-45c1bc6c5cb6_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/bbb786e2-dc07-4797-967f-45c1bc6c5cb6_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/bbb786e2-dc07-4797-967f-45c1bc6c5cb6_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/bbb786e2-dc07-4797-967f-45c1bc6c5cb6_3.mp3}	I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? 	diet	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:25.248228+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Zanele Mabhena is a 25–34 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Teacher, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: she has none. Religion: Christianity (Methodist). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: Vegan. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: Yes. Physical activity: 5–7 days. Support network: Family (parents, siblings, children). Health goals: How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?. her food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Zanele Mabhena and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "social_support", "audio_prompts": ["Sounds of quiet evening in Lusanda Mbewu's urban home kitchen: soft clinking of cutlery on plates, distant murmur of voices from the street outside. Lusanda's voice is low and tired as she says, \\"I feel dizzy sometimes after eating late... maybe it's the food?\\" Her breathing is slow and heavy, revealing fatigue and mild frustration. Background ambient noise includes faint traffic and a ticking clock.", "In a bright clinic room, Lusanda Mbewu listens as a friendly female nurse explains diabetes care. The nurse's voice is warm and clear, steady with gentle pacing and reassuring tone. Lusanda replies with short pauses, showing thoughtfulness and slight uncertainty. Background sound includes quiet shuffling of paper and distant corridor noise, creating a calm clinical atmosphere.", "Outside on an urban sidewalk, Lusanda Mbewu's footsteps tap steadily on pavement, interspersed with distant city sounds: cars passing, birds chirping, voices of neighbors chatting in isiXhosa nearby. Lusanda's breathing is moderate but a bit breathless as she says softly, \\"I'm trying to walk more, even if it's hard.\\" Her voice shows determination mixed with mild struggle.", "At a lively family gathering in a backyard, Lusanda Mbewu laughs softly while dancing with a younger sibling. Background includes light chatter of family speaking isiXhosa, occasional clinking of plates, and soft music playing. Lusanda's breath is even, tone warm and joyful as she says, \\"Now I can join in and feel good.\\" Sounds of children playing gently underscore a happy, supportive atmosphere."], "image_prompts": ["Medium shot of Lusanda Mbewu, a young IsiXhosa female student sitting at a small kitchen table in an urban Eastern Cape home, late evening lighting casting soft shadows. She looks tired and leans her head on one hand, with a plate of pap and fried chicken in front of her. The kitchen has modest, everyday utensils and a window showing city lights outside. Lusanda's posture shows fatigue and mild frustration.", "Medium shot of Lusanda Mbewu speaking with a female clinic nurse in a brightly lit local healthcare clinic room. The nurse holds an informational pamphlet about diabetes and gestures kindly as Lusanda listens attentively with slight hesitation. Medical posters and a small plant decorate the room. Lusanda sits upright but slightly unsure, her hands resting on a simple chair armrest.", "Wide shot of Lusanda Mbewu walking briskly on a paved sidewalk in an urban Eastern Cape neighborhood during late afternoon. She wears casual clothes, carrying a backpack, and looks thoughtful with slightly furrowed brows, focusing on her steps. Colorful township houses and a few pedestrians pass by. The scene captures her effort to change habits amid a lively city environment.", "Medium shot of Lusanda Mbewu smiling warmly while dancing with a younger sibling at a family gathering in a simple backyard with colorful decorations. Evening sunlight filters through trees, highlighting their joyful faces. Lusanda wears a bright, casual outfit and moves freely, her posture relaxed and confident. The background shows family members chatting and enjoying food on picnic tables."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 5.38300901055336, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are the best exercise routines for a young adult woman with type 2 diabetes living in an urban area in Eastern Cape, South Africa?\\",\\n  \\"Are there community fitness programs or youth sports groups in Eastern Cape that are popular among IsiXhosa students aged 18–24?\\",\\n  \\"What healthy recipes using locally available foods in Eastern Cape are suitable for managing type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How can I balance family gatherings and celebrations with maintaining my blood sugar levels as a student in South Africa?\\",\\n  \\"What culturally relevant ways do IsiXhosa families in urban areas support relatives with diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Is it common for students in Eastern Cape to use local healthcare clinics for diabetes management, and what should I expect during a typical visit?\\",\\n  \\"What diabetes support groups are available for young women in Eastern Cape who are atheists and want a non-religious space?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any mobile health apps in South Africa tailored for tracking diabetes and physical activity for students?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find affordable diabetes medication such as insulin or oral medication in urban areas of Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"What are the Eastern Cape-specific guidelines on physical activity for young women with diabetes who don’t have any other health conditions?\\",\\n  \\"How can I integrate socializing with friends into my exercise routine in an urban South African setting?\\",\\n  \\"What traditional IsiXhosa foods should I avoid or include to help control type 2 diabetes while still enjoying family events?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "Lusanda Mbewu is a young IsiXhosa student aged 18 to 24 living in an urban area in the Eastern Cape. She has type 2 diabetes and access to a local healthcare clinic regularly. Her daily routine involves attending classes and socializing with family and friends. Lusanda sometimes eats late at night but has a question about how this affects her blood sugar and what she can do to manage it better. She has some uncertainty about exercise duration and frequency for improving her health and takes insulin and oral medication but is still learning how to best balance her lifestyle to stay well.", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
