part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I live alone or have limited support</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1681fb36-033f-41dc-ae50-9d65a250adda</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Can I just walk for exercise, or should I do other types too?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Eish, life, neh? I’m Bongiwe Mabuza, and I’ve been a teacher for… well, too many years to count now! Retired, but still helping out at the school with marking papers, you see. It’s good to keep busy. I like my routine. Strong tea in the morning, always with a biscuit – a nice sweet one. Lunch is usually umngqusho, because it reminds me of my mother. And a cigarette with it, of course. Don’t tell anyone, but I still enjoy a little tot of umqombothi with friends on weekends. Yesterday, the headmistress asked me to check all the Grade 7 exam papers for maths. Simple enough, I thought. But after a few papers, my eyes… they just blurred. I couldn’t see the numbers properly. Had to ask my neighbour’s daughter to help finish. Shameful, really. I thought my eyes were just getting old, but maybe… maybe it's something else?\n\nOld Man Ndlovu, the clinic nurse, he’s a good man. I went to see him about my blurry vision, and he checked everything. Blood pressure, sugar levels… the works. Turns out, my sugar was high. Very high. He said it’s the diabetes, and it's affecting my eyes. 'Bongiwe,' he said, 'you need to watch what you eat, and you *need* to stop smoking. It’s making everything worse.' I told him, 'But I enjoy my tea and biscuit! And the cigarette calms my nerves!' He smiled, a kind smile. 'I understand, Mama Bongiwe. But small changes. Start with cutting down on sugar in your tea. Walk for 30 minutes every day. And try to reduce the cigarettes, even just one less a day. Think of your eyes, and those Grade 7 papers you need to mark!' It sounded… doable. But still, it felt like a lot to change.\n\nThis walking, it’s harder than it looks! I tried to walk to the shop yesterday, but my feet started aching after just ten minutes. And the biscuit… oh, the biscuit! I tried drinking my tea without it, but it felt… empty. I ended up having one anyway. Just one, I told myself. Then I remembered those exam papers, and the headmistress relying on me. I can't let her down. So, I forced myself to walk a little further today, even though my feet complained. I walked to Mrs. Dlamini’s house and back, and we chatted for a while. It was nice. I even managed to drink my tea with just a little sugar. It wasn’t the same, but it wasn’t terrible. I still had a cigarette, though. Baby steps, I suppose. It's a struggle, this changing habits, but I need to be able to see those numbers!\n\nIt’s been a week now, and things are… better. I’m walking for twenty minutes every day, and I’ve swapped the sweet biscuits for a small handful of nuts. I still have my tea, but with just half a teaspoon of sugar. And I’ve cut down to five cigarettes a day, instead of ten. My eyes aren’t blurry all the time anymore. Amazing, really. Today, I marked all the Grade 7 exam papers myself, without needing any help! I even double-checked them. I felt… proud. Old Man Ndlovu was pleased when I told him. He said I was doing well, and to keep it up. I’m starting to think maybe he’s right. Maybe taking care of myself isn’t so bad after all. Perhaps I'll even join that gospel choir at the church. It would be good to sing again, and maybe… maybe I’ll learn some new healthy recipes too. Who knows what else I can achieve if I just take things one step at a time?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/1681fb36-033f-41dc-ae50-9d65a250adda_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/1681fb36-033f-41dc-ae50-9d65a250adda_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/1681fb36-033f-41dc-ae50-9d65a250adda_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/1681fb36-033f-41dc-ae50-9d65a250adda_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/1681fb36-033f-41dc-ae50-9d65a250adda_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/1681fb36-033f-41dc-ae50-9d65a250adda_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/1681fb36-033f-41dc-