, with a slightly gravelly tone. She’s describing her morning routine, the sound of a kettle whistling in the background. A gentle sigh accompanies her mention of the exam papers. The pacing is slow and reflective. A faint rustling sound suggests she’s handling papers. The overall ambiance is quiet and domestic, with the distant sound of children playing.", "Old Man Ndlovu, a male nurse, speaking in a calm and reassuring tone in South African English. He’s explaining the importance of diet and exercise to Bongiwe Mabuza, 68, who is responding with hesitant agreement. Bongiwe’s voice is soft and questioning, with a slight tremor. The audio is recorded in a clinic setting, with faint background chatter and the occasional beep of medical equipment. The positioning is Bongiwe close to the microphone, Old Man Ndlovu slightly further back.", "Bongiwe Mabuza, 68, breathing slightly heavily as she describes her walk. Her voice is strained but determined. The sound of her footsteps on a gravel path is audible. She pauses briefly to catch her breath. The audio also includes the sound of birds chirping and distant voices. There is a slight crackle to her voice, conveying the effort of walking.", "Bongiwe Mabuza, 68, speaking in a clear and confident tone in South African English. She’s describing her success in marking the exam papers. Her voice is filled with pride and relief. The sound of a pen scratching on paper is audible in the background. The audio is recorded in a quiet classroom setting, with a sense of accomplishment and peace. Her voice shows a slight increase in volume and energy."], "image_prompts": ["Medium shot of Bongiwe Mabuza, a 68-year-old woman, sitting at a wooden kitchen table in a modestly furnished home in an Eastern Cape suburban setting. She's holding a teacup and a biscuit, looking slightly wistful. Soft, diffused natural light coming from a window. The table is cluttered with papers. Bongiwe’s posture is slightly slumped, conveying a sense of fatigue. A half-smoked cigarette sits in an ashtray nearby.", "Medium shot of Bongiwe Mabuza, 68, sitting across from Old Man Ndlovu, a clinic nurse, in a brightly lit but simply furnished clinic room in the Eastern Cape. Bongiwe is looking at him with a slightly skeptical expression. Old Man Ndlovu is smiling gently and holding a chart. The room is clean and functional, with posters about healthy eating on the walls. Bongiwe is holding her hands together in her lap.", "Wide shot of Bongiwe Mabuza, 68, walking slowly down a dusty suburban street in the Eastern Cape, slightly hunched over. She's wearing comfortable walking shoes and a colourful headscarf. In the background, Mrs. Dlamini is waving to her from her garden. The light is warm and golden. Bongiwe’s posture shows effort and determination.", "Medium shot of Bongiwe Mabuza, 68, sitting at a desk in a school classroom, diligently marking exam papers. She's wearing reading glasses and looks focused and content. Sunlight streams through the window, illuminating the papers. She has a small bowl of nuts beside her and a half-empty teacup. Bongiwe's posture is upright and confident."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 84.23266913340642, "task_parent_id": "ceaf2767-62db-4fb9-b3e2-c7579c1ece3f", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What types of exercise are best for a 65+ year old IsiXhosa woman with type 2 diabetes living in a suburban area of Eastern Cape, South Africa, besides walking?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any local gospel music groups or events in Eastern Cape where seniors can participate or perform?\\",\\n  \\"What gluten-free traditional IsiXhosa dishes are suitable for someone managing type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How can I manage anxiety or depression along with diabetes as a Christian Lutheran in South Africa?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find local healthcare clinics in suburban regions near East London or Mthatha that offer diabetes support for seniors?\\",\\n  \\"What are the best herbal or traditional medicines for diabetes recommended in IsiXhosa culture, and are they safe with insulin and Metformin?\\",\\n  \\"Are there support groups or church-based communities in the Eastern Cape for older women living alone with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What spiritual activities or Christian practices in the Lutheran tradition help elders manage health challenges like diabetes and anxiety?\\",\\n  \\"How does rare tobacco smoking impact diabetes management for older women, and are there cessation resources in Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"What are the recommended gluten-free snacks available at supermarkets in Eastern Cape suburbs for diabetic patients?\\",\\n  \\"Is it safe to perform physical activities 5 to 7 days a week for a senior with type 2 diabetes, or should I adjust my exercise routine?\\",\\n  \\"Are there local programs or services to support elderly teachers living alone in suburban South Africa, especially those with health conditions?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "culture", "t2d_status", "tobacco", "alcohol", "physical_activity", "diet_restrictions", "support_network", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "health_goals", "output_format", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
9d4c6414-793e-41ba-aca2-13039715af8d	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b816-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Eastern Cape</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Methodist)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Luzuko Mthetho</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Male</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">35–44</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Urban (city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">School teacher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Storytelling (iintsomi) and praise poetry (izibongo)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Learning new skills or attending classes or workshops</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">3–4 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No Restrictions</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I live alone or have limited support</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">9d4c6414-793e-41ba-aca2-13039715af8d</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	My name is Luzuko Mthetho, and I teach history at a school in East London. It’s a tough job, lots of kids, lots of paperwork – I’m always running around. Most days, I grab a quick pap and beans for breakfast, maybe a loaf of bread with a bit of cheese if I’m feeling fancy. Lunch is usually something from a spaza shop – maybe a wors or a samoosa. Dinner… well, dinner is often whatever’s easiest, sometimes just a packet of maize meal with a little gravy. I don't really think about my blood sugar much, you know? Just get through the day. Yesterday, I had to prepare a presentation on the Battle of Isandlwana for the grade 10s, and I was so busy rushing around getting everything ready that I forgot to eat properly. I felt lightheaded halfway through, and the whole thing just kind of… fizzled out. The kids were disappointed, and honestly, I was too. It wasn’t my best work.\n\nToday, I went to the clinic for my check-up. Nurse Zola was really kind. She looked at my blood pressure and then at my sugar levels – they were a bit high, she said. She explained that when I skip meals or eat sugary things, my body struggles to handle the sugar properly, especially with the insulin I take. She said it’s like my body is saying, ‘Hey, slow down! I need a break!’ She showed me a simple chart with what to eat – lots of vegetables, whole grains, and less meat. She told me to try and have a small snack, like some fruit or nuts, before the afternoon classes. It sounded complicated, but she made it seem really manageable. She also told me to check my sugar levels more often, just to see how I’m doing. It felt a bit strange, but also… helpful. It’s good to know there’s someone paying attention, and that it’s not just me struggling alone.\n\nI tried to follow her advice, honestly I did. I made sure to have a small bowl of porridge with some fruit for breakfast, and I packed a handful of peanuts for a snack during my break. But it was hard, you know? I’m used to grabbing a quick biscuit or a sugary drink when I’m feeling peckish. And then, after school, I was exhausted, and the thought of cooking a proper meal felt overwhelming. I ended up having a packet of biscuits with a cup of tea – and my sugar shot right up! I felt a bit shaky afterwards, and it made me realize just how much of a habit it is. It wasn’t a big deal, just a little slip-up, but it made me feel a bit discouraged. I almost gave up, thinking, ‘What’s the point?’\n\nBut then I remembered Mr. Dlamini, the school principal, told me about the school sports day next month. He needs volunteers to help with the activities, and I really want to be involved. I want to show my kids that even though I’m a teacher, I can still be active and healthy. So, I’ve started small – I’m walking to school instead of taking the bus whenever I can, and I’m trying to eat a bit healthier each day. My sugar levels are still a bit wonky sometimes, but they’re getting better. I feel more energetic, and I’m actually starting to enjoy the process of taking care of myself. It’s not about being perfect, it’s about making small changes that add up over time. And honestly, that feels like a real victory.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9d4c6414-793e-41ba-aca2-13039715af8d_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9d4c6414-793e-41ba-aca2-13039715af8d_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9d4c6414-793e-41ba-aca2-13039715af8d_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9d4c6414-793e-41ba-aca2-13039715af8d_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9d4c6414-793e-41ba-aca2-13039715af8d_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9d4c6414-793e-41ba-aca2-13039715af8d_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9d4c6414-793e-41ba-aca2-13039715af8d_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9d4c6414-793e-41ba-aca2-13039715af8d_3.mp3}	I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? 	diet	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:32.227613+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gemma-3-4b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Lwando Mlambo is a 35–44 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Teacher, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: he has none. Religion: Atheism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: No Restrictions. Smoking: No. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 3–4 days. Support network: Community or religious group. Health goals: What kinds of exercises are safe and helpful for someone with diabetes?. his food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Lwando Mlambo and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "social_support", "audio_prompts": ["Sound of a bustling classroom in East London – children’s voices, teacher’s instructions, the rustle of papers. Luzuko Mthetho’s voice, slightly strained, speaks with a low, conversational tone: ‘It’s just… so much work, you know? Trying to keep up with everything.’ Background sound of a clock ticking faintly.", "Sound of a clinic – gentle background music, the murmur of conversations, the beeping of medical equipment. Nurse Zola’s voice, clear and reassuring, explains something to Luzuko Mthetho. Luzuko responds with a slightly hesitant tone, ‘So, if I skip a meal… it gets worse?’ A brief pause, followed by the sound of a pen writing on paper.", "Sound of Luzuko Mthetho sighing heavily, followed by the crinkling of a biscuit packet. He speaks with a low, frustrated tone: ‘It’s just so easy to slip up, isn’t it? I tell myself, ‘Just one biscuit…’ Then suddenly, the whole day feels different.’ A faint, distant sound of children’s laughter.", "Sound of Luzuko Mthetho’s footsteps on pavement, accompanied by upbeat, hopeful music. He speaks with a slightly more energetic tone: ‘I’m actually looking forward to helping out at the sports day. It’s a good reminder to keep moving.’ A faint sound of birds chirping in the background – a sense of optimism and a brighter future."], "image_prompts": ["Luzuko Mthetho, a 38-year-old man, stands in a cluttered classroom in East London, surrounded by students. He is visibly frustrated, holding a crumpled presentation, with a slightly bewildered expression. The classroom is brightly lit, with children's artwork on the walls, creating a sense of controlled chaos.", "Nurse Zola, a middle-aged woman with a warm smile, is explaining something to Luzuko Mthetho at a clinic desk in East London. Luzuko is listening attentively, with a thoughtful expression, as Nurse Zola points to a chart with food recommendations. The clinic is clean and well-lit, with a friendly atmosphere.", "Luzuko Mthetho sits alone at a table in a spaza shop in East London, looking disappointed and holding a packet of biscuits. The spaza shop is bustling with activity, with shelves stocked with various goods. The lighting is warm and slightly hazy.", "Luzuko Mthetho, a 38-year-old man, is walking purposefully towards the school gates in East London, carrying a clipboard and a small backpack. He has a determined expression on his face, and the sun is shining brightly. The scene is captured with a sense of optimism and forward momentum."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 9.697208734659048, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"How can a vegan diet be adapted for someone with type 2 diabetes living in an urban area of the Eastern Cape in South Africa, especially with easy access to healthy foods?\\",\\n  \\"What are some culturally relevant isiXhosa vegan dishes that are suitable for managing blood sugar levels in adults with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any local support groups or educational workshops in Eastern Cape cities for men with diabetes who live alone and have limited support networks?\\",\\n  \\"How does late-night eating impact blood sugar management for adults using insulin and oral medication, and what are healthy late-night isiXhosa snacks or beverages?\\",\\n  \\"What are common challenges faced by school teachers in South African urban areas in maintaining regular physical activity when managing type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find Methodist health or wellness resources in the Eastern Cape that address diabetes self-management and emotional wellbeing?\\",\\n  \\"How does weekly tobacco use and daily alcohol consumption influence blood sugar control for middle-aged men with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What local Eastern Cape healthcare facilities offer diabetes education specifically tailored for urban isiXhosa-speaking adults?\\",\\n  \\"What are best practices for integrating izibongo (praise poetry) and iintsomi (storytelling) into diabetes awareness activities for adults in is