ng to keep up with things. Most days, I just grab whatever’s quick to eat, usually things without milk because of my stomach. I only move around a bit, maybe walking to the taxi rank or to the local shop, nothing serious. I reckon that’s enough, right? Last week, I had to help set up a table for my community group meeting, thinking I could just manage with my usual pace, but I found myself so breathless and tired I nearly had to sit down. That really shook me – how could I mess up a simple task like that?"\n\nThen I went to the clinic, where Nurse Thandi showed me it ain't just about moving here and there; I need to get regular exercise that’s just right for me. She said, 'Abuya, even just 30 minutes of brisk walking, five days a week, can help your sugar levels stay balanced.' I was a bit unsure at first, thinking, 'But I’m busy, and I don’t have fancy gym stuff.' She laughed and said, 'No need for fancy gyms – walking around your block or doing some simple exercises at home works fine.' The clarity made me remember: if I want to keep doing my work and walk without gasping, I’ve got to try this.'"\n\nThe next day, I started walking around the block after my morning tea. But, oh boy, after two days, my legs were aching and I thought about giving up. Old habits are hard, neh? Plus, smoking doesn’t help how I feel when I exercise, but I told myself: 'Remember the community meeting coming up. You want to be strong and ready.' So I slowed down my pace, took breaks, and added a few stretches. It wasn’t perfect, but I kept at it because I knew the bigger job was waiting, and I wanted to show up prepared."\n\nNow, a month later, I can actually walk longer with less trouble, and when setting up for my community events, I don’t get so tired. I even started talking to friends about how small changes can add up. I know it’s a journey, but learning to balance exercise with my life and looking after my diabetes feels like winning a small battle every day. Next time the community calls on me, I’ll be ready, and who knows, maybe I’ll keep pushing myself for better health after that too!"	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/767691f0-141b-492c-b70c-acf7e7ba6e3d_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/767691f0-141b-492c-b70c-acf7e7ba6e3d_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/767691f0-141b-492c-b70c-acf7e7ba6e3d_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/767691f0-141b-492c-b70c-acf7e7ba6e3d_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/767691f0-141b-492c-b70c-acf7e7ba6e3d_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/767691f0-141b-492c-b70c-acf7e7ba6e3d_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/767691f0-141b-492c-b70c-acf7e7ba6e3d_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/767691f0-141b-492c-b70c-acf7e7ba6e3d_3.mp3}	How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?	exercise	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:19:59.510455+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Owiti Odhiambo is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Luo male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Lecturer, lives in a Rural (small community or countryside) in Siaya. Disability: he has none. Religion: Christianity (other). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Ohangla dancing. Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet res