           \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1–2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Kosher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Close friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5acce7d7-9765-42ff-b10e-08597d739812</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Can I just walk for exercise, or should I do other types too?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, being a teacher in the city, my days are always busy. I wake up early, rush a cup of strong tea and maybe some buttered bread. Sometimes, I forget to check my blood sugar. My mind’s already in the classroom—marking, sorting lessons, keeping order. Last week, on Monday morning, I had to run a reading circle with the grade sixes. But halfway through, I started feeling a bit dizzy. Maybe it was the bread, maybe I skipped my medicine. I pushed myself to keep going though, thinking, 'Strong men finish what they start, right?' But the lesson didn’t go as planned. I fumbled with my words, and the kids could see I wasn’t myself. I felt so embarrassed that I wanted to hide. Who knew food and medicine could make my work so tough?\n\nLater that day, I went to the local clinic, just down the road. The nurse there, Sister Zama, saw me looking tired. She called me in with a gentle voice, 'Bhuti Luthando, let’s talk.' I told her how I didn’t understand which meals were best or if a bit of imifino now and then was enough. She nodded and said, 'No need for shame. Try umngqusho (samp and beans), imifino, and roasted sweet potatoes. Don’t use too much salt, and check that you are eating breakfast—with soft porridge, not white bread.' She drew me a simple menu in isiXhosa and English, then smiled and added, 'Next time, pack a small apple and water for your break. And don’t forget your medicine.'\n\nThe next morning, I tried Sister Zama’s suggestions. I made soft sorghum porridge, added a little milk, and packed leftover umngqusho for lunch. At first, it felt strange, you know? My friends in the staffroom teased me, 'Luthando, where’s your vetkoek?' But I remembered my big storytelling event was coming up, and I wanted to be sharp. That day, I accidentally left my apple at home and felt hungry before my next class. I started doubting myself, but I remembered why I was doing this. I wanted to perform well at the festival, not just survive the day.\n\nOver the next week, I got used to my new routine. My sugar stopped jumping up and down, and I had more energy in the classroom. On Saturday, I stood in front of a full hall for my praise poetry session. My voice was strong. I remembered every line. Afterwards, my friends hugged me, saying, 'You look good, bhuti!' I smiled, thinking how a little change in my meals gave me back so much confidence. Now, I want to keep learning new ways to live healthier, not just for work, but for all the tomorrows ahead.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5acce7d7-9765-42ff-b10e-08597d739812_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5acce7d7-9765-42ff-b10e-08597d739812_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5acce7d7-9765-42ff-b10e-08597d739812_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/5acce7d7-9765-42ff-b10e-08597d739812_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/5acce7d7-9765-42ff-b10e-08597d739812_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/5acce7d7-9765-42ff-b10e-08597d739812_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/5acce7d7-9765-42ff-b10e-08597d739812_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/5acce7d7-9765-42ff-b10e-08597d739812_3.mp3}	Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?	diet	male	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:23.315946+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gpt-4.1", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Lwazi Nkosi is a 18–24 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Entrepreneur, lives in a Suburban (near city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: he has none. Religion: Agnosticism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication), Herbal or traditional medicine. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: Anxiety or depression. Diet restrictions: Vegetarian. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: No. Physical activity: 5–7 days. Support network: Healthcare provider or counsellor. Health goals: Can you give me some simple meal ideas or a sample daily menu I could follow?. his food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Lwazi Nkosi and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an engaging story aimed at inspiring behavior change. List of relevant fields.\\nThen, Generate a five-paragraph health story written in English that centers around the needs of our persona, which is based on the following question: I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? . \\n Use spoken English, story-telling style, rhetorical question, with simple primary school vocabulary.  \\nThe story will have a small work task that foreshadows a larger work task. This will serve as external motivation for the persona to make health changes. The generated story should be in a default tone.\\n\\nUse only this medical ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start medical ground truth> \\n \\n<end medical ground truth> \\n\\nUse only this cultural ground truth to inform all story decisions and outcomes: \\n<start cultural ground truth> \\n \\n<end cultural ground truth> \\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n- Return only paragraphs written entirely in English.\\n- story should first person account\\n- Do not include explanations or any other language.\\n- All story paragraphs must be in English.\\n- The image prompts should be written entirely in English.\\n\\n\\n- **Paragraph 0:** Select persona fields related to routine, work responsibilities, and basic health knowledge gaps.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 1:**\\n  Introduce daily life and diet habits that reflect **limited awareness** of how health affects work.\\n  The small work task fails due to misunderstanding or neglect of health needs.\\n\\n- **Paragraph 2:**\\n  Help comes from a **formal health source** (clinic or health worker).\\n  Resistance is mild and based on uncertainty.\\n  Advice is clear, simple, personalized, culturally relevant, actionable, and practical.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 3:**\\n  The persona attempts the advice but struggles with **adjusting routines**.\\n  A minor setback occurs due to habit change.\\n  They persist because of the upcoming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "social_support", "audio_prompts": ["In the quiet morning of his apartment, Luthando’s soft sighs and the scrape of a dining chair are heard. His voice, clear but weary, mutters to himself, ‘Ai, I feel dizzy today, why?’ as he pushes aside a half-eaten slice of bread. There’s distant city noise outside. Occasionally, his breath catches in the middle of a phrase. After a pause, he groans lightly and closes a book with a dull thud, saying softly, ‘Who knew bread could cause such trouble?’", "Staff: Female nurse, Sister Zama, is nearby. The room echoes faintly. In a moderate, caring tone, she says in clear South African English, 'Luthando, it’s nothing to be ashamed of, bhuti.' Luthando’s voice answers, uncertain but open, 'Really, is umngqusho okay for me?' The nurse’s pen scratches as she writes. She continues slowly, describing the sample meals in isiXhosa-accented English, her words paced, ending with a comforting chuckle. Luthando’s breathing becomes steadier, indicating growing trust.", "In the lively staffroom, Luthando opens a plastic container with a light ‘snap’ and exhales. His speech is hesitant, ‘Today I only brought umngqusho and forgot my apple, hayi.’ Sipho, sitting close by, laughs warmly and teases, 'Where’s your vetkoek, mfowethu?' Other voices and laughter fill the background. Luthando gives a sheepish chuckle, replying with a playful protest. Chairs scrape and kettles whistle softly, creating a busy atmosphere around them.", "The soundscape is the gentle hum of a Saturday crowd in a hall. Luthando’s voice, mid-range and confident, recites izibongo with rhythmic hand claps echoing from the audience. Breath is deep, speech fluid and strong, ending with the crowd’s light applause. Between lines, Luthando laughs softly, relief in his tone, before declaring, ‘So, healthy living is not so hard after all, hey?’ Slight shuffling as he steps offstage, with a supportive friend in the wings saying ‘Well done, bhuti Luthando!’"], "image_prompts": ["Candid medium shot of Luthando Mvelase, a 60-year-old isiXhosa man, seated at his wooden dining table in a well-kept urban Eastern Cape apartment with sunlight filtering through the curtains. Luthando, in a neat checked shirt and simple black trousers, holds his forehead with one hand while books and mugs scatter beside a barely-touched slice of buttered white bread and a half-filled mug of tea. His posture shows fatigue and mild frustration. Textured laminate floor and faded family photos on the wall emphasise a modest, middle-class environment.", "Staff: Female nurse named Sister Zama, mid-40s, stands beside Luthando Mvelase in a brightly-lit Eastern Cape public clinic consultation room. Luthando sits upright in a plastic chair, shoulders slightly slouched but attentive, hands on knees. Sister Zama gestures at a colourful pamphlet and points to a notepad with a hand-drawn meal plan. Both wear calm, focused expressions. The setting includes a posters of healthy local foods, a metal filing cabinet, and sunlight from a high window. (medium shot)", "Candid wide shot in a teachers’ staffroom: Luthando Mvelase, in a navy cardigan, sits at a round lunch table with another male teacher, Sipho (mid-50s, isiXhosa), who laughs gently. Luthando opens a lunchbox showing umngqusho and an empty apple spot, eyes glancing at his friend. Sipho holds a vetkoek in one hand, gesturing playfully. The background features busy teachers, mugs, and schoolbooks scattered across simple tables. The lighting is bright and lively.", "Natural medium shot of Luthando Mvelase standing with an open stance on a small urban community stage, city hall chairs and isiXhosa posters behind him. Luthando gestures confidently to an unseen audience, mouth open in mid-recital, a slight smile and upright posture. Soft indoor lighting highlights his expressive hands and eyes; one hand rests on his chest. Another friend in the wings claps supportively. The scene radiates pride and connection."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 1.9909880101680755, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are the best gluten-free traditional isiXhosa foods available in urban areas of the Eastern Cape for someone managing diabetes and hypertension?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find cultural events or storytelling (iintsomi) and praise poetry (izibongo) gatherings in the Eastern Cape cities for adults around 55–64?\\",\\n  \\"What diabetes-friendly walking routes or parks are recommended in my Eastern Cape city for people with limited physical activity days per week?\\",\\n  \\"How do Roman Catholic beliefs influence diabetes management or health practices among isiXhosa men in South Africa?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any hypertension or diabetes support groups for teachers or working adults in urban Eastern Cape locations?\\",\\n  \\"What lifestyle changes are most effective for managing type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure in South African men over 55 who have easy access to healthy foods?\\",\\n  \\"Which local clinics or hospitals in the Eastern Cape offer integrated care for hypertension and diabetes, and do they support patients using both Western medication and traditional herbal remedies?\\",\\n  \\"What physical activities besides walking would be suitable and culturally relevant for a 55–64 year old isiXhosa male with type 2 diabetes living in an urban area?\\",\\n  \\"Are there isiXhosa-language educational resources or workshops about gluten-free cooking and diabetes management available in cities in the Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"How does being a teacher in a South African city impact daily stress levels and diabetes control, and what coping strategies work best in this context?\\",\\n  \\"What role does extended family and close friends play in diabetes and hypertension management for isiXhosa men living in urban Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"Are there specific challenges for isiXhosa-speaking male adults in accessing or adhering to regular healthcare for diabetes in Eastern Cape urban settings?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "Relevant persona fields: teaching job (structured routine, work stress), age (55–64), health (type 2 diabetes, hypertension), faith (Roman Catholic), living in an urban Eastern Cape city (easy access to healthy foods), social support from friends/family, insulin and oral medication, strong interest in isiXhosa storytelling and praise poetry, uncertainty about exercise beyond walking, limited local support groups.", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", 