chines and microwaveable meals. I know I should eat better, but it's hard to prioritize when work is piling up. My doctor told me to take breaks and get some exercise, but I'm not sure how to fit that into my busy schedule. I'm trying, but it's tough. Today's small task is to finish this report by the end of the day, and I'm not sure if I'll be able to do it if I take a break. But maybe I should.\n\nI visited my doctor yesterday and she told me to make some lifestyle changes. She said I need to start eating healthier and exercising regularly. I'm not sure if I can do that, but she gave me some advice that seems simple enough. She said I should try to eat more fruits and vegetables, and to take a 10-minute walk during my lunch break. I'm not sure if it'll work, but I'm willing to try. She also told me to stick to my medication and to keep track of my blood sugar levels. I'm a bit nervous about all of this, but I'm hoping it'll make a difference.\n\nI've been trying to follow my doctor's advice, but it's hard to make changes to my routine. I'm used to grabbing a quick bite to eat and rushing back to work. But today, I decided to take a walk during my lunch break. It was nice to get some fresh air and stretch my legs. I felt a bit more energized afterwards, and I was able to tackle the rest of my tasks with more focus. But I'm not sure if I'll be able to stick to it – old habits die hard, right?\n\nIt's been a few weeks since I started making changes to my lifestyle, and I can feel the difference. I'm not getting as tired as easily, and I'm able to focus better at work. I've also been able to accomplish more tasks without feeling overwhelmed. I'm not saying it's been easy – some days are still tough – but I'm proud of myself for sticking to it. I know I still have a long way to go, but I'm feeling more confident and motivated. Maybe I'll finally be able to tackle that big project I've been putting off.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/