verything done. I love my job at the school in East London, but honestly, by the time I get home, I'm just tired. I try to eat properly, you know, a nice pot of umngqusho with some chicken, or maybe some fish braaied on the weekend. But sometimes, after marking all those tests, I just crave something sweet. A little koeksister with my tea, maybe? Or a slice of cake my neighbour brings over. It’s just a small thing, a little treat before bed. Last week, I was supposed to prepare the class attendance report for the principal first thing on Monday morning. I stayed up late finishing it, had a little something sweet with my tea, and then…well, I couldn’t even focus on the report in the morning. My head was fuzzy, and I kept making mistakes. It took me almost the whole day to fix it, and the principal wasn't impressed. I just thought I was tired, but maybe…maybe it was something else.\n\nMy sister, Nombulelo, she’s a nurse, she noticed I was always a bit sluggish in the mornings. She told me, ‘Nomvula, you need to go to the clinic. You’ve been diagnosed with diabetes, you need to get it checked.’ I was a little scared, to be honest. I thought diabetes was something old people got. But Nombulelo insisted, and she even came with me to the clinic. The sister there, Sister Thandiwe, was so kind. She explained that eating late, especially sweet things, can mess with my blood sugar. She showed me how my body uses sugar, and how too much at night makes it hard for my body to cope while I’m sleeping. She said I should try a small handful of nuts or some Greek yogurt if I’m really hungry, instead of the sweet things. She even wrote down a few easy snack ideas for me, and told me to check with her again in a month. She said, ‘It's not about stopping everything you enjoy, Nomvula, it’s about finding a balance.’ It made sense, but it still felt…difficult.\n\nOkay, so I tried the nuts. It’s not the same as a koeksister, let me tell you! The first few nights were hard. I kept wanting something sweet, and I’d end up sneaking a biscuit or two. Then I remembered the school play was coming up. I was in charge of organizing the costumes, a big job with lots of parents and kids involved. If I was tired and unfocused, it would be a disaster. So I really tried. I started having a small apple with a little peanut butter before bed instead. Some nights I failed, and had a small piece of chocolate, but I tried to make up for it the next day with a walk after school. It wasn't easy, my body was used to that sugar. But I kept telling myself, 'You need to be sharp for the play, Nomvula!'\n\nSlowly, things started to change. I wasn't as tired in the mornings anymore. I could actually concentrate on the attendance reports without getting a headache. The school play went off without a hitch – the costumes were perfect, everything ran smoothly. Even the principal complimented me! And you know what? I started *enjoying* the apple and peanut butter. It felt good to take care of myself. Sister Thandiwe was pleased when I went back for my check-up. My blood sugar was better, and I felt better. I still have a sweet tooth, but now I understand it’s okay to have a little treat sometimes, just not right before bed. Nombulelo keeps encouraging me to join her for walks, and I think I will. Maybe I’ll even try some of those traditional games we used to play as children. It feels good to be in control, and to know that taking care of my health will help me be the best teacher I can be.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c6ef8273-6bae-455d-b433-d284e56b56a8_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c6ef8273-6bae-455d-b433-d284e56b56a8_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c6ef8273-6bae-455d-b433-d284e56b56a8_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/c6ef8273-6bae-455d-b433-d284e56b56a8_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/c6ef8273-6bae-455d-b433-d284e56b56a8_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/c6ef8273-6bae-455d-b433-d284e56b56a8_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/c6ef8273-6bae-455d-b433-d284e56b56a8_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/c6ef8273-6bae-455d-b433-d284e56b56a8_3.mp3}	I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? 	diet	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:29.797553+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gemma-3-27b-it", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Akhona Ndlovu is a 45–54 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Teacher, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: she has none. Religion: Agnosticism. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Traditional dancing (e.g. umxhentso, intlombe). Activities: Exercise or physical activities. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication). Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic or hospital. Other Healthcare conditions: None. Diet restrictions: No Restrictions. Smoking: Yes. Alcohol drinking: Yes. Physical activity: 0 days. Support network: I live alone or have limited support. Health goals: Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?. her food access Easy access to healthy foods. Tone of the output: default.\\n\\nUsing the profile of Akhona Ndlovu and understanding the persona's needs (\\"I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? \\"), identify the most relevant information of the persona's profile that can be leveraged to create an enga