oming larger task.\\n  {max_word_count}\\n\\n- **Paragraph 4:**\\n  Gradual, realistic improvement occurs.\\n  The larger task is accomplished with more confidence.\\n  The persona becomes open to continued health improvement.\\n  {max_word_count}", "language": "en", "story_arc": "social_support", "audio_prompts": ["Nomvula Matshoba, 38, speaking in South African English, with a slightly weary tone, recounting her day. Sounds of papers shuffling and a gentle sigh are audible. The background includes distant sounds of street traffic in East London, indicating a busy urban environment. Her voice is slightly hesitant, expressing a sense of being overwhelmed. A soft clinking sound of a spoon stirring tea can be heard.", "Sister Thandiwe, 45, a healthcare worker, speaking in clear, reassuring South African English to Nomvula Matshoba, 38. Sister Thandiwe's voice is calm and patient, explaining the importance of healthy eating. Nomvula’s voice is heard intermittently, asking clarifying questions with a cautious tone. The audio is recorded in a quiet healthcare clinic, with subtle background sounds of other patients and medical equipment. Sister Thandiwe is nearby, speaking directly to Nomvula.", "Nomvula Matshoba, 38, speaking to herself in a slightly frustrated tone, narrating her struggle to resist a sweet treat. Sounds of a cupboard opening and closing, followed by a muffled sigh. Her breathing is slightly faster, indicating internal conflict. The background is quiet, suggesting she is alone in her kitchen. She whispers 'Just a small piece...' then a pause.", "Nomvula Matshoba, 38, speaking in a cheerful and energetic South African English, describing the success of the school play. Sounds of children laughing and applause in the background. Her voice is filled with confidence and pride. The audio captures the lively atmosphere of a school hall, with the sounds of activity and excitement. A nearby parent is heard briefly congratulating her."], "image_prompts": ["Medium shot of Nomvula Matshoba, 38, a Black African woman, sitting at a wooden kitchen table in a modestly furnished East London home, looking slightly overwhelmed as she stares at a stack of school papers. A half-eaten koeksister and a mug of tea are on the table beside the papers. Soft, natural light from a window illuminates her face, showing fatigue and a hint of worry. Texture of the worn wood table and the slightly crumpled papers.", "Wide shot of Nomvula Matshoba, 38, seated across from Sister Thandiwe, a friendly-looking nurse, in a brightly lit but slightly worn healthcare clinic in East London. Nomvula is leaning forward slightly, listening attentively, with a hopeful but uncertain expression. Sister Thandiwe is gesturing with a pamphlet. The background shows other patients waiting, creating a sense of a busy, community healthcare setting. Focus is on their interaction.", "Medium shot of Nomvula Matshoba, 38, in her kitchen, looking determined as she prepares a snack of an apple slices and peanut butter. She is glancing towards a cupboard where a box of biscuits is visible. The kitchen is simply furnished but clean and tidy. Warm, diffused light emphasizes her focused expression. The texture of the apple skin and the creamy peanut butter are visible.", "Medium shot of Nomvula Matshoba, 38, smiling confidently while overseeing the costume preparations for a school play in a bustling school hall. She is surrounded by children and parents, all engaged in activity. She is pointing and giving instructions with a cheerful expression. Natural light streams through the hall windows, creating a vibrant and energetic atmosphere. Focus on her positive body language and engaged interaction."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 84.23266913340642, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"How does eating traditional isiXhosa meals in the Eastern Cape affect blood sugar in women with type 2 diabetes who follow a gluten-free diet?\\",\\n  \\"What are the best gluten-free, healthy snack options available in urban areas of Eastern Cape, South Africa, for teachers with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any community or religious (Islamic) support groups for women managing diabetes in the urban Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"What late-night snack alternatives are recommended for physically active adults with type 2 diabetes who are trying to maintain stable blood sugar?\\",\\n  \\"Which traditional games common in the Eastern Cape count as exercise activities suitable for women with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How can a teacher working in urban Eastern Cape balance insulin injections and oral diabetes medication with a busy work schedule and fasting during Ramadan?\\",\\n  \\"What local healthcare clinics in my city (Eastern Cape) offer diabetes education in isiXhosa for working women?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any culturally adapted, gluten-free meal recipes for Muslim isiXhosa families in South Africa with diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How does weekly alcohol consumption impact blood sugar control for urban-based isiXhosa Muslim women with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How can family support be leveraged in the isiXhosa culture to help someone stick to their diabetes management routine, especially with easy access to unhealthy foods?\\",\\n  \\"What are the effects of exercising 5–7 days per week on blood sugar regulation for middle-aged women with type 2 diabetes in the Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"How do local customs or religious practices in urban Eastern Cape impact meal timing and blood sugar management during special occasions or holidays for Muslim women with diabetes?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_location", "describe_food", "disability", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "health_conditions", "medication", "healthcare_resources", "healthcare_access", 