     <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5–7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Vegan</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Close friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">9fce192e-ef44-48df-9178-514339d8070b</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I sometimes eat late at night. How does this affect my blood sugar, and what can I do instead? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	Every morning, I wake up like any other teacher — the sun is rising, the school bell is ringing, and I’m already hungry. I grab a loaf of bread from the shop, and it’s white and soft, like the kind my mum used to buy. I eat it with a little butter and a banana. I think, 'Is this good for my body?' But I don’t know. I just know I feel tired after class, and my hands shake a bit. I play football with the boys after school, and we eat chips and soda. I don’t think about sugar or blood sugar — I just eat because I’m hungry. My small work task? To make a poster for the school health fair. I started with a drawing of a banana and a plate, but I didn’t know what to put next. My teacher said, 'Lutho, you need to show how food helps your body.' I didn’t know how. I just drew a big red circle and wrote 'Eat more!' — and that was it. It didn’t feel right.\n\nThen one day, I went to the clinic. I sat with Mrs. Nkosi, a kind lady with glasses and a calm voice. She asked me, 'Lutho, what do you eat every day?' I told her about the bread, the banana, the chips. She smiled and said, 'You’re doing good, but some foods can make your blood sugar go up fast. For a vegan teacher like you, try eating brown rice, lentils, beans, and fresh vegetables — like spinach, carrots, and cabbage. These don’t spike your sugar. And no sugar in drinks!' I said, 'But what about my football game? I need energy!' She said, 'You can have a little energy from a banana, but not too much. And eat it with a spoonful of chia or flaxseed — that’s better.' I didn’t believe it at first. I thought, 'Can I really eat beans and not feel tired?' But she gave me a list with pictures — a plate with brown rice, a green leaf, a red bean. I felt like I was learning something real. I said, 'I’ll try it.' I didn’t know if I could, but I had to try for the poster.\n\nThe next week, I tried the lentils and spinach. I cooked them with a little oil and ate them with a slice of wholemeal bread. I felt full, not tired. I didn’t feel shaky after class. I even had a little energy to help my students with their homework. But one day, I forgot to eat the lentils and ate chips again. I felt dizzy and my hands shook. I thought, 'Oh no, I failed.' But I remembered the poster — I had to show the school how to eat well. So I didn’t give up. I made a new poster: 'Eat Brown Rice, Beans, and Greens — They Keep Your Energy Strong!' I added a picture of a football player eating a bowl of beans with a smile. I showed it to my teacher, and she said, 'Lutho, you’re doing it right.' I felt proud. I knew I could do more — not just for the poster, but for my body and my future work.\n\nNow, I eat beans every day. I even teach my students about it. I show them how to cook lentils with spices, how to eat spinach with a little oil. I play football, and I eat a banana with chia seeds after. My blood sugar doesn’t spike. I feel strong. I don’t feel tired. I even got a chance to lead a health workshop at school — for the whole grade. I told them, 'Lutho Makhanya once didn’t know what food was good for his body. Now he knows. And so can you.' The kids clapped. I felt happy. I didn’t just change my food — I changed my life. And I know, if I keep going, I can help more people.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9fce192e-ef44-48df-9178-514339d8070b_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9fce192e-ef44-48df-9178-514339d8070b_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9fce192e-ef44-48df-9178-514339d8070b_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/9fce192e-ef44-48df-9178-514339d8070b_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9fce192e-ef44-48df-9178-514339d8070b_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9fce192e-ef44-48df-9178-514339d8070b_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/9fce192e-ef