       \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">5-7 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Halal</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Family (parents, siblings, children)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">f5a8c38d-8251-4cbf-a0f7-86a63b0c97c7</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Eating healthy can get expensiveâ€”how can I manage a nutritious diet on a tight budget? And can I still enjoy some traditional foods? </td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, every day starts early for me, setting up my little stall in the busy Kano market. I eat quick meals, often whatever's handy — sometimes leftover tuwo shinkafa and maybe a little soup. But I never really thought about how what I eat or how I move during my day might affect my sugar levels or energy. One afternoon, I tried to stay longer at the market to sell more, but I got tired so fast, and my sugar felt all over the place. I reckoned maybe I just need to push through, but after that day, I knew something was off. How else am I to keep up with my customers if I’m running out of steam?\n\nSo, I went to the local health clinic, and a nurse there explained things differently. She told me it wasn’t about pushing harder but about working smarter with my body. She suggested simple exercises I could do at home using everyday items, like carrying water jugs or stretching while watching my children play. She also talked about adjusting my meals, like adding some moringa leaves or eating beans more often — foods I already know and trust from back home but cooked a bit differently. I was a bit sceptical, thinking, 'How can small moves and simple foods really make a difference?' But she made it sound doable without fancy gyms or expensive foods.\n\nI tried those exercises the next morning, but oh boy, my muscles were tight, and I almost gave up after a few stretches. It isn't easy changing long habits, especially when the market calls early and I must be alert. I also found myself craving the usual heavy meals instead of the lighter ones the nurse mentioned. One day, I even skipped the exercises because I was too tired. Still, with a small event coming up where I had to manage a bigger order of goods at the market, I didn’t want to lose face or energy. So I kept telling myself, 'Just one more day, I can handle this.'\n\nSlowly, things began to click. I started walking around the stall more, stretching my arms and legs between customers, and packing small packets of beans or miyan kuka with extra veggies to eat during breaks. I wasn’t perfect, but I felt less drained by midday. The day I managed the big order, I was surprised at how much more confident and sharper I felt, even after hours on my feet. Now, I see how keeping active and eating right, even just a little differently, helps me run my business better. I’m starting to think maybe this health journey isn’t such a bother after all — who knows what I can do next?	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/f5a8c38d-8251-4cbf-a0f7-86a63b0c97c7_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/f5a8c38d-8251-4cbf-a0f7-86a63b0c97c7_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/f5a8c38d-8251-4cbf-a0f7-86a63b0c97c7_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/f5a8c38d-8251-4cbf-a0f7-86a63b0c97c7_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/f5a8c38d-8251-4cbf-a0f7-86a63b0c97c7_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/f5a8c38d-8251-4cbf-a0f7-86a63b0c97c7_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/f5a8c38d-8251-4cbf-a0f7-86a63b0c97c7_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/f5a8c38d-8251-4cbf-a0f7-86a63b0c97c7_3.mp3}	I don't have a gym—what can I do at home or with everyday items to stay active?	exercise	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:29:32.291945+00	{"label": "EXCELLENT", "model": "gpt-4.1-mini", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Amina Bello is a 18-24 year old, \\nCulture: Hausa female. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. she is a Trader, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Katsina. Disability: she has none. Religion: Islam. Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Storytelling. Activities: Socialising with family or friends. Currently taking Medication?: Insulin injections. Regular healthcare access: Yes. how often: Regularly (at least once a year). Health resources accessed: Local healthcare clinic o