 part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Healthcare provider or counsellor</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">f144cce7-dc1b-4c70-8073-4ebec66335b4</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	You know, every morning I wake up, I just grab whatever’s easiest to eat before heading out. Usually some white bread with jam, maybe a quick tea with sugar. I never really thought much about how these small things affected my work at the clinic or how I felt during the day. Last week, there was this big job to pack medical supplies, and I got all shaky halfway through. I just thought I was tired, but maybe it was my blood sugar dropping because of what I ate, huh?\n\nSo, I decided to talk with the nurse downstairs at the clinic. I was a bit unsure, thinking, 'Will this really make a difference?' She told me about eating stuff that keeps blood sugar steady, like wholegrain bread, fresh veggies, and lean chicken instead of sugary jams or heavily fried foods. Plus, she reminded me about my kosher diet and how I can still eat well without feeling left out. She gave me simple tips, saying, 'Sipho, just try small changes, like swapping white bread for wholegrain and snacking on nuts, then see how you feel.' That made sense, I thought I should give it a try.\n\nChanging my eating took some effort, no doubt. At first, I forgot and reached for the usual bread again, or I felt hungry in the evening and grabbed some biltong that wasn’t quite kosher. One day, I even felt a bit weak and wondered if this was all worth it. But then I remembered the big stock-taking task ahead at the clinic — something I wanted to do well. That pushed me to keep trying, to focus on those healthier choices even when it was a hassle or I felt tempted to go back to old habits.\n\nAfter a few weeks, things started to change slowly but surely. I noticed I had more steady energy during those long workdays, and the stock-taking went smoother than ever. I didn’t feel shaky or tired halfway anymore. It felt good to finish strong, and that gave me confidence that I could keep making these changes work. Now, I’m open to learning more about managing my blood sugar and living a better life while keeping my rituals and kosher way.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/f144