ican English accent, is heard speaking in a slightly rushed tone. ‘Hmm, these spices need a good price, you know? Gotta keep the customers happy.’ Background sounds include the chatter of customers, the clinking of scales, and the rustling of packaging. A subtle, underlying rhythm of traditional African music plays quietly in the background. The voice has a slight weariness, but also a determined quality.", "Sound of a clinic environment. Gentle, calming music plays in the background. Lwandle Nqweniso’s voice, slightly hesitant, is heard speaking to Dr. Zola. ‘Doctor, I don’t really understand… my blood sugar is high? What does that even mean?’ Dr. Zola’s voice, warm and reassuring, responds. ‘It means your body isn’t handling sugar properly, Lwandle. We need to make some changes.’  A faint sound of a medical device beeping quietly in the background. Lwandle’s voice sounds slightly confused and uncertain, punctuated by short pauses.", "Sound of Lwandle Nqweniso walking slowly down a street. His breathing is slightly labored. He mutters to himself, ‘Ten minutes… just ten minutes. It’s hot, man. So hot.’ Siyabonga’s voice, encouraging and upbeat, is heard in the background, ‘Come on, Lwandle! You can do it! Think about those chilli peppers!’ The sound of footsteps, traffic, and distant voices creates a busy urban soundscape. His voice has a tone of mild frustration and self-doubt, gradually shifting to a more determined rhythm.", "Sound of a successful transaction in a small shop. Lwandle Nqweniso’s voice, clear and satisfied, is heard speaking to Mr. Nkosi. ‘Thank you, Mr. Nkosi. I’ll make sure to get you the best quality chilli peppers.’ Mr. Nkosi’s voice, polite and appreciative, responds. ‘Excellent, Lwandle. You’ve earned it!’  The sounds of wrapping paper, a cash register, and the general bustle of a small business create a vibrant atmosphere. Lwandle’s voice exudes a feeling of accomplishment and quiet pride."], "image_prompts": ["Medium shot of Lwandle Nqweniso, a middle-aged man with a warm smile, standing in his spice shop in Khayelitsha. Shelves overflowing with colourful spices and herbs line the walls. He’s wearing a slightly stained apron and a worn hat. He’s gesturing with his hands as he talks, a look of slight frustration on his face. The lighting is warm and slightly dusty, reflecting the busy atmosphere of the shop. Focus on the texture of the spices and the worn wooden shelves.", "Close-up, candid shot of Lwandle Nqweniso, a man in his late 40s, sitting in Dr. Zola's clinic. He’s looking down at a hand with a printed blood sugar chart, a slightly confused expression on his face. Dr. Zola, a woman in her 50s with a kind face, is pointing to the chart with a pen. The clinic is clean and brightly lit, with a calming blue colour scheme. The focus is on the doctor's hand and the chart, conveying a sense of medical advice.", "Wide shot of Lwandle Nqweniso, a man in his late 40s, walking slowly down a busy street in Khayelitsha. He's wearing comfortable, slightly worn sneakers. Siyabonga, a younger man in athletic clothing, is jogging alongside him, encouragingly. The street is filled with people, cars, and colourful market stalls. The lighting is bright and sunny, capturing the energy of the neighbourhood. The scene conveys a sense of struggle and determination.", "Medium shot of Lwandle Nqweniso, a man in his late 40s, standing proudly with a large crate of bright red chilli peppers in front of him. Mr. Nkosi, a well-dressed older man, is smiling approvingly. They are both in the doorway of a small shop. The shop is small and cluttered, reflecting a modest but thriving business. The lighting is warm and inviting, suggesting a successful transaction."], "language_code": "en-ZA", "avg_time_taken": 9.697208734659048, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are the best exercise programs in urban Eastern Cape suitable for IsiXhosa men aged 45–54 with type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there any traditional IsiXhosa dances like umxhentso or intlombe that are recommended for physical activity for diabetic adults?\\",\\n  \\"Where can I find Kosher restaurants or healthy food stores with easy access in urban areas of the Eastern Cape?\\",\\n  \\"How can a high-frequency tobacco smoker with type 2 diabetes manage blood sugar during limited physical activity in city environments?\\",\\n  \\"What local support groups exist for male business owners in Eastern Cape managing type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What are the common health challenges for urban IsiXhosa men aged 45–54 who smoke and drink weekly, and how can they balance diabetes management?\\",\\n  \\"What diabetic-friendly IsiXhosa or South African traditional recipes adhere to Kosher guidelines?\\",\\n  \\"Are there urban Eastern Cape clinics that offer integrated diabetes care and counseling for people balancing cultural activities and business ownership?\\",\\n  \\"How can someone in the Eastern Cape with easy access to healthy food maintain a varied, Kosher diet while controlling type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"What is the impact of participating in traditional dancing like umxhentso on blood sugar levels for Baptist South Africans with diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"Are there city-based programs in South Africa that help adults increase physical activity from 1–2 days to a healthier frequency, especially for diabetics?\\",\\n  \\"What considerations should be taken when using both herbal or traditional medicine and prescribed diabetes medication in the context of IsiXhosa culture?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": ["city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "describe_location", "describe_food", "occupation", "daily_routine", "hobbies", "activities", "medication", "healthcare_access", "healthcare_frequency", "t2d_status", "alcohol", "Question", "generated_queries", "Category", "study_number", "label", "story_arc", "type", "parent", "output_tone"]}
61dfc14a-58f5-4ca3-911a-5860c261f5b1	e321ff7d-3b01-4a85-b816-000000006000			<div>\n    <div class="mt-6">\n\n        <div class="overflow-x-auto border rounded-lg">\n            <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">\n                <thead class="bg-slate-50 font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase">\n                    <tr>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Column</th>\n                        <th class="p-2 border-b">Value</th>\n                    </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">\n                    \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">None</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">40</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which province do you live in? (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Eastern Cape</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your religion?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Christianity (Methodist)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Name</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Lwandle Makhaya</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Gender  (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Female</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Age (select 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">35–44</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Suburban (near city or town)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How would you describe access to food in your local community or area?    (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Easy access to healthy foods</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have a disability? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">I do not have a disability</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What is your current occupation?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Teacher</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What best describes your daily routine right now?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Working adult</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What hobbies or interests do you enjoy regularly? (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Traditional games (varies by region)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Which activities or routines are most important to you? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Exercise or physical activities</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you currently have other health conditions besides diabetes? (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">None of the above</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently taking medication or following a treatment for diabetes?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Insulin injections, Oral medication (e.g., Metformin), Lifestyle changes only (no medication)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What kind of healthcare resources do you regularly access or use?  </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Local healthcare clinic or hospital</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you have regular access to healthcare services?</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How often do you access healthcare services?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Regularly (at least once a year)</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Culture (select all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">IsiXhosa</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Are you currently living with Type 2 Diabetes</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you smoke tobacco products? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Yes</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Do you consume alcohol beverages? </td>\n                    <td class="p-2">No</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">How many days per week do you participate in moderate to vigorous physical activity?  (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">1–2 days</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Select any dietary preferences or restrictions you may have (choose 1)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Gluten-Free</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Who forms part of your regular support network?  (check all that apply)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">Close friends</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">Study Number</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">61dfc14a-58f5-4ca3-911a-5860c261f5b1</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                <tr>\n                    <td class="p-2 font-bold">What are your main personal health goals? (You may select more than one)</td>\n                    <td class="p-2">How often and how long should I exercise to actually improve my health?</td>\n                </tr>\n            \n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n     \n    <style>\n            .guideline-html-content h1 {\n                font-size: 1.1rem;\n                font-weight: 800;\n                margin-bottom: 0.5rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h2 {\n                font-size: 1rem;\n                font-weight: 700;\n                margin-bottom: 0.4rem;\n                margin-top: 1rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content h3 {\n                font-size: 0.9rem;\n                font-weight: 600;\n                margin-bottom: 0.3rem;\n                margin-top: 0.8rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content p {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content ul,\n            .guideline-html-content ol {\n                margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\n                padding-left: 1.25rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content li {\n                margin-bottom: 0.2rem;\n            }\n\n            .guideline-html-content strong {\n                font-weight: 700;\n                color: #1e293b;\n            }\n        </style> </div> 	I used to eat bread with butter every morning before I went to work, and I thought, 'What’s wrong with that?' My blood sugar would go up fast, and I’d feel tired by midday. I didn’t know that bread, even white bread, could make my body work harder. I just thought, 'I’m fine, I’ve been doing it for years.' But then I saw my boss, Thandi, she had a headache and couldn’t focus. She said, 'Lwandle, I need you to help me with the community clean-up this weekend. We’re going to clean the old schoolyard near the bus stop.' I said yes, but I didn’t know it would mean I had to change what I eat. I didn’t think health would matter in work. How could it? I was just a worker, not a doctor.\n\nOne day, at the clinic, a nurse named Nomsa told me, 'Lwandle, you don’t need to eat bread with butter every day. Try eating brown rice or chapati instead. They don’t spike your sugar like white bread does. And you can add a little onion or green pepper, not sugar. Your body will feel better, and you’ll have more energy to work.' I said, 'But I’m used to bread, Nomsa. What if I feel weak?' She smiled and said, 'Try one meal with chapati this week. Just one. You’ll see how you feel.' I didn’t believe it at first, but I thought, 'What if it helps me do the clean-up better? Maybe I can help more than just clean.'\n\nI tried chapati on Tuesday, but I forgot to eat it. I went to work and had bread with butter again. By afternoon, I felt dizzy and my hands shook. I sat down at my desk and said, 'Lwandle, what did I do wrong?' I thought, 'I can’t keep doing this. I need to eat better.' The next day, I told Nomsa about it. She said, 'Just eat chapati with a little curry and a glass of water. No sugar. And try it every day for three days.' I did it. But on Thursday, I was too tired and ate a piece of cake because I was stressed. I felt worse. I thought, 'Maybe I can’t do it.' But then I remembered the clean-up. If I don’t feel strong, I won’t help the community. I had to try again.\n\nAfter five days, I started eating chapati every morning. I added a little green pepper and a small piece of boiled egg. I felt stronger, clearer, and I could walk to the bus stop without feeling weak. When the clean-up day came, I helped carry tools, and I even taught the kids how to sort waste. I saw Thandi smiling and said, 'Lwandle, you’re doing great!' I said, 'I didn’t think I could, but I did it. Maybe I can do more. Maybe I can help others eat better too.' I now know that food isn’t just about taste — it’s about how my body feels. And when I feel strong, I can do more for my family and my community.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/61dfc14a-58f5-4ca3-911a-5860c261f5b1_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/61dfc14a-58f5-4ca3-911a-5860c261f5b1_1.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/61dfc14a-58f5-4ca3-911a-5860c261f5b1_2.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/61dfc14a-58f5-4ca3-911a-5860c261f5b1_3.png}	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/61dfc14a-58f5-4ca3-911a-5860c261f5b1_0.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/61dfc14a-58f5-4ca3-911a-5860c261f5b1_1.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/61dfc14a-58f5-4ca3-911a-5860c261f5b1_2.mp3,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/audios/official/61dfc14a-58f5-4ca3-911a-5860c261f5b1_3.mp3}	Which everyday foods can I eat that won't make my blood sugar spike?	diet	female	overlapped	2026-03-23 13:02:32.9365+00	{"label": "BAD", "model": "Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507", "prompt": "Our persona has the following profile: Sizwe Mbeki is a 25–34 year old, \\nCulture: IsiXhosa male. \\nType 2 Diabetes status: Yes. he is a Teacher, lives in a Urban (city or town) in Eastern Cape. Disability: he has none. Religion: Christianity (Presbyterian). Daily routine: Working adult. Hobbies: Rugby. Activities: Exercise or phy