he best choices. Sometimes I feel tired or sluggish, but I just thought that was because of work. I had a small task at work to finish a project, but I kept putting it off because I didn't have the energy. Unfortunately, I missed the deadline, and my boss was not happy.\n\nI decided to visit my local healthcare clinic to see if they could help me figure out what was going on. The health worker was really nice and explained that my lack of energy could be related to my type 2 diabetes. She told me that I should try to exercise more regularly, but I was hesitant - I didn't know if I could just walk for exercise or if I needed to do other types too. She reassured me that walking was a great start, but I should also try to incorporate other activities, like swimming or cycling, to keep things interesting and prevent boredom.\n\nI tried to take the health worker's advice, but it was harder than I thought. I started walking every day, but I found it difficult to adjust my routine. I would often forget to pack my walking shoes or would get caught up in work and not have time. I had a minor setback when I slipped and twisted my ankle, but I didn't let that stop me. I knew I had a bigger task coming up at work - a major project that required a lot of energy and focus - and I was determined to be ready.\n\nIt's been a few weeks now, and I've been consistently exercising and eating healthier. I've noticed a gradual improvement in my energy levels, and I feel more confident in my ability to tackle challenges. I recently completed the major project at work, and it was a huge success. My boss was impressed, and I felt proud of myself. I'm open to continuing to improve my health and make positive changes in my life. I'm excited to see where this journey takes me.	{https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/22571758-6142-488a-b9ef-8414337fd3c8_0.png,https://prxquaevkggpwryikieg.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/images/official/22571758-6142