ere is one of domestic comfort and happiness."], "image_prompts": ["Medium shot of Chinedu Okafor, a 35-year-old Hausa man, wearing a worn blue shirt and a head wrap, sorting millet in a bustling grain market in Kano. Sunlight streams through the stalls, casting long shadows. He looks tired, with a slight furrow in his brow, and he’s holding a handful of millet, examining it closely. The background is filled with other merchants and customers, creating a vibrant, chaotic scene. The texture of the millet is visible, and the market stalls are laden with various grains.", "Wide shot of Chinedu Okafor, 35, sweating and struggling to lift a heavy sack of sorghum on a dusty road outside Kano. His brother, Musa, a younger man with a concerned expression, is urging him on. The sun is beating down, and the scene is filled with heat haze. Chinedu’s posture is slumped, and he’s visibly struggling, with his face showing discomfort. The scene captures the urgency and the physical exertion of the task. The ground is cracked and dry, reflecting the harsh sunlight.", "Medium shot of Chinedu Okafor, 35, sitting across from Dr. Ibrahim at a simple clinic desk. Dr. Ibrahim, a middle-aged man in a white coat, is pointing to a diagram of the human body and explaining something with a gentle smile. Chinedu looks slightly confused, with a questioning expression on his face. The clinic is small and brightly lit, with basic medical equipment visible in the background. The lighting is soft and clinical, highlighting the doctor’s face and Chinedu’s thoughtful gaze.", "Close-up of Chinedu Okafor, 35, smiling and holding a bowl of Tuwo Shinkafa (a Hausa rice pudding) with a side of cooked okra. The bowl is steaming slightly, and the colours of the dish are vibrant. He’s sitting at a wooden table in a simple Hausa home, his wife, Fatima, smiling warmly beside him. The lighting is warm and inviting, creating a sense of domestic comfort. The focus is on Chinedu’s contented expression and the delicious-looking meal."], "language_code": "en-NG", "avg_time_taken": 9.490534574557573, "task_parent_id": "", "audio_gen_model": "Azure Speech", "image_gen_model": "flux", "generated_queries": "[\\n  \\"What are effective at-home physical exercises suitable for a middle-aged male living in suburban Kano, Nigeria, with limited access to gym equipment?\\",\\n  \\"As a Hausa grain merchant in Kano, what daily activities can I incorporate into my routine to help manage type 2 diabetes?\\",\\n  \\"How can I safely balance weekly alcohol consumption with type 2 diabetes management, considering Hausa cultural practices in Kano?\\",\\n  \\"Are there popular football (soccer) groups or informal matches near suburban Kano for adults aged 35-44?\\",\\n  \\"What are some examples of healthy Nigerian meals with ingredients commonly found in Kano markets that help control blood sugar?\\",\\n  \\"Which Hausa traditional herbs or remedies are known to support diabetes management, and are they safe to use alongside insulin and metformin?\\",\\n  \\"What cultural or community-based exercise options exist for men in suburban Kano who want to be more physically active?\\",\\n  \\"How can easy access to healthy foods in suburban Kano help me create a weekly diabetes-friendly meal plan?\\",\\n  \\"What local resources or programs in Kano offer diabetes education or peer support, especially for men who work full-time?\\",\\n  \\"Are there spiritual or religious considerations I should keep in mind for Friday prayers or Ramadan fasting as a Muslim with type 2 diabetes in Nigeria?\\",\\n  \\"What practical ways can I increase my physical activity beyond 1-2 days per week without needing a gym in Kano?\\",\\n  \\"How do other Hausa-speaking men in suburban areas of northern Nigeria manage type 2 diabetes and stay physically active while working full-time?\\"\\n]", "relevant_profile_info": "", "selected_profile_info": [null, "culture", "city_town", "religion", "name", "gender", "age", "describe_